window.COURSE_SEARCH=[{"number": 1, "title": "Conservation, gradients, and Fick's laws", "url": "chapter-01.html", "text": "A protective coating can fail even when its surface remains intact. Atoms can migrate through the coating, react at an internal interface, and change the substrate. Predicting service life requires an amount balance, a transport law, and boundary conditions. A measured diffusion coefficient supplies only one part of that calculation. Source spine: official lectures 1–3 and the conservation/calculus parts of Tutorial 1 (lecture 5). The transform problems from that tutorial belong to Chapter 2. Examples, derivations, numerical inputs, and diagrams here are original teaching constructions unless identified otherwise. The synthetic coating model is not a measured engineering material. 1.1 Materials processes and the conserved amount L1 12:39 Diffusion is transport associated with microscopic motion and its statistical imbalance across space. Individual atoms can move in both directions while a nonzero net amount crosses an imaginary surface. Even when the net transport vanishes, microscopic motion can continue. A concentration profile describes the spatial distribution of an ensemble; it does not trace an individual atom's trajectory. In a solid, atoms occupy environments constrained by the local structure. An atom may move between interstitial sites, exchange with a vacancy, or travel along a boundary. A vacancy is an unoccupied atomic site; an interstitial atom occupies a position between the regular host sites. Those mechanisms determine rates, but conservation can be established before selecting one. Chapters 5–9 will connect the continuum description to atomic structure and jump statistics. Several materials processes require such transport. Carburization introduces carbon into a material from its surroundings. In interdiffusion , components of contacting materials redistribute and may form new phases. A phase is a region with a specified thermodynamic state and structure; adjacent phases can have different compositions. Oxide growth can require oxygen or metal transport through an existing oxide. These processes have different boundary conditions and driving forces, even though each must account for transported matter. The presence of atomic motion does not establish that diffusion is the rate-limiting step. For example, a growing phase can be limited by delivery through an existing layer, by an interface reaction, or by a coupled sequence. A demonstration of ink spreading in water can also involve convection, the collective motion of the fluid. It is a qualitative motivation for dispersal, not an isolated measurement of molecular diffusivity. We will use an idealized solid coating so that collective fluid flow does not enter the first model. Let a planar coating occupy \\(0\\leq x\\leq L\\) , where position \\(x\\) and thickness \\(L>0\\) are measured in meters. Its cross-sectional area is \\(A\\) , in square meters, and elapsed time is \\(t\\) , in seconds. Let \\(c(x,t)\\) be the amount concentration of one named species, in mol/m³. The amount within a slice from \\(a\\) to \\(b\\) is \\[\nn_{[a,b]}(t)=A\\int_a^b c(x,t)\\,dx. \\tag{1.1}\n\\] The slice boundaries are fixed in the selected reference frame. This matters: if a boundary moves, matter can cross it because the boundary itself sweeps through the material. The first model uses a stationary solid, fixed geometry, a uniform temperature, and no reaction producing or removing the tracked species inside the coating. Reactions at its outer faces may still set boundary fluxes. Concentration must name what is counted. A particle-number concentration has units of particles/m³; a mole fraction is dimensionless and counts one component relative to all components. They are interchangeable only after specifying the total site or molar concentration. We use molar concentration here. Consequently, every flux and source term below counts moles of the same species. 1.2 Derivatives, gradients, and local flux L2 03:23 L3 01:22 A concentration difference alone does not specify a local driving gradient. For a fixed face-to-face concentration difference, a thinner coating has a larger magnitude of the average gradient; its local gradient still depends on the profile. At fixed time, the spatial derivative is the limit \\(\\partial_x c=\\lim_{h\\to0}[c(x+h,t)-c(x,t)]/h\\) , when this limit exists. Its units are mol/m⁴. The partial derivative holds the other variable fixed; \\(\\partial_t c\\) instead measures concentration change at a fixed position. Differentiability is a local assumption about the continuum field. A sharp interface between phases may have a concentration jump, so one cannot insert a derivative across that jump into a smooth-field formula. The flux laws apply inside each phase; interface balances connect them. Even for a smooth graph, a horizontal tangent need not be a maximum or minimum. The function \\(x^3\\) has zero derivative at the origin and keeps increasing. Extrema require an appropriate sign change or additional derivative conditions. A useful arithmetic check concerns the dimensionless function \\(y=2x^2\\) . Its derivative is \\(4x\\) ; at \\(x=1\\) the point is \\((1,2)\\) and the slope is 4. Therefore its tangent is \\(y-2=4(x-1)\\) , or \\(y=4x-2\\) . This explicit calculation corrects inconsistent numbers in the caption discussion near L2 11:36. Tangent construction later supplies chemical potentials from free-energy curves, so the geometric operation deserves a check. Definition 1.1 — molar flux. Let \\(J(x,t)\\) be the signed amount crossing a unit area normal to the positive \\(x\\) direction per unit time, in mol/(m²·s). A positive value means net transport toward increasing \\(x\\) . For a uniform flux over area \\(A\\) , the amount transfer rate is \\(AJ\\) , in mol/s. The simplest constitutive assumption is Fick's first law : \\[\nJ(x,t)=-D\\,\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial x}(x,t),\\qquad D>0. \\tag{1.2}\n\\] The scalar \\(D\\) , the diffusivity or diffusion coefficient of this model, has units m²/s. Multiplying it by the concentration gradient gives the required mol/(m²·s). If the concentration decreases toward positive \\(x\\) , the derivative is negative and the flux is positive. The negative sign expresses transport down concentration for this particular positive scalar closure. Chapter 10 will replace the concentration gradient with a thermodynamic driving force when that closure is inadequate. Equation (1.2) does not require a steady profile. It relates flux to the gradient at the stated position and time. During transient diffusion, evaluate the gradient and flux at the same position and time; either may vary with time. A spatially or composition-dependent \\(D\\) can still be used locally, provided the material model justifies it. Chemical reactions do not invalidate the definition of flux; they introduce source terms into the amount balance. External fields and coupled species generally require additional constitutive terms. In three dimensions, let \\(\\mathbf{x}=(x,y,z)\\) be position and \\(\\mathbf{e}_x,\\mathbf{e}_y,\\mathbf{e}_z\\) the Cartesian unit vectors. The gradient is \\(\\nabla c=\\mathbf{e}_x\\partial_x c+\\mathbf{e}_y\\partial_y c+\\mathbf{e}_z\\partial_z c\\) . For isotropic diffusion, meaning the same scalar response in every direction, \\(\\mathbf{J}=-D\\nabla c\\) . Components must remain attached to their unit vectors; adding three scalar derivatives does not by itself produce a vector. Fick's original 1855 paper, On liquid diffusion , establishes the historical attribution. The continuum formulation and examples developed here are independent derivations. A historical successful constitutive relation does not establish its validity for every solid, driving field, or microscopic time scale. 1.3 From an amount balance to Fick's second law L3 06:28 Take an arbitrary fixed slice \\([a,b]\\) of the coating. Its left face receives \\(AJ(a,t)\\) moles per second, while its right face loses \\(AJ(b,t)\\) . If \\(S(x,t)\\) is the rate of local production per volume, in mol/(m³·s), inflow minus outflow plus production gives \\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\left[A\\int_a^b c\\,dx\\right]\n=A[J(a,t)-J(b,t)]+A\\int_a^b S\\,dx. \\tag{1.3}\n\\] Both sides have units mol/s. A missing slice volume in a local accumulation term would make the units disagree. This is the deliberate diagnostic in L3 near 10:38: a concentration rate must be multiplied by volume before it can be compared with total transfer rates. Dividing by area and using the fundamental theorem of calculus transforms (1.3) into \\(\\int_a^b(\\partial_t c+\\partial_x J-S)dx=0\\) . For smooth fields, an integral vanishing over every slice implies that its continuous integrand vanishes: Derived balance 1.1. \\[\n\\partial_t c=-\\partial_x J+S. \\tag{1.4}\n\\]\nThis local conservation statement does not yet select a diffusion mechanism. For a nonreacting species set \\(S=0\\) . Substitution of the constitutive relation (1.2) then gives the diffusion equation: \\[\n\\partial_t c=\\partial_x\\!\\left(D\\partial_x c\\right).\n\\quad\\text{If }D\\text{ is constant:}\\quad\n\\partial_t c=D\\partial_{xx}c. \\tag{1.5}\n\\] The last simplification requires constancy in space, including any indirect spatial dependence through composition or temperature. If \\(D=D(c)\\) is differentiable, the right side is \\(D(c)\\partial_{xx}c+D'(c)(\\partial_x c)^2\\) . Dropping the second term changes the model. A prescribed diffusivity depending on position gives a linear variable-coefficient equation; dependence on the unknown concentration generally makes the equation nonlinear. The sign has a direct physical check. At a smooth local concentration maximum, a constant- \\(D\\) profile has \\(\\partial_{xx}c\\leq0\\) , so its concentration cannot increase through source-free diffusion alone. At a minimum the inequality reverses. Flattening peaks and filling troughs are consequences of the local curvature, rather than a rule that concentration must decrease at every position. Solute entering from a boundary can raise concentrations throughout part of the sample. Integrating (1.4) over the full coating gives \\(d n_{[0,L]}/dt=A[J(0,t)-J(L,t)]\\) when \\(S=0\\) . Impermeable faces have zero normal flux and therefore conserve total amount. Holding a nonzero surface concentration instead permits exchange with an external reservoir. In that case the amount inside the specimen need not remain constant, even though the specimen-plus-reservoir system conserves the species. In three dimensions, the corresponding equation is \\(\\partial_t c=-\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{J}+S\\) . Here \\(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{J}=\\partial_xJ_x+\\partial_yJ_y+\\partial_zJ_z\\) is the divergence , the net outward flux per volume in an infinitesimal limit. With isotropic constant diffusivity and no sources, \\(\\partial_t c=D\\nabla^2c\\) , where \\(\\nabla^2c=\\partial_{xx}c+\\partial_{yy}c+\\partial_{zz}c\\) . Geometry will change the coordinate expression of this operator, not the conservation principle. The executable laboratory also checks a discrete version of conservation without assuming a particular flux law. For adjacent cells, the outgoing face flux of one cell is the incoming face flux of the next. Summing the cell balances cancels all interior faces. Only the two external face fluxes remain. This cancellation, called telescoping , is the reason a conservative numerical method can preserve global amount even before its local transport approximation has converged. Figure 1.1 shows a profile that changes while preserving its amount. To verify the illustration, define dimensionless position \\(\\xi=x/L\\) , dimensionless time \\(\\tau=Dt/L^2\\) , and normalized concentration \\(u=c/c_{\\rm ref}\\) , where \\(c_{\\rm ref}>0\\) is a reference concentration. The function \\(u=1+\\tfrac12\\cos(\\pi\\xi)e^{-\\pi^2\\tau}\\) satisfies \\(\\partial_\\tau u=\\partial_{\\xi\\xi}u\\) by differentiation. Its derivative in \\(\\xi\\) vanishes at both faces, and integrating its cosine term from zero to one gives zero. Its mean therefore remains one while its peak decays. Figure 1.1. A changing profile with conserved mean A closed planar specimen starts with normalized concentration 1 plus one-half cosine pi x over L. The cosine amplitude decays exponentially. The mean remains one; there is no flux through the faces. 1.5 1.0 0.5 0 1 Position x / L Concentration c / c(ref) Play Reset Diffusion time τ Playback speed 0.5× 1× 2× τ = 0.000; mean = 1.000 Figure 1.1. Original exact-solution animation for a nonreacting, constant-diffusivity planar specimen with impermeable faces. The gold dashed curve is the initial profile; the green curve evolves. The height above the mean on one side balances the deficit on the other. One playback cycle represents \\(0.5L^2/D\\) seconds, not a material-specific clock. With scripts or motion disabled, the initial profile and the exact formula remain available; print shows the selected static state. 1.4 Steady transport and resistance L3 04:24 A steady state has \\(\\partial_t c=0\\) in the chosen frame. With no internal production, equation (1.4) then requires \\(\\partial_x J=0\\) : the flux is constant across a planar coating of uniform area. Steadiness does not require zero flux. Two reservoirs can sustain a concentration difference and a continuous flow through an unchanging profile. Let the two prescribed face concentrations be \\(c_L=c(0)\\) and \\(c_R=c(L)\\) . For a uniform constant diffusivity, integrate \\(dc/dx=-J/D\\) from one face to the other. This yields \\[\nc(x)=c_L+\\frac{c_R-c_L}{L}x,\n\\qquad J=\\frac{D}{L}(c_L-c_R). \\tag{1.6}\n\\] For a prescribed gradient, doubling \\(D\\) doubles the flux. For prescribed face concentrations, doubling thickness halves the flux. These are different experimental constraints. A claim about dependence on distance must state which quantity is held fixed. A linear profile is a result of this steady, source-free, planar, constant-diffusivity model; it is not the definition of steady diffusion. A layered coating illustrates why the constraints matter. Let layer \\(i\\) have thickness \\(L_i\\) and diffusivity \\(D_i\\) . Assume no interfacial storage or reaction and the same concentration convention in both layers. Further assume concentration continuity at their interface: there is no equilibrium partition jump in this example. Each layer carries the same steady flux and has concentration drop \\(JL_i/D_i\\) . Adding the drops gives \\[\nJ=\\frac{c_L-c_R}{\\sum_i L_i/D_i}. \\tag{1.7}\n\\] The quantity \\(L_i/D_i\\) is a diffusion resistance per area for this concentration convention, with units s/m. A thick slow layer can dominate the sum. An arithmetic average of diffusivities would incorrectly treat successive layers as if they were parallel pathways experiencing the same gradient. If partitioning occurs at the interface, continuity of flux still holds under the stated no-storage assumptions, but continuity of concentration does not; the relation must then include the partition law. For synthetic values \\(L_1=10^{-3}\\) m, \\(D_1=10^{-12}\\) m²/s, \\(L_2=2\\times10^{-3}\\) m, and \\(D_2=4\\times10^{-12}\\) m²/s, the two resistances are \\(10^9\\) and \\(5\\times10^8\\) s/m. Taking \\(c_L=2\\) and \\(c_R=0.5\\) mol/m³ gives \\(J=10^{-9}\\) mol/(m²·s). The interface concentration is \\(2-J(10^9)=1\\) mol/m³. The second layer independently gives \\((1-0.5)/(5\\times10^8)=10^{-9}\\) , verifying continuity. The laboratory's inputs are deliberately synthetic. Its assertions verify the resistance calculation and interface balance, not the suitability of those constants for a real coating. A measured service-life prediction would also require temperature dependence, evolving phases, reservoir behavior, and a failure criterion. The next chapter addresses the initial and boundary data needed before a transient diffusion problem has a unique physical meaning. 1.5 Cylinders, spheres, and geometric conservation L3 15:45 A coating on a wire or spherical particle does not have the same transport area at every radius. Let \\(r>0\\) be distance from the cylinder axis or sphere center. Assume radial symmetry: concentration is independent of angle and, for the cylinder, axial position. The radial component of flux is \\(J_r=-D\\partial_r c\\) , positive outward. Let \\(A(r)\\) be the area of the surface through which this flux passes. A thin radial shell of thickness \\(\\Delta r\\) contains approximately \\(A(r)c(r,t)\\Delta r\\) moles. Inflow minus outflow is \\(A(r)J_r(r,t)-A(r+\\Delta r)J_r(r+\\Delta r,t)\\) . Dividing by shell volume and taking the limit yields \\(\\partial_t c=-A(r)^{-1}\\partial_r[A(r)J_r]\\) . For a cylinder of fixed length, area is proportional to \\(r\\) ; for a sphere it is proportional to \\(r^2\\) . Therefore \\[\n\\partial_t c=\\frac{1}{r^m}\\partial_r\\!\\left(r^mD\\partial_r c\\right),\n\\qquad m=0,1,2\\text{ for planar, cylindrical, spherical symmetry}. \\tag{1.8}\n\\] This derivation exposes the geometric factor rather than treating it as a coordinate-table identity. With constant diffusivity, cylindrical diffusion contains \\(D(\\partial_{rr}c+r^{-1}\\partial_rc)\\) ; spherical diffusion contains \\(D(\\partial_{rr}c+2r^{-1}\\partial_rc)\\) . At the center of a smooth symmetric profile, \\(\\partial_rc(0,t)=0\\) . The apparent coordinate singularity must be treated by its smooth limit, not by dividing a numerical zero by zero. More generally, cylindrical coordinates \\((r,\\theta,z)\\) use azimuthal angle \\(\\theta\\) and axial position \\(z\\) . The gradient is \\(\\nabla c=\\mathbf e_r\\partial_rc+\\mathbf e_\\theta r^{-1}\\partial_\\theta c+\\mathbf e_z\\partial_zc\\) . The factor \\(1/r\\) converts angular change into change per arc length. Constant- \\(D\\) diffusion is then \\(\\partial_t c=D[r^{-1}\\partial_r(r\\partial_rc)+r^{-2}\\partial_{\\theta\\theta}c+\\partial_{zz}c]\\) . Radial symmetry removes the angular and axial terms, recovering (1.8). Steady radial transport illustrates a useful distinction. The total rate \\(\\dot n=A(r)J_r\\) is independent of radius when there is no source. The flux density \\(J_r\\) itself is not constant because the transport area changes. For a hollow sphere with inner and outer radii \\(a<b\\) , and corresponding maintained concentrations \\(c_a,c_b\\) , integration gives \\[\n\\dot n=\\frac{4\\pi D(c_a-c_b)}{1/a-1/b},\\qquad\nc(r)=c_a-\\frac{\\dot n}{4\\pi D}\\left(\\frac1a-\\frac1r\\right). \\tag{1.9}\n\\] The positive denominator gives outward transport for \\(c_a>c_b\\) . To check the thin-shell limit, write \\(b=a+L\\) . Then \\((1/a-1/b)^{-1}=ab/L\\) , so the rate tends to \\(4\\pi a^2D(c_a-c_b)/L\\) when \\(L/a\\to0\\) . This is the planar formula multiplied by the nearly constant spherical area. For a hollow cylinder of length \\(H\\) , the same integration instead gives \\(\\dot n=2\\pi HD(c_a-c_b)/\\ln(b/a)\\) . The logarithm arises from integrating \\(1/r\\) . A linear radial profile would incorrectly impose a constant flux density rather than a constant total transfer rate. Geometry changes the profile even when the underlying material diffusivity is unchanged. 1.6 Directional diffusion and the tensor model L3 25:02 A crystal can present different jump environments in different directions. A scalar coefficient cannot describe that directional response. At fixed temperature and within a linear reciprocal diffusion model, replace it by a symmetric positive-definite matrix \\(\\mathbf D\\) , the diffusion tensor . Its Cartesian components have units m²/s, and \\[\nJ_i=-\\sum_{j=1}^3D_{ij}\\partial_jc,\\qquad\n\\mathbf J=-\\mathbf D\\nabla c. \\tag{1.10}\n\\] Positive definiteness means \\(\\mathbf u^\\mathsf T\\mathbf D\\mathbf u>0\\) for every nonzero real vector \\(\\mathbf u\\) . It ensures \\(\\mathbf J\\cdot\\nabla c=-\\nabla c^\\mathsf T\\mathbf D\\nabla c\\leq0\\) in this concentration-gradient model. The flux can nevertheless have a component perpendicular to the gradient. Down-gradient projection and exact antiparallel alignment are different assertions. The spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices supplies orthogonal principal directions , in which \\(\\mathbf D\\) is diagonal with positive entries \\(D_1,D_2,D_3\\) . For a unit direction vector \\(\\mathbf n\\) , impose a gradient \\(g\\mathbf n\\) , where the scalar \\(g\\) has concentration-gradient units. The flux component along that direction is \\(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf J=-g\\mathbf n^\\mathsf T\\mathbf D\\mathbf n\\) . Thus the projected directional diffusivity is \\[\nD_{\\mathbf n}=\\mathbf n^\\mathsf T\\mathbf D\\mathbf n\n=D_1n_1^2+D_2n_2^2+D_3n_3^2. \\tag{1.11}\n\\] The components \\(n_i\\) in (1.11) are measured along the principal axes and obey \\(\\sum_i n_i^2=1\\) . Therefore the projected coefficient lies between the smallest and largest principal values. If all three equal \\(D_0\\) , every direction has coefficient \\(D_0\\) . If two equal \\(D_\\perp\\) and the third is \\(D_\\parallel\\) , a direction at angle \\(\\vartheta\\) to the third principal axis has \\(D_{\\mathbf n}=D_\\perp\\sin^2\\vartheta+D_\\parallel\\cos^2\\vartheta\\) . For a synthetic two-dimensional example, take \\(\\mathbf D=\\operatorname{diag}(1,4)\\times10^{-12}\\) m²/s and \\(\\mathbf n=(1,1)/\\sqrt2\\) . A gradient \\(1000\\mathbf n\\) mol/m⁴ produces flux components approximately \\((-0.7071,-2.8284)\\times10^{-9}\\) mol/(m²·s). The projected coefficient is \\(2.5\\times10^{-12}\\) m²/s. Projection on the perpendicular direction \\((-1,1)/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(-1.5\\times10^{-9}\\) mol/(m²·s), confirming a transverse component. The code computes these values directly. Three eigenvalues describe the principal strengths of a symmetric tensor. They do not determine its components in an arbitrary laboratory frame without the principal-axis orientation. With an orthogonal matrix \\(\\mathbf Q\\) whose columns are principal directions, the laboratory tensor is \\(\\mathbf Q\\operatorname{diag}(D_1,D_2,D_3)\\mathbf Q^\\mathsf T\\) . Crystal symmetry can reduce the number of independent parameters, but one must state which symmetry and which frame are being used. Boundary constraints can also prevent the free transverse flux assumed in (1.11), so an apparent coefficient in a constrained specimen needs its own boundary-value calculation. The coating model now has a complete local structure: an amount balance and a selected flux law. It still needs an initial concentration and enough boundary information to determine a trajectory. The same diffusivity can produce a Gaussian plume, a maintained-surface penetration profile, or decay of a finite specimen's modes. Those differences are the subject of Chapters 2–4. 1.7 Exercises with worked solutions 1. Amount, concentration, and missing volume A source-free fixed slice has area \\(A\\) , thickness \\(\\Delta x\\) , and incoming and outgoing fluxes \\(J_-\\) and \\(J_+\\) . Derive its average concentration rate and state which quantities must be fixed. Explain why equating \\(A(J_--J_+)\\) directly with a concentration rate is dimensionally wrong. Worked solution The slice amount is \\(A\\Delta x\\bar c\\) , where \\(\\bar c\\) is its volume-averaged concentration. Fixed area and thickness give \\(A\\Delta x\\,d\\bar c/dt=A(J_--J_+)\\) . Thus \\(d\\bar c/dt=(J_--J_+)/\\Delta x\\) . The total transfer rate has units mol/s, while the concentration rate has units mol/(m³·s). Volume is required to connect them. A moving or swelling slice additionally needs the derivative of its volume and the correct relative flux through its faces. 2. A steady profile need not be linear Let a planar source-free layer have prescribed spatial diffusivity \\(D(x)=D_0(1+\\alpha x/L)\\) , with \\(D_0>0\\) and dimensionless \\(\\alpha>-1\\) . Derive its steady flux from the two face concentrations. Recover (1.6) as \\(\\alpha\\to0\\) . Worked solution Conservation makes \\(J\\) constant. Integrating \\(dc=-Jdx/D(x)\\) gives \\(c_L-c_R=J L\\ln(1+\\alpha)/(D_0\\alpha)\\) for nonzero \\(\\alpha\\) . Hence \\(J=(D_0/L)(c_L-c_R)\\alpha/\\ln(1+\\alpha)\\) . The ratio remains positive because numerator and logarithm have the same sign. Since \\(\\ln(1+\\alpha)/\\alpha\\to1\\) , the uniform result follows. The profile is logarithmic in \\(1+\\alpha x/L\\) , so steady state alone does not imply a line. 3. A local steady condition is not a global solution In dimensionless coordinates consider the tutorial field \\(\\mathbf J=(3x^2,2my,z^2)\\) , with constant parameter \\(m\\) . Find the value making accumulation zero at \\((1,1,1)\\) . Does that value make the field source-free steady everywhere? Worked solution The divergence is \\(6x+2m+2z\\) . At the specified point, zero divergence requires \\(6+2m+2=0\\) , so \\(m=-4\\) . Elsewhere the divergence is \\(6x+2z-8\\) , which vanishes only on a plane. A local condition cannot establish a globally steady field. This example adopts dimensionless coordinates because otherwise the polynomial coefficients would need distinct units; a bare polynomial flux is not an SI material law. 4. Compare a spherical shell with a planar estimate For the hollow sphere in (1.9), express the exact rate divided by the planar estimate based on the inner area \\(4\\pi a^2\\) and thickness \\(L=b-a\\) . Explain the sign of the error. Worked solution The exact rate is \\(4\\pi Dab(c_a-c_b)/(b-a)\\) . The inner-area planar estimate is \\(4\\pi Da^2(c_a-c_b)/(b-a)\\) . Their ratio is \\(b/a=1+L/a\\) . The exact magnitude is larger because most shells offer more area than the inner face. As the relative thickness tends to zero, the ratio tends to one. Equal endpoint concentrations make both rates zero, so the ratio should be interpreted through their conductances rather than division by a zero rate. 5. Tensor bounds and directional alignment Prove that the projected diffusivity lies between the smallest and largest eigenvalues of a symmetric positive-definite tensor. Under what condition is the flux exactly opposite a nonzero gradient? Verify the transverse flux in the synthetic example. Worked solution In principal coordinates \\(D_{\\mathbf n}=\\sum_iD_i n_i^2\\) . The nonnegative weights sum to one, so this is a weighted mean of the eigenvalues and obeys their minimum/maximum bounds. Exact antiparallel alignment requires \\(\\mathbf D\\mathbf n=\\lambda\\mathbf n\\) , meaning the gradient direction belongs to an eigenspace. In the example, the perpendicular unit vector is \\((-1,1)/\\sqrt2\\) . Dotting it into the computed flux gives \\(10^{-9}(1/2-4/2)=-1.5\\times10^{-9}\\) mol/(m²·s). A scalar projected coefficient does not encode this transverse component. 6. Diagnose a numerical conservation error A finite-volume program stores one flux per cell face but computes an interior face separately for each neighboring cell. Explain how inconsistent values can create matter. Design a check that detects this without requiring an exact continuum solution, and apply it to the laboratory's face fluxes. Worked solution Each interior transfer must leave one cell and enter the adjacent one with equal magnitude. If the two evaluations disagree, their sum leaves a residual source. Store a single shared face flux, or explicitly assert equality before updating. Then compare the sum of all cell amount changes with the time-integrated external inflow minus outflow. In the laboratory, the face values are \\((0.2,-0.1,0.4,0.05,0)\\) and the four cell widths are 0.25 in a consistent synthetic unit system. Summing \\(-[J_{j+1/2}-J_{j-1/2}]\\) gives \\(0.2-0=0.2\\) . The code returns this value to floating-point precision. This test checks conservation; it does not check that the flux approximation has the right continuum limit. 1.8 Sources and model boundaries The official NPTEL course supplies the topic order. L1 motivates solidification, phase growth, creep, surface treatment, interdiffusion, oxidation, and ionic transport; their detailed mechanisms are developed in later chapters. L2 supplies calculus preparation; L3 supplies flux, continuity, coordinate geometry, and anisotropy; L5 supplies tutorial checks. Source captions do not establish the accuracy of every slide. Corrections here concern explicitly stated mathematical relations, with the argument shown locally. Fick, On liquid diffusion , 1855, pp. 30–39 is linked for bibliographic attribution; the publisher metadata, rather than a claimed complete reading of the historical article, verifies that attribution. The coating resistance, radial balances, tensor example, conservative check, and motion are original constructions. The model omits reactions inside the solid, moving interfaces, variable site density, coupled species, and microscopic memory. Those omissions become explicit changes to the model in the chapters that follow."}, {"number": 2, "title": "Laplace transforms and diffusion initial data", "url": "chapter-02.html", "text": "A coating equation without an initial state cannot predict a heat treatment. A specimen that already contains solute differs from an initially empty one, even if the same surface concentration is imposed afterward. A time transform makes that initial state appear explicitly in the equation to be solved. Source spine: L4 develops elementary Laplace transforms and initial-value problems; L6 justifies parameter differentiation and transforms Fick's second law; the transform and integration exercises in L5 are integrated here. The boundary-value calculations are original developments. Maintained-surface error-function inversion is derived in Chapter 3. 2.1 A transform changes the time variable L4 04:25 An integral transform maps a function to weighted integrals of its values. Its kernel, integration interval, and convergence conditions define the particular transform. The one-sided Laplace transform is suited to a process specified from time zero onward. For a time-dependent function \\(f(t)\\) , define \\[\nF(s)=\\mathcal L[f](s)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}f(t)\\,dt. \\tag{2.1}\n\\] The transform variable \\(s\\) has units s⁻¹ when \\(t\\) is dimensional time, so the exponent is dimensionless. The value \\(F\\) has the units of \\(f\\) multiplied by time. We first use real positive \\(s\\) ; complex inversion extends it to a right half-plane, but the elementary inversions below can be checked directly without contour integration. The improper integral means \\(\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\int_0^T e^{-st}f(t)dt\\) . A sufficient existence condition is local piecewise continuity and exponential order: there are constants \\(K,a,T_0\\) with \\(|f(t)|\\leq Ke^{at}\\) for \\(t\\geq T_0\\) . For real \\(s>a\\) , the tail is bounded by \\(K\\int_{T_0}^\\infty e^{-(s-a)t}dt\\) , which converges. Values on a finite interval remain integrable under the stated local condition. A finite jump is allowed by piecewise continuity; an infinite one-sided limit is not. The tutorial function that equals \\(1/(t-1)\\) just to the right of \\(t=1\\) is not locally integrable there. Multiplication by the finite nonzero factor \\(e^{-s}\\) does not repair its logarithmically divergent integral. Thus convergence is a mathematical condition, not an optional formality suppressed by writing infinity as an integration endpoint. For the constant function \\(f=1\\) , direct integration gives \\(F=1/s\\) for \\(s>0\\) . For \\(f=e^{at}\\) , the integrand becomes \\(e^{-(s-a)t}\\) , so \\(F=1/(s-a)\\) for \\(s>a\\) . The sign of the exponent determines whether the discarded endpoint term actually vanishes. The same expression written outside its convergence domain is not the integral in (2.1). Linearity follows from linearity of integration: \\(\\mathcal L[\\alpha f+\\beta g]=\\alpha\\mathcal L[f]+\\beta\\mathcal L[g]\\) on a common convergence domain. The constants \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) must not depend on time. Linearity will permit individual modes and forcing terms to be transformed separately. It does not make a nonlinear concentration-dependent diffusivity disappear. 2.2 Derivatives retain the initial data L4 14:49 Assume \\(f\\) is locally absolutely continuous: on each finite interval it equals its initial value plus the integral of an integrable derivative. Assume also that \\(f'\\) has a convergent transform in the domain used and \\(e^{-sT}f(T)\\to0\\) . Integration by parts on a finite interval gives \\(\\int_0^T e^{-st}f'(t)dt=[e^{-st}f(t)]_0^T+s\\int_0^T e^{-st}f(t)dt\\) . Taking the limit produces \\[\n\\mathcal L[f']=sF-f(0),\\qquad\n\\mathcal L[f'']=s^2F-sf(0)-f'(0). \\tag{2.2}\n\\] The second identity applies the first to \\(f'\\) and requires corresponding regularity and decay. The initial values are boundary terms at the lower integration limit. Dropping them changes an initial-value problem into a different problem. The dimensional check also fixes their position: \\(sF\\) and \\(f(0)\\) both have the units of \\(f\\) . The trigonometric transforms can be derived as a pair. For real frequency \\(\\omega>0\\) , let \\(S=\\mathcal L[\\sin\\omega t]\\) and \\(C=\\mathcal L[\\cos\\omega t]\\) . Differentiation identities give \\(sS=\\omega C\\) and \\(sC-1=-\\omega S\\) . Eliminating either unknown gives \\[\n\\mathcal L[\\sin\\omega t]=\\frac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2},\\qquad\n\\mathcal L[\\cos\\omega t]=\\frac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2},\\quad s>0. \\tag{2.3}\n\\] The numerator in the sine transform matters. The inverse of \\(1/(s^2+\\omega^2)\\) is \\(\\sin(\\omega t)/\\omega\\) , not \\(\\sin(\\omega t)\\) . This factor must be retained when checking a verbal or captioned inverse-transform expression. Units expose it: integrating a dimensionless sine over time must produce a quantity with units of time. For nonnegative integer \\(n\\) , repeated integration by parts gives another useful relation. The base case is \\(\\mathcal L[1]=1/s\\) . For \\(n\\geq1\\) , the endpoint at zero of \\(t^n\\) vanishes, and exponential decay removes the endpoint at infinity. Thus \\(\\mathcal L[t^n]=(n/s)\\mathcal L[t^{n-1}]\\) , so \\[\n\\mathcal L[t^n]=\\frac{n!}{s^{n+1}},\\quad s>0. \\tag{2.4}\n\\] The factorial counts the successive differentiation factors. The laboratory checks this identity by numerical quadrature for several powers rather than only consulting a symbolic transform table. Such finite checks can detect implementation mistakes; the integration-by-parts recurrence supplies the general result. 2.3 Initial-value problems and inverse transforms L4 18:53 The inverse transform recovers a time function with the given transform, within an appropriate uniqueness class. For the elementary examples here, partial fractions reduce the expression to transforms already derived. The proposed inverse is then checked against both the differential equation and all initial data. In this section and its ODE exercises, time and transform variables are nondimensional after choosing a reference time; numerical coefficients are not material rate constants. First consider \\(y''-y'-2y=0\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\) and \\(y'(0)=0\\) . A prime denotes a derivative with respect to this time variable. Let \\(Y=\\mathcal L[y]\\) . Substituting (2.2) gives \\((s^2Y-s)-(sY-1)-2Y=0\\) . Therefore \\[\nY=\\frac{s-1}{(s-2)(s+1)}\n=\\frac{1}{3(s-2)}+\\frac{2}{3(s+1)},\\qquad\ny(t)=\\frac13e^{2t}+\\frac23e^{-t}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\] At zero, the two contributions sum to one; their derivative contributions are \\(2/3\\) and \\(-2/3\\) , giving zero. Each exponential independently solves the homogeneous equation. The growing term means the transform requires \\(s>2\\) . An algebraic rational expression alone does not record this convergence condition unless we carry it with the solution. The forced example is more sensitive to inverse-transform normalization. Solve \\(y''+y=\\sin2t\\) with \\(y(0)=2\\) and \\(y'(0)=1\\) . Transforming gives \\((s^2+1)Y-2s-1=2/(s^2+4)\\) . Separation into elementary pieces yields \\[\nY=\\frac{2s}{s^2+1}+\\frac{5/3}{s^2+1}-\\frac{2/3}{s^2+4},\n\\qquad y=2\\cos t+\\frac53\\sin t-\\frac13\\sin2t. \\tag{2.6}\n\\] The last coefficient is \\(-1/3\\) because the inverse of \\(1/(s^2+4)\\) contains a further factor \\(1/2\\) . Direct differentiation gives \\(y'(0)=5/3-2/3=1\\) . The homogeneous terms cancel in \\(y''+y\\) , while the last term contributes \\((-1/3)(-4+1)\\sin2t=\\sin2t\\) . Using \\(-2/3\\) for that coefficient fails both checks. The displayed source slide at L4 27:08 has the correct \\(-1/3\\) coefficient; the final captioned verbal correction is inconsistent with that slide. A shifted quadratic denominator is handled by completing the square. Tutorial 1 asks for \\(y''-3y'+4y=0\\) with \\(y(0)=1\\) and \\(y'(0)=5\\) . Its transformed numerator is \\(s+2\\) , and the denominator is \\((s-3/2)^2+7/4\\) . Split the numerator into \\((s-3/2)+7/2\\) . Multiplying a time function by \\(e^{at}\\) shifts its transform from \\(F(s)\\) to \\(F(s-a)\\) , directly from (2.1). Hence \\[\ny(t)=e^{3t/2}\\left[\\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt7}{2}t\\right)\n+\\sqrt7\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt7}{2}t\\right)\\right]. \\tag{2.7}\n\\] At zero, the bracket is one; its derivative is \\(7/2\\) . The exponential adds \\(3/2\\) , giving the required derivative five. The denominator has no real linear factors, but it has complex factors. Completing the square keeps the solution real and identifies its growth rate and oscillation frequency without claiming that factorization is impossible. The laboratory uses exact symbolic differentiation to check the three ODE residuals and initial values. It also transforms the proposed inverse of a tutorial rational function back into the original expression. These checks target different failure modes: initial data can be satisfied by a function that has the wrong forcing, and a differential residual can vanish while the initial constants are wrong. 2.4 Differentiation under the integral sign L6 03:23 The concentration transform still depends on position. To transform a spatial derivative, we must know when differentiation and integration can be interchanged. Fixed integration limits remove endpoint-motion terms, but they do not remove the need for a convergence bound. A sufficient local condition is that the integrand is integrable at one base parameter in the neighborhood, has the relevant parameter derivative, and that this derivative is bounded throughout the neighborhood by an integrable function independent of that parameter. Base-parameter integrability is essential: a parameter-independent nonintegrable function has zero derivative but no finite integral to differentiate. The reason is visible in the difference quotient. For a parameter \\(\\eta\\) and integrand \\(h(\\eta,u)\\) , the mean-value theorem bounds \\([h(\\eta+\\epsilon,u)-h(\\eta,u)]/\\epsilon\\) by a nearby parameter derivative. An integrable common bound permits dominated convergence to pass the limit through the integral. Thus \\(d\\int hdu/d\\eta=\\int\\partial_\\eta hdu\\) under these hypotheses. An improper integral without such control can invalidate the interchange. The lecture's auxiliary example makes this operation concrete. Let \\(u\\in(0,1)\\) and dimensionless parameter \\(\\eta>-1\\) . Define \\[\nG(\\eta)=\\int_0^1\\frac{u^\\eta-1}{\\ln u}\\,du. \\tag{2.8}\n\\] At \\(u=1\\) , numerator and denominator both vanish, but their ratio tends to \\(\\eta\\) ; this singularity is removable. Near zero, the stated range of \\(\\eta\\) ensures integrability. Differentiating the integrand with respect to \\(\\eta\\) gives \\(u^\\eta\\) , since the factor \\(\\ln u\\) cancels. On any compact parameter interval with lower endpoint \\(a>-1\\) , its magnitude is bounded by \\(u^a\\) , whose integral is finite. The justified interchange therefore gives \\(G'(\\eta)=\\int_0^1u^\\eta du=1/(\\eta+1)\\) . Since \\(G(0)=0\\) , integration with respect to the parameter yields \\(G(\\eta)=\\ln(1+\\eta)\\) . In particular, \\(G(3)=\\ln4\\approx1.38629436112\\) . Independent numerical quadrature in the laboratory gives the same value within its integration error estimate. This parameter is an auxiliary mathematical device, not the physical position in the coating. The distinction prevents a misleading unit assignment to \\(u^\\eta\\) . The calculation also shows why changing variables is sometimes less useful than introducing a parameter whose derivative simplifies the integrand. In the diffusion problem the desired interchange has the opposite purpose: it moves the spatial derivative outside the time integral. 2.5 Transforming Fick's second law L6 09:36 Return to dimensional position \\(x\\) in meters and time \\(t\\) in seconds. Let the nonreacting concentration satisfy \\(\\partial_t c=D\\partial_{xx}c\\) with constant \\(D>0\\) , in m²/s. Write the initial concentration as \\(c_0(x)=c(x,0)\\) and define its one-sided time transform \\(C(x,s)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}c(x,t)dt\\) , in mol·s/m³. Assume the required time boundary terms decay and weighted spatial derivatives admit integrable common bounds on each position neighborhood. Equation (2.2) transforms the time derivative into \\(sC-c_0(x)\\) . The parameter-interchange argument transforms \\(\\partial_{xx}c\\) into \\(\\partial_{xx}C\\) . Therefore \\[\nD\\,\\partial_{xx}C-sC=-c_0(x),\\qquad\n\\partial_{xx}C-\\frac{s}{D}C+\\frac{c_0(x)}{D}=0. \\tag{2.9}\n\\] This is an ordinary differential equation in position for each selected transform parameter. The initial spatial distribution becomes a forcing term. Its coefficient \\(1/D\\) in the divided form is essential: every term then has units mol·s/m⁵. The recorded derivation corrects this factor near L6 16:51. Keeping the undivided form alongside it provides a direct dimensional check. If \\(c_0=0\\) , the homogeneous solutions are \\(e^{\\pm kx}\\) , where \\(k=\\sqrt{s/D}\\) has units m⁻¹. On a half-line \\(x\\geq0\\) with bounded concentration as \\(x\\to\\infty\\) , the growing exponential is excluded. If the surface is suddenly maintained at concentration \\(c_s\\) , its transform is \\(c_s/s\\) , yielding \\[\nC(x,s)=\\frac{c_s}{s}\\exp\\!\\left(-x\\sqrt{\\frac{s}{D}}\\right). \\tag{2.10}\n\\] The boundary value applies for positive time; an initially empty interior can coexist with an abrupt boundary change at time zero in the usual limiting interpretation. The transform already displays a penetration length \\(\\sqrt{D/s}\\) . Chapter 3 derives its inverse and the associated \\(\\sqrt{Dt}\\) spreading scale. No arbitrary constant remains after imposing boundedness and the surface condition. A nonzero initial state requires a particular solution. For a uniform initially loaded half-line with concentration \\(c_b\\) , \\(C_p=c_b/s\\) satisfies the inhomogeneous equation. A maintained surface \\(c_s\\) then gives \\(C=c_b/s+(c_s-c_b)e^{-kx}/s\\) . Reusing the zero-initial-state homogeneous solution without this term would incorrectly erase the initial solute. The far-field transform must tend to \\(c_b/s\\) , not zero. A finite domain is also compatible with Laplace transformation. It has two spatial boundary conditions and generally retains both independent spatial solutions before those conditions are imposed. Eigenfunction expansions may expose long-time decay more transparently, but finite geometry does not make transforms inapplicable. What determines the physical solution is the domain, initial state, and boundary data together. The method must preserve all three. 2.6 Exercises with worked solutions 1. Convergence is part of the transform Find the real convergence domain of the transform of \\(e^{at}\\) , with real constant \\(a\\) . Explain why a finite jump is compatible with existence but a positive \\(1/(t-1)\\) singularity to the right of one is not repaired by exponential weighting. Worked solution The tail integral is \\(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(s-a)t}dt\\) . It equals \\(1/(s-a)\\) when \\(s>a\\) . At equality it integrates one forever; below equality it grows, so neither case converges. A bounded jump affects a finite integral on a finite interval. Near \\(t=1\\) , however, the factor \\(e^{-st}\\) remains bounded above and below by positive constants. The singular part behaves as \\(\\int_1^{1+\\epsilon}dt/(t-1)\\) , which diverges. A time-tail convergence factor does not remove a finite-time nonintegrable singularity. 2. Detect the missing frequency factor For the forced initial-value problem (2.6), someone proposes \\(2\\cos t+(5/3)\\sin t-(2/3)\\sin2t\\) . Test its initial derivative and forcing. Give the corrected function and identify the exact transform-table mistake. Worked solution The proposed derivative at zero is \\(5/3-4/3=1/3\\) , not one. Its second derivative plus itself is \\((-2/3)(-4+1)\\sin2t=2\\sin2t\\) , twice the specified forcing. The transform coefficient \\(-2/3\\) multiplying \\(1/(s^2+4)\\) must be divided by the frequency two upon inversion. The corrected sine coefficient is \\(-1/3\\) . It gives derivative one and forcing \\(\\sin2t\\) . Checking only \\(y(0)=2\\) would miss the error because both sine terms vanish there. 3. Recover the tutorial rational function Invert \\(F(s)=(8s^2-4s+12)/[s(s^2+4)]\\) . Show the coefficient comparison and verify the answer by taking its transform. Worked solution Write \\(F=A/s+(Bs+C)/(s^2+4)\\) . Multiplying by the denominator gives \\(8s^2-4s+12=(A+B)s^2+Cs+4A\\) . Thus \\(A=3\\) , \\(B=5\\) , and \\(C=-4\\) . Inversion gives \\(f(t)=3+5\\cos2t-2\\sin2t\\) . Its transform is \\(3/s+5s/(s^2+4)-4/(s^2+4)\\) , exactly the original numerator over the common denominator. The code verifies this identity symbolically. No numerical coefficient from the inconsistent caption expression is accepted without the algebraic check. 4. A shifted quadratic and its initial data Starting from \\(Y=(s+2)/(s^2-3s+4)\\) , derive (2.7). Why is writing a numerator proportional only to \\(s-3/2\\) insufficient? Worked solution The denominator is \\((s-3/2)^2+(\\sqrt7/2)^2\\) . The numerator is \\((s-3/2)+7/2\\) . The first part is a shifted cosine transform; the second is a shifted sine transform with coefficient \\((7/2)/(\\sqrt7/2)=\\sqrt7\\) . This yields (2.7). Omitting the constant part would leave only \\(e^{3t/2}\\cos(\\sqrt7t/2)\\) , whose derivative at zero is \\(3/2\\) instead of five. Completing the square locates the frequency, but matching the whole numerator supplies the initial derivative. 5. A parameter integral and an invalid interchange Derive \\(G(\\eta)=\\ln(1+\\eta)\\) from (2.8), naming a dominating function for the parameter derivative. What fails at \\(\\eta=-1\\) ? Worked solution On a compact parameter interval \\([a,b]\\subset(-1,\\infty)\\) , \\(\\partial_\\eta[(u^\\eta-1)/\\ln u]=u^\\eta\\leq u^a\\) for \\(0<u<1\\) . The integral of this bound is \\(1/(a+1)\\) . Differentiation is justified and gives \\(G'=1/(1+\\eta)\\) . Integrating in the parameter and using \\(G(0)=0\\) gives the result. At minus one the bound would be \\(1/u\\) , which is not integrable. The original integrand itself has a nonintegrable zero-endpoint behavior; the logarithm of \\(1+\\eta\\) also diverges as this boundary is approached from above. 6. Preserve a nonzero initial state A half-line initially has uniform concentration \\(c_b\\) ; its surface is maintained at \\(c_s\\) . Derive the transformed solution and show that its far-field value has the correct units and initial-state interpretation. What happens when the two concentrations are equal? Worked solution The transformed equation is \\(DC_{xx}-sC=-c_b\\) . The constant particular solution is \\(c_b/s\\) . Boundedness removes the growing exponential, giving \\(C=c_b/s+B e^{-x\\sqrt{s/D}}\\) . At the surface, \\(C=c_s/s\\) , so \\(B=(c_s-c_b)/s\\) . The far field tends to \\(c_b/s\\) , the transform of unchanged concentration \\(c_b\\) , with concentration-times-time units. Equal concentrations remove the exponential term entirely; no gradient or transient is created. An initially empty formula would instead predict a spurious redistribution. 7. Substitution before integration by parts Evaluate the tutorial integral \\(\\int t^7\\sin(2t^4)dt\\) . Verify the result by differentiation rather than relying only on the integration steps. Worked solution Set \\(u=t^4\\) , so \\(du=4t^3dt\\) and \\(t^7dt=u\\,du/4\\) . One integration by parts gives \\(\\tfrac14\\int u\\sin2u\\,du=-u\\cos2u/8+\\sin2u/16+C\\) . Substitute back to obtain \\(-t^4\\cos(2t^4)/8+\\sin(2t^4)/16+C\\) . Differentiation produces two opposite \\(t^3\\cos(2t^4)/2\\) terms that cancel; the remaining term is \\(t^7\\sin(2t^4)\\) . The laboratory checks this cancellation exactly. 2.7 Sources and limits L4 , L5 , and L6 supply the transform, ODE, and parameter-integral source spine. The original concentration boundary examples and explicit convergence/units checks are teaching developments. The mathematics is derived here and checked in the executable companion ; no experimental diffusion coefficient is inferred in this chapter. A transform is a solution method, not a physical closure. It preserves a specified linear diffusion model and its initial data. It does not select a microscopic mechanism, recover an unknown diffusivity from insufficient observations, justify an inconsistent boundary condition, or turn a nonlinear constitutive equation into a linear one automatically. Inverse formulas also require a suitable solution class; the direct checks here establish the stated elementary solutions under their explicit assumptions."}, {"number": 3, "title": "Gaussian and error-function diffusion solutions", "url": "chapter-03.html", "text": "A thin tracer deposit and a maintained carburizing atmosphere do not supply the same amount of solute. The tracer deposit is finite; the atmosphere can keep adding material. Their profiles have different amplitudes and amount balances, even when the solid has the same constant diffusivity. Selecting a formula by its shape alone can therefore give a wrong treatment time or inferred coefficient. Source spine: L7's instantaneous plane source, L8's maintained surface and diffusion couple, and the corresponding L10 tutorial problems. Finite-slab modes and the Scilab demonstrations are developed in Chapter 4. The solution checks, parameterized plots, and inverse computations here are original derivations. Where tutorial input values are used, that provenance is stated; they are not certified material constants. 3.1 Specify the source before solving the equation L7 00:20 Use one-dimensional, nonreacting diffusion with uniform constant \\(D>0\\) , in m²/s. Position \\(x\\) is measured in meters, time \\(t>0\\) in seconds, concentration \\(c\\) in mol/m³, and signed flux is \\(J=-D\\partial_xc\\) . The governing equation is \\(\\partial_tc=D\\partial_{xx}c\\) . Its domain and source specification will now change while this local equation remains fixed. An instantaneous plane source deposits a finite amount per area at one location and then supplies nothing further. A maintained surface fixes a concentration while allowing the total supplied amount to grow. A diffusion couple initially joins two regions with different uniform concentrations. In an effectively infinite couple, each side contains an unbounded reservoir in the mathematical approximation; only the amount transferred across a specified plane over a finite time is finite. The infinite-domain approximation is useful when real remote boundaries lie many diffusion lengths away from the region observed. The relevant length will be proportional to \\(\\sqrt{Dt}\\) . Increasing annealing time eventually makes a previously remote boundary important. There is no universal numerical definition of “semi-infinite” independent of the desired error tolerance and measurement location. For comparison, the steady planar membrane from L7 has endpoints \\(c_1,c_2\\) over thickness \\(L\\) . Chapter 1 gives \\(c(L/3)=(2c_1+c_2)/3\\) and \\(J=D(c_1-c_2)/L\\) . Solving that steady boundary-value problem needs no initial profile. A transient problem does. Boundary conditions may themselves vary with time; being prescribed does not make them constant. 3.2 A finite plane dose produces a Gaussian L7 10:33 Let \\(M>0\\) , in mol/m², be the deposited amount per area. Put the source at the origin in an effectively infinite solid. The total amount condition is \\(\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}c(x,t)dx=M\\) . An infinitely thin initial deposit is represented as \\(c(x,0)=M\\delta(x)\\) , where the Dirac delta is defined through \\(\\int\\delta(x)\\varphi(x)dx=\\varphi(0)\\) for a smooth test function \\(\\varphi\\) . It is not an ordinary finite concentration at the origin. Seek an even, smooth spreading profile with the conserved amount and no independent length scale. Define \\(\\xi=x/(2\\sqrt{Dt})\\) and write \\(c=M(Dt)^{-1/2}F(\\xi)\\) . The amplitude factor preserves the possibility of a constant integral while width grows. Substitution into the diffusion equation gives \\[\nF''+2\\xi F'+2F=0,\n\\qquad (F'+2\\xi F)'=0. \\tag{3.1}\n\\] Primes denote derivatives with respect to \\(\\xi\\) . Even symmetry gives \\(F'(0)=0\\) , so the integrated constant is zero. Solving \\(F'=-2\\xi F\\) gives \\(F=Ae^{-\\xi^2}\\) . Normalization requires \\(2A\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-\\xi^2}d\\xi=1\\) . The Gaussian integral is \\(\\sqrt\\pi\\) : squaring the integral and using polar coordinates gives \\(\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-r^2}rdrd\\theta=\\pi\\) . Hence Derived solution 3.1 — infinite plane source. \\[\nc(x,t)=\\frac{M}{\\sqrt{4\\pi Dt}}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{x^2}{4Dt}\\right). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nThe source is a plane in a three-dimensional specimen, uniform along the plane; \\(M\\) is an areal amount, not total moles from a three-dimensional point source. Three checks give the formula physical meaning. Its prefactor has units \\((\\mathrm{mol/m^2})/\\mathrm m=\\mathrm{mol/m^3}\\) . Its integral is \\(M\\) . Its normalized second moment is \\(M^{-1}\\int x^2c\\,dx=2Dt\\) , as follows by the same substitution and one integration by parts. The first moment is zero by symmetry. Thus width grows like the square root of time even though the mean position remains fixed. The singular initial state is recovered in an integral sense. Substitute \\(x=2\\sqrt{Dt}\\xi\\) into \\(\\int c(x,t)\\varphi(x)dx\\) . It becomes \\((M/\\sqrt\\pi)\\int e^{-\\xi^2}\\varphi(2\\sqrt{Dt}\\xi)d\\xi\\) , which tends to \\(M\\varphi(0)\\) for bounded continuous test functions by dominated convergence. Setting the initial concentration equal to zero everywhere would instead lose the entire dose. 3.3 Surface tracers and slope measurements L7 25:53 A thin source deposited at the surface of a half-space \\(x\\geq0\\) has a different normalization. After deposition, assume no further supply and no loss through the exposed surface. The boundary is reflecting: \\(\\partial_xc(0,t)=0\\) . Restrict the even Gaussian to positive positions and double it, so its half-line integral remains \\(M\\) : \\[\nc(x,t)=\\frac{M}{\\sqrt{\\pi Dt}}e^{-x^2/(4Dt)},\\qquad x\\geq0. \\tag{3.3}\n\\] The zero surface derivative verifies the boundary condition; doubling the amplitude supplies the lost negative half of the integral. This is a finite-dose experiment, unlike a maintained nonzero surface concentration. A maintained surface keeps its concentration fixed, whereas the finite-dose surface value in (3.3) decays as \\(t^{-1/2}\\) . At a known annealing time, natural logarithms give \\(\\ln c=\\ln[M/\\sqrt{\\pi Dt}]-x^2/(4Dt)\\) . If a measured activity is proportional to concentration by a position-independent factor, the proportionality changes the intercept but not the slope. A graph of natural-log activity against squared depth therefore has slope \\(m=-1/(4Dt)\\) , giving \\(D=-1/(4tm)\\) . For base-ten logarithms the slope is instead \\(-1/(4Dt\\ln10)\\) . A straight line is evidence compatible with the model, not proof of a unique atomistic mechanism. Initial source thickness, section averaging, instrument resolution, surface loss, multiple paths, and background can alter the profile. A finite-thickness Gaussian initial layer adds its initial variance to \\(2Dt\\) ; confusing that width with diffusion alone biases an inferred coefficient. Chapter 16 develops measurement broadening explicitly. For (3.3), the normalized second moment about the surface is still \\(2Dt\\) , but the mean depth is positive, \\(2\\sqrt{Dt/\\pi}\\) . The centered variance is therefore \\(2Dt(1-2/\\pi)\\) . A half-Gaussian surface profile and a whole-line Gaussian have different centered-variance conventions. Reporting merely a “width” without defining it can conceal this distinction. 3.4 A maintained surface gives an error-function profile L8 00:19 Now let a half-space initially have uniform concentration \\(c_0\\) , with maintained surface value \\(c_s\\ne c_0\\) and far-field value \\(c_0\\) . Define normalized excess \\(\\theta=(c-c_0)/(c_s-c_0)\\) . Its boundary value is one and its initial interior value is zero. Seek \\(\\theta=F(\\xi)\\) with the same \\(\\xi=x/(2\\sqrt{Dt})\\) . Chain differentiation yields \\(F''+2\\xi F'=0\\) . Integrate once: \\(F'=Ae^{-\\xi^2}\\) . The two boundary conditions require \\(F(\\xi)=-A\\int_\\xi^\\infty e^{-u^2}du\\) and \\(-A\\sqrt\\pi/2=1\\) . Define the error function and its complement by \\[\n\\operatorname{erf}z=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_0^z e^{-u^2}du,\n\\qquad \\operatorname{erfc}z=1-\\operatorname{erf}z.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\] The error function is odd because its integrand is even. It is zero at zero and tends to one at positive infinity. Its derivative \\(2e^{-z^2}/\\sqrt\\pi\\) is positive for every finite real \\(z\\) , so it has a unique real inverse on \\((-1,1)\\) . The normalized solution is \\(F=\\operatorname{erfc}\\xi\\) , giving \\[\nc(x,t)=c_0+(c_s-c_0)\\operatorname{erfc}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{Dt}}\\right). \\tag{3.5}\n\\] This independently derives the inverse of the half-line transform in Chapter 2. At the surface the complement is one; far away it tends to zero. At each fixed positive depth it tends to zero as time approaches zero, recovering the initial interior state. The incompatibility of the initial interior concentration and changed boundary at the single corner \\((0,0)\\) is part of the ideal sudden-switch model. Differentiate (3.5). The inward surface flux is \\(J(0,t)=(c_s-c_0)\\sqrt{D/(\\pi t)}\\) . Integrating the excess concentration over the half-line gives uptake per area \\[\nM_{\\rm excess}(t)=2(c_s-c_0)\\sqrt{\\frac{Dt}{\\pi}}. \\tag{3.6}\n\\] One derivation uses \\(\\int_0^\\infty\\operatorname{erfc}u\\,du=1/\\sqrt\\pi\\) , obtained by integration by parts. Differentiating (3.6) then reproduces the surface flux, a separate global balance check. A negative concentration difference gives outward loss instead of inward uptake. The initial \\(t^{-1/2}\\) flux singularity is integrable: finite time supplies a finite amount. Real surface-transfer kinetics can regularize this short-time idealization. A constant gas flow does not by itself establish a fixed solid-surface concentration. Surface reactions, gas-to-solid transfer, and equilibrium partitioning may impose a flux or mixed boundary condition instead. Likewise, composition-dependent diffusivity can change the error-function shape. The profile is exact for the stated scalar constant-diffusivity boundary problem, not a universal carburizing law. 3.5 A diffusion couple is a superposition of plane sources L8 10:33 On the whole line let the initial concentration be \\(c_L\\) for \\(x<0\\) and \\(c_R\\) for \\(x>0\\) . The values \\(c_L,c_R\\) are constant end-member concentrations in the same convention. Subtract the uniform right-side baseline. The initial excess on each infinitesimal source slice at position \\(y<0\\) is \\((c_L-c_R)dy\\) , an areal amount. Linearity permits these elementary sources to be added. With the normalized Gaussian kernel \\(G(x,t)=e^{-x^2/(4Dt)}/\\sqrt{4\\pi Dt}\\) , the excess at observation position \\(x\\) is \\((c_L-c_R)\\int_{-\\infty}^0G(x-y,t)dy\\) . The integration coordinate \\(y\\) labels source position; it is not the observation coordinate. Substitute \\(u=(x-y)/(2\\sqrt{Dt})\\) to obtain \\[\nc(x,t)=c_R+\\frac{c_L-c_R}{2}\\operatorname{erfc}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{Dt}}\\right)\n=\\frac{c_L+c_R}{2}-\\frac{c_L-c_R}{2}\\operatorname{erf}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{Dt}}\\right). \\tag{3.7}\n\\] At negative and positive infinity the solution approaches the respective end members. At the original contact plane it equals their mean. The midpoint equality is a result of this constant-coefficient symmetric scalar model. Real binary interdiffusion with composition-dependent coefficients or unequal partial volumes need not keep a fixed arithmetic midpoint at the original plane. The lecture motivates a dilute species moving much faster than its host. That is one physical route to a simple scalar approximation, not a mathematical requirement that all diffusion couples obey an inequality between two atom-specific diffusivities. Equation (3.7) assumes a single constant coefficient for the represented concentration field. Chapter 13 distinguishes the interdiffusion coefficient governing a binary composition profile from the individual intrinsic and tracer coefficients. The flux through the original plane is \\((c_L-c_R)\\sqrt D/(2\\sqrt{\\pi t})\\) . Integrating it from zero to time \\(t\\) gives transferred amount \\((c_L-c_R)\\sqrt{Dt/\\pi}\\) per area. This equals the excess on the initially right side, found by integrating (3.7) relative to \\(c_R\\) . Although the total amount on an infinite line may diverge when the end members are nonzero, the transferred excess is finite and has a well-defined balance. Figure 3.1 compares the three source classes. Let an arbitrary reference length be \\(\\ell_0>0\\) and define plotted position \\(X=x/\\ell_0\\) and time \\(\\tau=Dt/\\ell_0^2\\) . The finite-dose peak falls as it widens. The diffusion-couple midpoint stays fixed. The maintained surface keeps its boundary value fixed while uptake grows. Figure 3.1. Original analytic comparison, generated by the chapter program . Left ordinate is \\(c\\ell_0/M\\) ; middle is \\((c-c_R)/(c_L-c_R)\\) ; right is \\((c-c_0)/(c_s-c_0)\\) . The shared time colors are defined by the left-panel legend. These static time slices support side-by-side comparison and printing; the curve families are mathematical solutions, not measured profiles. 3.6 An imposed flux supplies a fourth boundary model As an original extension, impose a constant inward flux \\(q\\) , in mol/(m²·s), on an initially uniform half-space of concentration \\(c_0\\) . The boundary condition is \\(-D\\partial_xc(0,t)=q\\) . The transformed excess solves the zero-initial-state homogeneous spatial equation; its boundary derivative fixes the amplitude as \\(q/[s\\sqrt{Ds}]\\) , rather than \\((c_s-c_0)/s\\) . A direct construction avoids needing a new transform table. The spatial derivative \\(w=\\partial_xc\\) itself solves the constant-coefficient diffusion equation, has zero initial interior value, and maintained boundary \\(w(0,t)=-q/D\\) . Equation (3.5) therefore gives \\(w=-(q/D)\\operatorname{erfc}[x/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]\\) . Integrate from position \\(x\\) to infinity, using \\(c\\to c_0\\) , to find \\[\nc-c_0=\\frac qD\\left[\\frac{2\\sqrt{Dt}}{\\sqrt\\pi}e^{-x^2/(4Dt)}\n-x\\operatorname{erfc}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{Dt}}\\right)\\right]. \\tag{3.8}\n\\] The bracket is the integral of a nonnegative complement and is therefore nonnegative for an inward positive flux. At the surface, concentration grows as \\(c(0,t)-c_0=2q\\sqrt{t/(\\pi D)}\\) . Integrating the amount balance gives excess \\(qt\\) per area, because the imposed entry rate is constant. A fixed-flux surface and a fixed-concentration surface cannot generally be imposed simultaneously; their time-dependent concentration/flux responses differ. 3.7 Infer a depth or coefficient with a controlled inverse L10 09:52 A case depth must name a concentration threshold. For (3.5), let \\(c_*\\) lie strictly between the initial and surface values. Define the normalized deficit \\(r=(c_s-c_*)/(c_s-c_0)\\in(0,1)\\) . The target depth satisfies \\(\\operatorname{erf}[x_*/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]=r\\) . Its unique positive inverse gives \\[\nx_*=2\\sqrt{Dt}\\operatorname{erf}^{-1}(r),\\qquad\nt=\\frac{x_*^2}{4D[\\operatorname{erf}^{-1}(r)]^2}. \\tag{3.9}\n\\] The second form is appropriate when choosing a treatment duration. With the same coefficient and concentration threshold, doubling depth requires four times the duration. This scaling is exact within the model and independent of the particular inverse-function value. It fails if temperature, diffusivity, surface conditions, phase state, or target convention changes with the treatment. The caption-stated tutorial inputs \\(c_0=0.25\\) , \\(c_s=1.2\\) , \\(c_*=0.8\\) in a common mass-percent convention, depth 0.5 mm, and \\(D=1.6\\times10^{-11}\\) m²/s give \\(\\operatorname{erf}^{-1}(r)=0.3923898\\) and duration approximately 7.04729 hours. The use of mass fraction assumes the constant-density scalar approximation; it is not a conversion to molar concentration. The program substitutes the computed duration back into the profile and recovers the target. No temperature recommendation follows from this arithmetic alone. The approximation \\(\\operatorname{erf}z\\approx z\\) is not the correct leading Taylor approximation: the leading coefficient is \\(2/\\sqrt\\pi\\) . More precisely, \\(\\operatorname{erf}z=(2/\\sqrt\\pi)(z-z^3/3+\\cdots)\\) . Even if a restricted table suggests numerical proximity to the identity, inverse calculations square the inferred argument and can magnify a shape error. The code gives \\(\\operatorname{erf}(0.5)=0.5204999\\) and \\(\\operatorname{erf}(0.8)=0.7421010\\) , which show that an uncontrolled identity substitution changes direction and size of the discrepancy across the interval. A second tutorial uses couple end members 50% and 40%, target 42.5% at 20 mm after 40 hours. Equation (3.7) requires \\(\\operatorname{erf}z=0.5\\) . Its exact inverse yields \\(D=3.0529296\\times10^{-9}\\) m²/s. Replacing the inverse argument by 0.5 gives \\(2.7777778\\times10^{-9}\\) m²/s instead. The repeated-depth statement remains fourfold time in either calculation, but the inferred coefficient changes. Agreement with a scaling relation does not validate an approximate parameter estimate. 3.8 Exercises with worked solutions 1. A surface width needs a definition Normalize the reflecting half-space thin-source solution. Derive its mean depth, second moment about the surface, and centered variance. Explain why using the whole-line Gaussian variance for every quoted surface “width” is ambiguous. Worked solution The half-line normalized density is \\(p(x)=e^{-x^2/(4Dt)}/\\sqrt{\\pi Dt}\\) . Set \\(u=x/(2\\sqrt{Dt})\\) . Its integral is \\((2/\\sqrt\\pi)\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u^2}du=1\\) . The first moment is \\((4\\sqrt{Dt}/\\sqrt\\pi)\\int_0^\\infty u e^{-u^2}du=2\\sqrt{Dt/\\pi}\\) . The second moment is \\((8Dt/\\sqrt\\pi)\\int_0^\\infty u^2e^{-u^2}du=2Dt\\) . Subtracting the square of the mean gives \\(2Dt(1-2/\\pi)\\) . The whole-line symmetric Gaussian has zero mean and variance \\(2Dt\\) ; a half-line centered variance is smaller. The program independently integrates the moments. 2. Check a maintained-source amount balance Starting from (3.5), derive the surface flux and integrated excess amount. Why does the infinite initial flux not imply an infinite dose at every positive time? Worked solution The chain rule gives \\(\\partial_xc=-(c_s-c_0)e^{-x^2/(4Dt)}/\\sqrt{\\pi Dt}\\) . Thus the inward surface flux is \\((c_s-c_0)\\sqrt{D/(\\pi t)}\\) . Integrating this rate from zero to \\(t\\) gives \\(2(c_s-c_0)\\sqrt{Dt/\\pi}\\) . Integrating the profile in space gives the same answer because \\(\\int_0^\\infty\\operatorname{erfc}u\\,du=1/\\sqrt\\pi\\) . The integral of \\(t^{-1/2}\\) at zero is finite. The ideal flux diverges instantaneously but supplies a finite accumulated dose over each finite interval. 3. A finite initial layer approaches a plane source On the infinite line let \\(c(x,0)=C_0\\) for \\(|x|<a\\) and zero elsewhere, with \\(a,C_0>0\\) . Use Gaussian superposition to derive the profile. Identify its areal amount and its thin-layer approximation. Worked solution Convolution gives \\(c=C_0\\int_{-a}^{a}G(x-y,t)dy\\) . The substitution used for the couple yields \\(c=(C_0/2)\\{\\operatorname{erf}[(x+a)/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]-\\operatorname{erf}[(x-a)/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]\\}\\) . Integrating in \\(x\\) first gives amount \\(2aC_0\\) because every normalized kernel integrates to one. If \\(a\\ll\\sqrt{Dt}\\) on the observation scale, the kernel varies little across the initial layer, so the convolution is approximately \\(2aC_0G(x,t)\\) . This is (3.2) with \\(M=2aC_0\\) . The approximation is not justified at arbitrarily short times for a fixed finite layer. The laboratory checks the finite-layer amount by quadrature. 4. Infer time and interpret a limiting threshold Derive the treatment-time formula (3.9), reproduce the 0.5 mm tutorial result with the exact inverse function, and explain the limits as the target approaches the maintained surface value or the initial value. Worked solution The deficit ratio is \\(r=(1.2-0.8)/(1.2-0.25)=0.4/0.95\\) . Its inverse error-function value is \\(z=0.3923897758\\) . Using meters and seconds gives \\(t=(0.5\\times10^{-3})^2/[4(1.6\\times10^{-11})z^2]\\) , approximately 7.04729 hours. Substitution into (3.5) recovers 0.8 in the common concentration convention. As the target approaches \\(c_s\\) , \\(r\\to0\\) and \\(z\\to0\\) , so a fixed positive depth reaches that exact maintained value only asymptotically. As the target approaches the initial value, \\(r\\to1\\) and the required time tends to zero. These limits assume the initial and surface values remain distinct. 5. A logarithm convention changes the inferred coefficient A depth-profile fit at known time gives slope \\(m_{10}\\) when plotting \\(\\log_{10}\\) activity against \\(x^2\\) . Derive the diffusivity formula and quantify the multiplicative error if that slope is treated as a natural-log slope. Which calibration factors cancel? Worked solution Since \\(\\log_{10}c=\\ln c/\\ln10\\) , the slope is \\(m_{10}=-1/(4Dt\\ln10)\\) . Therefore \\(D=-1/(4tm_{10}\\ln10)\\) . Treating it as a natural-log slope instead gives \\(D_{\\rm wrong}=D\\ln10\\) . A uniform multiplicative activity-to-concentration factor only adds an intercept, as does the unknown finite dose. A depth-dependent sensitivity or additive background does not generally cancel; either can change the shape and fitted slope. 6. Choose between a fixed dose, concentration, and flux A surface experiment reports total excess amount proportional to time while its surface concentration grows approximately as the square root of time. Which of the three half-space source models is compatible with both observations? Show the balance and identify one additional check before inferring a mechanism. Worked solution A finite instantaneous dose preserves its total amount and has a surface concentration decreasing as \\(t^{-1/2}\\) , so it fails both trends. A maintained concentration fixes the surface value and gives uptake proportional to \\(\\sqrt t\\) , so it also fails. A fixed inward flux gives \\(M_{\\rm excess}=qt\\) and \\(c(0,t)-c_0=2q\\sqrt{t/(\\pi D)}\\) , matching both scalings. These observations are compatible with the boundary model, not a unique atomic mechanism. Check the entire scaled profile against (3.8), account for instrumental broadening and surface reactions, or independently measure the imposed entry rate. Several microscopic mechanisms can share the same scalar diffusion coefficient. 3.9 Sources and model boundaries L7 supplies the plane-source and tracer-slope spine; L8 supplies maintained-source carburizing and diffusion-couple superposition; L10 supplies tutorial inverse problems. The finite-layer and constant-flux constructions are original extensions. The executable program evaluates the analytic models and selected caption-stated tutorial inputs; it does not establish the experimental identity or calibration of a source table that has not been visually recovered. All profiles assume scalar constant diffusivity, a specified concentration convention, no reaction in the modeled region, and the stated effectively infinite geometry. The Gaussian plane dose, reflecting surface source, maintained surface, and constant-flux boundary have distinct normalization and amount balances. Finite specimen size, phase changes, concentration-dependent coefficients, coupled species, surface transfer resistance, and measurement convolution require explicit changes to the model. The next chapter replaces the infinite-domain approximation by finite-domain modes and checks a numerical solver against exact solutions."}, {"number": 4, "title": "Finite domains and reproducible diffusion computation", "url": "chapter-04.html", "text": "A finite specimen can become more uniform either by redistributing its solute internally or by losing solute through its surfaces. The concentration curves can look smoother in both cases. Their amount balances distinguish the processes, and the boundary conditions determine which calculation represents the experiment. Source spine: L9's finite slab and homogenization modes, L10's related tutorial checks, and L11's Scilab plotting demonstrations. The explicit finite-difference solver, positivity argument, refinement experiment, and Python implementation are original teaching extensions. They are separate from evaluating an analytic profile in plotting software. 4.1 A finite slab with absorbing faces L9 00:20 Let a planar specimen occupy \\(0<x<L\\) , with full thickness \\(L>0\\) in meters. Concentration \\(c(x,t)\\) is in mol/m³, time in seconds, and constant scalar diffusivity \\(D>0\\) is in m²/s. The initial interior concentration is uniform, \\(c_0>0\\) . At positive time impose ideal absorbing faces: \\(c(0,t)=c(L,t)=0\\) . The local equation remains \\(\\partial_tc=D\\partial_{xx}c\\) . An absorbing face means the environment removes solute quickly enough to maintain its prescribed zero concentration. A vacuum does not establish that idealization by itself: desorption or surface reactions may limit removal. The model also assumes lateral dimensions large enough that edge transport is negligible. At the two initial/boundary corners the uniform initial interior and suddenly zero surfaces are incompatible pointwise. The series solution will recover the initial state inside the open interval and in an integral sense, while satisfying the zero boundaries for positive time. Seek a separated contribution \\(c=X(x)T(t)\\) . On a region where neither factor vanishes, division gives \\(T'/(DT)=X''/X\\) . The left side depends only on time and the right only on position, so both equal a separation constant. A nonzero spatial solution that vanishes at both ends requires a negative constant, written \\(-k^2\\) . The spatial equation is \\(X''+k^2X=0\\) , giving a sine plus cosine. The first boundary removes the cosine, and the second requires \\(k=n\\pi/L\\) for positive integer \\(n\\) . The zero separation constant gives only the zero linear function under these boundaries. A positive separation constant gives hyperbolic functions whose two zero endpoints also force the zero solution. The nontrivial modes are therefore \\(\\sin(n\\pi x/L)e^{-Dn^2\\pi^2t/L^2}\\) . Each has the correct time sign: positive diffusivity makes it decay rather than grow. Linearity permits a sum of these modes. Orthogonality on the interval gives \\(\\int_0^L\\sin(n\\pi x/L)\\sin(m\\pi x/L)dx=(L/2)\\delta_{nm}\\) , where \\(\\delta_{nm}\\) is one if the integer indices agree and zero otherwise. This follows by expressing the sine product as a difference of cosines and integrating. Multiplying the initial expansion by one sine and integrating selects its coefficient: \\[\na_n=\\frac2L\\int_0^L c_0\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)dx\n=\\frac{2c_0}{n\\pi}[1-(-1)^n]. \\tag{4.1}\n\\] Even modes vanish; odd modes have coefficient \\(4c_0/(n\\pi)\\) . The resulting solution is \\[\n\\frac{c(x,t)}{c_0}=\\frac4\\pi\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty\n\\frac{\\sin[(2j+1)\\pi x/L]}{2j+1}\n\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{(2j+1)^2\\pi^2Dt}{L^2}\\right]. \\tag{4.2}\n\\] At every positive time the exponential factors suppress high modes, permitting the needed termwise differentiation away from the initial limit. The index \\(j\\) is dimensionless; \\(L\\) is the full interval thickness. A formula written on a centered interval with half-thickness as its length parameter must be converted before comparing decay times. A factor-of-two length change becomes a factor of four in a diffusion time. 4.2 Mean amount and the long-time mode L9 16:57 Define the spatial mean \\(\\bar c(t)=L^{-1}\\int_0^L c(x,t)dx\\) . This bar denotes a mean, not a Laplace transform. Integrating each odd sine gives \\(2L/[(2j+1)\\pi]\\) . Thus \\[\n\\frac{\\bar c(t)}{c_0}=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty\n\\frac{e^{-(2j+1)^2\\pi^2Dt/L^2}}{(2j+1)^2}. \\tag{4.3}\n\\] The total amount per area is \\(L\\bar c\\) . It decreases toward zero because both boundaries are sinks. At long time the first mode dominates; its relaxation time is \\(\\tau_0=L^2/(\\pi^2D)\\) . The next nonzero mode decays nine times faster. This separation of time scales explains the usefulness of a one-mode approximation after the initial transient. The approximation needs a time criterion. Define dimensionless time \\(\\vartheta=Dt/L^2\\) . In the mean series, the omitted remainder relative to its first term is \\(\\sum_{j\\geq1}(2j+1)^{-2}\\exp(-[(2j+1)^2-1]\\pi^2\\vartheta)\\) . It is bounded above by \\((\\pi^2/8-1)e^{-8\\pi^2\\vartheta}\\) , since the odd reciprocal-square sum is \\(\\pi^2/8\\) . The sum identity also follows from (4.3) at its uniform initial integral limit. One can instead compare converged sums numerically without invoking that closed form. At \\(\\vartheta=0.1\\) , the laboratory gives mean fraction 0.30211809 and first-mode fraction 0.30210560. The relative error against the full sum is approximately \\(4.1370\\times10^{-5}\\) . A short-time use of the same approximation would miss the many modes needed to represent nearly uniform concentration with thin depleted boundary layers. Flux signs provide a second check. The signed left-face flux points toward decreasing \\(x\\) ; the signed right-face flux points toward increasing \\(x\\) . Net outward rate per area is \\(J(L,t)-J(0,t)\\) , not their signed sum. Differentiating (4.2) at both faces yields \\[\nJ(L,t)-J(0,t)=\\frac{8Dc_0}{L}\n\\sum_{j=0}^\\infty e^{-(2j+1)^2\\pi^2Dt/L^2}\n=-L\\frac{d\\bar c}{dt}. \\tag{4.4}\n\\] The final equality follows independently by differentiating the mean series. The code also checks it with a centered finite difference of the mean. At \\(\\vartheta=0.1\\) , the dimensionless outward rate in units \\(Dc_0/L\\) is 2.98277293. A negative amount derivative and positive outward loss are consistent descriptions of the same balance. If both surfaces instead maintain the same nonzero concentration \\(c_s\\) , subtract it first. The difference \\(c-c_s\\) has zero boundary values and initial value \\(c_0-c_s\\) , so (4.2) applies to that difference. The final uniform concentration is \\(c_s\\) , approached from above during desorption and from below during absorption. The reservoir can change specimen amount throughout that approach. 4.3 Closed homogenization preserves the mean L9 24:06 A composition modulation inside a closed specimen poses a different boundary problem. For a simple one-wavelength example, set \\(c(x,0)=c_m+A_0\\cos(2\\pi x/L)\\) , with mean concentration \\(c_m>0\\) and amplitude \\(|A_0|\\leq c_m\\) . Its derivative vanishes at both faces, making it compatible with impermeable boundaries. The same function is also compatible with a periodic interval. Substitution shows that the mean remains constant while the amplitude obeys \\(dA/dt=-D(2\\pi/L)^2A\\) . Therefore \\[\nc(x,t)=c_m+A_0\\cos(2\\pi x/L)e^{-4\\pi^2Dt/L^2}. \\tag{4.5}\n\\] The cosine integrates to zero, so the amount per area remains \\(Lc_m\\) . More generally, a Fourier mode of wavenumber \\(k\\) , in m⁻¹, decays at rate \\(Dk^2\\) . If its wavelength is \\(\\lambda=2\\pi/k\\) , its amplitude relaxation time is \\(\\lambda^2/(4\\pi^2D)\\) . Finer composition variations smooth sooner at the same diffusivity. The L11 slide at 22:00 labels an odd sine series of the form (4.2) a homogenization problem. That displayed formula has absorbing endpoints and loses its mean. It cannot describe closed-system homogenization to a nonzero mean. Equation (4.5) supplies a mass-conserving model of that physical question. The distinction is visible in Figure 4.1, not merely a change of terminology. Figure 4.1. Original calculated time slices at \\(Dt/L^2=0.01,0.05,0.2\\) . Left: initially uniform concentration normalized by \\(c_0\\) , with two absorbing faces. Right: \\(c/c_m=1+0.4\\cos(2\\pi x/L)e^{-4\\pi^2Dt/L^2}\\) , with zero face flux. The smoothing curves have different amount balances. The static comparison and reproducible generator expose both boundary models without implying measured material behavior. 4.4 From a Scilab demonstration to a reproducible Python calculation L11 00:20 The source demonstration evaluates analytic expressions on arrays and plots the results. That is different from numerically integrating the diffusion PDE. Both can be useful, but they require different checks. An analytic profile implementation must preserve the formula, units, and source/boundary conditions. A PDE solver must additionally resolve spatial and temporal discretization errors and boundary updates. Scilab's range construction corresponds to creating a NumPy position array. An elementwise square evaluates a function separately at each array coordinate; a matrix product computes a different operation. In Python, NumPy arrays use ** for elementwise powers, whereas ^ is not exponentiation. A loop over times should label the resulting curves by the physical time used in the formula, not merely the loop index. Curve labels and axis units are part of reproducibility. The source Scilab code visible at L11 15:25 uses activation parameter \\(Q=200000\\) J/mol, prefactor \\(D_0=10^{-5}\\) m²/s, and gas constant \\(R=8.314\\) J/(mol·K). It converts Celsius to kelvin by adding 273.15 and days to seconds by multiplying by 86400. These displayed values resolve a caption ambiguity that reduced the activation value to 200. They are illustrative source-code inputs, not a verified diffusivity database for a named steel. The Arrhenius expression is \\(D=D_0e^{-Q/(RT)}\\) , with absolute temperature \\(T\\) in kelvin. Using the displayed inputs at 1000 °C gives \\(T=1273.15\\) K and \\(D=6.2248442\\times10^{-14}\\) m²/s. For one day and depth 1 mm, the normalized maintained-source excess is approximately \\(5.3180\\times10^{-22}\\) . The small value is a consequence of these particular input parameters and threshold location; it is not evidence that diffusion has a sharp finite front. The chapter program has no network access or external side effects when run without --figures ; it returns a JSON record of model checks. With --figures , it writes the original SVG under figures/ . The scientific inputs and assumptions are in the source. Its plotting routines evaluate the analytic solutions; its separate finite-difference routine advances a discretized PDE. The saved output makes those two computations distinguishable. 4.5 A conservative explicit diffusion update As an original numerical extension, introduce equally spaced nodes \\(x_i=i\\Delta x\\) and time levels \\(t_n=n\\Delta t\\) . Let \\(u_i^n\\) approximate concentration at that node and time. Here the integer \\(n\\) indexes time levels, not the spatial eigenmodes in (4.1). Approximate the face flux between neighboring nodes by \\(J_{i+1/2}^n=-D(u_{i+1}^n-u_i^n)/\\Delta x\\) . An interior cell's accumulation is minus the difference of its two face fluxes divided by cell width. A forward time step therefore gives \\[\nu_i^{n+1}=u_i^n+r(u_{i+1}^n-2u_i^n+u_{i-1}^n),\n\\qquad r=\\frac{D\\Delta t}{\\Delta x^2}. \\tag{4.6}\n\\] The mesh ratio \\(r\\) is dimensionless. Sharing one flux value at each face makes interior transfers cancel when cell balances are summed. This provides conservation for a finite-volume implementation with the appropriate boundary accounting. The laboratory uses fixed-value endpoint nodes, so a sum over interior nodes changes by the fluxes through its adjacent boundary faces; it does not claim that the absorbing specimen's amount stays constant. Rearrange (4.6) as \\(u_i^{n+1}=r u_{i-1}^n+(1-2r)u_i^n+r u_{i+1}^n\\) . When \\(0\\leq r\\leq1/2\\) , these weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Each interior update is a convex average, so it stays between the previous neighboring minimum and maximum. Nonnegative initial data remain nonnegative if the boundary updates also preserve nonnegativity. A boundary algorithm that injects a negative concentration can break that property even when the interior ratio is valid. The upper bound is necessary for this interior positivity property over arbitrary nonnegative data. Set a selected interior value to one and its neighbors to zero. The new central value is \\(1-2r\\) , negative if \\(r>1/2\\) . At \\(r=0.6\\) it becomes \\(-0.2\\) . This counterexample is more informative than interpreting any one apparently smooth unstable run as evidence that the time step is safe. A Fourier check gives the related stability mechanism. For a periodic spatial mode \\(u_i^n=G^n e^{\\mathrm i i\\kappa}\\) , where \\(\\mathrm i^2=-1\\) and \\(\\kappa\\) is the dimensionless phase increment, substitution yields \\(G=1-4r\\sin^2(\\kappa/2)\\) . All modes in the continuum phase range have \\(|G|\\leq1\\) exactly for \\(0\\leq r\\leq1/2\\) . At the upper endpoint the alternating mode has \\(G=-1\\) and does not decay. Thus this limiting stability bound does not promise faithful damping of the shortest resolved wavelengths. A fixed finite Dirichlet grid samples a restricted eigenmode set and can have a slightly different sharp spectral bound. The convex-averaging limit remains a simple uniform positivity condition. We do not transfer the Fourier formula to arbitrary irregular grids, nonlinear coefficients, or complicated boundary updates. Those models require their own discrete operators and checks. 4.6 Check a solver by refinement against an exact mode Use dimensionless position \\(X=x/L\\) and time \\(\\vartheta=Dt/L^2\\) . The smooth initial profile \\(u(X,0)=\\sin\\pi X\\) is compatible with zero concentration at both ends. Its exact solution is \\(u=\\sin\\pi X\\,e^{-\\pi^2\\vartheta}\\) . This removes the initial corner discontinuity of the uniform absorbing slab and isolates a clean spatial/time accuracy test. On a grid with \\(N\\) equal intervals, the sampled sine is also an exact eigenvector of the discrete update. Its per-step amplification is \\(G_N=1-4r\\sin^2[\\pi/(2N)]\\) . After \\(m\\) time steps its numerical amplitude is \\(G_N^m\\) . This supplies an independent algebraic check on the implementation, beyond comparing two numerical solvers that might share the same error. At fixed \\(r\\) and final dimensionless time, \\(m\\) scales like \\(N^2\\) . Expanding the sine and \\(\\ln G_N\\) shows that the accumulated exponent differs from \\(-\\pi^2\\vartheta\\) by order \\(N^{-2}\\) . Halving grid spacing should therefore reduce the error by approximately four for this smooth single-mode test, away from accidental cancellation in the error coefficient. The actual run uses \\(r=0.4\\) , final \\(\\vartheta=0.05\\) , and 40, 80, and 160 intervals. Maximum errors are approximately \\(2.1703351\\times10^{-4}\\) , \\(5.4216574\\times10^{-5}\\) , and \\(1.3551534\\times10^{-5}\\) . Consecutive ratios are 4.00308 and 4.00077. These are executed numerical checks of the stated scheme and mode, not a proof of accuracy for every initial state or boundary condition. The cost consequence is immediate. Doubling resolution in one dimension doubles the number of nodes and requires roughly four times as many explicit steps for the same physical time. The arithmetic work therefore grows by roughly eight in this simple implementation. An implicit method can change the stability restriction, but then requires solving a coupled linear system and still needs an accuracy study. Stability, accuracy, conservation, and cost are separate design questions. 4.7 Exercises with worked solutions 1. Derive the odd-mode coefficients A slab initially has uniform concentration \\(c_0\\) , while both surfaces maintain \\(c_s\\) for positive time. Derive the coefficients for the difference from the final state. State the full-thickness convention and explain the initial corner mismatch. Worked solution Let \\(v=c-c_s\\) . Its endpoints vanish and its initial interior value is \\(c_0-c_s\\) . Orthogonality on \\([0,L]\\) gives \\(a_n=(2/L)(c_0-c_s)\\int_0^L\\sin(n\\pi x/L)dx=2(c_0-c_s)[1-(-1)^n]/(n\\pi)\\) . Only odd modes remain. Here \\(L\\) is the full thickness; the slowest decay time is \\(L^2/(\\pi^2D)\\) . The initial interior limit can be uniform even though the positive-time boundary values differ at the two endpoints. Pointwise equality at those corners is not claimed; interior and integral initial convergence are the appropriate statements. 2. Relate mean loss to signed face fluxes Derive (4.4) directly from conservation. For an initially loaded absorbing slab, state the signs of the two Cartesian face fluxes and explain why their signed sum is the wrong measure of total loss. Worked solution Integrating \\(\\partial_tc=-\\partial_xJ\\) across the specimen gives \\(L\\,d\\bar c/dt=J(0,t)-J(L,t)\\) . Thus outward loss is \\(J(L,t)-J(0,t)\\) . Near the left face concentration increases with \\(x\\) , so \\(J(0,t)<0\\) ; near the right face it decreases, so \\(J(L,t)>0\\) . Symmetry makes their signed sum zero even though both faces lose solute. The outward normals have opposite directions, so their outward contributions add as magnitudes in this symmetric desorption problem. The mean series derivative and profile-gradient calculation give the same positive rate. 3. Diagnose an incorrectly labeled homogenization plot A program evaluates the odd sine series (4.2) and calls the result closed-system homogenization. Identify two checks that reveal the mismatch. Give a compatible alternative model with a nonzero conserved mean. Worked solution The series forces zero concentration at both endpoints for positive time, which is an absorbing Dirichlet condition, not impermeability. Its integral from (4.3) decreases to zero, contradicting conservation of nonzero initial amount in a closed specimen. The alternative (4.5) has zero derivatives at the faces and integral \\(Lc_m\\) at every time. Its cosine amplitude decays while its mean survives. A graph becoming flatter is insufficient to distinguish loss from internal redistribution; check the boundary data and integrated amount. 4. Find a positivity failure before running a long simulation Show that (4.6) maps every nonnegative interior data vector to nonnegative values when \\(0\\leq r\\leq1/2\\) , assuming nonnegative boundary values. Construct a counterexample at \\(r=0.6\\) . Does this positivity result alone establish accurate diffusion? Worked solution The update weights are \\((r,1-2r,r)\\) . In the stated range they are nonnegative and sum to one, so each new value is a convex average of nonnegative neighbors. At \\(r=0.6\\) , a nonnegative unit spike surrounded by zeros gives central value \\(1-1.2=-0.2\\) . This one-step counterexample is independent of long-time visual appearance. Positivity does not establish accuracy: a stable but coarse grid can damp a concentration pattern at the wrong rate, and the limiting alternating mode at \\(r=1/2\\) is not damped at all. Refinement against a known solution addresses that separate question. 5. Predict the discrete single-mode solution For nodes \\(i=0,\\ldots,N\\) with zero endpoints, insert \\(u_i=\\sin(\\pi i/N)\\) into the explicit update. Derive its amplitude after \\(m\\) steps and explain how it checks the implementation independently of the continuum solution. Worked solution The sine-addition identity gives \\(u_{i+1}+u_{i-1}=2\\cos(\\pi/N)u_i\\) . Therefore one step multiplies the vector by \\(1+2r[\\cos(\\pi/N)-1]=1-4r\\sin^2[\\pi/(2N)]\\) . After \\(m\\) steps the factor is its \\(m\\) th power. The program compares its time-stepped array with this discrete exact result, detecting indexing, in-place-update, or step-count errors. Comparing that amplitude with \\(e^{-\\pi^2\\vartheta}\\) then tests discretization error. The two checks isolate implementation from approximation. 6. Design a reproducibility and cost check Specify a compact check of a Python counterpart to the source plotting demonstration. Include units, a boundary-value check, and a numerical-refinement test if a PDE solver is added. Explain the cost of doubling resolution for the explicit one-dimensional scheme. Worked solution Record the concentration convention, \\(D_0,Q,R\\) with consistent units, absolute temperature, elapsed seconds, domain, initial concentration, and surface condition. Convert Celsius and days explicitly. Evaluate the analytic maintained-source profile at the surface and far into the solid; it must approach the specified values. Check that its integrated uptake equals time-integrated surface flux. For an added PDE solver, use a compatible exact sine mode, keep the mesh ratio fixed, and compare errors after each grid refinement. The executed errors here decrease by roughly four when spacing halves. Doubling node count requires roughly four times as many explicit steps at the same final time, giving roughly eight times the work in this implementation. These are arithmetic scaling estimates, not measured universal wall times. 4.8 Sources, visual checks, and limits L9 supplies the finite-slab and modulation source spine; L10 supplies supporting tutorial checks, including the steady flux whose distinction from transient uptake is developed in Chapters 1 and 3; L11 supplies the Scilab demonstration. Selected 720p source frames verify the first-mode loss formula (L9 22:03), the displayed Scilab constants and conversions (L11 15:25), and the boundary-condition mismatch on the slide labeled homogenization (L11 22:00). This is a bounded visual inspection, not a complete audiovisual audit. The series assume a fixed planar specimen, constant scalar diffusivity, and the stated ideal boundary conditions. Numerical verification concerns the specified explicit scheme and smooth test mode. It does not certify an industrial heat treatment or a proprietary software simulation. Closed homogenization, absorbing degassing, and maintained-surface uptake retain separate amount balances throughout the notes. Crystal geometry and atomic defects next explain which microscopic paths can produce the continuum rates used so far."}, {"number": 5, "title": "Crystal geometry, sublattices, and defect bookkeeping", "url": "chapter-05.html", "text": "A diffusion coefficient does not tell us which neighboring sites an atom can reach. Before assigning a jump length or counting empty sites, we need a structural model. A geometric hole, a missing host atom, and an atom on the wrong sublattice are different objects. Confusing their counts changes both the predicted jump rate and the inferred activation energy. Source spine: L12–13, with geometric exercises from L15. The ideal lattices, hard-sphere calculations, defect counts, and boundary-spacing example are original teaching constructions following the lecture topics. No radius, defect population, or boundary width below is presented as a measured property of a named material. The vacancy free-energy minimum is developed in Chapter 6. 5.1 A translation lattice needs an atomic basis L12 05:33 Imagine comparing two solids that share the same cube-shaped drawing. In one, every marked position contains the same chemical species. In the other, the corners contain species A and the cube center contains species B. A displacement from a corner to the center preserves the first pattern if all those positions are equivalent; it exchanges A and B in the second. A geometrical drawing alone therefore does not specify the crystal's chemical translation symmetry. A translation lattice is the set of vectors \\(n_1\\mathbf a_1+n_2\\mathbf a_2+n_3\\mathbf a_3\\), where the three primitive vectors \\(\\mathbf a_i\\) are linearly independent and the \\(n_i\\) are integers. A basis or motif specifies the atoms and their positions repeated at each lattice point. A periodic crystal structure combines the lattice and basis. Lattice points are equivalent mathematical positions; they are not necessarily individual atoms. This distinction agrees with the International Tables discussion of the direct lattice, §2.2.2.1 . A primitive cell spans one lattice point's share of space. Its volume is \\(V_p=|\\mathbf a_1\\cdot(\\mathbf a_2\\times\\mathbf a_3)|\\). A conventional cell may contain several primitive cells because its shape makes symmetry easier to display. For a face-centered cubic lattice, abbreviated FCC, a conventional cube of side \\(a\\) has lattice points at its corners and face centers. Sharing corners among eight cubes and faces among two gives \\(8/8+6/2=4\\) lattice points per cube. Primitive FCC vectors can be chosen as \\((a/2)(0,1,1)\\), \\((a/2)(1,0,1)\\), and \\((a/2)(1,1,0)\\). Their determinant has magnitude \\(a^3/4\\), confirming four primitive cells per conventional cube. The executable companion checks this determinant rather than treating the familiar cubic picture as a primitive cell. A body-centered cubic lattice, BCC, has corners and one body center, giving \\(8/8+1=2\\) points per conventional cube. The seven crystal systems classify lattice metrics and symmetry; the fourteen Bravais lattice types further distinguish translation patterns. Cubic metrics have three equal edge lengths and right angles. Tetragonal metrics have two equal lengths and three right angles; hexagonal axes conventionally have two equal basal lengths with a 120° included angle. Orthorhombic metrics have three independently variable lengths and right angles. Monoclinic and triclinic metrics admit progressively fewer metric restrictions. Trigonal symmetry can be described with a rhombohedral primitive cell or a related hexagonal setting. A choice of coordinates is not an extra Bravais lattice. The lecture also contrasts bonding descriptions. Ionic models emphasize electrostatic interactions between charged species; covalent models emphasize directional electronic bonding; metallic bonding involves electronic states extended over many atoms. Molecular solids can additionally be governed by comparatively weak intermolecular interactions. These descriptions help anticipate which distortions a jump creates, but they do not give a universal ranking of bond strength or diffusivity across categories. Diffusion samples an entire path and its saddle configuration, not simply the equilibrium separation or one bond label. Likewise, hexagonal close packing is a structure with a basis, rather than an additional Bravais type. Close-packed layers stacked ABAB and ABCABC give different structures even though their ideal equal-sphere packing fractions coincide. Chemical order adds another distinction: the ordered corner-A/center-B structure uses a simple-cubic translation lattice with a two-atom basis. The body-center displacement does not preserve its labeled pattern. Chapter 8 will use such structural distinctions when defining which sites are equivalent in a jump network. 5.2 Packing and interstitial sites are geometric models L12 09:42 The hard-sphere model replaces each host atom by a nonoverlapping sphere of radius \\(R\\). This is a geometric idealization, not a literal boundary of its electronic charge density. In ideal FCC packing, neighboring spheres touch along a face diagonal. That diagonal has length \\(a\\sqrt2=4R\\), so \\(a=2\\sqrt2R\\). Four spheres' volumes divided by the conventional-cell volume give \\[\\phi_{\\rm FCC}=\\frac{4(4\\pi R^3/3)}{(2\\sqrt2R)^3}=\\frac{\\pi}{3\\sqrt2}\\simeq0.74048049.\\tag{5.1}\\] Here \\(\\phi\\) is the dimensionless occupied-volume fraction. BCC contact occurs along a body diagonal, \\(a\\sqrt3=4R\\), with two spheres per conventional cell. Consequently \\(\\phi_{\\rm BCC}=\\pi\\sqrt3/8\\simeq0.68017476\\). A larger unoccupied fraction does not by itself prove a larger diffusivity. Jumps must pass saddle configurations, and both bond energies and defect populations can dominate a simple geometric comparison. An interstitial site is a position between the regular host sites where an additional atom can be accommodated. An octahedral site has six surrounding host neighbors in its ideal coordination; a tetrahedral site has four. These names describe the surrounding geometry, not a new chemical species. For the central FCC octahedral hole, two opposite face-center host spheres and an inserted sphere of radius \\(r_o\\) fit along a cube edge length. Thus \\(2R+2r_o=a\\), and \\[\\frac{r_o}{R}=\\sqrt2-1\\simeq0.41421356.\\tag{5.2}\\] For a tetrahedral hole, its center is a distance \\(\\sqrt3a/4\\) from the surrounding FCC sites, giving \\(R+r_t=\\sqrt3a/4\\). Therefore \\(r_t/R=\\sqrt{3/2}-1\\simeq0.22474487\\). Larger inserted atoms can still occupy sites by distorting their surroundings; the ideal sphere fit is a reference geometry, not an absolute solubility criterion. An empty interstitial site also differs from a vacancy: the former was not a regular occupied host site in the reference structure. Counting sites requires the same sharing discipline as counting atoms. FCC has one octahedral hole at the cube center and twelve edge-center holes shared among four cells: \\(1+12/4=4\\) octahedral sites per conventional cube. There is therefore one such site per host atom in this ideal structure. The tetrahedral positions have fractional coordinates whose entries are independently one-quarter or three-quarters, giving eight sites per cube, or two per host atom. Directions and planes provide a coordinate language for jumps. In cubic coordinates a direction \\([uvw]\\) is parallel to \\(u\\mathbf e_x+v\\mathbf e_y+w\\mathbf e_z\\); angle brackets denote symmetry-related directions. Plane indices \\((hkl)\\), called Miller indices , specify reciprocal intercepts in lattice-coordinate units. A zero index means an infinite intercept. Thus the cubic (110) plane cuts the x and y axes at \\(a\\) and is parallel to z. In a noncubic metric, identical numerical indices do not imply the same Euclidean angles as in a cube. The planar number density counts atom centers in a plane per area of a repeating planar cell. For BCC (100), the square repeat contains one net corner atom over area \\(a^2\\), giving \\(1/a^2\\). A (110) rectangular repeat has area \\(\\sqrt2a^2\\), contains one net corner contribution and a body-center atom, and therefore has density \\(\\sqrt2/a^2\\). A (111) triangular cut has area \\(\\sqrt3a^2/2\\) and one-half atom after vertex sharing, giving \\(1/(\\sqrt3a^2)\\). The densest of these three is (110); that comparison alone does not determine the migration barrier along every possible path. 5.3 A Burgers vector measures a closure failure L13 04:25 A dislocation is a line defect around which the local crystalline arrangement cannot be matched to a perfect reference lattice without a closure mismatch. An edge-dislocation picture contains an extra half-plane ending along the defect line. That picture makes the spatial character visible, but a quantitative definition should also work when the line bends or changes character. Construct a closed sequence of lattice steps around the defect using the local crystal directions. Compare it with the same step sequence in a perfect reference crystal. The vector needed to close the corresponding circuit, with a specified traversal/sign convention, is the Burgers vector \\(\\mathbf b\\). It has units of length. It is not a unit vector. Reversing the circuit convention reverses its sign, while the physical dislocation and the magnitude \\(b=|\\mathbf b|\\) remain the same. Let \\(\\mathbf l\\) be a unit tangent to the dislocation line. A pure edge segment has \\(\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf l=0\\); a pure screw segment has parallel \\(\\mathbf b\\) and \\(\\mathbf l\\). A general segment has both characters. Motion of a dislocation can shift one part of a crystal relative to another by its Burgers vector, allowing plastic slip through successive local rearrangements rather than simultaneous rigid displacement of a whole atomic plane. That mechanical role is distinct from the possibility that a dislocation core supplies a fast diffusion path. For a monatomic FCC lattice, the shortest full lattice translations have the form \\((a/2)\\langle110\\rangle\\), with magnitude \\(a/\\sqrt2\\). In BCC they have the form \\((a/2)\\langle111\\rangle\\), with magnitude \\(\\sqrt3a/2\\). These are geometric statements about full translations; partial dislocations and ordered compounds require additional defect bookkeeping. In a synthetic FCC example with \\(a=0.36\\) nm, the hard-sphere radius is 0.12727922 nm and the shortest full Burgers magnitude is 0.25455844 nm. Both follow from the same cell geometry and are computed in the companion. Dislocation density is commonly expressed as total line length divided by specimen volume, with units m⁻². Counting line intersections on a section also produces an inverse-area quantity, but its conversion to length density depends on the distribution of line orientations. Equal units do not make the two measurements identical. A section parallel to a family of straight lines can miss them even when their total length density is substantial. Chapter 21 will treat dislocation transport with an explicitly specified geometry. 5.4 Grain boundaries join differently oriented crystals L13 09:35 A grain is a region with a common crystal orientation, allowing local elastic distortion and defects. A grain boundary separates neighboring grains whose orientations differ. Its structure depends on both the relative orientation and the boundary-plane orientation. A single misorientation angle therefore cannot uniquely identify a boundary, its energy, or its diffusivity. A low-angle symmetric tilt boundary supplies a model whose geometry can be derived. The two lattices rotate by equal and opposite angles around an axis lying in the boundary. A regular array of edge dislocations accommodates their mismatch. Let the total misorientation be \\(\\theta\\), and let \\(d\\) be the spacing between dislocations with Burgers magnitude \\(b\\). Over a boundary interval \\(d\\), the mismatch of the two rotated directions has magnitude \\(2d\\sin(\\theta/2)\\). Requiring one Burgers displacement per interval gives \\[d=\\frac{b}{2\\sin(\\theta/2)}\\simeq\\frac{b}{\\theta}\\quad(\\theta\\ll1\\text{ in radians}).\\tag{5.3}\\] The small-angle expression is geometric, not a universal high-angle structural model. As dislocation cores become closely spaced, their separate-core interpretation loses validity. Boundaries with rotations around an axis normal to the interface are called twist boundaries and require a different dislocation arrangement. The often used low/high-angle division near fifteen degrees is a classification convention, not a sharp microscopic transition shared by every material and boundary. For a synthetic \\(b=0.25\\) nm, equation (5.3) gives \\(d=7.16233606\\) nm at 2° and 1.43421416 nm at 10°. The small-angle values are 7.16197244 and 1.43239449 nm. Figure 5.1 shows why increasing misorientation makes the separate-dislocation picture progressively more crowded. Special high-angle boundaries can have relatively favorable structural matching; a monotone extrapolation from the low-angle array would miss them. Figure 5.1. Original geometric calculation for a symmetric edge-dislocation array, with synthetic b = 0.25 nm. The curve is a model prediction, not a measured boundary width or transport coefficient. Its high-angle continuation should not be interpreted as a resolved atomistic boundary structure. 5.5 Chemical order makes site labels consequential L13 16:50 An ordered compound preferentially assigns species to distinct families of sites, called sublattices. In the ideal B2 example, A occupies the corners and B the centers of the cubic conventional drawing. In an L1₂ pattern one species occupies cube corners and the other the face centers; in L1₀ the chemical pattern distinguishes alternating layer/site families. These labels encode ordering patterns. Their stability and composition ranges require thermodynamics, rather than geometry alone. A vacancy on the A sublattice is an empty A-type site. An antisite \\(A_B\\) is an A atom occupying a site assigned to B in the ordered reference. A pair \\(A_B+B_A\\) exchanges chemical occupations without changing the total atom counts. By contrast, a single antisite changes the composition unless another defect or reservoir transfer compensates it. This is why a nominal alloy composition constrains the allowed defect populations. Some defects are constitutional : they accommodate an overall composition displaced from the ideal stoichiometry. Others are thermally excited populations at the specified composition. The same structural defect type can participate in either role. For example, an excess of one species may be accommodated by antisites or by a different combination involving vacancies, depending on free energies and site constraints. Composition balance identifies the admissible combinations; energy and entropy select their populations. A formation law cannot be assigned without saying which composition and reservoir are held fixed. The source introduces a triple-defect combination in a B2 setting: two A-sublattice vacancies and one \\(A_B\\) antisite. Its stoichiometry is easiest to inspect by counting. Start with \\(N\\) A sites and \\(N\\) B sites, all correctly occupied. After making the combination, A occupies \\(N-2\\) A sites and one B site, while B occupies \\(N-1\\) B sites. Both species then have \\(N-1\\) atoms. The AB ratio remains one-to-one even though two sites are empty and one is occupied by the wrong species. For \\(N=100\\), the companion returns 99 A atoms and 99 B atoms on 200 sites. One A and one B have been transferred to a reservoir such as a surface in this counting construction. A triple-defect model need not mean that all three defects stay bound together in a compact cluster. Their free energies, interactions and mass-action equations determine the equilibrium populations. The present result establishes admissible bookkeeping, not which mechanism is dominant in every ordered alloy. 5.6 Ionic defects must preserve species and effective charge L13 23:03 An ionic crystal contains positively and negatively charged ionic species. A defect's effective charge is its charge relative to the correctly occupied reference site, rather than the bare charge of an isolated ion. Removing a monovalent positive ion leaves a vacancy with effective charge minus one elementary-charge unit. Moving that ion to a previously empty interstitial site gives an effective positive interstitial defect. The two changes sum to zero. A Frenkel combination moves an ion from a normal site to an interstitial site, creating one vacancy and one interstitial ion without removing the species from the crystal. Let \\(n\\) be the number of such pairs, \\(N_s\\) the number of normal sites and \\(N_i\\) the number of available interstitial sites. The fractions are \\(x_v=n/N_s\\) and \\(x_i=n/N_i\\). Equal defect numbers therefore imply \\[N_sx_v=N_ix_i,\\tag{5.4}\\] rather than universally \\(x_v=x_i\\). If a dilute independent-defect equilibrium model supplies the product \\(x_vx_i=K_F\\), with dimensionless \\(K_F\\), combining it with (5.4) gives \\(x_v=\\sqrt{K_FN_i/N_s}\\) and \\(x_i=\\sqrt{K_FN_s/N_i}\\). For synthetic \\(N_i=3N_s\\) and \\(K_F=10^{-8}\\), the fractions are \\(1.73205081\\times10^{-4}\\) and \\(5.77350269\\times10^{-5}\\). Their numbers agree and their product is the specified equilibrium constant. A Schottky combination transfers a stoichiometric set of ions from normal sites to a reservoir, leaving charge-balanced vacancies. In a one-to-one monovalent crystal this involves one vacancy of each sign. In an ideal \\(MX_2\\) crystal with divalent M and monovalent negative X, neutrality instead requires two X vacancies for each M vacancy: effective charges \\(-2\\) and two contributions of \\(+1\\) sum to zero. If there are twice as many X sites as M sites, the corresponding vacancy site fractions can be equal even though the vacancy numbers differ. Charge balance alone does not determine an equilibrium concentration. Electronic carriers, dopants, several charge states and additional defects can all enter the neutrality equation. A Frenkel or Schottky combination is one admissible reaction, not an exhaustive list of all charge compensation. Chapter 27 will combine these balances with mass action and electrical transport. For now, a diffusion calculation should name the site set, counted species, defect numbers and normalization before assigning an activation law. 5.7 Exercises with worked solutions 1. Primitive and conventional descriptions Use the three FCC primitive vectors in §5.1 to find their cell volume. Why does adding a different species at the center of an A-corner cube prevent using the body-center displacement as a translation symmetry? Worked solution The determinant of the integer matrix with rows (0,1,1),(1,0,1),(1,1,0) is two. Multiplying by \\((a/2)^3\\) gives \\(a^3/4\\). Four such cells fill the conventional FCC cube. For the chemically ordered corner/center pattern, a body-center translation maps an A site to a B site and changes the species-labeled structure. A geometric mapping that exchanges species is not a translation symmetry of that ordered reference. 2. Count a hole before assigning its occupancy Derive the FCC octahedral-hole count per host atom and its maximum undistorted hard-sphere radius. Does an inserted atom larger than that radius prove the structure impossible? Worked solution One central hole plus twelve edge holes each shared by four cubes gives four holes per conventional cube. There are four host atoms, giving one octahedral site per host atom. Along the central-hole line, \\(2R+2r_o=a=2\\sqrt2R\\), hence \\(r_o/R=\\sqrt2-1\\). A larger atom requires relaxation of the host geometry; its energetic cost and possible competing structures must be considered. The fit result restricts the rigid equal-sphere model, not every relaxed crystal. 3. Length density is not a section count A specimen contains parallel straight dislocations. Explain why a plane parallel to the lines can give a different intersection density from a plane normal to them. Identify what extra information a conversion needs. Worked solution A normal plane intersects every traversing line once; a generic parallel plane can intersect none. The total line length per specimen volume remains unchanged. The intersection measurement weights line orientations through their projection on the plane normal. A conversion therefore needs an orientation distribution or a suitable stereological averaging assumption, as well as the sampled area and treatment of lines coinciding with the section. 4. Resolve low-angle spacing numerically For the synthetic boundary, calculate exact and small-angle spacing at 2° and 10°. Explain why inserting degrees directly into \\(b/\\theta\\) is wrong. What fails as the array becomes dense? Worked solution Convert angles to radians before using the expansion of sine. The exact spacings are 7.16233606 and 1.43421416 nm; \\(b/\\theta\\) gives 7.16197244 and 1.43239449 nm. Degrees are a different numerical angle unit, so using their unconverted values introduces a fixed angular conversion error. At larger misorientation, overlapping/strongly interacting cores defeat the picture of well-separated identical edge dislocations even if the trigonometric expression can still be evaluated. 5. Test a triple-defect composition Count A, B and vacancies after forming one \\(2V_A+A_B\\) combination in the 100-plus-100-site B2 reference. Compare it with one antisite pair. Worked solution The triple combination leaves 98 A atoms on A sites and one A on a B site, totaling 99 A. There are 99 B atoms and two vacancies. Both species lose one atom to a reservoir, so their ratio stays one-to-one. An antisite pair instead puts one A on B and one B on A while leaving all 200 sites occupied: both species retain 100 atoms. Equal overall composition does not imply identical vacancy populations or identical chemical order. 6. Diagnose an equal-fraction assumption For a Frenkel model with three interstitial sites per normal site and \\(K_F=10^{-8}\\), solve the two balances. Then state the vacancy-number ratio required by a Schottky combination in the divalent-M/monovalent-X \\(MX_2\\) model. Worked solution Number conservation gives \\(x_v=3x_i\\), so \\(3x_i^2=10^{-8}\\). Thus \\(x_i=5.77350269\\times10^{-5}\\) and \\(x_v=1.73205081\\times10^{-4}\\). Setting the fractions equal would violate number conservation under these site multiplicities. For \\(MX_2\\), neutrality requires \\(-2n_{V_M}+n_{V_X}=0\\), giving \\(n_{V_X}=2n_{V_M}\\). With twice as many X sites, those unequal numbers correspond to equal vacancy fractions. 5.8 Sources and limits L12 , L13 and the structural exercises in L15 provide the topic spine. The International Tables, §2.2.2.1 , supports the direct-lattice convention; the accessible section text was consulted. All geometrical formulas and count balances used here are derived locally. No crystal image or source slide is redistributed. The Python companion computes the printed synthetic examples and regenerates Figure 5.1. Real defect energies, charge states and mobilities are material-dependent inputs, not consequences of packing fraction alone."}, {"number": 6, "title": "Vacancy populations, binding, and defect measurements", "url": "chapter-06.html", "text": "A vacancy can enable a jump, but an observed transport rate does not tell us how many vacancies existed. A larger rate could reflect more defects, faster motion of each defect, or weaker backtracking. We first need a population model and a measurement model, and then must identify which combination of their parameters the observations constrain. Source spine: vacancy thermodynamics in L12, self-interstitial context in L13, and binding, defect measurements and mechanisms in L16; vacancy-count exercises in L15 and L20. Formation energies used numerically are source exercise inputs or explicitly synthetic constants, not newly measured material properties. Positron and dilatometry equations below are reduced teaching models with stated limits. 6.1 Vacancy equilibrium balances formation cost and configurational entropy L12 16:52 Consider a specified set of \\(N_s\\) regular lattice sites in contact with an atom reservoir. Let \\(n_v\\) sites be empty and \\(x_v=n_v/N_s\\) the vacancy site fraction. The reservoir is essential bookkeeping: the removed atom has not ceased to exist. Define \\(g_f\\) as the free-energy cost per vacancy including the reservoir contribution and local relaxation, but excluding the configurational entropy of arranging vacancies among these sites. Assume equivalent sites, independent occupancy and no vacancy interactions. There are \\(W=N_s!/[n_v!(N_s-n_v)!]\\) ways to choose the empty sites. Boltzmann's entropy formula is \\(S_{\\rm conf}=k_B\\ln W\\), where \\(k_B\\) is the Boltzmann constant. For large counts, Stirling's approximation \\(\\ln n!\\simeq n\\ln n-n\\) gives the free-energy change per site \\[g(x_v)=x_vg_f+k_BT[x_v\\ln x_v+(1-x_v)\\ln(1-x_v)].\\tag{6.1}\\] The mixing term is negative between zero and one. It rewards the many arrangements available to a dilute population, while the first term penalizes creating defects when \\(g_f>0\\). Here \\(T\\) is absolute temperature and all per-defect energies use the same units as \\(k_BT\\). If molar formation energies are used instead, the corresponding expression uses the molar gas constant \\(R\\). Differentiate (6.1) at fixed temperature and fixed formation free energy. Setting \\(g'=g_f+k_BT\\ln[x_v/(1-x_v)]=0\\) gives \\[\\frac{x_v}{1-x_v}=e^{-g_f/(k_BT)},\\qquad x_v=\\frac{1}{1+e^{g_f/(k_BT)}}.\\tag{6.2}\\] The second derivative is \\(k_BT[1/x_v+1/(1-x_v)]>0\\), proving a strict interior minimum of this independent-site free energy. When \\(x_v\\ll1\\), equation (6.2) reduces to \\(x_v\\simeq e^{-g_f/(k_BT)}\\). The exact logistic form belongs to this simple occupancy model; it does not establish physical accuracy at large vacancy fractions, where interactions and structural instability can invalidate the model. Writing \\(g_f=h_f-Ts_f\\) separates formation enthalpy \\(h_f\\) and nonconfigurational formation entropy \\(s_f\\). In the dilute limit, \\(x_v\\simeq e^{s_f/k_B}e^{-h_f/(k_BT)}\\). The configurational entropy has already been included in (6.1), so it must not be added again inside \\(s_f\\). Temperature-dependent formation parameters require their own derivative terms; Chapter 9 treats the interpretation of slopes. For a hypothetical temperature-independent \\(g_f=1.2\\) eV, the computed fractions are \\(6.93245818\\times10^{-21}\\) at 300 K and \\(9.12468439\\times10^{-6}\\) at 1200 K. These results use the SI ratio \\(k_B/e=8.617333262145179\\times10^{-5}\\) eV/K. They show sensitivity within the specified model, rather than a universal vacancy fraction at melting. A quenched or irradiated specimen can retain populations that have not equilibrated with the reservoir and sinks. 6.2 A divacancy counts as one pair and two missing sites L16 03:27 A divacancy consists of two neighboring vacancies. Let \\(x_1\\) be the number of isolated monovacancies per site and \\(x_2\\) the number of bound pairs per site. Their contribution to the missing-site fraction is \\(x_1+2x_2\\), not \\(x_1+x_2\\). A measurement sensitive to empty sites and a measurement sensitive to pair clusters can therefore report different quantities without disagreeing. Define a positive binding free energy \\(g_b=2g_1-g_2\\), where \\(g_1\\) is the isolated-vacancy formation free energy and \\(g_2\\) the pair formation free energy. Favorable binding lowers the pair cost. If each site has \\(z\\) equivalent nearest neighbors, the number of undirected nearest-neighbor bonds is \\(zN_s/2\\), because counting from both ends would count each bond twice. In the dilute, nonoverlapping-pair approximation, the statistical weight per candidate bond is \\(e^{-g_2/(k_BT)}\\). Using \\(x_1\\simeq e^{-g_1/(k_BT)}\\) gives \\[x_2\\simeq\\frac z2e^{g_b/(k_BT)}x_1^2,\\qquad \\frac{x_2}{x_1}\\simeq\\frac z2e^{g_b/(k_BT)}x_1.\\tag{6.3}\\] The square of a small monovacancy population competes with the enhancement from binding. This competition prevents the universal claim that divacancies must outnumber monovacancies at high temperature. Their relative importance depends on formation and binding free energies, multiplicities, and the temperature range in which the crystal and dilute model remain meaningful. For synthetic \\(z=12\\), \\(g_1=0.9\\) eV and \\(g_b=0.15\\) eV, the pair-to-monovacancy ratios at 800, 1200 and 1600 K are approximately 0.00011307, 0.00424903 and 0.02604685. The ratio rises across this example yet remains below one. At the highest temperature, the computed pair fraction is \\(3.80960543\\times10^{-5}\\); its missing-site contribution is twice that. These are model parameters, not an extrapolation of a particular real metal above or below its melting point. 6.3 Solutes change the local vacancy environment L16 06:29 A dilute substitutional solute creates local environments that differ from the solvent. Let \\(N_B\\) be its site fraction and suppose each solute has \\(z\\) neighboring sites whose vacancy formation free energy is \\(g_B\\), compared with \\(g_A\\) far away. Here a B-associated vacancy means a vacancy neighboring a solute; it does not mean that a vacant site permanently remembers which species was removed. For \\(zN_B\\ll1\\), neglect overlap between solute neighbor shells. Their site fraction is approximately \\(zN_B\\), leaving \\(1-zN_B\\) bulk-like sites. A first-order independent-environment model then gives \\[x_v\\simeq(1-zN_B)e^{-g_A/(k_BT)}+zN_Be^{-g_B/(k_BT)}\n=e^{-g_A/(k_BT)}[1-zN_B+zN_Be^{g_b/(k_BT)}],\\tag{6.4}\\] where \\(g_b=g_A-g_B\\) is positive for favorable association under this convention. Binding can increase a local defect population while also trapping defects and changing their mobility. Equation (6.4) alone therefore cannot establish faster long-range transport. The tutorial's unpaired-site ratio \\(1-zN_B\\) is a local-environment approximation. With \\(z=12\\) and \\(N_B=0.005\\), it gives 0.94. An independent random-site calculation gives the probability of no solute among the neighbors as \\((1-N_B)^z=0.94162281\\); its first-order expansion is \\(1-zN_B\\). Using \\(N_B=0.05\\) would instead give 0.4 in the linear approximation, with \\(zN_B=0.6\\) already too large for a well-controlled nonoverlap argument. An inconsistent decimal in a caption must not replace the count model. Self-interstitials introduce a different population. Their creation moves a host atom away from its regular site, rather than merely occupying an already available geometric hole with an externally supplied solute. Irradiation can create vacancy/interstitial combinations far from thermal equilibrium. Comparing their roles requires formation rates, recombination and sink removal as well as migration rates. The distinction between a dilute interstitial solute and a thermally generated self-interstitial will matter in Chapter 7. 6.4 Differential dilatometry compares two volume changes L16 10:33 Heating expands a specimen and changes its lattice parameter. If the lattice simply stretches without changing the number of cells, the macroscopic and microscopic volume ratios agree. Extra empty lattice sites allow the specimen to contain more cells per fixed number of atoms. Differential dilatometry exploits the resulting difference between specimen expansion and lattice expansion, usually measured by diffraction. Consider a uniform cubic monatomic specimen with a fixed number of host atoms \\(N_a\\). Let \\(L\\) measure its isotropic linear dimension and \\(a\\) its cubic lattice parameter. The number of sites is proportional to \\(L^3/a^3\\); the number of sites per cell cancels between temperatures. Define the reference state by subscript 0 and the heated state by T. Then \\[q\\equiv\\frac{N_s(T)}{N_s(0)}\n=\\left[\\frac{L(T)/L(0)}{a(T)/a(0)}\\right]^3,\n\\qquad\nx_v(T)=1-\\frac{1-x_v(0)}{q}.\\tag{6.5}\\] The second equation follows from atom conservation, \\(N_a=N_s(0)[1-x_v(0)]=N_s(T)[1-x_v(T)]\\). It explicitly retains an initial vacancy population. A quenched or irradiated starting specimen need not be a vacancy-free reference. For small strains \\(\\epsilon_L=[L(T)-L(0)]/L(0)\\) and \\(\\epsilon_a=[a(T)-a(0)]/a(0)\\), expand the first equation: \\(q\\simeq1+3(\\epsilon_L-\\epsilon_a)\\). With negligible initial vacancies and small vacancy fraction, \\[x_v(T)\\simeq3(\\epsilon_L-\\epsilon_a).\\tag{6.6}\\] The subtraction, rather than either thermal strain separately, carries the vacancy signal. In a synthetic example, \\(\\epsilon_L=0.0100\\) and \\(\\epsilon_a=0.0099\\). Equation (6.5) gives \\(x_v=2.97000295\\times10^{-4}\\), while the linear estimate gives \\(3.00000000\\times10^{-4}\\). The approximation is sensible but its difference from the exact geometric expression is detectable when precision matters. The cube is a convenience, not a requirement. In a tetragonal crystal the unit-cell volume changes as \\(a^2c\\); its linearized fractional change is \\(2\\epsilon_a+\\epsilon_c\\). More generally the comparison uses specimen volume divided by unit-cell volume, with the appropriate strain tensor and texture information. Treating one directional strain as an isotropic volume change can create a fictitious vacancy population. Interpreting a small expansion difference requires matched temperatures, reliable reference lengths and an account of other structural changes. Grain growth, porosity, dislocations, surfaces and additional defect types can alter specimen dimensions. Atom loss or uptake violates the fixed-atom assumption. Differential dilatometry supplies an inference under a count model; it does not label every measured excess volume as a monovacancy. 6.5 A positron lifetime measures trapping through a model L16 16:42 A positron can annihilate in the bulk or become trapped at a vacancy-like region with a different local electron density and annihilation rate. A lifetime spectrum therefore contains information about defects, but the map from lifetime to concentration depends on the capture process and the available trap species. We develop an original minimal model to make that dependence explicit; it is not a fit to a lecture dataset. Let \\(p_f(t)\\) and \\(p_t(t)\\) be probabilities that an initially free positron is still alive in the free and trapped states. Let \\(\\lambda_f\\) and \\(\\lambda_t\\) be annihilation rates, and \\(\\kappa\\) a trapping rate, all in s⁻¹. Neglect escape from traps, positron diffusion limitations and multiple trap species. With \\(p_f(0)=1\\), \\(p_t(0)=0\\), the population equations are \\[\\dot p_f=-(\\lambda_f+\\kappa)p_f,\n\\qquad\n\\dot p_t=\\kappa p_f-\\lambda_t p_t.\\tag{6.7}\\] Survival is \\(p_f+p_t\\), so the mean lifetime is the integral of that sum over nonnegative time. Integrating the first equation gives \\(\\int p_f\\,dt=1/(\\lambda_f+\\kappa)\\). Integrating the second, whose endpoint populations both vanish, gives \\(\\int p_t\\,dt=\\kappa/[\\lambda_t(\\lambda_f+\\kappa)]\\). Therefore \\[\\bar\\tau=\\frac{1+\\kappa/\\lambda_t}{\\lambda_f+\\kappa}.\\tag{6.8}\\] Every term has the right units: the numerator is dimensionless and the denominator is a rate. With synthetic bulk lifetime \\(1/\\lambda_f=100\\) ps, trap lifetime \\(1/\\lambda_t=200\\) ps and \\(\\kappa=10^{10}\\) s⁻¹, equation (6.8) gives 150 ps. As \\(\\kappa\\) increases, the mean approaches 200 ps. That plateau is saturation of this trapping model; it is not evidence that an equilibrium vacancy concentration has stopped changing. Figure 6.1. Original synthetic single-trap calculation. A plateau can arise because almost every positron reaches a trap before bulk annihilation. The curve provides no independent measurement of a real vacancy concentration. When the rates are known and the mean lies strictly between the two distinct limiting lifetimes, the model can be inverted: \\[\\kappa=\\frac{1-\\bar\\tau\\lambda_f}{\\bar\\tau-1/\\lambda_t}.\\tag{6.9}\\] The synthetic 150 ps result recovers \\(\\kappa=10^{10}\\) s⁻¹. Concentration still requires a capture coefficient: if \\(\\kappa=\\mu x_v\\), with dimensionless vacancy fraction \\(x_v\\), then \\(\\mu\\) has units s⁻¹. An unknown \\(\\mu\\) leaves a concentration ambiguity even after the trapping rate is inferred. Near saturation, a small lifetime uncertainty can imply a large uncertainty in \\(\\kappa\\); the inversion denominator tends to zero. Multiple vacancy clusters, dislocations, detrapping and instrumental resolution demand a richer forward model before interpreting an experimental spectrum. 6.6 Exercises with worked solutions 1. Entropy and the vacancy minimum Derive the equilibrium fraction from equation (6.1), check that its stationary point is a minimum, and explain why adding a second configurational entropy to \\(g_f\\) would be wrong. Worked solution Differentiating per-site free energy gives \\(g_f+k_BT\\ln[x_v/(1-x_v)]\\). Setting it to zero gives equation (6.2). Its second derivative is \\(k_BT[1/x_v+1/(1-x_v)]\\gt 0\\) inside the interval, establishing the unique minimum of this model. The binomial count already supplies the mixing/configurational entropy. The formation entropy inside \\(g_f=h_f-Ts_f\\) describes local nonconfigurational changes relative to the atom reservoir; including the same binomial contribution there would count it twice. 2. Recompute the source-given copper exercise L15 gives 1000 °C, formation free energy 0.9 eV per vacancy, density 8.4 g cm⁻³ and molar mass 63.5 g mol⁻¹. Treat these as exercise inputs, with independent vacancies. Find the equilibrium vacancy fraction and missing-site number per cubic meter, retaining the atom/site distinction. Worked solution The temperature is 1273.15 K, density is 8400 kg m⁻³ and molar mass is 0.0635 kg mol⁻¹. Equation (6.2) gives \\(x_v=2.73666286\\times10^{-4}\\). Let \\(N_{\\rm Av}\\) denote the Avogadro constant, in mol⁻¹. The density implies an occupied-atom number density \\(n_a=(8400/0.0635)N_{\\rm Av}=7.96629644\\times10^{28}\\) m⁻³. The site density is \\(n_s=n_a/(1-x_v)\\), so the vacancy number density is \\(n_sx_v=2.18070355\\times10^{25}\\) m⁻³. Replacing site density by atom density is a dilute approximation. These source-given parameters have not been independently established as measured copper data at that temperature. 3. Can binding guarantee pair dominance? Using \\(z=12\\), monovacancy formation free energy 0.9 eV and positive divacancy binding free energy 0.15 eV, compare \\(x_2/x_1\\) at 800, 1200 and 1600 K. State what fraction counts pairs and what fraction counts missing sites. Worked solution Insert \\(x_1\\simeq\\exp[-0.9/(k_BT)]\\) into equation (6.3). The ratios are 0.00011307, 0.00424903 and 0.02604685. Pair abundance increases relative to monovacancies in this synthetic parameter set but remains smaller throughout. At 1600 K, \\(x_2=3.80960543\\times10^{-5}\\) counts pairs per site; its missing-site contribution is \\(2x_2\\), whereas the monovacancy contribution is \\(x_1=0.00146259731\\). A favorable binding energy and a rising ratio do not establish universal high-temperature pair dominance. 4. Audit a local-environment approximation Compare the fraction of sites with no solute neighbor, approximated by \\(1-zN_B\\), with the independent random-occupation expression \\((1-N_B)^z\\), for \\(N_B=0.005\\) and \\(z=12\\). Why is \\(N_B=0.05\\) a materially different input? Worked solution The linear estimate is 0.94 and the independent-occupation expression is 0.94162281. The former truncates the binomial expansion after its first-order term and neglects overlapping solute neighborhoods in the population split. At 0.05 the linear estimate becomes 0.4 and \\(zN_B=0.6\\) is not small. A result of 0.94 cannot be paired with 0.05 under that formula. Neither expression includes solute correlations or environment-dependent multi-solute defect energies. 5. Recover a strain-based vacancy fraction Derive equation (6.5) for a fixed-atom cubic specimen, then use strains 0.0100 and 0.0099 with negligible initial vacancies. What changes if the reference has a known nonzero fraction \\(x_v(0)\\)? Worked solution Site number scales as specimen volume divided by unit-cell volume. The ratio is \\(q=(1.0100/1.0099)^3\\). Fixed atom number gives \\(x_v=1-q^{-1}=0.000297000295\\); the linear result is 0.000300000000. For a nonzero reference, use \\(1-(1-x_v(0))/q\\). The reference contribution is retained rather than silently discarded. A measured excess expansion alone is insufficient unless other sources of volume change and atom exchange have been controlled. 6. Distinguish capture from concentration Derive the mean lifetime in the two-state model without solving the full time traces. Invert a 150 ps mean using 100 ps bulk and 200 ps trap lifetimes. Can the vacancy fraction then be reported without a capture coefficient? Worked solution Integrate equation (6.7) from zero to infinity. The integrated free population is \\(1/(\\lambda_f+\\kappa)\\), and the trapped integral is \\(\\kappa/\\lambda_t\\) times that value. Their sum gives equation (6.8). Equation (6.9) recovers \\(10^{10}\\) s⁻¹. Only the product \\(\\mu x_v\\) is identified by this inference. Changing \\(\\mu\\) while inversely changing \\(x_v\\) leaves the mean unchanged. The saturation limit also makes inference ill-conditioned even when \\(\\mu\\) is known. 6.7 Sources and limits The thermodynamic spine is L12 ; defect bookkeeping is continued from L13 . Binding, differential dilatometry and positron probing follow L16 , with tutorial checks from L15 and L20. The two-state lifetime model and strain comparison are original explicit teaching models, not reconstructed fits. The Python companion computes all printed numerical examples and Figure 6.1; SI constants come from SciPy's constants module. Equilibrium, geometric measurement and trapping inference are different layers of evidence, each requiring its stated assumptions."}, {"number": 7, "title": "Random walks and atomistic diffusion mechanisms", "url": "chapter-07.html", "text": "An atom can travel a long zigzag path while making little net progress. Macroscopic diffusion measures the growth of displacement fluctuations, not the distance accumulated along that path. Connecting a jump mechanism to a diffusion coefficient therefore requires three separate decisions: which jumps are possible, how often they occur, and how their directions are statistically related. Source spine: L14–16 and the mechanism/geometry tutorial in L20. The ensemble simulation and parameter choices below are synthetic. The source's mean-square-displacement exercises are recomputed with explicit units; captioned numerical statements are not used as an independent numerical authority. 7.1 A random displacement is a sum of vectors L14 04:27 Let \\(\\boldsymbol\\xi_j\\) be the displacement vector of a tracer's jth successful jump. Its position relative to its starting point after n jumps is \\(\\mathbf R_n=\\sum_{j=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\xi_j\\). If every jump has length \\(\\ell\\), the accumulated path length is exactly \\(n\\ell\\); the displacement magnitude \\(|\\mathbf R_n|\\) generally is much smaller and may vanish after many jumps. Use \\(\\mathbb E\\) for an ensemble expectation over the specified stochastic process. Expanding the squared displacement before making statistical assumptions gives \\[\\mathbb E|\\mathbf R_n|^2\n=\\sum_{j=1}^n\\mathbb E|\\boldsymbol\\xi_j|^2\n+2\\sum_{1\\le i\\lt j\\le n}\\mathbb E(\\boldsymbol\\xi_i\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi_j).\\tag{7.1}\\] This identity is exact. “Random” alone does not set the cross terms to zero. If distinct jumps are independent, their cross expectation factorizes into \\(\\mathbb E\\boldsymbol\\xi_i\\cdot\\mathbb E\\boldsymbol\\xi_j\\). If they are also unbiased, each mean is zero. Fixed-length jumps then give \\[\\mathbb E\\mathbf R_n=0,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E|\\mathbf R_n|^2=n\\ell^2,\\qquad\nR_{\\rm rms}\\equiv\\sqrt{\\mathbb E|\\mathbf R_n|^2}=\\sqrt n\\,\\ell.\\tag{7.2}\\] The root-mean-square displacement, abbreviated RMS, is not the expectation of the displacement magnitude. A finite sample of tracers need not have exactly zero mean even when the underlying process does. The sample is an estimate of an expectation, with sampling uncertainty. Figure 7.1 uses 40,000 independently simulated tracers on a simple cubic lattice, each selecting one of six directions with equal probability at each step. After 200 jumps, the sample mean squared displacement is \\(200.02775\\ell^2\\), close to the exact \\(200\\ell^2\\). Its sample mean displacement has components \\((0.006625,0.001225,-0.03835)\\ell\\), illustrating that symmetry constrains the distribution, rather than forcing exact cancellation in every realization. The companion fixes the random seed and verifies the measured moments against the exact result. Figure 7.1. Original synthetic simple-cubic ensemble. The expectation grows linearly with jump count, while individual paths wander irregularly. These markers validate a specified independent-walk model; they do not establish independence for vacancy-mediated diffusion in a real solid. Unbiased and independent are different requirements. A vacancy left immediately behind a tracer makes a return jump comparatively likely, even in an inversion-symmetric crystal with zero ensemble drift. Chapter 8 retains the cross terms in (7.1). Conversely, independent biased steps have nonzero mean and a raw mean-square displacement containing a term quadratic in time. 7.2 A jump rate needs a counting convention L14 29:13 Let \\(\\Gamma\\) be the total successful jump rate per tracer , in s⁻¹, summed over all accessible neighbors. For a homogeneous continuous-time walk with independent exponentially distributed waiting times, the number of jumps by time t is Poisson with mean \\(\\Gamma t\\). Conditioning on that number and applying equation (7.2) gives \\(\\mathbb E|\\mathbf R(t)|^2=\\Gamma t\\ell^2\\). The same long-time result applies more broadly when an appropriate finite mean waiting time and independent zero-mean increments exist; a broad waiting-time distribution can invalidate normal diffusion. For isotropic diffusion in d spatial dimensions, \\(\\mathbb E|\\mathbf R(t)|^2=2dDt\\). Hence \\[D=\\frac{\\Gamma\\ell^2}{2d},\\qquad\nD_{\\rm cubic,3D}=\\frac{\\Gamma\\ell^2}{6}.\\tag{7.3}\\] The units are length squared divided by time. For synthetic \\(\\Gamma=6.0\\times10^6\\) s⁻¹ and \\(\\ell=0.20\\) nm, this is \\(4.0\\times10^{-14}\\) m² s⁻¹. One must convert nanometers to meters before squaring. If z equivalent neighbor directions each have a successful transition rate w, then \\(\\Gamma=zw\\). A thermal attempt frequency \\(\\nu\\) is different again: only some attempts cross the migration free-energy barrier. For a per-neighbor activated rate, one model is \\(w=\\nu\\exp[-g_m/(k_BT)]\\), where \\(g_m\\) is a free-energy barrier per jump. Using a molar barrier instead requires R in place of \\(k_B\\). A vacancy-exchange opportunity must also be available; multiplying a host atom's per-neighbor exchange rate by every occupied neighbor would miscount successful tracer jumps. Cubic symmetry gives a scalar diffusivity, but unequal jump geometries require a second-moment tensor. For independent zero-mean jumps \\(\\boldsymbol\\delta_\\alpha\\) with rates \\(w_\\alpha\\), \\[\\mathbf D=\\frac12\\sum_\\alpha w_\\alpha\n\\boldsymbol\\delta_\\alpha\\boldsymbol\\delta_\\alpha^{\\mathsf T}.\\tag{7.4}\\] The outer product has components \\(\\delta_{\\alpha i}\\delta_{\\alpha j}\\); its rate-weighted sum determines spreading along each direction. For twelve FCC nearest-neighbor unit directions, the companion enumerates all permutations of \\((\\pm1,\\pm1,0)/\\sqrt2\\). Their outer products sum to \\(4\\mathbf I\\), where \\(\\mathbf I\\) is the identity matrix. With equal per-direction rate w and jump length \\(\\ell=a/\\sqrt2\\), equation (7.4) gives \\(D=2w\\ell^2=(12w)\\ell^2/6\\). This also explains the tutorial's sum of squared direction cosines, four along any cubic axis. When drift is present, diffusion measures spreading about the mean. In a one-dimensional discrete-time walk with probabilities p and \\(1-p\\) for \\(\\pm\\ell\\), the mean after n jumps is \\(n(2p-1)\\ell\\) and the variance is \\(n[1-(2p-1)^2]\\ell^2\\). For \\(p=0.6,n=100\\), they are \\(20\\ell\\) and \\(96\\ell^2\\), while the raw second moment is \\(496\\ell^2\\). Substituting the latter into an unbiased diffusion formula would fold directed motion into apparent diffusion. A Poisson clock adds jump-count fluctuations and must be treated with its own centered moment; the discrete-time variance above assumes exactly n jumps. 7.3 The diffusion equation is a long-scale limit L14 13:41 A continuum equation should emerge from an explicit discrete balance. Let \\(c_j(t)\\) be an expected tracer concentration on a one-dimensional lattice with spacing \\(\\ell\\), normalized per physical volume using the same cell volume at each site. A tracer jumps to each neighbor at rate w, independent of other tracers. Gain from both neighbors minus loss to both neighbors gives the exact master equation \\[\\frac{dc_j}{dt}=w[c_{j-1}-2c_j+c_{j+1}].\\tag{7.5}\\] This is an equation for a spatially discrete stochastic process's expectation, not a numerical approximation introduced to solve an already assumed PDE. With a concentration smooth over many sites, expand \\(c(x\\pm\\ell,t)\\) about x. Odd terms cancel; the first surviving term is \\(\\ell^2\\partial_{xx}c\\), followed by \\(\\ell^4\\partial_{xxxx}c/12\\). Retaining the second derivative gives \\(\\partial_tc=D\\partial_{xx}c\\) with \\(D=w\\ell^2=\\Gamma\\ell^2/2\\). A controlled diffusion scaling sends \\(\\ell\\) to zero and w to infinity with \\(w\\ell^2\\) fixed, or interprets this equation as a coarse-grained long-wavelength approximation at finite spacing. The wavelength restriction can be quantified without Taylor's theorem. Insert \\(c_j\\propto\\exp(ikj\\ell)\\) into (7.5), where k is a wave number in m⁻¹. Its decay rate is \\(2w[1-\\cos(k\\ell)]\\), whereas the PDE predicts \\(w\\ell^2k^2\\). Their ratio is \\[\\frac{\\hbox{lattice decay rate}}{\\hbox{diffusive decay rate}}\n=\\frac{2(1-\\cos q)}{q^2},\\qquad q=k\\ell.\\tag{7.6}\\] The limit is one as \\(q\\to0\\). At synthetic \\(q=0.1,0.5,2\\), the ratios are 0.99916694, 0.97933950 and 0.70807342. Structure varying over just a few lattice spacings can decay substantially differently from the continuum prediction. A formally successful second-order expansion is not an exact finite-spacing identity. The lecture also writes a finite-time transition relation. If \\(p(X,\\tau)\\) is the probability mass of displacement X during a time interval \\(\\tau\\), spatial homogeneity gives \\(c(x,t+\\tau)=\\sum_Xp(X,\\tau)c(x-X,t)\\). The masses sum to one. If displacements are continuous, replace the sum by an integral and use a probability density with inverse-length units. Expanding both time and space produces a diffusion equation only under scaling and regularity assumptions that control the discarded terms. In a drifting process, terms from temporal variation must be retained consistently; the central covariance after subtracting mean motion defines diffusive spreading. A complementary flux count uses dilute interstitial tracers on a simple-cubic network. Let \\(c_1,c_2\\) be volume number concentrations in adjacent planes separated by \\(\\ell\\). Their associated plane populations per area are \\(\\sigma_1=c_1\\ell,\\sigma_2=c_2\\ell\\). Equal choice among six directions gives forward minus backward number flux \\(J=(\\Gamma/6)(\\sigma_1-\\sigma_2)\\). For a slowly varying profile, \\(c_2-c_1\\simeq\\ell\\,\\partial_xc\\), hence \\(J=-\\Gamma\\ell^2\\partial_xc/6\\). The result agrees with (7.3). Occupation blocking, nonequivalent sites and correlated jumps require modifications to that microscopic count. 7.4 A jump mechanism supplies the rate and memory L16 18:47 For an interstitial mechanism , a solute occupying one interstitial site moves to another. If solutes are dilute and sites equivalent, the availability of empty destination sites is nearly unity. Its successful jump rate can then be dominated by migration across an energy barrier. This is different from self-diffusion by thermally generated host self-interstitials: the latter also needs a population of those defects, whose creation has a formation cost. A fixed imposed interstitial-solute concentration should not acquire that host-defect formation factor by analogy. For a vacancy mechanism , a neighboring atom exchanges positions with an empty regular site. In a dilute random monovacancy approximation, a tracer has roughly \\(zx_v\\) vacant nearest neighbors on average. If the per-neighbor exchange rate when a vacancy is present is w, its total successful tracer rate is approximately \\(zx_vw\\). Its long-time tracer diffusivity is \\(D^*=f\\,zx_vw\\ell^2/6\\), with f a dimensionless correlation factor. The star denotes tracer diffusion. Even without a macroscopic force, the vacancy just used by the tracer remains behind it and can induce backtracking. Neither vacancy concentration nor a migration barrier alone determines the transport coefficient. A divacancy mechanism uses a bound pair whose possible rearrangements differ from those of an isolated vacancy. Chapter 6 counted pairs per site separately from missing sites; here one must additionally count actual tracer moves generated by each pair configuration. A pair population cannot simply be inserted for \\(x_v\\) in an isolated-vacancy expression without checking geometry and rates. In interstitialcy , an interstitial atom displaces a host atom and\nthe displaced atom carries the defect onward, possibly through a sequence of\nsubstitutions. Tagged-atom motion need not coincide with the path of the defect.\nDirect two-atom exchange and larger cyclic or ring rearrangements move occupied\natoms cooperatively. To see the limit of a pure exchange model, consider fixed,\nequal-volume lattice sites: one A/B swap moves A by a vector\n\\(\\boldsymbol\\delta\\) and B by \\(-\\boldsymbol\\delta\\). Their summed atomic\ndisplacement is zero, and each such swap produces equal and opposite component\ntransfers. A model built only from these swaps cannot independently adjust the\ntwo component transfers. In an interdiffusing specimen, inert markers attached\nto the lattice can move relative to the laboratory; this observation is called\nthe Kirkendall effect . Its connection to unequal component fluxes in\nthe lattice frame is derived in Chapter 13. The restricted exchange model is\ntherefore not a complete explanation of those observations. This does not prove\nthat individual exchanges or collective ring events are forbidden in all\nsolids. Disordered solids may have collective rearrangements for which a single\nfixed lattice jump length is itself an approximation. Mechanisms can interconvert. Let \\(B_i,B_s\\) denote solute B on interstitial and substitutional sites, \\(V\\) a host vacancy and \\(A_i\\) a host self-interstitial. A schematic dissociative balance is \\(B_i+V\\rightleftharpoons B_s\\). A kick-out balance is \\(B_i\\rightleftharpoons B_s+A_i\\): B enters a regular host position while the displaced A becomes interstitial. Each expression conserves the atoms under its site convention; a charged crystal needs effective-charge balance too. If interconversion is slow, transport must track several populations and reaction rates instead of averaging unrelated single-species diffusivities. Irradiation creates vacancies and self-interstitials out of thermal equilibrium. Their migration, recombination and loss to sinks determine their evolving populations. An equilibrium formation factor is then an additional assumption to test, not a universal prefactor to apply. Chapter 9 separates thermodynamic formation costs, migration costs and the apparent activation parameters of a measured coefficient. 7.5 Exercises with worked solutions 1. Separate path length from RMS progress An independent unbiased fixed-length walk takes \\(10^6\\) jumps of 0.1 nm. Find the accumulated path length and RMS displacement. How can the RMS become nine times larger if the length is fixed, or if each jump becomes three times longer? Worked solution The path length is \\(10^6(0.1\\ {\\rm nm})=10^5\\) nm, or 100 μm. The RMS is \\(\\sqrt{10^6}(0.1\\ {\\rm nm})=100\\) nm. At fixed length, nine times the RMS needs 81 times the jump count. If the length triples, the jump count need only increase ninefold: \\(\\sqrt9\\times3=9\\). These are comparisons within the specified walk model. A hypothetical change in jump length should not be presented as a physically achievable large thermal expansion of the same crystal. 2. Recompute an MSD tutorial A three-dimensional isotropic tracer diffusivity is \\(2\\times10^{-9}\\) m² s⁻¹. Find the MSD and RMS after one second. Explain why a quantity in square meters cannot be reported directly as a penetration distance. Worked solution The MSD is \\(6Dt=1.2\\times10^{-8}\\) m². Its square root is \\(1.09544512\\times10^{-4}\\) m, or about 109.5445 μm. The square root must be taken before converting a squared displacement to a length. These are ensemble moments, not a sharp front or the farthest tracer distance. The source-caption numerical discussion around L15 19:55 is dimensionally ambiguous; the recomputed values follow the explicitly stated D, t and dimensionality. 3. Audit a neighbor-rate convention For twelve FCC directions with equal rate w per direction and nearest-neighbor distance \\(a/\\sqrt2\\), derive D from the tensor sum. Explain the error in interpreting w as the total rate. Worked solution Each direction has two nonzero components \\(\\pm1/\\sqrt2\\). Eight directions have a nonzero x component, so the sum of x-direction cosines squared is \\(8/2=4\\); sign symmetry cancels off-diagonal sums. Equation (7.4) gives \\(D_{xx}=w\\ell^2(4)/2=2w\\ell^2=wa^2\\), and the other diagonal components agree. The total rate is \\(12w\\). Replacing it by w in \\(D=\\Gamma\\ell^2/6\\) undercounts this walk by twelve. 4. Find the drift contribution Take exactly 100 independent one-dimensional jumps with probabilities 0.6 and 0.4 for \\(\\pm\\ell\\). Compute the mean, variance and raw second moment. Which quantity describes spreading about the mean? Worked solution One jump has mean \\(0.2\\ell\\), second moment \\(\\ell^2\\) and variance \\(0.96\\ell^2\\). Independence makes the variances additive. Thus the mean is \\(20\\ell\\), variance \\(96\\ell^2\\), and raw second moment is \\(96\\ell^2+(20\\ell)^2=496\\ell^2\\). The variance describes centered spreading. In a time-dependent walk the mean-squared drift contributes a term proportional to \\(t^2\\) if velocity is constant; it cannot be absorbed into a constant diffusion coefficient. 5. Test the continuum approximation Find the decay rate of a lattice Fourier mode in equation (7.5), and compare it with the diffusion equation for \\(k\\ell=0.5\\) and 2. State which limit justifies the approximation. Worked solution Neighbor shifts multiply the mode by \\(e^{\\pm ik\\ell}\\), so its time derivative is \\(-2w[1-\\cos(k\\ell)]\\) times its amplitude. The continuum rate is \\(w\\ell^2k^2\\). Ratios are 0.97933950 and 0.70807342. They approach one for \\(k\\ell\\ll1\\), meaning wavelengths much larger than the spacing. Small jump spacing alone is not sufficient unless it is small relative to the profile's variation scale, and the appropriate scaling of rate preserves finite D. 6. Count mobile populations before choosing an activation law Compare a fixed dilute interstitial-solute population with host self-diffusion carried by equilibrium monovacancies. What extra ingredients are needed for an irradiated specimen or a reversible \\(B_i+V\\rightleftharpoons B_s\\) system? Worked solution The dilute interstitial tracer can have \\(\\Gamma\\simeq zw\\), with temperature dependence set mainly by migration for equivalent empty destination sites. A host tracer moving through monovacancies has \\(\\Gamma\\simeq zx_vw\\) and a correlation factor; equilibrium \\(x_v\\) contributes a formation free energy as well as the exchange migration free energy. Irradiation requires defect production, recombination and sink kinetics because equilibrium \\(x_v\\) need not apply. Reversible interstitial/substitutional transport requires population balance, forward/backward reaction rates, the mobility of each state and any charge constraints. A single barrier cannot be assigned from mechanism names alone. 7.6 Sources and limits L14 develops walk moments and the continuum connection; L15 supplies tutorial prompts, and L16 develops the mechanism and flux-count arguments. The FCC direction count follows the L20 tutorial. All formulas used numerically are derived here and computed in the Python companion . The source's Scilab Gaussian plotting activity evaluates an analytic solution; it is not a numerical PDE solution. Chapter 3 develops that analytic profile, while this chapter's laboratory tests discrete moments. A finite independent-walk simulation validates its own specified stochastic assumptions; physical correlations and concentration interactions remain separate questions."}, {"number": 8, "title": "Correlation factors and tracer memory", "url": "chapter-08.html", "text": "Two tracers can make the same number of jumps of the same length and spread by different amounts. One repeatedly undoes its progress; the other tends to keep its direction. A correlation factor measures this difference through the complete history of jump directions. A single immediate backjump probability is useful evidence, but it is not generally the entire calculation. Source spine: L17–18, with L20 tutorial connections. The two-direction Markov walk is an original soluble teaching model. The FCC result is stated for the standard dilute nearest-neighbor five-frequency model and checked against a primary derivation. Selected 720p L18 frames were inspected for the rate labels and displayed formulas; this is a bounded visual check, not a full-video review. 8.1 Count correlations by their jump separation L18 00:21 Retain Chapter 7's jump vectors \\(\\boldsymbol\\xi_j\\) and fixed jump length \\(\\ell\\). Assume their statistics are stationary with respect to jump index: joint averages depend on separation in the sequence, rather than on the starting index. Assume also zero mean displacement per jump. Define the normalized lag correlation \\[C_k=\\frac{\\mathbb E(\\boldsymbol\\xi_j\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi_{j+k})}{\\ell^2},\\qquad C_0=1.\\tag{8.1}\\] Stationarity is a model assumption; it may fail during defect equilibration or changing chemical order. The expectation averages actual jump sequences, rather than independently sampled angles chosen without a physical transition rule. In the displacement expansion, there are \\(n-k\\) pairs of jumps separated by k positions. Dividing equation (7.1) by \\(n\\ell^2\\) therefore gives the exact finite-step factor \\[f_n\\equiv\\frac{\\mathbb E|\\mathbf R_n|^2}{n\\ell^2}\n=1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\left(1-\\frac{k}{n}\\right)C_k.\\tag{8.2}\\] The weights matter for a short trajectory. If \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}|C_k|\\lt \\infty\\), the bounded weights converge to one at every fixed k and a dominated-convergence argument yields \\[f=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}f_n=1+2\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}C_k.\\tag{8.3}\\] Absolute summability is a sufficient condition, not a claim about every solid. Since \\(f_n\\ge0\\), a finite limit is nonnegative, but no general probability law requires \\(f\\le1\\). Backtracking can give \\(f\\lt 1\\); persistent directions can give \\(f\\gt 1\\). A nondecaying correlation can prevent a finite normal-diffusion limit entirely. For an isotropic stationary process with a suitable asymptotic jump clock of rate \\(\\Gamma\\), and no additional waiting-time/direction coupling that changes this conversion, \\(D^*=f\\Gamma\\ell^2/6\\). The star again indicates tracer diffusion. Counting jumps in a simulation gives \\(\\Gamma\\); measuring the long-time centered MSD gives \\(D^*\\). Comparing both can reveal memory that a rate count alone misses. The physical source of vacancy memory is simple. Immediately after a tracer exchanges with a vacancy, that vacancy is at the tracer's old site. An immediate reverse tracer jump is now available, whereas many other tracer jumps require the vacancy to move around it first. Other host atoms compete for exchanges with the vacancy, so reversal is probable under some rates, not inevitable. An unforced homogeneous specimen can still have zero macroscopic drift while these conditional directional probabilities are strongly unequal. 8.2 A geometric correlation requires a conditional rule L18 03:30 A common shortcut replaces \\(C_k\\) by \\(C_1^k\\). Cubic symmetry alone does not justify that replacement. To see an assumption that does, suppose the conditional mean of the next jump depends on the full past only through the last jump and obeys \\[\\mathbb E[\\boldsymbol\\xi_{j+1}\\mid\\boldsymbol\\xi_j,\\boldsymbol\\xi_{j-1},\\ldots]\n=\\rho\\boldsymbol\\xi_j.\\tag{8.4}\\] The scalar \\(\\rho\\) is dimensionless. Applying conditional expectation repeatedly gives \\(\\mathbb E(\\boldsymbol\\xi_{j+k}\\mid\\hbox{history through }j)=\\rho^k\\boldsymbol\\xi_j\\), and hence \\(C_k=\\rho^k\\). The conditional rule, not the name of the crystal system, closes the hierarchy. In a simple-cubic directional Markov model, let the next jump go forward with probability \\(p_+\\), backward with \\(p_-\\), and into each of four perpendicular directions with probability \\(p_\\perp\\). Their sum is \\(p_++p_-+4p_\\perp=1\\). Equal opposite perpendicular probabilities cancel their conditional vector contribution, giving \\(\\rho=p_+-p_-\\). A path that turns sideways need not individually have zero projection on a much later step; it is conditional averaging over symmetry-related continuations that produces cancellation. Replacing that average with a claim about every individual path is incorrect. For the simplest exactly computable example, use only two directions, \\(\\pm\\ell\\), with probability \\((1+\\rho)/2\\) to keep the last direction and \\((1-\\rho)/2\\) to reverse. Start the first direction uniformly, so the process is stationary and unbiased. For \\(|\\rho|\\lt 1\\), the geometric series in (8.3) gives \\[f=\\frac{1+\\rho}{1-\\rho},\\qquad\nf_n=\\frac{1+\\rho}{1-\\rho}\n-\\frac{2\\rho(1-\\rho^n)}{n(1-\\rho)^2}.\\tag{8.5}\\] The second expression follows by summing the finite weighted series in (8.2); it also gives \\(f_1=1\\), as any one-jump result must. In the synthetic cases \\(\\rho=-0.5,0,0.5\\), the limiting factors are \\(1/3,1,3\\). At \\(n=200\\), they are 0.33555556, 1 and 2.98. The executable companion independently propagates the complete position/direction probability distribution at 40 steps and verifies its MSD against the correlation sum. This is a deterministic probability calculation rather than a Monte Carlo estimate. Figure 8.1. Original exact two-direction Markov model. Positive memory increases spreading; negative memory reduces it. The parameters describe this teaching process, not a fitted vacancy correlation factor in a named crystal. The endpoints require separate reasoning. With \\(\\rho=1\\), the initial direction never changes: \\(|R_n|=n\\ell\\) and \\(f_n=n\\), corresponding to ballistic growth rather than normal diffusion. With \\(\\rho=-1\\), directions alternate: \\(R_n=0\\) for even n and has magnitude \\(\\ell\\) for odd n, so \\(f_n\\to0\\). The absolute-summability proof does not apply at either endpoint. Substitution into a formula derived from a convergent infinite series cannot replace these checks. 8.3 Crystal geometry is a return problem L17 13:42 A dilute interstitial solute on equivalent empty sites can approach the independent-jump limit \\(f=1\\). At greater concentration, blocked destinations and interactions change that conclusion. For vacancy-mediated self-diffusion, a tagged host atom and the vacancy have different histories: a homogeneous vacancy walk can have equal rates in all directions while the tagged atom backtracks conditionally on the vacancy's location. The lecture's rough coordination estimate \\(f\\simeq1-2/z\\) gives 0.83333333 for FCC coordination \\(z=12\\). It is not an exact FCC lattice result. It suppresses the detailed return paths, their projections and repeated encounters. Coordination number describes one shell of connectivity; correlation involves a vacancy wandering through the surrounding lattice and returning after arbitrarily many moves. An explicit route to a calculation is a first-passage problem. Hold the tracer at the origin until its next exchange. For every other vacancy site r, let \\(h_\\alpha(r)\\) be the probability that this vacancy eventually causes the next tracer jump in direction \\(\\alpha\\). Include absorption when that exchange occurs; an escaping vacancy contributes no further exchange in that encounter. If \\(w_{rr'}\\) are vacancy rates between ordinary sites, \\(w_{r0}\\) is the exchange rate from r when available, and \\(b_\\alpha(r)\\) is one for exchange in direction \\(\\alpha\\) and zero otherwise, conditioning on the next event gives \\[\\sum_{r'\\ne0}w_{rr'}[h_\\alpha(r')-h_\\alpha(r)]\n+w_{r0}[b_\\alpha(r)-h_\\alpha(r)]=0.\\tag{8.6}\\] The rate multiplying a nonexistent edge is zero. This backward equation follows by multiplying each possible next-state probability by its outcome and rearranging; its unknowns are probabilities, not concentrations. A remote escape condition or a lattice Green-function treatment completes the problem. A finite artificial absorbing boundary discards trajectories that leave that boundary and later return, so enlarging the domain or correcting its boundary is a scientific convergence requirement. Having a numerically solved linear system is insufficient if the boundary changed the intended return problem. A strictly one-dimensional isolated vacancy gives a useful singular limit. After its exchange with a tagged atom, it cannot pass around that atom. Repeated tagged jumps undo earlier ones, and the tag stays in the two positions adjacent to that vacancy as the vacancy explores either side. Its displacement remains bounded and its long-time factor tends to zero under this single-vacancy setup. Multiple vacancies, finite concentrations, a different jump network or a different thermodynamic limit are different problems; the example does not prove that all one-dimensional transport is zero. Interstitialcy adds another bookkeeping issue: successive tagged jumps may alternate between different states or lengths. As an original simplified illustration, group two displacements of lengths \\(\\ell_A,\\ell_B\\) into one cycle, assume distinct cycles are independent and have zero vector mean, and let the mean intracycle angle cosine be c. Then the cycle has second moment \\(\\ell_A^2+\\ell_B^2+2\\ell_A\\ell_Bc\\). Relative to the uncorrelated contribution of its two constituent moves, the factor is \\(1+2\\ell_A\\ell_Bc/(\\ell_A^2+\\ell_B^2)\\). Actual interstitialcy can retain memory across cycles and needs additional correlations. A formula for one selected sequence cannot be transferred to every interstitialcy mechanism merely by name. 8.4 The dilute FCC five-frequency model L18 18:59 A substitutional solute B changes nearby vacancy energies and jump barriers. The standard five-frequency model assumes a dilute solute, dilute monovacancies, FCC nearest-neighbor jumps, and solute–vacancy interactions restricted to the first neighbor shell. Outside that local environment, equivalent solvent exchanges recover one bulk rate. Five per-edge successful jump rates, all in s⁻¹, distinguish the events: Rate Event Role in a tracer encounter \\(\\omega_0\\) Bulk solvent–vacancy exchange Controls remote wandering. \\(\\omega_1\\) Solvent exchange keeping the vacancy in B's nearest-neighbor shell Rotates the pair orientation without moving B. \\(\\omega_2\\) B–vacancy exchange Moves the tagged solute. \\(\\omega_3\\) Vacancy leaves that shell Dissociates the nearest-neighbor pair. \\(\\omega_4\\) Vacancy enters that shell Associates the pair again. From a nearest-neighbor vacancy site there is one exchange with B, four within-shell moves and seven outward moves. Competing exponential clocks therefore give the probability that the next vacancy event immediately reverses the preceding solute exchange: \\[p_{\\rm immediate}=\\frac{\\omega_2}{\\omega_2+4\\omega_1+7\\omega_3}.\\tag{8.7}\\] This is not the probability of every eventual reverse tracer jump. The vacancy can rotate around B or leave the first shell and return before B moves again. A completed encounter includes all those solvent moves. For these standard rate conventions, the full dilute model has the solute tracer factor \\[f_B^*=\\frac{2\\omega_1+7F\\omega_3}\n{2\\omega_2+2\\omega_1+7F\\omega_3}.\\tag{8.8}\\] Here F is the dimensionless lattice-return/escape function appropriate to the model, dependent on \\(\\omega_4/\\omega_0\\). It accounts for returns after dissociation; it must come from the corresponding exterior-lattice problem or a documented approximation. The displayed expression is checked against Bocquet, §3.3, PDF page 11 . The L18 25:00 slide has inconsistent rotation coefficients between its approximate and “exact” expressions. Equation (8.8) uses the verified standard per-edge convention instead of combining those displayed coefficients. Several checks expose the physics without solving the exterior problem here. For fixed positive rotation/escape contribution, \\(\\omega_2\\to0\\) gives \\(f_B^*\\to1\\): long solvent rearrangements largely erase direction memory before the slow solute moves again. If \\(\\omega_1=0\\) and \\(F\\omega_3=0\\) while \\(\\omega_2\\gt 0\\), the factor vanishes because exchange just retraces itself. Large \\(\\omega_2\\) at fixed other rates makes many rapid tracer exchanges mostly ineffective for net spreading. Multiplying every rate by a common positive factor leaves f unchanged; it changes the physical time scale, and thus D, rather than directional correlations. For a synthetic algebraic example take \\(\\omega_1=1\\), \\(\\omega_2=2\\), \\(\\omega_3=0.5\\) s⁻¹ and supply \\(F=0.8\\). Equation (8.7) gives 0.21052632, while (8.8) gives 0.54545455. These numbers answer different questions. The F input is illustrative; the companion does not pretend to solve an FCC lattice-return integral or identify a real alloy's \\(\\omega_4/\\omega_0\\). BCC has a different nearest-neighbor shell connectivity: there is no analogous within-first-shell nearest-neighbor rotation. The restricted expression displayed in L18 26:00 is \\(f_B^*=7F_3\\omega_3/(2\\omega_2+7F_3\\omega_3)\\), with its BCC escape function \\(F_3\\). This change is geometric and model-specific. Longer-ranged interactions, more vacancy shells, interstitial mechanisms and concentrated solutes require a larger network; one cannot set \\(\\omega_1=0\\) in a formula and thereby establish every aspect of an arbitrary BCC alloy. 8.5 Binding constrains ratios, not every rate L18 23:06 Let the free energy of a vacancy at a specified near-solute site be lower than at a specified distant site by positive binding free energy \\(g_b\\). The equilibrium probability ratio for those individual configurations is \\(p_{\\rm near}/p_{\\rm far}=\\exp[g_b/(k_BT)]\\). Detailed balance on an outward/inward edge gives \\(p_{\\rm near}\\omega_3=p_{\\rm far}\\omega_4\\), so \\[\\frac{\\omega_3}{\\omega_4}=\\exp[-g_b/(k_BT)].\\tag{8.9}\\] This statement uses configuration free energies including their nonconfigurational entropy. It compares reverse rates on the same edge, not total outward/inward rates with different neighbor multiplicities. Counting the multiplicities belongs in the population balance. A shared saddle energy determines the two barrier heights relative to their different starting energies, and fixes this ratio; its absolute height is still an independent kinetic input. Binding may increase the probability of finding a vacancy beside B while also increasing return memory or slowing dissociation. The solute tracer coefficient is schematically \\(D_B^*=\\ell^2\\omega_2 P_{\\rm near}f_B^*/6\\), where \\(P_{\\rm near}\\) is the mean number of available nearest-neighbor vacancies per solute in the dilute single-occupancy limit. If a per-neighbor vacancy probability is used instead, the factor z belongs explicitly in that definition. Thus binding, the exchange rate and correlation all enter; favorable binding alone does not imply faster long-range diffusion. Inference is correspondingly limited. A measured D supplies a combination of population, rate and correlation, while f itself depends on rate ratios. Multiplying all rates by a common factor changes D but leaves f fixed. Distinct changes in population and exchange rate can compensate each other in D. Independent defect-population information, several transport observables or atomistic barrier calculations are needed to separate them. The broader-range treatment in Bocquet's paper, introduction and §§3–4.2 , also makes clear why a five-frequency truncation needs a stated interaction range. Only those specified parts were consulted here; the complete numerical lattice-integral implementation is outside this chapter's laboratory. 8.6 Exercises with worked solutions 1. Recover the finite-step weights Starting from equation (7.1), derive equation (8.2). Why is replacing \\(1-k/n\\) by one unjustified for an arbitrary short walk? Worked solution At separation k, pairs are \\((1,1+k),\\ldots,(n-k,n)\\), giving \\(n-k\\) equal expectations \\(\\ell^2C_k\\) under stationarity. The diagonal sum is \\(n\\ell^2\\). Divide the resulting expression by that quantity to obtain (8.2). For k comparable to n, the weight is appreciably smaller than one: there are few such pairs. Passing to the infinite series requires an additional limiting argument, with absolute summability sufficient to control the tail. 2. Check the Markov model by two methods For the two-direction model with \\(\\rho=-0.5\\), find the 40-step and limiting factors. Describe how the executable distribution calculation checks the formula without sampling random angles. Worked solution Keeping the last direction has probability 0.25, so the process favors reversal. Equation (8.5) gives \\(f_{40}=0.34444444\\) and \\(f=1/3\\). The code stores the probability at every allowed position and previous direction, sends each probability to the next states with probabilities 0.25 and 0.75, and sums position squared times final probability. Dividing its MSD by 40 reproduces the finite correlation sum to floating-point precision. Normalization is checked at the same time. 3. Disprove a universal geometric closure Choose two independent unbiased unit jump vectors A and B from the six cubic directions, then repeat A,B,A,B indefinitely. The joint law is unchanged by shifting the sequence one place. Compute \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\). Does cubic symmetry force \\(C_2=C_1^2\\)? Worked solution Independence and zero means give \\(C_1=\\mathbb E(A\\cdot B)=0\\), whereas every second jump is identical, giving \\(C_2=1\\). The sequence is stationary under index shifts because interchanging independently identically distributed A and B preserves the joint law; it is also cubically symmetric. Its long memory violates the conditional closure (8.4). After \\(n=2M\\) steps, \\(R=M\\ell(A+B)\\), so \\(\\mathbb E|R|^2=2M^2\\ell^2\\) and \\(f_n=M\\), not a finite normal-diffusion factor. Symmetry does not establish temporal mixing. 4. Distinguish immediate reversal from a complete encounter Use the synthetic FCC rates in §8.4 to calculate the immediate-reversal probability and full factor. Test the slow-solute limit and explain why the two quantities cannot be equated. Worked solution The immediate probability is \\(2/(2+4+3.5)=0.21052632\\). The correlation factor is \\((2+2.8)/(4+2+2.8)=0.54545455\\), using the supplied F. Immediate reversal classifies the next vacancy event; the full factor combines net tracer displacement with all rotations, dissociations, returns and repeated tracer exchanges. Letting \\(\\omega_2\\to0\\) at fixed positive numerator makes f approach one, even though a vacancy may perform many moves before that slow tracer exchange. F must be supplied from a justified return calculation for a real application. 5. Audit binding and multiplicities Derive equation (8.9). Would multiplying \\(\\omega_3\\) by seven while leaving \\(\\omega_4\\) as a per-edge rate preserve that equation? Does a known binding free energy determine the absolute exchange rates? Worked solution Detailed balance equates equilibrium flows on one reversible edge, \\(p_{\\rm near}\\omega_3=p_{\\rm far}\\omega_4\\). The Boltzmann probability ratio gives the stated exponential. Multiplying only the outward rate changes the counted object to a sum over edges and mixes incompatible conventions. The near/far state counts would then need explicit multiplicities. Binding fixes an equilibrium free-energy difference and a rate ratio; raising the common saddle changes both absolute rates without changing their ratio. 6. Identify what a tracer experiment determines Suppose only \\(D_B^*\\) is measured and vacancy population is unknown. Explain two sources of ambiguity in extracting the five rates. What observations could help? Worked solution The coefficient contains the product of a near-solute vacancy population, exchange rate and correlation factor. Population and exchange rate can compensate each other. In addition, f depends on rate ratios and the exterior return function, so several sets of rates can yield the same correlation and coefficient. Independent vacancy measurements constrain population; measurements sensitive to different rate combinations, or computed formation and saddle free energies with detailed balance enforced, add information. A single measured number cannot uniquely determine five kinetic rates merely because a five-frequency formula exists. 8.7 Sources and limits L17 motivates tracer memory; L18 supplies the lag-sum and five-frequency topics, supplemented by L20 . Selected L18 frames at 24:05, 25:00 and 26:00 were inspected. The standard FCC expression and its scope were checked in J. L. Bocquet, “Correlation factor for diffusion in cubic crystals with solute-vacancy interactions of arbitrary range,” introduction and §§3–4.2 . The Python companion verifies the original Markov example and algebraic limiting cases; it does not reproduce the paper's full exterior-lattice solution. No unverified source-table number or escape-function fit is needed for these demonstrations."}, {"number": 9, "title": "Temperature, pressure, and activation parameters", "url": "chapter-09.html", "text": "An activation energy extracted from a straight line is a derivative of a measured coefficient. Its interpretation depends on the model behind that coefficient. A vacancy formation cost, migration barrier, changing population, correlation factor and competing pathway can all influence the same slope. Pressure provides a second derivative probe, but its “activation volume” needs equally careful conventions. Source spine: L19–20, drawing on Chapters 6–8. All explicitly synthetic temperature/pressure data below are original teaching constructions. Source exercise data are treated separately from these constructions, and missing experimental details are not reconstructed from captioned fit coefficients. 9.1 Define the slope before naming its barrier L19 00:21 The familiar Arrhenius form is \\(D=D_0\\exp[-Q/(RT)]\\), with positive diffusivity D, prefactor \\(D_0\\) in the same units, absolute temperature T in kelvins, gas constant R, and molar activation parameter Q in J mol⁻¹. One can equivalently use a per-particle energy q and Boltzmann constant \\(k_B\\), with \\(Q=N_{\\rm Av}q\\). Here \\(N_{\\rm Av}\\) is the Avogadro constant, in mol⁻¹. An electronvolt is an energy per event, not an energy per mole; conversion must precede substitution into a molar formula. Taking a logarithm of a dimensioned quantity is shorthand for choosing a reference unit. Write \\(y=\\ln[D/D_{\\rm ref}]\\), with constant \\(D_{\\rm ref}=1\\) m² s⁻¹ in the laboratory plots, and \\(u=1/T\\). With constant \\(D_0,Q\\), \\(y=\\ln(D_0/D_{\\rm ref})-(Q/R)u\\). Its negative slope times R returns Q. Changing D's units shifts the intercept; using base-ten logarithms changes the slope by a factor \\(\\ln10\\). For a general positive D, define a local apparent activation enthalpy at fixed pressure by \\[Q_{\\rm app}(T,P)\\equiv\n-R\\left(\\frac{\\partial\\ln D}{\\partial(1/T)}\\right)_P\n=RT^2\\left(\\frac{\\partial\\ln D}{\\partial T}\\right)_P.\\tag{9.1}\\] The derivative is local even if a straight-line fit averages over a broad interval. Curvature, a phase transition or a changed defect population can make the fitted value depend strongly on the chosen temperatures. To connect the derivative to thermodynamics, write \\(D=A(T,P)\\exp[-G^\\ddagger(T,P)/(RT)]\\), with molar activation free energy \\(G^\\ddagger\\). The prefactor A includes the explicitly retained geometric and kinetic factors. Define \\(H^\\ddagger=G^\\ddagger-T(\\partial G^\\ddagger/\\partial T)_P\\). Differentiating gives \\[Q_{\\rm app}=H^\\ddagger+\nRT^2\\left(\\frac{\\partial\\ln A}{\\partial T}\\right)_P.\\tag{9.2}\\] A constant activation entropy changes the prefactor when \\(G^\\ddagger=H^\\ddagger-TS^\\ddagger\\); it should not be counted again in A if already retained inside G. Attempt frequencies, lattice spacing, correlations and changing entropies can contribute to apparent slopes. A straight line is evidence for an approximately constant aggregate over the observed range, not a unique microscopic mechanism. For example, an equilibrium vacancy-mediated host tracer has \\(D^*\\propto f\\ell^2\\nu\\exp[-(G_f+G_m)/(RT)]\\), with molar formation and migration free energies \\(G_f,G_m\\). Under constant enthalpies and negligible temperature variation of the remaining factors, \\(Q_{\\rm app}\\simeq H_f+H_m\\). An imposed dilute interstitial-solute population can instead have \\(Q_{\\rm app}\\simeq H_m\\), while equilibrium host self-interstitial transport includes its formation cost. Quenched or irradiated vacancy populations can remove or replace the equilibrium formation contribution. Counting the number of named barriers alone cannot rank carbon and chromium diffusivities in iron; prefactors, actual energies, structures and populations are required. 9.2 Parallel contributions produce a weighted slope L19 04:32 Suppose several statistically additive pathways contribute \\(D=\\sum_iD_i\\), each positive. This sum is an assumed transport model: connected parallel routes sharing a concentration gradient can support it, whereas sequential bottlenecks or slowly exchanging populations may not. Define \\(w_i=D_i/D\\), so the weights are positive and sum to one. Differentiating the logarithm gives the exact identity \\[Q_{\\rm app}=\\sum_iw_iQ_{{\\rm app},i}.\\tag{9.3}\\] If every path has constant prefactor and constant molar enthalpy \\(Q_i\\), its local apparent parameter is \\(Q_i\\), but the weights still change with temperature. For two paths, equality occurs at \\(T_\\times=(Q_2-Q_1)/[R\\ln(D_{02}/D_{01})]\\) when the quotient is positive. The larger barrier can become relevant at higher temperature when accompanied by a larger prefactor. Take synthetic inputs \\(D_{01}=10^{-8}\\), \\(D_{02}=10^{-5}\\) m² s⁻¹ and \\(Q_1=100,Q_2=180\\) kJ mol⁻¹. The contributions cross at 1392.89654 K. At 800, 1000 and 1600 K, path 2 supplies fractions 0.00594359, 0.06215035 and 0.70974327. The apparent parameters are consequently 100.475487, 104.972028 and 156.779462 kJ mol⁻¹. There is no third elementary barrier with those intermediate values; they describe a temperature-dependent mixture. Figure 9.1. Original synthetic two-path model. The total follows a smooth logarithmic sum and changes its local slope as the dominant contribution changes. No material data are fitted in this figure. A useful original diagnostic follows by differentiating again with respect to \\(u=1/T\\). For constant \\(D_{0i},Q_i\\), \\[\\frac{d^2\\ln D}{du^2}\n=\\frac{\\sum_iw_iQ_i^2-(\\sum_iw_iQ_i)^2}{R^2}\\ge0.\\tag{9.4}\\] The numerator is the weighted variance of the pathway parameters. Thus this particular additive model produces convex logarithmic Arrhenius curves and a local Q between the component values. Curvature with the opposite sign requires another ingredient, such as varying prefactors, population changes, transitions or a different transport topology. This criterion is conditional on the stated model; it is not a universal shape theorem for every diffusion dataset. 9.3 Activation volume is a pressure derivative L19 07:38 Define the molar apparent activation volume at fixed T by \\[V_{\\rm app}\\equiv-RT\\left(\\frac{\\partial\\ln D}{\\partial P}\\right)_T.\\tag{9.5}\\] Pressure P is in pascals, so \\(RT\\) in J mol⁻¹ multiplied by an inverse-pressure derivative gives m³ mol⁻¹. A per-particle convention uses \\(v_{\\rm app}=-k_BT\\,\\partial_P\\ln D\\), and \\(V_{\\rm app}=N_{\\rm Av}v_{\\rm app}\\). Mixing these conventions creates an Avogadro-scale error. A positive apparent volume means diffusivity decreases with increasing pressure at fixed temperature; a negative one means the opposite. For \\(D=A\\exp[-G^\\ddagger/(RT)]\\), define the thermodynamic activation volume \\(V^\\ddagger=(\\partial G^\\ddagger/\\partial P)_T\\). Direct differentiation yields \\[V_{\\rm app}=V^\\ddagger-RT\\left(\\frac{\\partial\\ln A}{\\partial P}\\right)_T.\\tag{9.6}\\] The activation volume is a derivative between activation and reference states, not the physical specimen volume and not necessarily the volume of a spherical hole. When \\(V^\\ddagger\\) and A are approximately pressure independent over an interval, \\(D(P)/D(P_0)=\\exp[-V^\\ddagger(P-P_0)/(RT)]\\). At high pressure or across a structural change, extending that linear free-energy approximation is unjustified. For a synthetic \\(V^\\ddagger=4.0\\times10^{-6}\\) m³ mol⁻¹, or 4 cm³ mol⁻¹, and \\(T=1000\\) K, one gigapascal changes the activation free energy by 4000 J mol⁻¹. The diffusivity ratio is 0.61810964. The equivalent per-particle volume is \\(6.64215627\\times10^{-30}\\) m³. Its small-looking SI value is not evidence for a negligible pressure effect; the dimensionless quantity \\(PV^\\ddagger/(RT)=Pv^\\ddagger/(k_BT)\\) controls the exponential. For additive paths at fixed temperature, the same logarithmic differentiation gives \\(V_{\\rm app}=\\sum_iw_iV_{{\\rm app},i}\\). Pressure changes those weights too. Inferring a single migration geometry from an aggregate pressure slope requires evidence that the same pathway remains dominant over the fitting interval. 9.4 Formation volume includes the atom reservoir L19 10:44 Vacancy relaxation and vacancy formation are different volume comparisons. Define \\(\\Omega\\) as the volume per atom in a specified bulk reservoir. Start with a perfect reference crystal containing N sites and atoms. Remove an atom into that reservoir and let the defective N-site crystal relax. Let its volume change relative to the original perfect crystal be \\(\\Omega_{\\rm rel}^v\\), the vacancy relaxation volume . The reservoir gains one atom and volume \\(\\Omega\\). The total formation volume under this convention is \\[\\Omega_f^v=\\Omega+\\Omega_{\\rm rel}^v.\\tag{9.7}\\] Inward relaxation often makes \\(\\Omega_{\\rm rel}^v\\) negative, but its magnitude and even details of sign are material-dependent. A missing host atom is not a rigid empty sphere with an automatically specified thermodynamic volume. For a self-interstitial, one atom is taken from the reservoir into the crystal. If the defective crystal's excess volume at fixed original lattice-site reference is \\(\\Omega_{\\rm rel}^i\\), the reservoir loses \\(\\Omega\\), giving \\(\\Omega_f^i=-\\Omega+\\Omega_{\\rm rel}^i\\). These expressions define the convention before assigning signs. Different site references or reservoirs require corresponding bookkeeping; the measurable thermodynamic derivative must be compared with the same formation process. A migration volume compares the saddle configuration and the stable initial defect configuration. For vacancy-mediated transport at equilibrium, the formation and migration contributions add when the rate expression factorizes accordingly. For a solute whose favorable binding lowers its near-solute vacancy formation free energy, a model with \\(D_B^*=A_0f_B^*\\exp[-(G_f-G_b+G_m)/(RT)]\\) gives \\[V_{{\\rm app},B}=V_f-V_b+V_m\n-RT\\left(\\frac{\\partial\\ln(A_0f_B^*)}{\\partial P}\\right)_T,\\quad\nV_b\\equiv\\left(\\frac{\\partial G_b}{\\partial P}\\right)_T.\\tag{9.8}\\] Binding's minus sign follows the positive-binding-energy convention: favorable \\(G_b\\) lowers the activation free energy. Its volume derivative need not itself be positive. A pressure-dependent correlation factor can modify the apparent result without changing the list of defect species. Any interpretation should start with the complete defined D expression and then differentiate it, rather than assemble signs from the words “formation” and “binding.” 9.5 Melting-point trends are conditional comparisons L19 18:59 The lecture compares diffusion parameters with melting temperatures. Within related bonding and crystal-structure families, the same energetic scales can influence cohesion and defect barriers, making empirical correlations useful for estimates. They do not establish that every material has the same D at melting, that every FCC solid diffuses more slowly than every BCC solid, or that melting fixes a unique vacancy fraction. Ordering, phase changes, anisotropy and different carriers can defeat a simple trend. It is especially important to distinguish increasing pressure at fixed T from moving along a melting curve \\(T_m(P)\\). Let \\(D_m(P)=D[T_m(P),P]\\). Applying the chain rule and definitions (9.1) and (9.5) gives \\[V_{\\rm app}\n=\\frac{Q_{\\rm app}}{T_m}\\frac{dT_m}{dP}\n-RT_m\\frac{d\\ln D_m}{dP},\\tag{9.9}\\] with the local apparent quantities evaluated on that curve. If D at melting is approximately pressure-independent over the interval, the second term is neglected. Interpreting the remaining Q and V as constant microscopic enthalpy and volume needs the additional single-path/prefactor assumptions. The chain rule itself does not establish empirical constancy of \\(D_m\\). For synthetic \\(Q_{\\rm app}=200\\) kJ mol⁻¹, \\(T_m=1500\\) K and \\(dT_m/dP=30\\) K GPa⁻¹, neglecting the second term gives \\(V_{\\rm app}=4.0\\times10^{-6}\\) m³ mol⁻¹. Converting the pressure unit is essential: the slope is \\(30\\times10^{-9}\\) K Pa⁻¹. This exercise illustrates dimensions and the path derivative; it does not validate constant melting-point diffusivity in a named element. 9.6 Fit data with their units and volume reference L20 28:07 A useful fitting exercise must preserve the data table, temperature scale, pressure units and logarithm convention. A captioned final fit coefficient cannot recover those inputs by itself. Before recomputing the source table below, the companion constructs an explicitly synthetic single path with \\(D_0=2.0\\times10^{-6}\\) m² s⁻¹ and \\(Q=150.0\\) kJ mol⁻¹: T (K) Synthetic D (m² s⁻¹) 800 \\(3.21533496\\times10^{-16}\\) 900 \\(3.93936881\\times10^{-15}\\) 1000 \\(2.92406470\\times10^{-14}\\) 1100 \\(1.50752185\\times10^{-13}\\) 1200 \\(5.91326689\\times10^{-13}\\) A least-squares line through \\(\\ln(D/D_{\\rm ref})\\) versus \\(1/T\\) recovers the input prefactor and Q to floating-point precision when full-precision values are used. This verifies implementation and units. It does not estimate experimental error: the values were generated by the same exact law and contain no measurement noise. Real fits need residual inspection, uncertainty propagation and a defensible model for errors in D and T. The intercept can be strongly correlated with the slope over a narrow temperature range. The synthetic pressure data from §9.3 have \\(\\ln[D(P)/D(0)]\\) slope \\(-0.48108942\\) per GPa. Equation (9.5) returns 4 cm³ mol⁻¹ only after converting the slope to per pascal. A pressure recorded in GPa while interpreted as Pa changes the inferred volume by a billion. Recomputed source tutorial, separate from the synthetic test The selected 720p L20 frame at 28:10 makes the Q14 data table readable. Its temperature dataset is labeled P = 1 atm, and its separate pressure dataset is labeled T = 800 K. The temperature entries are explicitly Celsius; the pressure column is explicitly in units of \\(10^5\\) Pa. Transcribing those labels gives: T (°C) D (m² s⁻¹) P (\\(10^5\\) Pa) D at 800 K (m² s⁻¹) 700 \\(7.0\\times10^{-20}\\) 0.5 \\(5.0\\times10^{-17}\\) 800 \\(5.50\\times10^{-19}\\) 500 \\(4.60\\times10^{-17}\\) 900 \\(3.40\\times10^{-18}\\) 2500 \\(3.00\\times10^{-17}\\) 1000 \\(4.20\\times10^{-17}\\) 4000 \\(2.50\\times10^{-17}\\) 1100 \\(2.10\\times10^{-16}\\) 5500 \\(2.00\\times10^{-17}\\) After converting the left column by \\(T_K=T_{\\circ{\\rm C}}+273.15\\), an unweighted natural-log linear fit gives \\(Q=224.879154\\) kJ mol⁻¹, \\(D_0=5.94642329\\times10^{-8}\\) m² s⁻¹ and \\(R^2=0.98764447\\). The regression's slope-based standard error corresponds to 14.521750 kJ mol⁻¹ under the usual independent equal-variance log-residual model. This is a conditional regression statistic from five supplied points, not a reported experimental uncertainty. The largest absolute log residual is about 0.53006, so the points do not exactly follow one Arrhenius line. Fitting the right-hand dataset at its stated 800 K gives slope \\(-1.68466536\\times10^{-4}\\) per \\(10^5\\) Pa and \\(R^2=0.98928754\\). Equation (9.5) gives \\(V_{\\rm app}=1.12056697\\times10^{-5}\\) m³ mol⁻¹, or \\(v_{\\rm app}=1.86074523\\times10^{-29}\\) m³ per particle. The conditional regression standard error of the molar volume is \\(6.73225387\\times10^{-7}\\) m³ mol⁻¹. The selected 30:10 frame displays the same pressure slope to its shown precision. Its handwritten diffusivity units on that logarithmic slope should be read as a dimensional slip: \\(\\partial_P\\ln D\\) has inverse-pressure units. These are source-given exercise data; their originating material, experimental errors and measurement protocol are not established by the table. Normalizing by an “atomic volume” introduces a second convention. For a crystal with conventional-cell volume \\(V_{\\rm cell}\\) containing n atoms, the actual volume per atom is \\(\\Omega=V_{\\rm cell}/n\\), or equivalently molar volume divided by \\(N_{\\rm Av}\\). For ideal hard-sphere FCC, \\(a=2\\sqrt2r\\) and \\(\\Omega=a^3/4=4\\sqrt2r^3\\). The geometric sphere volume is \\(4\\pi r^3/3\\); their ratio is the packing fraction \\(\\pi/(3\\sqrt2)\\), not one. With synthetic \\(r=0.128\\) nm, these two volumes are 0.0118632832 and 0.00878452976 nm³. Dividing an activation volume by the sphere volume gives a larger dimensionless number than dividing by the lattice volume per atom. Neither convention may be silently substituted for the other. The source tutorial uses a sphere-radius construction; a reported fraction of atomic volume must state whether it means that geometric sphere or a thermodynamic volume per atom. Other L20 checks are distributed where their assumptions are developed: Chapter 6 treats the 0.005/0.05 solute-fraction ambiguity, divacancy abundance and dilatometry; Chapter 7 treats mechanisms and FCC direction counts; Chapter 8 treats the single-vacancy one-dimensional limit, interstitialcy and slow-solute correlation. Their answers follow from models and units, rather than potentially inconsistent captioned option letters. 9.7 Exercises with worked solutions 1. Interpret a temperature-dependent prefactor Let \\(D=A_0T^m\\exp[-Q/(RT)]\\), with constant \\(A_0,m,Q\\). Find \\(Q_{\\rm app}\\). How does a vacancy population held fixed by preparation change the usual formation-plus-migration interpretation? Worked solution The logarithm is \\(\\ln A_0+m\\ln T-Q/(RT)\\), up to the fixed diffusivity reference. Equation (9.1) gives \\(Q_{\\rm app}=Q+mRT\\). The fitted slope includes the prefactor's temperature dependence even though Q is constant. For equilibrium monovacancies, an independent population factor can supply \\(H_f\\) in addition to \\(H_m\\). If preparation fixes that population during the experiment, its equilibrium formation exponential does not describe the measured variation; migration and any prefactor/correlation variation may dominate instead. One must establish population kinetics before subtracting enthalpy contributions. 2. Derive the two-path curvature For constant-prefactor Arrhenius contributions, derive (9.3) and (9.4). Use the synthetic pair to find the apparent Q at 1000 K and explain its meaning. Worked solution Since \\(dD_i/du=-(Q_i/R)D_i\\), differentiating \\(\\ln\\sum_iD_i\\) gives \\(-\\sum_iw_iQ_i/R\\). Further differentiation uses \\(dw_i/du=-(w_i/R)(Q_i-\\sum_jw_jQ_j)\\), yielding the weighted variance divided by \\(R^2\\). At 1000 K the weights are 0.93784965 and 0.06215035, so \\(Q_{\\rm app}=104.972028\\) kJ mol⁻¹. It is the derivative of the aggregate, between the two component values; it is not evidence for a separate 104.972028 kJ mol⁻¹ microscopic saddle. 3. Recover a pressure volume in two conventions At 1000 K, a synthetic natural-log diffusivity ratio has pressure slope \\(-0.48108942\\) GPa⁻¹. Infer the molar and per-particle volumes, and predict \\(D(1\\ {\\rm GPa})/D(0)\\) under the constant-volume model. Worked solution The slope is \\(-0.48108942\\times10^{-9}\\) Pa⁻¹. Multiplying by \\(-RT\\) gives \\(4.0\\times10^{-6}\\) m³ mol⁻¹. Dividing by \\(N_{\\rm Av}\\) gives \\(6.64215627\\times10^{-30}\\) m³ per particle. The ratio is \\(\\exp[-(10^9)(4\\times10^{-6})/(R\\,1000)]=0.61810964\\). The per-particle expression with \\(k_B\\) yields the same result. Using the unconverted per-GPa number would produce a volume a billion times too large. 4. Separate relaxation from formation A vacancy's defective crystal has relaxation volume \\(-0.3\\Omega\\) relative to the N-atom perfect reference. Under the reservoir convention of §9.4, what is the formation volume? If positive binding free energy enters as \\(-G_b\\) in the activation free energy, what sign accompanies its volume derivative? Worked solution The atom transferred to the reservoir adds \\(\\Omega\\), giving \\(\\Omega_f^v=\\Omega-0.3\\Omega=0.7\\Omega\\). Reporting \\(-0.3\\Omega\\) as the formation volume would omit the reservoir contribution. Differentiating \\(G_f-G_b+G_m\\) with pressure gives \\(V_f-V_b+V_m\\), where \\(V_b=\\partial_PG_b\\) can have either sign. Pressure-dependent geometry, attempt rates and correlation add the derivative term in (9.8). 5. Audit a temperature fit Describe how the synthetic table recovers its prefactor and Q. What changes if the plotted ordinate is \\(\\log_{10}D\\), if Q is inserted in kJ mol⁻¹ with R in J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, or if temperatures are inserted in Celsius? Worked solution Fit \\(y=\\ln(D/D_{\\rm ref})\\) to \\(b+mu\\), \\(u=1/T\\). Then \\(Q=-Rm\\) and \\(D_0=D_{\\rm ref}e^b\\), recovering 150,000 J mol⁻¹ and \\(2\\times10^{-6}\\) m² s⁻¹ from full-precision data. A base-ten slope obeys \\(Q=-R\\ln10\\,m_{10}\\). A kJ/J mismatch changes the exponential energy scale by a thousand. Celsius is offset from absolute zero, so \\(1/T_{\\rm Celsius}\\) is not an allowed substitution in the thermal law. Exact recovery of synthetic inputs checks the calculation; it does not provide confidence intervals for real measurements. 6. Compare a melting derivative and a volume normalization Assume D remains constant along a melting curve with local Q of 200 kJ mol⁻¹, \\(T_m=1500\\) K and \\(dT_m/dP=30\\) K GPa⁻¹. Infer V. Then explain the difference between hard-sphere and lattice atomic-volume normalization in ideal FCC. Worked solution The chain rule gives \\(V=(Q/T_m)dT_m/dP=4\\times10^{-6}\\) m³ mol⁻¹ under the stated constancy assumption. If D along that curve changes, the omitted term is \\(-RT_m\\,d\\ln D_m/dP\\). In ideal FCC, \\(\\Omega_{\\rm lattice}=4\\sqrt2r^3\\), while \\(\\Omega_{\\rm sphere}=4\\pi r^3/3\\). Their ratio is 0.74048049. A volume fraction normalized by the sphere is therefore larger than one normalized by lattice volume per atom. Both the path of differentiation and the normalization must be named before comparing reported activation volumes. 9.8 Sources and limits L19 develops temperature, pressure, defect-volume and melting-point comparisons; L20 supplies the tutorial. The chapter derives the local-slope and chain-rule identities explicitly. The Python companion generates the synthetic fits and Figure 9.1, verifies pressure conventions in molar and per-particle units, and compares sphere with lattice volumes. Liquid ink or dissolution demonstrations may contain convection and other transport processes; they motivate temperature sensitivity but do not quantitatively measure solid-state diffusion. Selected source frames at28:10 and30:10 support the explicitly separated Q14 fit. No empirical material ranking follows merely from the number of formation or migration terms in a schematic mechanism."}, {"number": 10, "title": "Free energy, interdiffusion, and phase formation", "url": "chapter-10.html", "text": "Two polished alloys are pressed together and annealed. Does the junction become a smooth composition gradient, develop a new layer, or separate into two compositions? A diffusivity supplies a rate only after thermodynamics has identified the direction in which redistribution can lower free energy. Lectures 21–23 supply the free-energy, chemical-potential, diffusion-couple, and phase-development spine. The regular-solution calculation is a synthetic teaching model, not an assessed Cu–Ni phase diagram. Its parameters and all printed numerical results are computed in the companion laboratory . 10.1 From a specimen to a molar free-energy curve L21 01:21 Consider a closed binary specimen containing chemical components A and B at fixed temperature \\(T\\) , in kelvin, and pressure \\(p\\) , in pascals. A component is an independently specified chemical constituent; a phase is a macroscopically homogeneous state with a particular structure and thermodynamic constitutive function. A single crystal structure can support many compositions. Two regions with the same structure can also be distinct phases when they have different equilibrium compositions. Let \\(n_A,n_B\\) be amounts in moles, \\(n=n_A+n_B\\) , and \\(z=N_B=n_B/n\\) the B mole fraction. Then \\(N_A=1-z\\) . For a homogeneous phase with negligible surface contributions, extensivity permits \\(G=n g(z,T,p)\\) , where \\(G\\) is Gibbs energy in joules and \\(g\\) is molar Gibbs energy in J/mol. The plot of \\(g\\) against \\(z\\) is always associated with a specified phase, temperature, and pressure. A reference state separates mixing from the energies of pure components in that phase. Write \\(g=(1-z)g_A^0+zg_B^0+g_{\\rm mix}\\) . The superscript zero denotes the chosen pure reference, not zero numerical energy. Changing either reference adds a straight line to the entire curve. Such a line changes the individual chemical-potential values but leaves curvature and the equilibrium compositions of a self-consistently transformed model unchanged. Comparing phases requires common references; independently shifting one phase is a physical change to their relative stability. For an ideal substitutional solution, all arrangements of atoms on equivalent sites have the same energy at a specified composition. The number of arrangements produces the molar mixing entropy \\(s_{\\rm mix}=-R[(1-z)\\ln(1-z)+z\\ln z]\\) , where \\(R\\) is the molar gas constant. The logarithm acts on dimensionless fractions, and the limiting convention is \\(z\\ln z\\to0\\) at zero. With zero mixing enthalpy, the definition \\(g=h-Ts\\) gives \\[\ng_{\\rm mix}^{\\rm ideal}=RT[(1-z)\\ln(1-z)+z\\ln z]. \\tag{10.1}\n\\] The plus sign before the bracket is essential: its contents are nonpositive. At equal proportions the laboratory gives \\(g_{\\rm mix}=-5763.15\\) J/mol at the synthetic temperature 1000 K. Disorder lowers Gibbs energy in this ideal model. The temperature derivative at fixed pressure and composition is \\(\\partial_T g=-s\\) ; free energy is not generally inversely proportional to temperature. A regular solution retains this random configurational entropy but adds an interaction enthalpy \\(\\Omega z(1-z)\\) , with \\(\\Omega\\) in J/mol. Positive \\(\\Omega\\) penalizes unlike neighbors in this mean-field representation. The model is useful because one adjustable competition generates either complete miscibility or a tendency to separate. It does not establish the actual phase diagram of a named alloy; ordering, magnetic contributions, vibrational entropy, and composition-dependent interactions can all change that diagram. 10.2 The quantity transported down a thermodynamic force L21 10:33 Moving one component between two neighboring regions changes their amounts, so the relevant derivative is with respect to amount, not composition alone. Define the chemical potential of A as \\(\\mu_A=(\\partial G/\\partial n_A)_{T,p,n_B}\\) , in J/mol; define \\(\\mu_B\\) by holding \\(n_A\\) fixed. The subscripts matter because changing \\(n_A\\) changes both the total amount and the mole fraction. Chemical potential is a partial molar Gibbs energy, rather than the energy of an isolated atom. At fixed B amount, \\(\\partial n/\\partial n_A=1\\) and \\(\\partial z/\\partial n_A=-z/n\\) . The product and chain rules applied to \\(ng(z)\\) therefore give \\(\\mu_A=g-zg'\\) , where a prime denotes differentiation with respect to \\(z\\) . For a B addition, \\(\\partial z/\\partial n_B=(1-z)/n\\) . Thus \\[\n\\mu_A=g-zg',\\qquad \\mu_B=g+(1-z)g',\\qquad\n\\mu_B-\\mu_A=g',\\qquad g=(1-z)\\mu_A+z\\mu_B. \\tag{10.2}\n\\] A tangent drawn at composition \\(z\\) has equation \\(y(u)=g(z)+(u-z)g'(z)\\) . Its intercept at \\(u=0\\) is \\(\\mu_A\\) ; its value at \\(u=1\\) is \\(\\mu_B\\) . The tangent-intercept construction is therefore a graphical expression of the amount derivative, not a separate definition. The slope gives the difference between two chemical potentials, so equal slope does not imply equal individual potentials unless the tangent intercepts also agree. Substitution of (10.1) gives \\(\\mu_A=g_A^0+RT\\ln(1-z)\\) and \\(\\mu_B=g_B^0+RT\\ln z\\) . The regular-solution model adds \\(\\Omega z^2\\) to the A potential and \\(\\Omega(1-z)^2\\) to the B potential. For the synthetic example used here and below, take \\(T=1000\\) K, \\(\\Omega=3RT\\) , and zero pure-reference energies. At \\(z=0.3\\) , the computed values are \\(\\mu_A=-720.656\\) and \\(\\mu_B=2211.873\\) J/mol. Their weighted average is \\(g=159.103\\) J/mol, checking extensivity explicitly. Activity packages nonideality into a dimensionless quantity: \\(\\mu_i=\\mu_i^0+RT\\ln a_i\\) . With the pure-component reference, \\(a_i=\\gamma_iN_i\\) defines the activity coefficient \\(\\gamma_i\\) . An ideal solution has \\(\\gamma_i=1\\) . A pure stable phase has activity one relative to itself at the same conditions, even though its chemical potential is not zero. “Pure silver has unit activity” is consequently a reference-state statement; it does not assign unit activity to dilute silver in every host. 10.3 Common tangents and the amount of each phase L21 15:40 Suppose the specimen can split into phases \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) , with compositions \\(z_\\alpha,z_\\beta\\) . Transferring a small A amount from alpha to beta changes total Gibbs energy by \\((\\mu_A^\\beta-\\mu_A^\\alpha)dn_A\\) . If either transfer direction is allowed at equilibrium, the first-order change must vanish. Repeating for B gives two equalities: \\(\\mu_A^\\alpha=\\mu_A^\\beta\\) and \\(\\mu_B^\\alpha=\\mu_B^\\beta\\) . Equilibrium does not require the A potential to equal the B potential. By (10.2), those two equalities require a line tangent to both phase curves. If the phases share a single regular-solution curve, the line touches that curve at two distinct compositions. For a total B fraction \\(\\bar z\\) between them, let \\(f_\\beta\\) be the fraction of total moles in beta. Conservation gives \\(\\bar z=(1-f_\\beta)z_\\alpha+f_\\beta z_\\beta\\) , hence the lever rule \\[\nf_\\beta=\\frac{\\bar z-z_\\alpha}{z_\\beta-z_\\alpha},\\qquad\ng_{\\rm pair}=(1-f_\\beta)g(z_\\alpha)+f_\\beta g(z_\\beta). \\tag{10.3}\n\\] This fraction is a molar fraction of phases. A volume fraction additionally needs their molar volumes. Between the two contact compositions, the equilibrium energy follows the straight segment, while the homogeneous energy follows the curved branch. The stable equilibrium is the lower convex envelope of all candidate phase curves: the lowest energy achievable by conserving composition while combining phases. The sign of mixing energy relative to an unmixed reference does not alone answer the stability question. The synthetic calculation sets \\(\\Omega=3RT\\) . Symmetry makes the common tangent horizontal, so its contact compositions solve \\(g'(z)=RT\\ln[z/(1-z)]+\\Omega(1-2z)=0\\) . The noncentral roots are 0.0707202 and 0.9292798. At \\(\\bar z=0.3\\) , the B-rich phase fraction is 0.267052. The two-phase energy is −485.077 J/mol, lower than the homogeneous 159.103 J/mol. Figure 10.1 visualizes the energy reduction; its values describe the declared model only. Figure 10.1. Synthetic regular solution at 1000 K with interaction parameter 3RT. The common-tangent segment gives a lower conserved-composition energy than the homogeneous branch between the contact compositions. The curve is an original calculation, not an experimental phase diagram. The Gibbs phase rule counts independent intensive variables at equilibrium. With \\(C\\) independent components and \\(P\\) phases, each phase contributes \\(C-1\\) independent compositions; temperature and pressure add two. Equality of each component potential between a reference phase and each other phase imposes \\(C(P-1)\\) constraints. The remaining count is \\(F=P(C-1)+2-C(P-1)=C-P+2\\) , assuming independent constraints and no additional fields or reactions. Fixing pressure subtracts one; fixing temperature as well subtracts another. A binary two-phase equilibrium at fixed temperature and pressure has zero intensive composition freedom. Its two compositions are fixed, but the phase amounts can vary through (10.3). Phase fraction is an extensive partition of total material, not an additional intensive degree of freedom. This distinction will matter when a diffusion path crosses a two-phase region. 10.4 Downhill free energy can mean uphill composition L22 00:20 A concentration gradient is useful only when it represents the thermodynamic force of interest. To expose that restriction, take a binary lattice with constant molar site concentration \\(c_s=1/V_m\\) , where \\(V_m\\) is a constant molar volume. Exchange of B for A leaves the number of occupied sites unchanged. The exchange potential is \\(\\Delta\\mu=\\mu_B-\\mu_A=g'(z)\\) . For one spatial coordinate \\(x\\) , in meters, adopt the simple isothermal kinetic closure \\(J_B=-\\mathcal K(z)\\partial_x\\Delta\\mu\\) with \\(J_A=-J_B\\) . Here \\(J_B\\) is the exchange molar flux in mol/(m²·s), and the nonnegative exchange coefficient \\(\\mathcal K\\) has units mol²/(J·m·s). The closure is a constitutive assumption, rather than a consequence of conservation. Species balance gives \\(c_s\\partial_t z=-\\partial_xJ_B\\) . On a fixed interval with zero flux at both ends, the total free energy per area is \\(\\mathcal G=\\int c_s g(z)dx\\) . Differentiate, substitute balance, and integrate by parts. The boundary term is zero, yielding \\[\n\\frac{d\\mathcal G}{dt}=\\int (\\partial_x\\Delta\\mu)J_B\\,dx\n=-\\int\\mathcal K(\\partial_x\\Delta\\mu)^2dx\\leq0. \\tag{10.4}\n\\] The sign is controlled by the kinetic coefficient. Within a homogeneous branch, \\(\\partial_x\\Delta\\mu=g''\\partial_xz\\) , so comparison with a Fick form gives \\(\\widetilde D=\\mathcal K g''/c_s\\) . Positive curvature produces ordinary down-gradient diffusion. Negative curvature gives a flux up the composition gradient while still reducing total Gibbs energy. Uphill diffusion therefore need not violate the second law. The regular solution has \\(g''=RT/[z(1-z)]-2\\Omega\\) . At \\(\\Omega=3RT\\) , curvature is negative between 0.211325 and 0.788675. This spinodal interval is the local instability region: infinitesimal long-wavelength composition fluctuations lower the homogeneous free energy. It is narrower than the coexistence interval. Between a common-tangent contact and the adjacent spinodal boundary, separation may lower global energy even though small fluctuations cost energy; creating a new phase then entails an interfacial barrier that this bulk curve omits. A negative coefficient in the local second-order diffusion equation amplifies ever shorter wavelengths without bound, so it is not by itself a well-posed continuum phase-separation model. A finite interfacial penalty supplies a length scale. For example, adding \\(\\tfrac12\\kappa(\\partial_xz)^2\\) to the free-energy density, with \\(\\kappa>0\\) in J/m, changes the exchange potential to \\(g'(z)-(\\kappa/c_s)\\partial_{xx}z\\) . The resulting conserved fourth-order model damps sufficiently short wavelengths. This is an original model extension, not a claim that a local negative diffusivity alone predicts an alloy microstructure. For several independent fluxes, linear irreversible thermodynamics instead writes a vector flux as a matrix times chemical-potential gradients. Thermodynamic admissibility constrains the symmetric part of that kinetic matrix to be positive semidefinite. Cross coefficients can make one component flux oppose its own isolated force; the nonnegative total dissipation is the requirement. Thus “each species always moves down its own chemical potential” needs the additional diagonal approximation. Chapter 13 will make the reference-frame and mobility conventions explicit. 10.5 Why a phase diagram does not predict a layer thickness L23 00:20 A diffusion couple joins two initially uniform specimens with different compositions. A useful planar experiment needs a clean contact, controlled temperature, an atmosphere that limits oxidation or evaporation, and an annealing time short enough that the far ends retain their original compositions when semi-infinite analysis is intended. A fixture maintains contact; inert marker particles can identify lattice motion. None of these preparations alone proves that the interface is equilibrated or that every phase is visible in a later microscope. If one solid-solution phase spans the relevant compositions, a smooth profile can develop. With composition-dependent interdiffusion coefficient \\(\\widetilde D(c)\\) , balance in a fixed-volume frame becomes \\(\\partial_t c=\\partial_x[\\widetilde D(c)\\partial_xc]\\) , with \\(c\\) in mol/m³. The product rule gives \\(\\partial_t c=\\widetilde D\\partial_{xx}c+(d\\widetilde D/dc)(\\partial_xc)^2\\) . Dropping the gradient-squared term while allowing a variable coefficient is inconsistent. The concentration-independent equation is a special case, not the generic binary alloy equation. If an intermediate phase has lower Gibbs energy, its formation can produce a layer bounded by composition jumps. Under local equilibrium, each side of an interface approaches the corresponding phase composition selected by equality of component chemical potentials. The concentration jump is compatible with a continuous chemical potential across an ideal equilibrated interface. A smooth measured jump may reflect finite instrumental resolution rather than a true homogeneous intermediate composition. Phase existence and layer growth are separate questions. A stable phase can grow slowly because its mobility is small, fail to nucleate during a finite anneal, occupy a layer below the instrument's spatial resolution, or be bypassed by nonequilibrium kinetics. Temperature and time change diffusion lengths as well as thermodynamic stability. Changing either end member changes which phases a composition path can reach. An equilibrium phase diagram supplies admissible coexistence, not all these kinetic and observational facts. For an ideal isothermal planar binary construction with locally equilibrated two-phase material, the coexistence compositions and chemical potentials are fixed. Changing only the local phase fraction does not create a chemical-potential gradient through that equilibrium mixture. This explains why a simple layer sequence is often built from single-phase diffusion regions separated by interfaces. It is not a universal theorem forbidding two-phase binary reaction zones: morphology, moving phase fractions, stress, interface kinetics, and departures from local equilibrium require a richer model. Ternary systems add intensive composition freedom and permit paths through two-phase fields with changing tie-lines. The laboratory consumes only declared synthetic thermodynamic parameters, solves the symmetric common-tangent problem, checks the lever-rule balance, and writes a figure and saved numerical output . A numerical root finder brackets the noncentral root to avoid selecting the central stationary point. Successful convergence verifies an equation of the chosen model; it does not validate its parameters for any real alloy. This distinction becomes decisive when a database supplies hundreds of coupled phase models in Chapter 14. 10.6 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Differentiate the amount, not just the fraction Derive the two chemical potentials from \\(G=(n_A+n_B)g[n_B/(n_A+n_B)]\\) . Why is simply identifying \\(g'\\) with the B chemical potential wrong? Worked solution At fixed B amount, differentiating total amount gives one, while differentiating the fraction gives \\(-z/n\\) . The product rule gives \\(\\mu_A=g-zg'\\) . At fixed A amount the fraction derivative is \\((1-z)/n\\) , giving \\(\\mu_B=g+(1-z)g'\\) . Subtraction yields \\(g'=\\mu_B-\\mu_A\\) . The composition derivative describes exchange at fixed total amount; adding only B also increases total amount. The two operations have different constraints. 2. A reference change Replace \\(g(z)\\) by \\(g(z)+a+bz\\) , with constants in J/mol. Determine the chemical-potential shifts and whether a coexistence pair changes. Worked solution The derivative shifts by \\(b\\) . Formula (10.2) gives an A shift \\(a\\) and a B shift \\(a+b\\) . Applying the same reference transformation to every candidate phase leaves equality of each component potential unchanged. Curvature is unchanged, and a common tangent acquires the same added straight line. Its contact compositions consequently remain unchanged. Shifting only one phase independently would alter relative stability and is a different thermodynamic model. 3. Reconstruct the synthetic phase mixture For coexistence fractions 0.0707202 and 0.9292798 and total composition 0.3, compute the B-rich molar phase fraction. Explain why it need not equal a measured volume fraction. Worked solution Subtracting the A-rich composition from the amount balance gives \\(f_\\beta=(0.3-0.0707202)/(0.9292798-0.0707202)=0.267052\\) , with rounding. Substitution returns 0.3. If the molar volumes differ, the beta volume fraction is \\(f_\\beta V_m^\\beta/[(1-f_\\beta)V_m^\\alpha+f_\\beta V_m^\\beta]\\) . It equals the molar fraction only for equal molar volumes. Microscopy measures geometry, so the conversion is part of comparison with a thermodynamic lever rule. 4. Locate local instability For a regular solution with \\(\\Omega=3RT\\) , derive the spinodal limits. Explain why they are not the common-tangent contact compositions. Worked solution Setting curvature to zero gives \\(1/[z(1-z)]=6\\) , or \\(z^2-z+1/6=0\\) . The roots are \\((1\\pm\\sqrt{1/3})/2\\) , numerically 0.211325 and 0.788675. Between them curvature is negative. Common tangency compares a finite composition separation and minimizes the conserved total energy; the spinodal tests the second variation about one homogeneous composition. A locally stable state can still be globally metastable, so the two intervals need not coincide. 5. Check dissipation with cross coupling Let two dimensionless force gradients be the components of \\(\\boldsymbol f\\) , and let \\(\\boldsymbol J=-\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\2&5\\end{pmatrix}\\boldsymbol f\\) in a consistently scaled kinetic model. Can an individual flux go uphill while total dissipation remains nonnegative? Worked solution The symmetric matrix has positive first principal minor one and determinant one, so it is positive definite. For \\(\\boldsymbol f=(1,-1)\\) , the flux is \\((1,3)\\) : the first flux has the same sign as its own gradient. Nevertheless \\(-\\boldsymbol f\\cdot\\boldsymbol J=2>0\\) . Equivalently the quadratic form is \\((f_1+2f_2)^2+f_2^2\\) . Cross coupling permits the individual uphill contribution; total free-energy decrease constrains the sum. 6. A missing intermediate phase A phase diagram permits three intermediate phases, but an annealed couple shows two layers. Give a defensible inference and design a follow-up that distinguishes two explanations. Worked solution The observation establishes only that two layers were resolved under the preparation and characterization conditions. It does not establish thermodynamic absence of the third phase. A nucleation barrier and an unresolved thin layer are two different explanations. Longer anneals with controlled interfaces test time dependence; higher-resolution cross-sectional composition and diffraction measurements test whether a thin structurally distinct region already exists. A seeded couple can test nucleation sensitivity. Each test must retain atmosphere and temperature control, because changing those also changes possible phases and rates. 10.7 Sources, calculations, and limits L21 , L22 , and L23 establish the subject sequence. The equations and synthetic calculations are derived locally. Source thought experiments about particular Cu–Ni temperatures are used only to motivate competing outcomes; no assessed Cu–Ni phase boundary is inferred here. Experimental micrograph phase labels obscured in captions are not reconstructed as facts. The homogeneous regular-solution model omits interface energy until the explicitly labeled extension, elastic strain, vacancy transport, and explicit atomistic correlations. Equal chemical potentials describe equilibrium; finite-rate interfaces can depart from that condition. The next chapter makes the spatial reading of composition profiles and diffusion paths precise before an inverse diffusivity is extracted from them."}, {"number": 11, "title": "Reading composition profiles and diffusion paths", "url": "chapter-11.html", "text": "A composition scan across an annealed junction shows slopes, plateaus, and abrupt steps. Before converting that scan into diffusivities, decide which features represent homogeneous material, interfaces, phase mixtures, and instrument averaging. The same-looking plateau can encode very different thermodynamic constraints. Lectures 24–26 supply profile interpretation, the third tutorial, incremental couples, and pseudobinary constructions. The spatial profiles and flux values used below are explicitly synthetic. Named experimental systems motivate categories; unresolved source micrograph labels are not used as quantitative evidence. 11.1 A profile has a domain, an amount balance, and a history L24 00:21 A composition profile assigns a measured or modeled composition to position in a specimen. Let \\(x\\) denote the coordinate normal to an initially planar junction and \\(t\\) the annealing time. Write \\(z(x,t)=N_B\\) for a dimensionless B mole fraction. Conversion to molar concentration requires the molar volume: \\(c_B=z/V_m\\) , in mol/m³. A profile drawn in atomic percent, mass percent, or concentration is not interchangeable with a mole-fraction profile without the appropriate conversion. The far-field behavior separates two physically different experiments. In a semi-infinite couple , the diffusion zone remains small relative to the distance to the outer faces, so each side retains its initial composition. The profile broadens while the remote reservoirs remain different. In a finite closed couple, material eventually reaches the outer faces; if one stable phase can hold the total composition, the long-time state becomes uniform. A semi-infinite solution cannot be extrapolated indefinitely to predict that final state. For two finite blocks with amounts \\(n_L,n_R\\) and initial B fractions \\(z_L,z_R\\) , conservation fixes the final overall fraction, regardless of diffusivity: \\[\n\\bar z=\\frac{n_Lz_L+n_Rz_R}{n_L+n_R}. \\tag{11.1}\n\\] Uniformity additionally requires that a homogeneous phase at this fraction be stable and kinetically accessible. If equilibrium instead contains two phases, the final overall fraction remains (11.1), while local compositions remain at the coexistence endpoints and phase fractions obey the lever rule. “Diffusion makes everything uniform” is therefore a statement about a particular stable single-phase model, not a general definition of diffusion. The laboratory joins synthetic amounts two and one moles at fractions 0.2 and 0.8. Equation (11.1) gives 0.4, whereas the unweighted average gives 0.5. Equal geometrical lengths imply equal amounts only when cross-sectional areas and molar volumes support that inference. A composition line scan does not automatically reveal total amounts. An experimental comparison should record specimen dimensions, porosity, and possible evaporative or reactive losses before treating an average as a conserved value. Within a stable solid solution, a smooth composition variation generally gives smoothly changing chemical potentials. For an ideal binary solution, \\(\\mu_B=\\mu_B^0+RT\\ln z\\) , with temperature \\(T\\) , gas constant \\(R\\) , and reference potential \\(\\mu_B^0\\) . The relation is logarithmic rather than universally proportional to composition. For a nonideal phase, the derivative of its Gibbs-energy function supplies the relation. A phase label must therefore accompany the profile when its shape is interpreted thermodynamically. 11.2 A jump is a boundary condition, not an enormous gradient L24 10:37 Suppose two phases meet at \\(x=s(t)\\) . Their limiting concentrations are \\(c_L=c_B(s^-,t)\\) and \\(c_R=c_B(s^+,t)\\) , each in mol/m³. The superscripts mean approach from the left and right. A locally equilibrated interface can have \\(c_L\\ne c_R\\) while both component chemical potentials agree across it. The common tangent selects the two compositions. An actual finite-width atomic interface is here idealized as a mathematical surface. Apply species conservation to a thin control interval that moves with that surface. Let \\(J_L^{\\rm lab},J_R^{\\rm lab}\\) be signed laboratory-frame fluxes immediately outside it, and let \\(v=ds/dt\\) , in m/s. Relative to the moving interval, the flux is \\(J^{\\rm lab}-vc\\) . If the interface stores no finite excess amount and contains no reaction source for the selected conserved component, the relative incoming and outgoing fluxes agree. Thus \\[\nJ_L^{\\rm lab}-vc_L=J_R^{\\rm lab}-vc_R,\n\\qquad v(c_R-c_L)=J_R^{\\rm lab}-J_L^{\\rm lab}. \\tag{11.2}\n\\] This jump condition uses fluxes in one common frame. Substituting intrinsic lattice-frame fluxes without adding lattice motion can give a wrong interface velocity. If the concentration jump vanishes, (11.2) constrains flux continuity but does not determine velocity by division. If the interface stores an appreciable surface excess, its balance contributes an additional term. For a synthetic jump from 10000 to 20000 mol/m³ with laboratory fluxes \\(10^{-8}\\) and \\(3\\times10^{-8}\\) mol/(m²·s), (11.2) gives \\(v=2\\times10^{-12}\\) m/s to the right. Both relative fluxes are \\(-10^{-8}\\) mol/(m²·s). The negative relative flux does not conflict with positive laboratory flux: the boundary overtakes some material as it moves. The laboratory checks the signed jump balance rather than comparing magnitudes alone. A finite experimental probe convolves the true profile with its spatial response. It can round a composition discontinuity into a steep continuous-looking transition. Dividing a flux by that measured slope would assign a coefficient to the instrument-blurred interface, not to either homogeneous phase. Identify phase boundaries before applying a single-phase inverse formula. The spatial jump and the corresponding chemical-potential continuity answer different questions. 11.3 Narrow phases and changing phase fractions L24 14:41 An intermediate phase can occupy a substantial spatial layer while admitting only a narrow homogeneity range : the composition interval over which its homogeneous structure exists under specified conditions. The width of that interval is dimensionless; the thickness of its layer is a length. A broad layer with an almost flat composition profile therefore does not imply a broad phase field in the phase diagram. Let the exchange potential change by \\(\\Delta\\mu\\) across such a layer while composition changes by only \\(\\Delta z\\) . A large derivative \\(d(\\mu_B-\\mu_A)/dz\\) permits a finite potential variation with a small composition change. The flux law based on potential can remain finite even though a composition-based coefficient becomes very large or poorly identifiable. In the exact line-compound idealization, composition is fixed; a gradient inferred by dividing two unresolved numbers has no pointwise meaning. Integrated diffusion measures or explicit defect/mobility models are needed. Figure 11.1 compares two original piecewise profiles with the same chosen spatial layer width and different allowed composition ranges. The drawing specifies no annealing time or material coefficient; it illustrates the independent meanings of spatial thickness and compositional width. Phase identification in a real sample also needs evidence such as diffraction, crystallography, and calibrated composition. Image contrast or a flat segment alone is not sufficient. Figure 11.1. Original schematic profiles. Left: the spatial thickness of an intermediate layer does not determine its homogeneity range. Right: equal spectator fractions in both end members do not establish uniformity; the trough and peak conserve spectator inventory relative to the uniform 0.25 starting value under constant molar volume. A different kind of apparent plateau is an equilibrium two-phase mixture. At fixed temperature and pressure in a binary system, the endpoint compositions and both chemical potentials are fixed along one tie-line. Two specimens can have different overall compositions solely because their phase fractions differ. Joining them supplies no chemical-potential difference if their phases are already in equilibrium on that same tie-line. There can be a visible difference in phase fraction without a driving force for bulk chemical interdiffusion in this ideal construction. This observation requires the same tie-line, the same temperature and pressure, and no additional driving fields. Regions in different two-phase fields need not have the same potentials. Interface curvature, stored strain, or an externally imposed field can also drive redistribution. A statement about zero chemical force in a specified equilibrium state is not a general claim that mixtures never evolve. 11.4 Diffusion paths, incremental couples, and spectators L26 00:21 A diffusion path is the sequence of local compositions encountered while moving through a diffusion zone, represented in composition space. In a binary system it lies on a line because \\(N_A+N_B=1\\) . Its phase jumps skip compositions that are not occupied as a homogeneous phase. In a ternary or higher-component system, the path can curve: relative fluxes and kinetic cross coupling change local component ratios. A straight line joining end-member compositions need not be the actual path. Experimental systems in the source span broad solid solutions, narrow intermetallics, and multiple reaction layers. They include Au–Cu, Ni–Sn, W–Zr, Mg–Al, Ag–Zn, Ni–Al, and several silicide couples. Their common teaching role is to break the inference from phase-diagram width to measured layer thickness. Exact reported temperatures, widths, and phase labels require the corresponding original micrograph and attribution; the schematic reasoning here does not reproduce those empirical measurements or infer unread stoichiometries. An incremental couple joins compositions chosen close together within a target phase. For example, compositions on either side of an ordered alloy's reference composition can restrict the observed zone to a small portion of its homogeneity range. This makes a local effective interdiffusion coefficient easier to interpret, provided the phase remains single and the difference is large enough to measure accurately. The approximation is a local linearization of a constitutive response; it does not prove that diffusivity is composition-independent over the full phase field. A pseudobinary couple is a multicomponent construction in which only two components vary appreciably through the diffusion zone. The other components are spectators whose profiles must remain uniform. The source end members have nominal percentages Cr25Fe25Co20Ni30 and Cr25Fe25Co30Ni20. The active Co and Ni fractions sum to 0.5, so a normalized Co coordinate for the active subspace is \\(Y_{\\rm Co}=N_{\\rm Co}/(N_{\\rm Co}+N_{\\rm Ni})\\) , varying initially from 0.4 to 0.6. This normalization is useful only after the spectator constraint is verified throughout the zone. Equal spectator endpoints are necessary but not sufficient. In a multicomponent kinetic law, a spectator flux can be driven by the other chemical-potential gradients. A uniform initial spectator concentration can therefore redistribute internally while retaining equal distant endpoint values. The right panel of Figure 11.1 constructs \\(N_s(\\xi)=0.25+0.02\\sin^3(\\pi\\xi)\\) on \\(-1\\leq\\xi\\leq1\\) , where \\(\\xi=x/L_{\\rm ref}\\) and \\(L_{\\rm ref}>0\\) is a reference length. Both endpoints are 0.25, the minimum is 0.23 at \\(\\xi=-1/2\\) , and the maximum is 0.27 at \\(\\xi=1/2\\) . The deviation is odd, so its integral over the interval is zero. With the same constant molar volume as the initial state, the depletion exactly balances the excess and spectator inventory is conserved. This schematic is a counterexample to endpoint sufficiency; it is not a solved multicomponent diffusion history or a measured alloy profile. A credible pseudobinary analysis measures every component, checks the spectator profiles against uncertainty, establishes phase identity and molar-volume conventions, and then applies the reduced inverse model only within its verified domain. If a spectator hump exceeds measurement uncertainty, a binary coefficient may no longer represent the underlying transport matrix. Calling an alloy “high entropy” does not remove these constraints; the local path and the observation model still determine which parameters are identifiable. 11.5 Tutorial checks: missing zones and the dilute limit L25 00:21 A binary single-phase profile is commonly described by one interdiffusion coefficient function \\(\\widetilde D(z)\\) , whereas its constituents have separate intrinsic coefficients relative to the lattice. “One coefficient” means one constitutive function for the constrained binary profile, not one universal number for every composition and temperature. Constant \\(\\widetilde D\\) remains a legitimate approximation; the fact that concentration varies does not logically force that coefficient to vary. A specimen with no visible interdiffusion zone admits several explanations. The chemical potentials could already agree, the diffusion length could be smaller than the resolution, or the anneal could be too short. Poor contact and an oxide barrier add experimental possibilities. A null profile alone does not select among them. Varying annealing time, improving spatial resolution, and inspecting the interface answer different parts of the diagnosis. A square-root-time spreading law is a prediction under a fixed diffusion model, not an all-purpose test for every reaction zone. The tutorial's mixing-energy calculation compares a mixed value of 3 J/mol with an unmixed reference of 8 J/mol, giving \\(\\Delta g=-5\\) J/mol. If the mixing enthalpy is zero and the mixing entropy is 5 mJ/(mol·K), conversion to 0.005 J/(mol·K) gives \\(T=-\\Delta g/\\Delta s=1000\\) K. The sign and the milli-prefix both matter. A reported negative mixing energy supports mixing relative to that reference, but phase stability still requires comparison with every allowed competing state. The regular-solution derivative is \\(dg_{\\rm mix}/dz=\\Omega(1-2z)+RT\\ln[z/(1-z)]\\) . It tends to negative infinity as \\(z\\to0^+\\) at positive temperature with finite \\(\\Omega\\) . Nevertheless \\(g_{\\rm mix}\\to0\\) , because both \\(z\\ln z\\) and \\(z(1-z)\\) vanish. A diverging slope at a boundary does not mean an infinite finite-composition energy change. Integrating the slope over a shrinking interval gives a shrinking change. Consequently this mathematical limit does not, by itself, prove that a finite isolated pure specimen is impossible or determine its impurity content. Impurity exchange requires a reservoir chemical potential, allowed defect states, and a finite-system or thermodynamic-limit specification. The logarithmic ideal activity tells how the continuum model responds near dilution; a claim about achievable purity adds a physical preparation problem. The laboratory takes fixed synthetic concentrations, fluxes, and endpoint fractions. It writes Figure 11.1 and the numerical record , checking the signed interface balance and constructing the spectator counterexample. It does not fit raw microscopy or produce a phase identity. A user replacing the synthetic data must supply a common flux frame and concentrations, not just percentages with unspecified volume conversion. 11.6 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Finite and semi-infinite couples Two and one moles of a stable binary solution start at B fractions 0.2 and 0.8. Find their closed-system final composition. What changes if the same initial fractions represent two semi-infinite reservoirs? Worked solution The B amount is \\(2(0.2)+1(0.8)=1.2\\) moles in three total moles, giving 0.4. For semi-infinite reservoirs the total amounts are not finite inputs to this formula. At any finite time the far-field compositions remain 0.2 and 0.8 under that idealization, while a diffusion zone broadens between them. There is no finite global homogeneous composition supplied by averaging two infinite reservoirs. A finite specimen eventually leaves that approximation when its outer boundaries become influential. 2. Check an interface sign Use the concentrations and fluxes in Section 11.2 to calculate both moving-frame fluxes and the interface velocity. What error results from using only the larger laboratory flux? Worked solution The flux difference is \\(2\\times10^{-8}\\) and the concentration jump is \\(10^4\\) , so \\(v=2\\times10^{-12}\\) m/s. On the left, subtracting \\(vc_L=2\\times10^{-8}\\) from \\(10^{-8}\\) gives \\(-10^{-8}\\) . On the right, subtracting \\(vc_R=4\\times10^{-8}\\) from \\(3\\times10^{-8}\\) gives the same result. Using only the right flux ignores solute supplied or removed on the left and violates the interface balance. 3. A two-phase couple with no chemical force Two equilibrated binary mixtures occupy the same tie-line but have different overall compositions. Explain how their chemical potentials can agree and their microscopy images can differ. Worked solution At fixed temperature and pressure the two endpoint phase compositions are fixed by equality of both component potentials. Each mixture is assembled from those same phases, so each component has the same potential in either specimen. Changing overall composition changes the lever-rule phase fractions. Different phase fractions can alter image contrast or connectivity without changing the equilibrium potentials. The conclusion requires a common tie-line and absence of other driving fields; it cannot be extended to arbitrary mixtures with different coexistence endpoints. 4. Endpoints do not establish a pseudobinary path Construct a spectator fraction with equal endpoint values but nonuniform interior, conserving its inventory relative to an initially uniform 0.25 fraction under constant molar volume. State what data would make a pseudobinary reduction defensible. Worked solution On \\(-1\\leq\\xi\\leq1\\) , use \\(0.25+0.02\\sin^3(\\pi\\xi)\\) . The sine vanishes at both ends. Its cube is −1 at \\(\\xi=-1/2\\) and +1 at \\(\\xi=1/2\\) , giving minimum 0.23 and maximum 0.27. Because \\(\\sin^3(\\pi\\xi)\\) is odd, its integral over this symmetric interval is zero. Multiplication by the common spatial scale and inverse molar volume preserves zero excess amount. Equal endpoints therefore coexist with redistribution and conserved inventory. A defensible reduction needs measured profiles for all spectators that are uniform within quantified uncertainty, plus a phase-consistent active-component path and appropriate concentration/volume conventions. Equal nominal starting compositions alone provide none of the interior information. 5. Divergent derivative, finite energy Explain how \\(d(z\\ln z)/dz\\) can diverge at zero while \\(z\\ln z\\) tends to zero. What does this imply for the tutorial purity argument? Worked solution The derivative is \\(1+\\ln z\\) , which diverges negatively. Setting \\(z=e^{-u}\\) with \\(u\\to\\infty\\) gives \\(z\\ln z=-ue^{-u}\\to0\\) . The integral of the logarithmic singularity over a shrinking interval is finite and tends to zero. A large local derivative is therefore compatible with a small total energy change. The derivative alone cannot determine finite specimen impurity content; reservoir and defect assumptions must be supplied. 6. Interpret a narrow plateau A thick layer has a composition variation smaller than the probe uncertainty. Can one conclude that diffusion within it is zero? Design a next measurement. Worked solution No. A narrow homogeneity range can support a finite chemical-potential gradient and finite flux while its concentration variation is unresolved. Instrument convolution can also hide a small gradient. First establish phase identity and calibrate the composition resolution; use independent diffusion or tracer measurements, repeated-time layer growth, or an integrated-flux model to constrain transport. Dividing an inferred flux by a slope statistically indistinguishable from zero yields an unstable coefficient, not evidence of zero transport. A line-compound treatment should use its own conserved-flux and defect model. 11.7 Sources and model boundaries L24 supplies the single-phase, miscibility-gap, and intermediate-phase sequence; L25 supplies tutorial diagnostics; L26 supplies experimental-profile categories, incremental couples, and the Cr–Fe–Co–Ni endpoint construction. The jump-balance derivation and spectator counterexample are original teaching developments. Experimental layer ratios and obscured phase labels are not reconstructed from captions. This chapter determines which mathematical object a scan can represent. It does not yet recover diffusivity, establish an atomistic mechanism, or certify local equilibrium from profile appearance. The next chapter derives an inverse formula under explicit single-phase and constant-volume assumptions, then tests how differentiation and endpoint tails limit what that formula can identify."}, {"number": 12, "title": "Boltzmann–Matano analysis as an inverse problem", "url": "chapter-12.html", "text": "An annealed diffusion couple supplies a composition profile rather than a diffusivity. Recovering a coefficient from that profile requires both an integral and a derivative. Their combination can reveal a constitutive function, but it can also magnify a misplaced origin, a truncated tail, or a small measurement ripple. Lecture 27 supplies the Boltzmann transformation and Matano-plane construction. The full derivation here is confined to constant molar volume and a single homogeneous phase. The numerical profiles are synthetic and have a known coefficient, making inversion error measurable. 12.1 Specify the inverse problem before integrating L27 00:21 Let \\(c(x,t)\\) be the molar concentration of component B, in mol/m³, with position \\(x\\) in meters and elapsed time \\(t>0\\) in seconds. The coordinate is volume-fixed. Assume a binary single-phase couple at fixed temperature and pressure, constant molar volume, no source or reaction, no external drift, and no spatially heterogeneous shortcut transport. Adopt a local scalar interdiffusion coefficient \\(\\widetilde D(c)>0\\) , in m²/s, depending on concentration but not explicitly on position or time. The initial state is an ideal planar step: \\(c=c_-\\) on the left and \\(c=c_+\\) on the right, with \\(c_+>c_-\\) . The far fields retain these values throughout the anneal. This semi-infinite assumption excludes an experiment whose outer faces have already altered the diffusion zone. Local conservation and the constitutive flux give \\[\n\\partial_t c=\\partial_x[\\widetilde D(c)\\partial_xc],\\qquad\nJ_B^V=-\\widetilde D(c)\\partial_xc. \\tag{12.1}\n\\] The superscript V identifies the volume-fixed flux. A variable coefficient makes the equation nonlinear: expanding its spatial derivative gives a gradient-squared term. Calling it inhomogeneous merely because it is nonlinear confuses two mathematical properties. The inverse question is to determine a function \\(\\widetilde D(c)\\) from a measured profile at known time, conditional on this entire model. Constant molar volume makes a mole-fraction profile an affine rescaling of concentration. One may therefore replace \\(c\\) throughout the inverse expression by a normalized fraction \\(Y=(c-c_-)/(c_+-c_-)\\) . The constant scale cancels between the composition integral and the derivative. This cancellation does not justify the same substitution when volume changes with composition. Ideal mixing concerns Gibbs energy; it does not imply constant volume. 12.2 Similarity removes time without making the equation linear L27 04:29 The step problem has no imposed length scale. Its diffusion scaling suggests a profile depending on \\(\\lambda=(x-x_M)/\\sqrt t\\) , where \\(x_M\\) is a fixed balance-plane position to be determined. This variable has units m/s½; it becomes dimensionless only after division by a reference square-root diffusivity. Write \\(c(x,t)=C(\\lambda)\\) . A prime below means differentiation with respect to this dimensional similarity variable. At fixed position, \\(\\partial_t\\lambda=-\\lambda/(2t)\\) ; at fixed time, \\(\\partial_x\\lambda=t^{-1/2}\\) . Thus \\(c_t=-\\lambda C'/(2t)\\) and \\(\\partial_x(\\widetilde D c_x)=t^{-1}(\\widetilde D C')'\\) . Canceling time gives \\[\n-\\frac{\\lambda}{2}C'=\\frac{d}{d\\lambda}(\\widetilde D(C)C'). \\tag{12.2}\n\\] The equation is now ordinary in the similarity coordinate, but it remains nonlinear through the unknown coefficient function. A point of fixed composition has a fixed \\(\\lambda\\) and moves as \\(x=x_M+\\lambda\\sqrt t\\) . Its velocity is \\(\\lambda/(2\\sqrt t)\\) . This is a geometric motion of a contour, not automatically the lattice velocity, marker velocity, or velocity of an atom. Similarity itself is testable. Profiles measured at several times should collapse when plotted against an appropriately registered \\((x-x_M)/\\sqrt t\\) if the same constitutive function and the assumed initial step apply. Failure can indicate finite initial width, thermal transients, evolving phases, finite-size effects, or a time-dependent coefficient. Collapse supports the assumed scaling; it does not independently establish every microscopic mechanism. 12.3 The inverse integral and the balance plane L27 11:42 Assume the interior profile is smooth and strictly increasing, so position\nis a single-valued function of concentration. At the fixed observation time,\nassume the balance integral\n\\(\\int_{c_-}^{c_+}|x(c)-x_0|\\,dc\\) is finite for a finite reference position\n\\(x_0\\), and that \\((x-x_0)(c-c_-)\\to0\\) at the left infinity and\n\\((x-x_0)(c-c_+)\\to0\\) at the right infinity. These are the tail-decay and\nfinite-area conditions used below. Also require vanishing terminal fluxes and\n\\(c_x(x_*,t)\\gt0\\) at an evaluated interior point. Integrate(12.2) from the\nunaffected left terminal to that point, marked by an asterisk. Changing the\nintegration variable from\\(\\lambda\\) to\\(C\\) gives\n\\(\\widetilde D(C_*)C'_*=-\\tfrac12\\int_{c_-}^{C_*}\\lambda(C)dC\\).\nSince\\(C'=\\sqrt t\\,c_x\\), division by the nonzero slope gives the recovered\ncoefficient \\[\n\\widetilde D(c_*)=-\\frac{1}{2t\\,c_x(x_*)}\n\\int_{c_-}^{c_*}[x(c)-x_M]dc. \\tag{12.3}\n\\] This is the Boltzmann–Matano relation under the specified assumptions. The units are \\((\\mathrm{mol/m^2})/[\\mathrm{s}\\,\\mathrm{mol/m^4}]=\\mathrm{m^2/s}\\) . For the increasing profile, the full composition-weighted integral is zero; its partial integral is negative before returning to zero at the right terminal. The minus sign consequently gives positive diffusivity. Reversing coordinate or component conventions requires a consistent rederivation of the limits and signs, rather than replacing the integral by its unsigned area. The right terminal also has zero flux. Integrating over the entire concentration range therefore requires \\(\\int_{c_-}^{c_+}(x-x_M)dc=0\\) , giving the balance plane \\[\nx_M=\\frac{\\int_{c_-}^{c_+}x(c)dc}{c_+-c_-}. \\tag{12.4}\n\\] The Matano plane is this mass-balance plane for the selected concentration and coordinate conventions. Its coincidence with an original geometrical contact plane requires the constant-volume, conserved-amount registration used here. A marker plane is a different object: markers follow lattice motion when the intrinsic component rates differ. Interchanging the two planes erases exactly the information explored in Chapter 13. Integration by parts links the composition integral to measurable areas. With \\(u=c-c_-\\) , the lower endpoint contribution vanishes, and \\[\n\\int_{c_-}^{c_*}(x-x_M)dc\n=(x_*-x_M)(c_*-c_-)-\\int_{-\\infty}^{x_*}(c-c_-)dx. \\tag{12.5}\n\\] The complete profile balance is equivalently equality of solute gained on the low-concentration side and solute lost on the high-concentration side, compared with a step at \\(x_M\\) . The actual measured endpoints must be sufficiently far into the unaffected material to approximate the infinite limits. A finite scan ending inside a tail assigns a missing area to the wrong place and changes the inferred plane. The integral and derivative form is documented in Tas Kavakbasi and colleagues, PDF pp. 1–2, Eqs. 1–3 . Their fitting comparisons on pp. 4–6 make a practical point: two fits that appear close as concentration curves can have materially different derivatives and recovered diffusion coefficients. The mathematical inverse expression is not an assurance that every curve fit supplies a reliable constitutive estimate. 12.4 Sensitivity, endpoints, and volume conventions L27 20:58 Suppose the inferred plane is displaced by \\(\\delta x_M\\) while the measured profile is held fixed. The numerator integral in (12.3) changes by \\(-\\delta x_M(c_*-c_-)\\) . To first order, and exactly for this isolated plane change, \\[\n\\delta\\widetilde D=\\frac{\\delta x_M(c_*-c_-)}{2t\\,c_x(x_*)}. \\tag{12.6}\n\\] The error grows where the slope is small and generally differs between the two sides of the profile. An apparently harmless origin shift can therefore create an artificial composition dependence. Translating both measured positions and the correctly calculated balance plane together leaves (12.3) unchanged; changing one without the other does not. Near a terminal, both the integral and the slope tend to zero. The expression is a limiting ratio, not a reliable division of two noisy endpoint numbers. A line compound, an internal turning point, a composition discontinuity, or unresolved multiphase material invalidates the required local inversion. Negative inferred values in a nominal stable single-phase couple are diagnostic: check noise, tails, smoothing, and model assumptions before calling them negative physical diffusion coefficients. A useful inference workflow retains raw observations, composition calibration, spatial calibration, terminal estimates, and uncertainty. Fit physically admissible monotone profiles, vary the fitting family and smoothing strength, and repeat the integral and derivative calculation. Restrict reporting to an interior range supported by resolution and slope. If uncertainty is available, resample observations and calibration parameters to obtain an ensemble of recovered functions. A single smooth line without sensitivity information can hide the dominant uncertainty. Volume assumptions remain physical rather than cosmetic. Different component partial molar volumes change the relation between concentration, mole fraction, material displacement, and flux frame. The owning NIST abstract by Sekerka, McFadden, and Boettinger explicitly distinguishes local center-of-volume and center-of-moles frames in the variable-volume extension. That abstract establishes the need for frame care; it is not used here as an inspected proof of a generalized formula. Likewise, the Sauer–Freise publisher abstract addresses volume change. Avoiding an explicit Matano-plane calculation does not remove all volume and constitutive assumptions. 12.5 Recover a known function before fitting an unknown one The executable laboratory starts from the constant-coefficient normalized solution \\(Y(x,t)=\\{1+\\operatorname{erf}[x/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]\\}/2\\) , where \\(D=2\\times10^{-14}\\) m²/s and \\(t=10^5\\) s are synthetic inputs. The scale \\(\\sqrt{Dt}=44.7214\\) micrometers. It computes the plane by quadrature, accumulates the integral in composition space, and divides by the analytic derivative to isolate quadrature error first. On the central 5–95% range, the maximum relative coefficient error is \\(4.17\\times10^{-8}\\) . This is a numerical check of the discrete inverse implementation, not a statistical uncertainty for an experiment. Displacing the plane by 4.47214 micrometers produces inferred coefficients approximately \\(2.1010,2.1772,2.4040\\times10^{-14}\\) m²/s near normalized fractions 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8, although the true value is constant. The second experiment adds the deterministic observation ripple \\(0.001\\sin(50x/\\sqrt{Dt})Y(1-Y)\\) and estimates its derivative numerically. This is a deliberately imposed measurement perturbation, not a stochastic noise model or a solution of a new transport equation. The recovered interior coefficient spans about 0.9576 to 1.0463 times the true coefficient. Its rapid oscillation reveals differentiation sensitivity. Smoothing can suppress it, but the resulting bias also needs assessment; choosing a smooth graph is not the same as identifying the true coefficient. Figure 12.1. Known-coefficient inversion and two controlled errors. The right panel compares the correctly registered smooth profile, a plane displaced by one tenth of the diffusion scale, and a small imposed concentration ripple. All data are synthetic; the comparison tests inference sensitivity. The code writes its actual output and the figure. Its assertion targets only the interior recovery of a known model. Production use would need checks for monotonicity, meaningful terminal values, positive annealing time, nonzero interior slopes, and units. These are input conditions to be diagnosed, not values to be silently repaired by clipping an inferred coefficient. 12.6 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Chain-rule audit Derive (12.2) from the similarity variable. Is that variable dimensionless? Worked solution With fixed plane, \\(\\lambda_t=-\\lambda/(2t)\\) and \\(\\lambda_x=1/\\sqrt t\\) . Thus \\(c_t=-\\lambda C'/(2t)\\) , \\(c_x=C'/\\sqrt t\\) , and the flux derivative is \\((\\widetilde D C')'/t\\) . Multiplying the PDE by time produces (12.2). The variable has units m/s½. Dividing by \\(\\sqrt{D_{\\rm ref}}\\) creates a dimensionless variable, but that reference diffusivity must then be retained consistently in the transformed equation. 2. Recover constant diffusivity analytically For the normalized error-function solution, show that the inverse numerator equals \\(-2tD Y_x\\) . Worked solution Here \\(Y_x=e^{-x^2/(4Dt)}/[2\\sqrt{\\pi Dt}]\\) . Converting the composition integral gives \\(I=\\int_{-\\infty}^{x}\\xi Y_\\xi d\\xi\\) . The antiderivative of \\(\\xi e^{-\\xi^2/(4Dt)}\\) is \\(-2Dt e^{-\\xi^2/(4Dt)}\\) . The left tail vanishes, so \\(I=-2DtY_x\\) . Substitution into (12.3) returns D at every finite interior point. The two tails remain limiting ratios, even though this exact expression has a well-defined limit. 3. Translation and plane error Why does translating all positions by a constant leave the recovered coefficient unchanged, whereas moving only the chosen plane changes it? Worked solution If \\(x\\mapsto x+a\\) , (12.4) gives \\(x_M\\mapsto x_M+a\\) , so the difference \\(x-x_M\\) and local derivative are unchanged. If only the plane moves by \\(\\delta\\) , the integral changes by \\(-\\delta(c_*-c_-)\\) . The coefficient therefore changes by \\(\\delta(c_*-c_-)/(2tc_x)\\) . A coordinate convention and a physically misregistered balance plane are different operations. 4. A terminal value A measured terminal slope is zero within uncertainty. Should a software implementation return an infinite diffusivity there? Worked solution No. In the semi-infinite model both numerator and derivative tend to zero; their ratio must be obtained as a controlled limit. Finite measurement noise and tail truncation usually destroy reliable endpoint division. The appropriate output is an unsupported endpoint estimate with an explicitly restricted interior reporting range. A fitted asymptotic model may extend that range, but the extension depends on that model and should carry sensitivity bounds. Infinity is not an experimentally identified coefficient. 5. A nonmonotone profile A scan has an interior maximum and then decreases. Can the same global inverse \\(x(c)\\) be used in (12.3)? Worked solution No: one concentration corresponds to multiple positions, so the required inverse is not single-valued. The turning point also has zero slope. Check whether this is noise, a multiphase boundary, a multicomponent uphill profile, or an initial state outside the single-step assumptions. Splitting the curve into branches does not automatically restore the derivation, because the integration constant and terminal boundary condition came from the full single-phase semi-infinite problem. A different model must establish any branchwise inference. 6. Design an inverse validation Specify three independent checks before accepting a recovered composition-dependent coefficient. Worked solution First, recover a known analytic profile with the same sampling and numerical operations, as the laboratory does; this checks implementation. Second, vary terminal estimates, smoothing, and spatial calibration within uncertainty; this checks inference stability. Third, use the recovered function in a forward solver and compare a separate-time profile with conserved amount and appropriate boundary conditions; this tests prediction outside the fitted scan. None alone proves microscopic validity. Phase identity and volume/frame assumptions remain independently necessary. 12.7 Sources and scope L27 supplies the inverse-problem sequence. The cited profile-analysis paper supports the formula and fitting sensitivity at the inspected locations. The NIST and Sauer–Freise pages were inspected at abstract level only; no full generalized proof is claimed. This chapter's derivation establishes the constant-volume expression locally. A successful inverse fit identifies a coefficient conditional on a model, geometry, calibration, and thermal history. It does not separate the two intrinsic component coefficients or determine lattice motion. Those require the additional marker information and frame transformations developed next."}, {"number": 13, "title": "Kirkendall markers, intrinsic fluxes, and Darken relations", "url": "chapter-13.html", "text": "A diffusion profile reveals how the alloy composition changes, but it does not say how far the lattice itself has moved. Inert particles initially placed at the junction supply that missing reference. Their displacement makes unequal intrinsic component transport measurable and forces every flux to carry a frame. Lectures 28–30 supply Kirkendall markers, Darken relations, mobility, and tutorial inference. The final L30 numerical inputs were checked directly in the high-resolution frames at 18:49, 22:56, and 23:59. The molar volume and illustrative marker clock used in the laboratory are separately labeled synthetic. 13.1 What an inert marker follows L28 00:19 In a substitutional alloy, atoms exchange with vacancies while the lattice can translate or deform relative to a laboratory reference. A marker particle that remains attached to its local lattice environment measures that motion. The Kirkendall effect is the displacement of such inert markers during interdiffusion, demonstrating unequal intrinsic transport of the components. The historical brass/copper experiment used embedded inert wires to reveal movement that a concentration scan alone could not identify. A suitable marker must remain chemically inert, sufficiently small and sparse to avoid altering transport, visible after annealing, and attached to the intended lattice environment. Dissolution, reaction, migration along grain boundaries, or mechanical dragging can compromise interpretation. A line of particles is an experimental proxy for a material reference, not an infallible geometrical object. The original contact, the final marker position, and the composition-balance plane should be registered separately. Let \\(J_i\\) denote the intrinsic molar flux of component \\(i\\) relative to the lattice, in mol/(m²·s). Let \\(J_i^V\\) be its flux in a volume-fixed frame, and \\(v_K\\) the lattice/marker velocity relative to that frame, in m/s. With concentration \\(c_i\\) in mol/m³, a translating reference carries an advective amount \\(c_iv_K\\) , giving \\[\nJ_i^V=J_i+c_iv_K. \\tag{13.1}\n\\] This is a kinematic transformation, not an added diffusion mechanism. The intrinsic flux can be nonzero even when a species has zero volume-fixed flux, because lattice translation compensates it. Conversely, a laboratory flux can include bulk motion. The frame must be established before comparing a constitutive coefficient or balancing a moving interface. Tracer, self, intrinsic, and interdiffusion coefficients describe different experiments or closures. A tracer diffusivity \\(D_i^*\\) describes spreading of a dilute label in a chemically uniform host. Self diffusion is physical tagged-atom transport in a chemically homogeneous substance and includes jump correlations. If \\(D_{\\rm jump}\\) is an explicitly uncorrelated random-walk rate coefficient, a correlation factor \\(f\\) gives \\(D^*=fD_{\\rm jump}\\) . One must not redefine physical self diffusion to remove its correlations. Stable and radioactive isotopes can both serve as labels; isotope mass effects and tracer dilution need consideration. 13.2 Derive the frame change and the Darken weighting L29 00:20 Use a binary substitutional model with constant equal partial molar volumes \\(V_m\\) , in m³/mol. Then \\(c_A+c_B=1/V_m\\) , \\(N_i=V_mc_i\\) , and \\(\\partial_xc_A=-\\partial_xc_B\\) . More generally, the volume identity is \\(\\bar V_Ac_A+\\bar V_Bc_B=1\\) , where bars denote partial molar volumes. The simple derivation below does not silently replace composition-dependent partial volumes by constants. By definition the volume-fixed frame has zero net volume flux. Multiplying (13.1) by the partial volumes and summing gives \\(v_K=-\\sum_i\\bar V_iJ_i\\) . In the equal constant-volume model, adopt intrinsic Fick laws \\(J_A=-D_A\\partial_xc_A\\) and \\(J_B=-D_B\\partial_xc_B\\) . The coefficients \\(D_A,D_B\\) are local intrinsic coefficients of the specified homogeneous phase. They are not tracer coefficients merely because their units agree. Substitution gives the signed lattice velocity \\(v_K=(D_B-D_A)\\partial_xN_B=(D_A-D_B)\\partial_xN_A\\) . Then transform the B flux: \\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_B^V&=-D_B\\partial_xc_B+c_BV_m(D_B-D_A)\\partial_xc_B\\\\\n&=-[N_AD_B+N_BD_A]\\partial_xc_B.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13.2}\n\\] Thus the binary interdiffusion coefficient is \\[\n\\widetilde D=N_AD_B+N_BD_A,\\qquad\nv_K=(D_A-D_B)\\partial_xN_A. \\tag{13.3}\n\\] The weighting is crossed: the A mole fraction weights the B intrinsic coefficient. It comes from eliminating lattice drift under zero volume flux. The result recovers \\(\\widetilde D=D_B\\) as B becomes dilute in A, within this constitutive model. If the intrinsic coefficients are equal, the marker velocity vanishes even when both component fluxes are nonzero. Darken's original 1948 paper, printed p. 188, Eqs. 7–9 , derives these relations under constant gram-atomic volume and a marker-relative transport convention. The linked file begins with a separate later commentary; the original printed pagination identifies the intended source. The derivation here states its modern molar units and frame explicitly rather than treating the historical formula as frame-independent. For a similarity trajectory \\(x_K-x_M=k\\sqrt t\\) with constant \\(k\\) and fixed balance plane, differentiation gives \\(v_K=(x_K-x_M)/(2t)\\) . Dividing displacement by time instead gives twice the instantaneous velocity. Figure 13.1 uses a synthetic clock to show this distinction. It is not a measured marker trajectory for the source couple. Figure 13.1. Lattice-following marker position and a fixed mass-balance plane in a prescribed square-root-time model. The instantaneous slope equals half the displacement divided by elapsed time. The reference displacement is 0.9 micrometers at 100000 s; this clock is synthetic. 13.3 Separate kinetics from the thermodynamic factor L29 14:50 A chemical-potential formulation distinguishes an atom's kinetic response from the energetic cost of a composition fluctuation. Define molar chemical potential \\(\\mu_A=\\mu_A^0+RT\\ln a_A\\) , with dimensionless activity \\(a_A=\\gamma_AN_A\\) . Under a diagonal mobility approximation, write \\(J_A=-c_AB_A\\partial_x\\mu_A\\) . The mobility \\(B_A\\) has units mol·m²/(J·s), so the Onsager coefficient multiplying the gradient is \\(L_{AA}=c_AB_A\\) , in mol²/(J·m·s). At fixed temperature, differentiate the activity relation. Define the dimensionless thermodynamic factor \\(\\Phi_A=d\\ln a_A/d\\ln N_A=1+d\\ln\\gamma_A/d\\ln N_A\\) . With constant volume, \\(\\partial_x\\mu_A=RT\\Phi_A\\partial_xc_A/c_A\\) . Comparison with the intrinsic Fick law gives \\(D_A=RTB_A\\Phi_A\\) . A tracer Einstein approximation \\(RTB_A=D_A^*\\) then yields \\(D_A=D_A^*\\Phi_A\\) . The concentration factor belongs in \\(L_{AA}\\) ; it must not disappear when naming the mobility. For a binary at fixed temperature and pressure, Gibbs–Duhem gives \\(N_A d\\mu_A+N_Bd\\mu_B=0\\) . Since \\(dN_B=-dN_A\\) , substituting the activity derivatives implies a common \\(\\Phi_A=\\Phi_B=\\Phi\\) in the homogeneous phase. Equation (13.3) becomes, within the same diagonal approximation, \\[\n\\widetilde D=(N_AD_B^*+N_BD_A^*)\\Phi. \\tag{13.4}\n\\] The tracer-weighted relation therefore needs more than constant volume: it adds a kinetic approximation relating tracer motion to force-driven response. Darken, printed p. 192, Eq. 18 , makes the activity/mobility connection with stated isotope assumptions. The thermodynamic factor equals one for ideal mixing, but a thermodynamically ideal solution can still have unequal component mobilities. Conversely, a large interdiffusion coefficient may reflect a large restoring thermodynamic factor rather than unusually fast tagged-particle motion. Vacancy-mediated motion can introduce off-diagonal kinetic correlations. A vacancy exchange changes the local environment and influences subsequent jumps. The resulting vacancy-wind correction modifies the relation between tracer coefficients and collective interdiffusion. It is conceptually different from a macroscopic excess vacancy population. An average vacancy concentration close to equilibrium does not erase local jump correlations. Manning's 1965 publisher abstract supports the role of repeated correlated atom–vacancy exchanges; a full numerical wind factor is not derived from that abstract here. In a simplified description one may label a correction factor \\(S\\) in \\(\\widetilde D=(N_AD_B^*+N_BD_A^*)\\Phi S\\) , but assigning \\(S\\) requires a specified lattice and kinetic model. This notation does not turn an unknown correction into a measured constant. Equations (13.1)–(13.3) are the frame transformation; the additional tracer relation and wind correction have separate evidence and assumptions. 13.4 Two observations identify two intrinsic coefficients locally L30 18:49 The source tutorial specifies marker velocity \\(4.5\\times10^{-12}\\) m/s toward the A-rich side at \\(N_A=0.38\\) , gradient \\(\\partial_xN_A=2.5\\times10^2\\) m⁻¹, and interdiffusion coefficient \\(3.25\\times10^{-14}\\) m²/s. These values, including the exponents, are readable in the inspected frame at 18:49 and the handwritten setup at 22:56. Choose positive x toward increasing A fraction so both gradient and velocity are positive. The velocity supplies the difference \\(\\Delta D=D_A-D_B=v_K/(\\partial_xN_A)=1.8\\times10^{-14}\\) m²/s. Substitute \\(D_A=D_B+\\Delta D\\) into the crossed weighting to obtain \\(\\widetilde D=D_B+N_B\\Delta D\\) . With \\(N_B=0.62\\) , the two coefficients are \\[\nD_B=2.134\\times10^{-14}\\ \\mathrm{m^2/s},\\qquad\nD_A=3.934\\times10^{-14}\\ \\mathrm{m^2/s}. \\tag{13.5}\n\\] These results are independently computed from the visually verified source inputs; they are not garbled caption exponents. They apply at the marker-plane composition. A single measured marker trajectory does not determine both intrinsic coefficient functions over the entire composition range. The functions can exist throughout the phase while the experiment identifies them only at its sampled composition. The laboratory also selects a synthetic molar volume \\(7\\times10^{-6}\\) m³/mol to check actual flux transformations. It obtains intrinsic fluxes \\(J_A=-1.4050\\times10^{-6}\\) and \\(J_B=7.62143\\times10^{-7}\\) mol/(m²·s), but volume-fixed fluxes \\(J_A^V=-1.160714\\times10^{-6}\\) and \\(J_B^V=1.160714\\times10^{-6}\\) . The latter cancel, as the zero-volume-flux condition requires. The source problem does not provide this volume, so those molar fluxes are a separate illustrative construction. Saved output records the distinction. Unequal intrinsic fluxes also imply a compensating vacancy transport in a site-conserving substitutional model. Vacancies may be created or annihilated at dislocations, grain boundaries, and surfaces. If vacancy supply, removal, stress relaxation, and pore nucleation permit it, an excess can condense into voids. Unequal diffusion does not compel visible porosity in every couple. Hollow and core–shell structures exploit this family of mechanisms, but assigning a particular nanostructure to it needs direct structural and kinetic evidence beyond the presence of a shell. The tutorial's line-compound exercise returns to identifiability. A nearly flat composition profile can carry finite potential-driven transport. Substituting an exactly zero gradient into a pointwise Boltzmann–Matano denominator is a failed parameterization, not a measurement of infinite diffusion. Phase-order exercises likewise require chemical stability and the imposed path, while the spatial thickness sequence remains kinetic. A metastable phase must be labeled as such; a garbled formula is not evidence for a new phase identity. 13.5 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Derive crossed weighting Starting with intrinsic Fick laws and zero volume-fixed flux, obtain the binary interdiffusion coefficient for equal constant partial molar volumes. Worked solution The volume condition applied to \\(J_i^V=J_i+c_iv_K\\) gives \\(v_K=-V_m(J_A+J_B)\\) . Since \\(c_{A,x}=-c_{B,x}\\) , this is \\(V_m(D_B-D_A)c_{B,x}\\) . Insert it into the B flux to obtain \\(J_B^V=-D_Bc_{B,x}+N_B(D_B-D_A)c_{B,x}=-[N_AD_B+N_BD_A]c_{B,x}\\) . The crossed weights follow from eliminating convection, not from averaging two arbitrary measurements. 2. The source ratio problem If \\(N_A=0.2\\) and \\(D_A=2D_B\\) , find \\(\\widetilde D/D_B\\) . Is it the ordinary arithmetic mean? Worked solution Using \\(N_B=0.8\\) , the ratio is \\(0.2+0.8(2)=1.8=9/5\\) . The arithmetic mean ratio would be 1.5 and has no basis here. The weights are local opposite-component fractions imposed by the frame transformation. The code evaluates this tutorial result independently of the final numerical inference problem. 3. Displacement versus velocity A marker follows \\(x_K-x_M=k\\sqrt t\\) . Find its instantaneous velocity and the position ratio after doubling time. Worked solution Differentiation gives \\(v_K=k/(2\\sqrt t)=(x_K-x_M)/(2t)\\) . Replacing time by twice its value multiplies displacement by \\(\\sqrt2\\approx1.414214\\) , not two. Its velocity decreases by the reciprocal square root. These are contour/marker trajectory facts under the assumed similarity scaling, not evidence that every marker follows that law during thermal transients or finite-size evolution. 4. Solve the two-coefficient inverse problem Use the verified source values in Section 13.4 to recover both intrinsic coefficients and check both original equations. Worked solution The difference is \\(4.5\\times10^{-12}/250=1.8\\times10^{-14}\\) . Then \\(D_B=3.25\\times10^{-14}-0.62(1.8\\times10^{-14})=2.134\\times10^{-14}\\) and \\(D_A=3.934\\times10^{-14}\\) . Their difference times 250 returns the positive marker velocity. The weighted sum \\(0.38D_B+0.62D_A\\) returns \\(3.25\\times10^{-14}\\) . A sign reversal of the coordinate must reverse the gradient and velocity together, leaving the inferred coefficients unchanged. 5. Mobility dimensions Identify the missing factor in the claim \\(L_{AA}=D_A^*/(RT)\\) when the flux law is \\(J_A=-L_{AA}\\mu_{A,x}\\) . Worked solution The ratio \\(D_A^*/RT\\) has mobility units mol·m²/(J·s), while the coefficient multiplying a molar-potential gradient in a molar flux needs mol²/(J·m·s). Multiplication by \\(c_A\\) , in mol/m³, supplies the difference. Under the adopted diagonal Einstein approximation, \\(B_A=D_A^*/RT\\) and \\(L_{AA}=c_AB_A\\) . Neither relation establishes that off-diagonal vacancy correlations vanish in a real alloy. 6. Markers without pores A couple has clear marker displacement but no resolved pores. Is that inconsistent with vacancy-mediated diffusion? Worked solution No. Marker displacement indicates unequal intrinsic transport under the stated frame interpretation. Vacancies can be supplied and annihilated at sinks, and stress can accommodate local volume changes without forming a resolvable cavity. Pore nucleation has its own energetic and kinetic requirements. Conversely, pores alone do not uniquely identify Kirkendall transport because gas, reactions, or preparation damage can also produce cavities. Correlated marker, composition, time, and structural evidence is needed. 13.6 Sources and limits L28 , L29 , and L30 supply the experimental and tutorial spine. The original Darken scan was inspected at printed pp. 185–193, particularly pp. 188 and 192. Manning's 1965 reference was inspected at publisher-abstract level; no detailed Manning factor is reproduced from unread full text. The derived simple weighting assumes equal constant partial molar volumes and a binary homogeneous phase. A variable-volume or multicomponent system requires its full frame and coupling treatment. Tracer relations require further kinetic assumptions. The next chapter separates thermodynamic assessment from mobility assessment so a software calculation cannot conceal these distinctions behind a single database selection."}, {"number": 14, "title": "CALPHAD thermodynamics and kinetic databases", "url": "chapter-14.html", "text": "A simulation can reproduce the correct final phase fractions and still predict the wrong annealing time. Its Gibbs-energy model selects equilibrium, while its mobility model supplies a kinetic clock. Treating a database as one undifferentiated collection of “material properties” conceals that distinction. Lectures 31–33 introduce CALPHAD, phase models, database construction, and graphical demonstrations. The software workflow is described at the level supported by the inspected owning guide. The accompanying Python calculation is an original reduced model and does not execute DICTRA or reproduce a licensed database. 14.1 An assessed database contains models, not just a phase diagram L31 00:21 CALPHAD , calculation of phase diagrams, is a framework for representing Gibbs energies of phases with parameterized functions and assessing their parameters against thermodynamic and phase-equilibrium evidence. A database encodes those functions, reference states, constituent models, and parameters. A phase diagram is then an output of equilibrium minimization rather than a replacement for the underlying free-energy description. At fixed temperature \\(T\\) and pressure \\(p\\) , let candidate phases be indexed by \\(\\alpha\\) . Each phase has amount \\(n^\\alpha\\geq0\\) in moles, component fractions \\(N_i^\\alpha\\) , and molar Gibbs energy \\(g^\\alpha\\) in J/mol. Given total amounts \\(b_i\\) , the ideal bulk equilibrium problem is \\[\n\\min_{n^\\alpha,N_i^\\alpha}\\sum_\\alpha n^\\alpha g^\\alpha(T,p,\\boldsymbol N^\\alpha),\n\\qquad \\sum_\\alpha n^\\alpha N_i^\\alpha=b_i,\n\\quad \\sum_iN_i^\\alpha=1. \\tag{14.1}\n\\] Nonnegative phase amounts allow phases to disappear. Lagrange multipliers enforcing elemental balances become equilibrium component chemical potentials. Equality of those potentials between present phases recovers the common-tangent construction in a binary system. The minimization extends the same principle to many components and constituent variables. It does not include a nucleation barrier merely because a previously absent phase is allowed to acquire a positive amount. Assessment combines phase boundaries, calorimetric enthalpies and heat capacities, chemical activities, and other compatible evidence. First-principles energies can constrain poorly measured states, especially metastable end members. Measurements have uncertainties and correlated errors; a parameter fit must therefore distinguish data selection, optimization, and independent validation. Reproducing the points used to fit a model does not establish its extrapolative accuracy. A phase omitted from the candidate set cannot emerge from minimization. Likewise, deliberately suspending a stable phase computes a constrained metastable equilibrium rather than the unrestricted one. That can be useful when rapid processing suppresses a transformation, but the constraint must be recorded. A numerical minimum of the selected model is not automatically the physical equilibrium if model scope or candidate phases are incomplete. 14.2 Reference, ideal, and excess contributions L31 18:53 For a disordered binary substitutional phase, let \\(z=N_B\\) . A common model separates pure reference functions, random mixing entropy, and an excess term: \\[\ng=(1-z)g_A^0(T,p)+zg_B^0(T,p)\n+RT[(1-z)\\ln(1-z)+z\\ln z]+g^{\\rm ex}(T,p,z). \\tag{14.2}\n\\] The reference functions carry elemental heat capacities, entropies, and phase differences. The ideal term is a specific configurational model, not a claim that real interactions vanish. A regular solution sets \\(g^{\\rm ex}=L_0z(1-z)\\) . A Redlich–Kister representation adds composition asymmetry through \\(g^{\\rm ex}=z(1-z)\\sum_{k=0}^K L_k(T)(1-2z)^k\\) , where each \\(L_k\\) has units J/mol. The sign convention in the odd powers depends on the chosen component ordering and must match the database. These are functions, so their derivatives matter. A fit close in \\(g\\) can differ in chemical potential, curvature, or heat capacity. Parameterization must therefore be assessed against derivative-sensitive evidence as well as energies. Thermodynamic consistency connects quantities: entropy is \\(-\\partial_Tg\\) , enthalpy is \\(g-T\\partial_Tg\\) , and heat capacity is \\(-T\\partial_{TT}g\\) at fixed pressure and composition. Fitting each independently without compatibility can produce an incoherent material model. Multicomponent extensions combine binary and higher-order interactions. If pair terms are written as a sum over \\(i<j\\) , each pair is counted once; summing over all unequal ordered pairs would require compensating factors. Having all binary subsystems assessed is valuable but does not fix every ternary or higher-order interaction. Eight elements generate 28 binary pairs and 56 ternary triples, as the laboratory computes. The combinatorial count describes possible subsystems, not independent proof that all are adequately measured. Other contributions may include magnetic ordering, gas pressure, vacancies, or electronic effects. A magnetic transition can have real thermodynamic consequences even if a simplified structural phase diagram does not draw it as a separate structural phase field. A compressed caption formula is not enough to specify a magnetic model's normalization, critical temperature, and composition dependence, so no universal magnetic expression is inferred here. 14.3 Ordered phases require site fractions L33 16:46 A disordered solution assigns all substitutional atoms to equivalent sites. An ordered phase distinguishes sublattices: sets of sites that have different occupation probabilities in the ordered structure. For an illustrative B2 model with two equal sublattices, let \\(y_i^{(s)}\\) be the fraction of sublattice \\(s=1,2\\) occupied by species \\(i=A,B\\) . Each sublattice satisfies \\(y_A^{(s)}+y_B^{(s)}=1\\) . The overall A fraction is \\(N_A=[y_A^{(1)}+y_A^{(2)}]/2\\) . In a simple compound-energy description normalized per mole of atomic sites, the molar energy has end-member contributions weighted by site fractions, configurational mixing on each sublattice, and possible excess terms: \\[\ng=\\sum_{i,j}y_i^{(1)}y_j^{(2)}g_{i:j}^0\n+\\frac{RT}{2}\\sum_{s=1}^2\\sum_i y_i^{(s)}\\ln y_i^{(s)}+g^{\\rm ex}. \\tag{14.3}\n\\] The notation \\(i:j\\) identifies a fully occupied end member with species i on the first sublattice and j on the second. Some end members are metastable hypothetical reference states; that does not mean they are experimentally observed phases. Sublattice ratios other than one-to-one change the entropy weights and the composition constraint. Real ordered-phase models can include vacancies, several sublattices, and interaction terms beyond this illustrative expression. At equiatomic composition, parameterize ordering by \\(\\eta\\in[0,1]\\) with \\(y_A^{(1)}=(1+\\eta)/2\\) and \\(y_A^{(2)}=(1-\\eta)/2\\) . The state \\(\\eta=0\\) is disordered; \\(\\eta=1\\) has complete A/B sublattice separation. Writing \\(y=(1+\\eta)/2\\) , the configurational entropy is \\(s_{\\rm conf}=-R[y\\ln y+(1-y)\\ln(1-y)]\\) . It is positive at partial order and zero only at the perfectly ordered limit of this model. The laboratory constructs \\(g(\\eta)=-W\\eta^2/2-Ts_{\\rm conf}(\\eta)\\) , using synthetic \\(T=1000\\) K and \\(W=1.5RT\\) for the free-energy curve in Figure 14.1. Differentiation gives \\(\\partial_\\eta g=-W\\eta+RT\\operatorname{atanh}\\eta\\) . Its nonzero minimum solves \\(\\operatorname{atanh}\\eta=1.5\\eta\\) , yielding \\(\\eta=0.858560\\) . The sublattice A fractions are 0.929280 and 0.070720, and the entropy remains 2.12433 J/(mol·K), compared with the disordered 5.76315 J/(mol·K). Calling a phase B2 therefore does not justify deleting configurational entropy at finite partial order. Figure 14.1 exposes this balance between ordering energy and configurational entropy. Its free-energy curve is an original mean-field model; it is not a database assessment for a particular B2 alloy. The same representation illustrates what a phase model must specify before an equilibrium solver can minimize over its internal occupations. 14.4 A reproducible software calculation has explicit inputs L32 00:20 An equilibrium workflow first selects components, a thermodynamic database and version, candidate phases, composition variables and units, pressure, and temperature or an explicitly defined calculation axis. It then solves the constrained equilibrium problem and plots selected quantities. A Fe–C diagram plotted in mass percent is not directly comparable with one plotted in mole fraction without conversion. A plotting module does not repair inconsistent calculation conditions. The source demonstrates graphical system definition, equilibrium calculation, and rendering, and names several CALPHAD software families. The transferable workflow is more durable than individual menu positions or license claims. This chapter makes no unsupported current comparison of commercial pricing, free editions, or package coverage. The scientific record should retain the model and input choices rather than only a screenshot of the user interface. A transient diffusion calculation needs a second category of input. The Thermo-Calc owning DICTRA guide , in its sections on database selection, geometry, regions, boundary conditions, and thermal profiles, requires both thermodynamic and mobility databases. Geometry can be planar, cylindrical, or spherical in a single spatial coordinate. Regions carry phase selections, sizes, composition profiles, and meshes. Boundary conditions and thermal history complete the initial-boundary-value problem; the guide describes zero-flux closed boundaries as the default, which still must be checked for the chosen case. The thermodynamic functions determine chemical potentials, phase stability, and local equilibrium relations. A mobility database supplies kinetic response functions needed to turn forces into fluxes. Selecting a thermodynamic database alone cannot determine diffusion time. A Fe–Ni homogenization demonstration therefore has both an equilibrium model and a kinetic model, as well as spatial initial data. A single nominal average composition does not specify its transient state. A complete reproducibility record includes software version, database names and versions, component and phase lists, dependent-component conventions, geometry, region dimensions, initial profiles, boundaries, mesh policy, temperature schedule, and requested outputs. Compare predicted profiles and phase fractions with independent measurements and assess grid sensitivity. An attractive phase diagram or smooth profile certifies none of those choices automatically. 14.5 Database scope and kinetic sensitivity L33 00:20 A database's element list is only a first screening criterion. Check which phases, binary and ternary subsystems, properties, composition intervals, and temperature ranges were assessed. An interpolation within a measured binary system and an extrapolation into an unmeasured many-component alloy have different evidential support even if the software accepts both inputs. Version changes may revise phase descriptions or interaction parameters; a later diagram need not match an older demonstration exactly. The source's thermodynamic-database file discussion distinguishes comments, element references, functions, phase definitions, constituent lists, and parameters. A parameter record is interpreted within the phase's constituent model and reference functions; its numerical coefficient is not a standalone experimental datum. Metastable end-member functions can be necessary bookkeeping. Copying isolated coefficients into another normalization can change the model silently. Database syntax, version, and dependency references are therefore part of scientific provenance. Reassessment can alter a phase diagram when new evidence contradicts earlier assignments, as the source's Co–Sn discussion illustrates. This is a reason to preserve uncertainty and evidence lineage, rather than treating an assessed diagram as permanently exact. A machine-learning surrogate can accelerate evaluation or suggest parameters, but it must preserve mass constraints, consistent derivatives, valid constituent domains, and meaningful uncertainty outside its training support. Fast interpolation is not new thermodynamic evidence. The kinetic distinction can be demonstrated without proprietary software. On a closed interval of length \\(L\\) , a constant-coefficient concentration perturbation proportional to \\(\\cos(\\pi x/L)\\) satisfies no-flux boundaries. Substitution into \\(c_t=Dc_{xx}\\) gives amplitude \\(A(t)=A(0)e^{-\\pi^2Dt/L^2}\\) . Every positive D gives the same uniform equilibrium, but a different approach time. For synthetic \\(L=10^{-4}\\) m and \\(t=10^5\\) s, coefficients \\(10^{-14}\\) and \\(4\\times10^{-14}\\) m²/s leave amplitude fractions 0.372708 and 0.0192963. Both models conserve mean composition and eventually reach the same uniform state. Equilibrium validation alone cannot discriminate those clocks. Figure 14.1 pairs this result with the ordering-energy example because both expose information that a phase diagram alone does not supply. Figure 14.1. Two independent roles of assessed models. Sublattice occupation changes thermodynamic energy; mobility changes a transient time scale even when the equilibrium is unchanged. Both panels are original synthetic calculations, not DICTRA outputs. The companion computes sublattice entropy, solves the order-parameter stationary equation, checks its positive entropy, and evaluates exact diffusion-mode decay. It saves numerical output and the figure. It reads no licensed thermodynamic or mobility database. Its meaningful checks concern the declared analytic models; software/database validation remains an additional task for a real calculation. 14.6 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Recover chemical-potential equality Explain how minimizing (14.1) leads to equality of component chemical potentials between present phases. Worked solution Introduce a multiplier \\(\\lambda_i\\) for each elemental balance and minimize \\(G-\\sum_i\\lambda_i(\\sum_\\alpha n_i^\\alpha-b_i)\\) . An allowed infinitesimal component addition to a present phase changes this expression by \\((\\mu_i^\\alpha-\\lambda_i)dn_i^\\alpha\\) . At an interior equilibrium this derivative vanishes, so \\(\\mu_i^\\alpha=\\lambda_i\\) for each phase in which that variation is allowed. The multiplier is common across phases, giving equality. Absent phases instead obey stability inequalities; one cannot demand equality for every phase in the database. 2. Ordered does not mean zero entropy For the equiatomic two-sublattice model, show that partial order has positive configurational entropy and identify the zero-entropy limit. Worked solution For \\(0\\leq\\eta<1\\) , both \\(y=(1+\\eta)/2\\) and \\(1-y\\) lie strictly between zero and one. Their logarithms are negative, so \\(-R[y\\ln y+(1-y)\\ln(1-y)]>0\\) . At \\(\\eta=1\\) , the nonzero occupancy is one and the other is zero; with \\(0\\ln0=0\\) , the entropy vanishes. The computed partially ordered minimum at 0.858560 has entropy 2.12433 J/(mol·K). The phase name alone does not select the limiting occupancy. 3. Find the ordering threshold Expand the synthetic ordering model near \\(\\eta=0\\) . Under what condition does that state become locally unstable? Worked solution The derivative is \\(-W\\eta+RT\\operatorname{atanh}\\eta\\) . Since \\(\\operatorname{atanh}\\eta=\\eta+\\eta^3/3+\\cdots\\) , integration gives \\(g=g(0)+(RT-W)\\eta^2/2+RT\\eta^4/12+\\cdots\\) . The disordered state's curvature becomes negative when \\(W>RT\\) . This is a local mean-field threshold for the synthetic model. Real phase assessments can include asymmetry and coupling terms that alter its character. 4. Same equilibrium, different time Derive the no-flux mode decay and explain why matching its final state does not validate D. Worked solution For \\(c=\\bar c+A(t)\\cos(\\pi x/L)\\) , the spatial derivative vanishes at zero and L. The PDE gives \\(A'=-D\\pi^2A/L^2\\) , so \\(A=A_0e^{-D\\pi^2t/L^2}\\) . Every positive coefficient drives the perturbation to zero while preserving the mean. Only transient observations constrain the decay rate. The laboratory's fourfold change in D changes the remaining amplitude from 0.372708 to 0.0192963 at the selected time without changing the limit. 5. Audit a database claim An eight-element database contains all 28 binaries. Does that establish accurate eight-component diffusion? Worked solution No. Binary coverage does not determine all ternary and higher-order thermodynamic interactions, and element coverage does not establish phase-model validity at the target composition and temperature. The system has 56 possible ternary triples. Diffusion additionally requires kinetic mobilities and appropriate coupling/frame treatment. A defensible claim needs assessed scope, independent validation near the target state, version information, and uncertainty for extrapolation. Counting subsystems is an inventory check, not a predictive validation. 6. Turn a screenshot into a reproducible model A report shows a transient concentration plot and names only a thermodynamic database. List the missing scientific inputs most consequential for reproduction. Worked solution The mobility database and version supply the kinetic model. The geometry, region dimensions, phases, complete initial profiles, boundary conditions, mesh, and thermal schedule specify the initial-boundary-value problem. Component units and dependent-component conventions are needed to interpret composition. Software version and selected outputs support numerical reproduction. If a phase was suspended or a seed imposed, those constraints must also be stated. A plot alone cannot identify which missing choice caused a discrepancy. 14.7 Sources and boundaries L31 , L32 , and L33 establish the CALPHAD and database sequence. The owning DICTRA guide supports the stated workflow at the inspected input-setting sections. No local DICTRA run, licensed database assessment, current commercial license comparison, or unread magnetic model is claimed. The next chapter uses a deliberately reduced moving-boundary model. Its purpose is to expose conservation, interface assumptions, and parabolic growth before a multiphase simulation embeds them in a larger numerical system."}, {"number": 15, "title": "Moving boundaries and multiphase simulation", "url": "chapter-15.html", "text": "An intermediate layer grows by receiving solute through material that it has already formed. The same layer that records progress also increases the transport resistance. A moving-boundary calculation must couple that changing diffusion field to the amount needed to transform the next increment of material. Lectures 34–35 provide moving-interface and carburization demonstrations and a database tutorial. The mathematical Stefan problem below is an original reduced teaching calculation. It does not execute DICTRA, reproduce licensed databases, or represent measured Fe–C growth constants. 15.1 A moving front couples phase equilibrium to conservation L34 00:20 Consider planar product material occupying \\(0<x<s(t)\\) and an initially uniform parent material at \\(x>s(t)\\) . The interface coordinate \\(s\\) is in meters, and \\(v=ds/dt\\) is positive for growth to the right. Let the selected conserved solute have molar concentration \\(c(x,t)\\) in the product, with constant diffusivity \\(D>0\\) in m²/s. Use a fixed laboratory coordinate and neglect bulk motion in this reduced equal-volume setting. Impose a maintained surface concentration \\(c_s\\) at zero and a product-side interfacial concentration \\(c_i\\) . The parent remains at \\(c_0\\) and is taken to have zero diffusive flux. Choose \\(c_s>c_i>c_0\\) so solute travels toward the advancing front. Local equilibrium is the assumption that fixes the two interfacial concentrations through thermodynamics; it is not derived from the diffusion equation. Define the positive concentration jump requirement \\(q_c=c_i-c_0\\) , in mol/m³. The general interfacial balance is \\(v(c_R-c_L)=J_R-J_L\\) , with both fluxes in the same frame. Here \\(c_L=c_i\\) , \\(c_R=c_0\\) , and \\(J_R=0\\) . Therefore \\[\nq_c\\frac{ds}{dt}=J_L=-D\\left.\\partial_xc\\right|_{s^-},\n\\qquad c_t=Dc_{xx}\\quad(0<x<s(t)). \\tag{15.1}\n\\] The left side is the amount per area and time required for interface conversion. The right side is the flux supplied through the product. Their units both equal mol/(m²·s). A reaction that consumes a nonconserved chemical species would need its stoichiometric balance; the present component is conserved through phase conversion. This formulation is a one-phase Stefan problem : a diffusion field and a moving boundary tied by a flux jump. “One-phase” refers to solving diffusion in only one region while the other supplies a fixed state, not to denying the existence of two physical phases. Including parent diffusion, unequal volumes, nonlocal equilibrium, or a finite surface supply changes the problem and its interface law. 15.2 Parabolic growth is a derived regime L34 12:39 First approximate the product concentration as quasisteady: diffusion across the current layer equilibrates much faster than the interface moves appreciably. Setting \\(c_{xx}=0\\) at fixed current thickness gives \\(c=c_s-(c_s-c_i)x/s\\) . The flux is then \\(J_L=D(c_s-c_i)/s\\) . Substitute this into (15.1) and define \\(\\beta=(c_s-c_i)/q_c>0\\) , a dimensionless concentration ratio: \\[\n\\frac{ds}{dt}=\\frac{D\\beta}{s},\\qquad\ns^2(t)=s_0^2+2D\\beta t. \\tag{15.2}\n\\] The initial thickness \\(s_0\\) regularizes the zero-thickness singularity of this approximation. A prescribed seed states that product already exists; it does not model the probability or kinetics of nucleation. For negligible seed thickness, growth proportional to \\(\\sqrt t\\) is called parabolic growth because thickness squared is linear in time. The growing resistance \\(s/D\\) explains the slowing rate. A larger fixed concentration drop or diffusivity increases the parabolic constant; a larger concentration jump needed to convert parent to product decreases it. These are conditional comparisons with geometry, boundary states, and phase identities held fixed. An observed parabolic law alone does not identify which microscopic diffusion path controls D. The approximation has an internal scale check. The diffusion time through the layer is approximately \\(s^2/D\\) , while the interface growth time is \\(s/(ds/dt)=s^2/(D\\beta)\\) . Their ratio is \\(\\beta\\) . Thus small \\(\\beta\\) supports quasisteady diffusion; values near one demand a transient calculation. This estimate explains why a linear profile can be useful in a slowly moving layer but inaccurate when its front advances on the diffusion time scale. Neither (15.2) nor its similarity counterpart applies indefinitely to a finite closed specimen. As reservoirs deplete and the total phase fractions approach equilibrium, the driving concentration or potential difference changes. Growth can slow more strongly and saturate. Saturation in a numerical trajectory should be checked against conserved inventory and the equilibrium phase amounts, rather than identified from a flat curve alone. 15.3 An exact similarity solution tests the approximation For the ideal zero-seed problem, a similarity solution retains concentration storage inside the growing product. Let \\(s(t)=2\\lambda\\sqrt{Dt}\\) with a constant dimensionless \\(\\lambda>0\\) . The error-function solution satisfying the two concentration boundaries is \\[\nc(x,t)=c_s-(c_s-c_i)\n\\frac{\\operatorname{erf}[x/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]}{\\operatorname{erf}\\lambda},\n\\quad 0<x<2\\lambda\\sqrt{Dt}. \\tag{15.3}\n\\] Substituting into the diffusion equation confirms it directly. Differentiating at the front gives \\(J_L=(c_s-c_i)\\sqrt D e^{-\\lambda^2}/[\\sqrt{\\pi t}\\operatorname{erf}\\lambda]\\) . The interface velocity is \\(\\lambda\\sqrt D/\\sqrt t\\) . Their equality in (15.1) reduces the moving-boundary problem to \\[\n\\lambda e^{\\lambda^2}\\operatorname{erf}\\lambda\n=\\frac{\\beta}{\\sqrt\\pi}. \\tag{15.4}\n\\] The left side increases from zero for positive lambda, so a unique positive root exists for each positive beta. For small lambda, \\(\\operatorname{erf}\\lambda\\approx2\\lambda/\\sqrt\\pi\\) and \\(e^{\\lambda^2}\\approx1\\) , giving \\(\\lambda\\approx\\sqrt{\\beta/2}\\) . Then \\(s\\approx\\sqrt{2D\\beta t}\\) , recovering the quasisteady law as a controlled small-beta limit. The laboratory uses synthetic \\(D=10^{-14}\\) m²/s and \\(t=10^5\\) s. At beta 0.1, the computed root is 0.220016 and the exact layer thickness is 13.9151 micrometers, compared with 14.1421 micrometers quasisteady: a 1.632% overestimate. At beta one, the root is 0.620063 and thickness is 39.2162 micrometers versus 44.7214 micrometers, a 14.038% overestimate. Both follow square-root-time growth, so observing that exponent would not distinguish them. Figure 15.1. Original planar Stefan model. Left: exact similarity and quasisteady trajectories. Right: the exact normalized product concentration at a fixed scaled position. The comparison isolates concentration storage within the product; it does not compare a Python run with DICTRA. The code brackets the positive root and checks (15.4), evaluates profiles and trajectories, and writes the saved output . A solver for more general initial data would additionally need conservative spatial discretization, interface tracking, and refinement checks. This exact case can serve as a benchmark for such a solver; plotting the analytic curve is not equivalent to having implemented that general solver. 15.4 Reading a multiphase demonstration as a model L34 17:49 The source Fe–C demonstration places a small ferrite region next to austenite and follows an interface through diffusion. Its phase-specific compositions, geometry, grids, thermodynamic database, mobility database, and thermal schedule are inputs, not details that can be inferred from the final line plot. Continuation to a longer annealing time retains an evolved spatial state. Restarting with a nominal average composition would instead pose a different problem. Selected source screenshots establish an initially homogeneous Fe–0.15 mass% C composition, an isothermal condition of 1050 K, and a BCC ferrite seed with displayed width 0.001 micrometers, or one nanometer. The seed uses ten linear-grid points in the displayed setup. An equilibrium calculation supplies an approximately 0.01 mass% carbon content for BCC at those conditions; this is a phase-specific input, distinct from the specimen's overall 0.15 mass%. The plotted carbon-profile distance axis spans zero to 2000 micrometers and the displayed output times include zero, \\(10^{-4}\\) hours, and 300 hours. These are observed demonstration settings, not parameters of the synthetic solution in Figure 15.1. A finite ferrite seed makes an initially absent region numerically available. It does not calculate ferrite nucleation, interface creation energy, or a nucleation delay. Similarly, local equilibrium at the moving boundary is a modeling choice. A finite interface mobility would relate chemical driving force to interface velocity and permit departure from the equilibrium boundary compositions. The reduced solution above holds those compositions fixed and cannot quantify that departure. The source carburization example introduces another description: a local region can contain a matrix and carbides, with phase fractions changing across depth. A homogenized transport calculation assigns an effective response to that mixture. It is distinct from explicitly tracking every carbide interface. Effective-property bounds constrain possible responses under stated geometrical and constitutive assumptions; selecting an upper bound is a topology-sensitive modeling choice, not a measured diffusivity of every mixture. The inspected multiphase setup uses a planar austenite region of width 0.003 m, or 3 mm, with 100 geometrically spaced points and a displayed spacing ratio of 1.02. Its selected phases are FCC_A1, M3C2, and M7C3. The source phase-fraction plot places the M3C2 region nearer the carbon-rich surface and M7C3 farther into the material. These labels are read from the screen; the calculation is not evidence that any unmodeled carbide must be absent in every real Ni–Cr–C alloy. The displayed homogenization choice is the general upper Hashin–Shtrikman bound. Selecting that option embodies an effective-medium assumption whose suitability must be checked against actual morphology. An imposed carbon activity is also distinct from an imposed carbon concentration. Activity is defined through \\(\\mu_C=\\mu_C^0+RT\\ln a_C\\) . At fixed activity, the surface concentration follows from the selected thermodynamic phase state and can change when that state changes. The reference, such as graphite, is part of the boundary definition. A zero chromium flux boundary states a transport constraint on chromium; it is not a statement that chromium concentration is uniform throughout the evolving specimen. The owning DICTRA guide supports the need for thermodynamic and mobility databases, regions, profiles, meshes, boundary conditions, and thermal history. Its inspected setup sections support the workflow, not an independent validation of source demonstration numbers. The source screenshots separately verify the settings stated here, including a graphite carbon reference; viewing those selected frames does not establish that a local calculation with matching inputs was executed. Full database versions, complete initial profiles, and solver history would still be needed for exact reproduction. The associated tutorial distinguishes data capture, assessment, optimization, storage, and validation; counts six binary subsystems for four components; and notes the need for metastable elemental end-member functions. Magnetic ordering can contribute a thermodynamic transition even when a conventional structural diagram suppresses its line. Diffusion calculations always require spatial initial composition information, even if the interface asks for profiles rather than one bulk-composition box. These distinctions prevent interface conventions from becoming mistaken physical claims. 15.5 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Recover the interface condition Derive (15.1) from the general jump balance and explain its sign for an advancing solute-rich product. Worked solution Use \\(v(c_R-c_L)=J_R-J_L\\) with parent values on the right. The parent flux is zero, \\(c_R=c_0\\) , and the product limit is \\(c_L=c_i\\) . Thus \\(v(c_0-c_i)=-J_L\\) , or \\(q_cv=J_L\\) . With decreasing concentration toward the front, \\(c_x<0\\) and \\(J_L=-Dc_x>0\\) , giving positive growth speed because \\(q_c>0\\) . Using an unsigned concentration jump would obscure this directional check. 2. Derive and test the quasisteady law Integrate the quasisteady growth equation with a finite seed. What dimensionless ratio supports the approximation? Worked solution The linear concentration profile gives \\(ds/dt=D\\beta/s\\) . Multiplying by twice s gives \\(d(s^2)/dt=2D\\beta\\) ; integration gives \\(s^2=s_0^2+2D\\beta t\\) . Diffusion across the layer takes approximately \\(s^2/D\\) , while appreciable interface motion takes \\(s^2/(D\\beta)\\) . Their ratio is beta. Small beta separates those time scales; beta near one warns that the neglected concentration storage can be consequential. 3. Obtain the exact root equation Differentiate (15.3) and use the moving-front balance to obtain (15.4). Worked solution The derivative of the error function supplies \\(e^{-\\lambda^2}/\\sqrt{\\pi Dt}\\) at the front. Hence \\(J_L=(c_s-c_i)\\sqrt D e^{-\\lambda^2}/[\\sqrt{\\pi t}\\operatorname{erf}\\lambda]\\) . The speed from \\(s=2\\lambda\\sqrt{Dt}\\) is \\(\\lambda\\sqrt D/\\sqrt t\\) . Canceling the common factor in \\(q_cv=J_L\\) yields \\(\\lambda e^{\\lambda^2}\\operatorname{erf}\\lambda=(c_s-c_i)/(q_c\\sqrt\\pi)\\) . The ratio on the right is beta divided by the square root of pi. 4. An exponent does not identify a model Both the exact and quasisteady trajectories are proportional to square root of time. How can an experiment discriminate them? Worked solution The growth exponent is the same, so one must compare the prefactor using independently constrained D and concentration ratios, or compare internal concentration profiles. At beta one, the laboratory predicts 39.2162 micrometers exact and 44.7214 micrometers quasisteady at the declared time. Fitting an unconstrained effective D to either thickness can hide the difference. Multiple times do not necessarily break that degeneracy when both retain the same exponent; internal profiles or separate diffusivity evidence add information. 5. Activity versus concentration Why can a fixed carbon activity boundary yield a changing surface concentration? Worked solution Fixed activity fixes chemical potential relative to a stated reference at the specified temperature. The concentration corresponding to that potential follows from the phase's thermodynamic constitutive function. If temperature, phase fractions, or surface phase identity changes, the concentration at the same activity can change. Imposing a fixed concentration instead constrains another variable and generally gives a different boundary problem. The reference chemical potential and thermal history must therefore accompany an activity boundary. 6. Diagnose a saturated interface A closed-system simulation's interface appears to stop. What checks distinguish equilibrium saturation from numerical arrest? Worked solution Check total conserved component amounts against the initial inventory, local partition relations and equality of relevant chemical potentials, and final phase fractions against a separately calculated constrained equilibrium. Refine the mesh and time integration and tighten solver tolerances to test numerical sensitivity. Examine remaining gradients and fluxes. A seed reaching the end of a region or a failed nonlinear iteration can also stop apparent motion. A flat trajectory alone is insufficient evidence of equilibrium. 15.6 Sources and boundaries L34 supplies the screenshot-based moving-boundary and carburization examples; L35 supplies the tutorial distinctions. The owning training curriculum lists moving-boundary Fe–C and carburization modules; only its curriculum, not the complete lessons, was inspected. The explicit Stefan derivation assumes a planar fixed-volume geometry, constant product diffusivity, fixed boundary concentrations, no parent flux, and local interfacial partitioning. Finite reservoirs, multidimensional morphology, nucleation, elastic stress, and coupled component transport are outside that model. The next chapter asks what measured depth profiles can identify when sample preparation and instrumental broadening enter the observation."}, {"number": 16, "title": "Tracer profiles, SIMS, and electron-probe measurements", "url": "chapter-16.html", "text": "A depth profile has two histories: atoms moved during annealing, then the measurement process converted their distribution into counts, ion intensities, or X-ray signals. Recovering diffusion requires an observation model for that second history as well as a transport model for the first. Lectures 36–37 supply radiotracer, SIMS, and electron-probe methods. Lecture 40 contributes the method-comparison tutorial and a radiotracer table, directly inspected at 20:49 and 21:51. The table fit is a source-input calculation; the separate Gaussian broadening example is synthetic. 16.1 Select a measurable displacement, not a universal diffusivity range L36 00:22 A tracer labels a dilute subset of atoms while leaving the host's chemical state approximately unchanged. In a homogeneous alloy, its coefficient \\(D^*\\) measures tagged-particle spreading at that composition; an impurity tracer in a pure host measures a different species–host combination. An unrelated element used as a convenient probe does not automatically measure host self diffusion. A validated kinetic relation is required to convert one into the other. Let \\(x\\) be depth in meters and \\(t\\) annealing time in seconds. Here and in (16.1), \\(c^*(x,t)\\) is a stable-label molar concentration or a decay-corrected radioisotope distribution; the same approximation applies when decay during annealing is negligible. In a uniform host with constant scalar coefficient, the dilute-label transport model is \\(c_t^*=D^*c_{xx}^*\\) . It assumes label dilution, no changing chemical driving force, and a specified surface condition. For a spatially uniform first-order decay constant \\(\\lambda_d\\) , in s⁻¹, the uncorrected radioactive population instead obeys \\(c_{{\\rm raw},t}=D^*c_{{\\rm raw},xx}-\\lambda_dc_{\\rm raw}\\) ; multiplication by \\(e^{\\lambda_dt}\\) recovers the diffusion-only equation. Uniform decay changes a single-time profile's amplitude, not its depth-squared logarithmic slope, but sections counted at different times require decay correction. Isotope labels are chemically close to the host but not guaranteed dynamically identical: isotope mass can change vibrational rates. A useful diffusion distance is of order \\(\\sqrt{D^*t}\\) . It must exceed depth resolution enough to identify spreading while remaining small enough to avoid unwanted sample boundaries or mixing with other transport paths. Thus a method has a joint time, length, material, and sensitivity window rather than a universal lower or upper D threshold. Lengthening the anneal can make a smaller coefficient measurable, but also increases risks from reactions, evaporation, radioactive decay, or microstructural evolution. Direct profile methods estimate where labels or chemical components went. Mechanical, electrical, NMR, and scattering methods instead respond to relaxation, carriers, or correlation functions. These can probe short local jumps without measuring long-range penetration. The next three chapters develop those distinctions; a method name by itself does not establish which diffusion coefficient it returns. 16.2 A radiotracer profile is a section-average activity measurement L36 09:44 A thin labeled layer can be deposited by an appropriate solution, evaporation, or electrodeposition procedure, then annealed under controlled temperature and atmosphere. The deposit should be thin relative to the resulting diffusion distance and dilute enough not to change the host composition appreciably. Removing an outer rim after annealing can reduce side-surface penetration contributions. That preparation does not remove internal grain-boundary or dislocation transport. Mechanical serial sectioning removes successive layers. If removed mass is \\(\\Delta m\\) , density is \\(\\rho\\) , and cross-sectional area is \\(A\\) , the estimated thickness is \\(\\Delta x=\\Delta m/(\\rho A)\\) for a uniform slice. Accumulating thicknesses assigns a depth interval to each sample. Count rate, after background, efficiency, dead-time, and decay corrections, is related to tracer amount. Specific activity normalizes activity by section mass; a becquerel is one decay per second. A section reports an interval average, not the exact concentration at one geometrical point. For a slice from a to b, the appropriate model value is \\(\\bar c^*=(b-a)^{-1}\\int_a^b c^*(x,t)dx\\) when density is uniform. A midpoint approximation is accurate only when the profile curvature over the slice is sufficiently small. Sputter sectioning accesses shallower intervals but adds its own erosion and mixing response. The instrument and removal method must match the length scale being inferred. For an ideal instantaneous plane deposit of areal tracer amount \\(M\\) , in mol/m², at a reflecting surface of a half-space, the normalized solution is \\[\nc^*(x,t)=\\frac{M}{\\sqrt{\\pi D^*t}}\n\\exp\\left(-\\frac{x^2}{4D^*t}\\right),\\qquad x\\geq0. \\tag{16.1}\n\\] The integral over nonnegative depth equals M. The surface derivative is zero for positive time, expressing no tracer loss after deposition. Taking a natural logarithm of activity relative to a fixed reference gives a straight line against depth squared, with slope \\(m=-1/(4D^*t)\\) . The unknown deposited amount and detection scale enter the intercept, provided sensitivity is depth-independent. Thus \\(D^*=-1/(4tm)\\) . A base-10 fit instead needs \\(D^*=-1/[4t\\,m_{10}\\ln10]\\) . Matrix evaporation changes the depth coordinate and tracer loss changes the boundary. A simple surface-loss closure would specify outward flux proportional to surface concentration, rather than retain the reflecting condition. Fast grain-boundary or dislocation paths can generate deeper tails. A finite deposit thickness broadens the initial profile. Curvature in a log-activity versus depth-squared plot therefore calls for diagnosis, not automatically a second fitted diffusion coefficient. Radioactive work requires the appropriate facility, detector, and contamination controls; destructive sectioning also removes spatial context. Those practical constraints do not prove that only one laboratory can perform the method or that all later microscopy is physically impossible. Correlative characterization depends on approved procedures and specimen preparation. At a fixed harmonic force constant, a simple vibrational frequency scales as mass to the power −1/2, not inverse mass; collective jump mechanisms can further modify an isotope effect. 16.3 Fit all the tutorial values and inspect residuals L40 20:49 The source tutorial deposits isotope B on alloy AB, anneals at 700 °C for three days, and reports the table below after sectioning. The displayed values and units were read directly from the selected source frame, rather than reconstructed from captions. Their statistical uncertainties and slice boundaries are not supplied, so the calculation treats the tabulated depths as representative point depths and uses an unweighted natural-log fit. Source tutorial inputs, L40 20:49 Depth (µm) Specific activity (Bq/mg) 0.2 1 × 10⁶ 0.5 5 × 10⁵ 1 2 × 10⁵ 1.5 1.2 × 10⁵ 2 6 × 10⁴ 2.5 1 × 10⁴ 3 5.8 × 10³ 4 5.1 × 10² 5 1 × 10² Three days is 259200 s. Let \\(X_j=x_j^2\\) in µm² and \\(Y_j=\\ln[a_j/(1\\ \\mathrm{Bq/mg})]\\) . Minimizing \\(\\sum_j(Y_j-b-mX_j)^2\\) gives \\(m=\\sum_j(X_j-\\bar X)(Y_j-\\bar Y)/\\sum_j(X_j-\\bar X)^2\\) and \\(b=\\bar Y-m\\bar X\\) . The laboratory obtains slope −0.35906875 µm⁻², intercept 12.60687, and coefficient of determination 0.935472. The slope is close to the displayed source plot's −0.3591; its displayed intercept 12.603 and fit statistic 0.9356 differ slightly from this direct recomputation of the rounded table. Since one inverse square micrometer equals \\(10^{12}\\) m⁻², the slope in SI is \\(-0.35906875\\times10^{12}\\) m⁻². Equation (16.1) gives a nominal coefficient \\(D_B^*=2.68613\\times10^{-18}\\) m²/s. It is a fitted coefficient conditional on the thin-layer, reflecting, constant-coefficient observation model. A high-looking fit statistic does not establish that every point follows that model. Figure 16.1 plots the residuals as well as the line. The first and last points lie above it, while several central points lie below; the deviations are too structured to ignore merely because the trend spans many decades. Omitting the first two points changes the nominal coefficient to \\(2.99145\\times10^{-18}\\) m²/s. This is a sensitivity check, not permission to discard inconvenient observations. Without measurement uncertainties or section bounds, no defensible physical uncertainty interval or unique cause of the deviations follows from the table alone. Figure 16.1. Independent fit of the visually verified source tutorial table. The residual panel exposes departures hidden by the broad logarithmic trend. The nominal coefficient is 2.68613 × 10⁻¹⁸ m²/s under the stated point-depth model. 16.4 SIMS separates isotope identity from concentration calibration L37 00:21 Secondary ion mass spectrometry (SIMS) bombards a surface with primary ions, ejecting atoms and clusters in a collision cascade. A small fraction of the sputtered material leaves as secondary ions that can be separated by mass-to-charge ratio. The primary beam removes material; the analyzed ions carry information about the specimen. Oxygen or cesium bombardment can change secondary-ion yields, so beam chemistry is part of the measurement response. A depth profile measures signal versus sputtering time. Crater profilometry converts the total erosion time into a depth scale; assuming a constant erosion rate then assigns intermediate depths. Composition-dependent sputter yield can invalidate that assumption. Selecting the crater interior reduces edge artifacts, while atomic mixing, surface roughness, and finite information depth broaden the response. Instrumental depth resolution is consequently not just the nominal step in acquisition time. Time-of-flight separation gives a useful tutorial check. For an ion of mass \\(m_i\\) , charge magnitude \\(Q_i\\) , and acceleration voltage \\(V\\) , kinetic energy \\(m_iv^2/2=Q_iV\\) gives flight time \\(t_f=L\\sqrt{m_i/(2Q_iV)}\\) over a field-free distance L. With equal charge and voltage, flight time scales as square root of mass. Magnetic-sector SIMS separates ions by another analyzer architecture; it should not be described as if all SIMS instruments use the same flight-time scheme. Ion intensity is not automatically a concentration. Matrix effects change ionization probability, and isotope or molecular interferences can overlap mass channels. NIST's owning magnetic-sector SIMS description and current-activity sections emphasize standard-derived relative sensitivity factors for quantification. A calibrated isotope ratio can reveal tracer spreading, but a cross-matrix comparison of raw intensities can mimic a composition jump without one. Finite initial width and instrumental broadening can be separated in a restricted model. On the infinite line, a normalized Gaussian initial distribution of variance \\(\\sigma_0^2\\) undergoing constant-D diffusion has variance \\(\\sigma_0^2+2Dt\\) . Convolution with an independent centered Gaussian response of variance \\(\\sigma_r^2\\) adds that variance. This follows because convolution represents the sum of independent displacements, whose centered variances add. Thus \\[\n\\sigma_{\\rm measured}^2=\\sigma_0^2+2Dt+\\sigma_r^2. \\tag{16.2}\n\\] This infinite-line result is not the centered variance of (16.1). The half-Gaussian surface-source profile has mean \\(2\\sqrt{Dt/\\pi}\\) , second moment about the surface \\(2Dt\\) , and centered variance \\(2Dt(1-2/\\pi)\\) . Width convention and boundary condition must be stated before applying variance subtraction. Arbitrary sputter-response kernels also need not be Gaussian. For the synthetic infinite-line example, \\(D=10^{-18}\\) m²/s, \\(t=2\\times10^5\\) s, initial standard deviation 0.1 micrometers, and instrument standard deviation 0.2 micrometers give measured standard deviation 0.670820 micrometers. Ignoring both initial and instrumental width gives \\(1.125\\times10^{-18}\\) m²/s, a 12.5% overestimate. Correct subtraction recovers the declared D. That recovery tests the Gaussian model, not an unknown instrument response. 16.5 Electron-probe profiles require spectral and spatial interpretation L37 19:56 Electron-probe microanalysis (EPMA) excites characteristic X-rays with an electron beam. Removing an inner-shell electron permits a higher-shell electron to fill the vacancy; the energy difference can leave as an X-ray or through an Auger-electron process. The characteristic spectrum identifies elements. The X-rays originate from an interaction volume, so chemical spatial resolution depends on beam energy, specimen composition, geometry, and X-ray generation, rather than only the electron spot diameter. Energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) sorts photon events by energy, while wavelength-dispersive spectroscopy (WDS) uses diffraction by an analyzing crystal. Bragg's condition \\(n\\lambda=2d\\sin\\theta\\) links wavelength lambda to lattice-plane spacing d and diffraction angle theta for integer order n. Selecting a crystal and angle discriminates nearby wavelengths. WDS can offer better spectral separation for interfering lines, while acquisition geometry and scanning impose time and coverage tradeoffs. The Rowland-circle focusing construction relates source, analyzing crystal, and detector positions. In Johann geometry the crystal is bent; Johansson geometry additionally grinds the reflecting surface to the focusing geometry. This is the distinction tested in the tutorial. It is a spectrometer focusing condition, not a statement that both techniques have identical depth resolution or concentration detection limits. Quantification compares intensities with standards and corrects matrix effects. The conventional ZAF labels refer to atomic-number effects, absorption, and secondary fluorescence. Armstrong's owning NIST abstract describes the development and uncertainty of quantitative atomic-number and absorption corrections; only that abstract is claimed inspected here. Standards, specimen preparation, and correction models remain part of a quantitative diffusion profile. A narrow reaction layer can be blurred into neighboring phases by the interaction volume. Before interpreting its slope with a bulk inverse formula, compare spatial resolution with layer thickness and combine chemical measurements with structural evidence. EPMA generally does not separate isotope labels by characteristic X-ray energy in the manner of mass spectrometry; it measures chemical composition profiles. This distinction makes it useful for interdiffusion but prevents automatically treating every EPMA scan as a tracer measurement. The laboratory fits the verified tutorial table, checks natural- versus base-10 slope conversion, plots residuals, and calculates the independent broadening example. It writes the actual numerical record . No raw source spectra, detector calibration, count uncertainty, or slice bounds are supplied; the code therefore produces a nominal teaching estimate rather than a metrologically complete diffusion measurement. 16.6 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Normalize the half-space source Verify that (16.1) contains total areal amount M and has zero surface flux for positive time. Worked solution Set \\(u=x/(2\\sqrt{Dt})\\) , so the integral becomes \\(2M\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u^2}du/\\sqrt\\pi=M\\) . Differentiating the profile produces a factor \\(-x/(2Dt)\\) , so the surface derivative is zero and the Fick flux vanishes there for positive time. The initial deposit is represented by a limiting surface distribution; it is not a maintained concentration boundary. Using the full-line Gaussian normalization over only positive depths would lose half the stated amount. 2. Units and logarithm base in the table fit Convert the fitted slope −0.35906875 µm⁻² into a coefficient for three days. What factor changes if base-10 logarithms are used? Worked solution The SI slope is \\(-0.35906875\\times10^{12}\\) m⁻² and time is 259200 s. Thus \\(D=-1/(4tm)=2.68613\\times10^{-18}\\) m²/s. Since \\(\\ln a=(\\ln10)\\log_{10}a\\) , the base-10 slope is the natural slope divided by ln10. The coefficient formula must therefore divide by the extra ln10 as well. Using the natural-log formula with a base-10 slope overestimates D by ln10. 3. The section is an integral For a section from a to b, derive its average tracer concentration under (16.1). Worked solution The Gaussian antiderivative is \\(\\sqrt{\\pi Dt}\\operatorname{erf}[x/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]\\) . Therefore the average is \\(M\\{\\operatorname{erf}[b/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]-\\operatorname{erf}[a/(2\\sqrt{Dt})]\\}/(b-a)\\) . For a sufficiently thin slice it tends to the point concentration. Thick slices sample a curved profile unevenly, so replacing them by midpoint values can bias a log-slope fit. Actual slice boundaries permit fitting this integrated model directly. 4. Distinguish width conventions Why does a half-Gaussian surface source not have centered variance 2Dt? Worked solution Its second moment about the surface is 2Dt, but its mean is positive: \\(2\\sqrt{Dt/\\pi}\\) . Centered variance subtracts the square of that mean, giving \\(2Dt-4Dt/\\pi=2Dt(1-2/\\pi)\\) . The factor is approximately 0.363380. The infinite-line Gaussian has zero mean in its symmetric coordinate, so its centered variance and second moment coincide. Confusing these two distributions changes a diffusivity estimate even with exact data. 5. What does equal flight time require? Two ions have equal charge and are accelerated through the same voltage. One has four times the mass. Find the flight-time ratio and explain why SIMS intensity still needs calibration. Worked solution Energy conservation gives \\(t_f\\propto\\sqrt m\\) , so the heavier ion takes twice as long over the same path. That relation sorts mass-to-charge identity, not abundance. Sputtering and ionization probabilities, detector response, and matrix chemistry control detected intensity. Relative sensitivity standards are needed to map counts to concentration. Correct mass separation therefore does not remove matrix effects or establish a calibrated depth scale. 6. Design a method comparison A thin reaction layer has a chemical gradient and an isotope label. What complementary questions can EPMA, SIMS, and radiotracer sectioning answer? Worked solution EPMA can quantify chemical composition with appropriate standards and matrix corrections, subject to its interaction-volume resolution. SIMS can resolve isotope-dependent depth signals but requires sputter-depth and sensitivity calibration. Radiotracer sectioning measures activity per removed material and can be highly sensitive, but is destructive and subject to slice averaging, decay corrections, and facility constraints. None alone establishes a microscopic mechanism. Compare their length scales with the layer and diffusion width, then combine structural phase evidence and time-dependent profiles to distinguish interdiffusion from tagged-atom spreading. 16.7 Sources and evidence limits L36 and L37 supply the method sequence; L40 supplies comparisons and the fitted table. Only the stated table and displayed fit frames are claimed visually checked here. The NIST SIMS page was directly inspected at its description and current-activity sections; the EPMA publication was inspected at abstract level. Instrument broadening, unknown errors, phase mixing, fast paths, and surface loss can remain confounded in one profile. The calculations expose that limitation instead of inferring an unwarranted mechanism from a nominal D. Mechanical relaxation next supplies a different observation: a redistribution time whose connection to long-range mass transport must be established separately."}, {"number": 17, "title": "Mechanical relaxation and electrical diffusion probes", "url": "chapter-17.html", "text": "A bent strip relaxes after its load changes. That response can arise from local interstitial reorientation, from solute moving across the whole thickness, or from mechanisms unrelated to diffusion. A relaxation time becomes a diffusion coefficient only after geometry, coupling, and the measured loss convention have been specified. Lecture 38 supplies mechanical and electrical methods; Lecture 40 supplies relaxation, Haven-ratio, carrier-profile, Snoek, and Gorsky problems. The Snoek and Gorsky numerical inputs were visually checked at 16:47–19:49. The standard-linear-solid and RC plots use separately declared synthetic parameters. 17.1 Delayed reversible strain produces a frequency-dependent loss L38 00:22 Elastic strain responds reversibly and effectively instantaneously on the observation time scale. Anelastic strain is reversible but delayed: an internal population or configuration approaches the state favored by applied stress. Plastic deformation instead leaves a permanent strain in the elementary distinction used here. Real specimens can combine these responses, so a measured phase lag need not have a single microscopic origin. Let stress \\(\\sigma\\) and strain \\(\\varepsilon\\) vary sinusoidally at angular frequency \\(\\omega\\) , in rad/s. Represent the linear response with complex Young's modulus \\(E^*=E'+iE''\\) under an \\(e^{i\\omega t}\\) convention. The storage modulus \\(E'\\) and positive loss modulus \\(E''\\) are in pascals. For imposed strain amplitude \\(\\varepsilon_0\\) , the dissipated energy per volume in one cycle is \\(\\Delta W=\\pi E''\\varepsilon_0^2\\) ; the storage-energy scale is \\(W=E'\\varepsilon_0^2/2\\) . The conventional inverse quality factor is consequently \\(Q^{-1}=\\Delta W/(2\\pi W)=E''/E'=\\tan\\delta\\) , with loss angle delta. Different energy normalizations must be identified rather than hidden in a factor of pi. For the real imposed strain \\(\\varepsilon(t)=\\varepsilon_0\\cos\\omega t\\), the stress is \\(\\sigma(t)=\\varepsilon_0(E'\\cos\\omega t-E''\\sin\\omega t)\\). Therefore the work per cycle is \\(\\Delta W=\\int_0^{2\\pi/\\omega}\\sigma\\dot\\varepsilon\\,dt=\\pi E''\\varepsilon_0^2\\): the mixed sine–cosine term integrates to zero and the squared-sine term supplies the positive dissipation. To construct a single-time constitutive model, introduce a dimensionless internal strain coordinate \\(h(t)\\) that approaches the imposed strain according to \\(\\tau_\\sigma\\dot h+h=\\varepsilon\\), where \\(\\tau_\\sigma\\gt0\\) is in seconds. Let \\(\\sigma=E_\\infty\\varepsilon-(E_\\infty-E_0)h\\), with relaxed modulus \\(E_0\\gt0\\) and unrelaxed modulus \\(E_\\infty\\gt E_0\\), both in pascals. Immediately after a strain step from equilibrium, h has not changed and the modulus is \\(E_\\infty\\); after h reaches the imposed strain, the modulus is \\(E_0\\). For harmonic amplitudes, the internal equation becomes \\((1+i\\omega\\tau_\\sigma)\\hat h=\\hat\\varepsilon\\). Substituting into the stress equation gives the standard-linear-solid response \\(E^*=\\hat\\sigma/\\hat\\varepsilon=E_\\infty-(E_\\infty-E_0)/(1+i\\omega\\tau_\\sigma)\\). Eliminating h also gives \\(\\sigma+\\tau_\\sigma\\dot\\sigma=E_0\\varepsilon+E_\\infty\\tau_\\sigma\\dot\\varepsilon\\). At fixed strain the stress relaxes with time \\(\\tau_\\sigma\\); at fixed stress the strain relaxes with creep time \\(\\tau_\\varepsilon=\\tau_\\sigma E_\\infty/E_0\\). Rationalizing the complex modulus gives \\[\nE'=E_\\infty-\\frac{E_\\infty-E_0}{1+y^2},\\qquad\nE''=\\frac{(E_\\infty-E_0)y}{1+y^2},\\quad y=\\omega\\tau_\\sigma. \\tag{17.1}\n\\] The normalized Debye modulus loss peaks at \\(y=1\\) , since differentiating \\(y/(1+y^2)\\) gives \\((1-y^2)/(1+y^2)^2\\) . But the loss tangent is \\((E_\\infty-E_0)y/(E_0+E_\\infty y^2)\\) , whose peak is at \\(y=\\sqrt{E_0/E_\\infty}\\) . Defining \\(\\tau_R=\\tau_\\sigma\\sqrt{E_\\infty/E_0}=\\sqrt{\\tau_\\sigma\\tau_\\varepsilon}\\) , with creep time \\(\\tau_\\varepsilon=\\tau_\\sigma E_\\infty/E_0\\) , places that peak at \\(\\omega\\tau_R=1\\) . The geometric mean is not an arithmetic mean. The distinction matters in a temperature sweep. If the relevant relaxation time obeys \\(\\tau=\\tau_0\\exp[H/(RT)]\\) , with activation enthalpy \\(H>0\\) in J/mol, a fixed-convention peak gives \\(\\ln\\omega=-\\ln\\tau_0-H/(RT_p)\\) . The slope against inverse peak temperature is \\(-H/R\\) . Relaxation time decreases as temperature increases; the positive sign in its Arrhenius exponent is consistent with the inverse rate. Temperature-dependent strengths or broad distributions can shift and broaden the peak beyond this single-time model. 17.2 Snoek relaxation measures local orientation changes L38 11:40 An interstitial atom can distort its local lattice anisotropically. A stress then favors some orientations of that distortion over others. Redistribution among equivalent orientations produces delayed strain even if an atom moves only over a few site spacings. The Snoek effect in a dilute BCC interstitial model arises from this stress-driven reorientation among octahedral-site classes. A relaxation peak reports an orientation-population time; connecting it to diffusion requires the jump geometry and rate convention. For the simplest ideal BCC octahedral network, each site has four nearest allowed neighbors at distance \\(a/2\\) , where \\(a\\) is the cubic lattice parameter. Let \\(w\\) , in s⁻¹, be the rate to each individual neighbor. The total jump rate is \\(\\Gamma=4w\\) . With unbiased uncorrelated jump vectors, the three-dimensional random-walk relation gives \\(D=\\Gamma(a/2)^2/6=wa^2/6\\) . The three orientation classes each connect to two neighbors in either other class. If \\(p_x,p_y,p_z\\) are their probabilities, the unforced rate equation is \\(\\dot p_x=2w(p_y+p_z-2p_x)\\) , and similarly for the others; their sum remains one. Write \\(p_\\alpha=1/3+\\delta p_\\alpha\\) , where \\(\\alpha=x,y,z\\) and \\(\\delta p_x+\\delta p_y+\\delta p_z=0\\) . The anisotropic deviations then obey \\(\\dot{\\delta p}_x=2w(\\delta p_y+\\delta p_z-2\\delta p_x)=-6w\\delta p_x\\) , with the same rate for the other components. Their population relaxation time is \\(\\tau_S=1/(6w)\\) . Combining this with the jump diffusivity gives \\[\nD=\\frac{a^2}{36\\tau_S}. \\tag{17.2}\n\\] This factor 36 encodes the specific octahedral geometry and rate definitions. Interactions, trapping, blocked sites, or another jump network can change it. Strong coupling between stress and strain can also distinguish an inferred mechanical loss time from the bare orientation-population time; the dilute weak-relaxation approximation must be stated when equating them. The visually checked tutorial gives \\(a=0.287\\) nm and relaxation time one microsecond. In the model above, conversion to meters and seconds gives \\(D=2.28803\\times10^{-15}\\) m²/s, consistent with the displayed rounded answer \\(2.3\\times10^{-15}\\) m²/s. The original statement says only a cubic metal, but the supplied Snoek relation presumes this BCC interstitial model. Cubic symmetry by itself does not specify the jump network. 17.3 Gorsky relaxation requires transport across a specimen L38 18:52 Bending a strip produces a stress gradient across its thickness. If a solute changes the local strain energy, the gradient can drive it toward the more favorable side. This Gorsky effect involves long-range redistribution and therefore depends on specimen dimensions, unlike a purely local orientation process. A time scale that changes with thickness is a useful discriminator, provided geometry and boundary conditions are controlled. After subtracting the stress-biased equilibrium profile, let a small concentration perturbation \\(u(x,t)\\) satisfy \\(u_t=D_{\\rm chem}u_{xx}\\) on a strip of thickness \\(d\\) , with zero normal flux at both faces. The slowest nonconstant mode is \\(u\\propto\\cos(\\pi x/d)e^{-\\pi^2D_{\\rm chem}t/d^2}\\) . Hence \\[\n\\tau_G=\\frac{d^2}{\\pi^2D_{\\rm chem}},\\qquad\nD_{\\rm chem}\\simeq\\Phi D^* \\tag{17.3}\n\\] The second relation is an additional dilute diagonal-mobility approximation with thermodynamic factor Phi. The mode derivation supplies the thickness-squared dependence; it does not supply the tracer coefficient or Phi from the relaxation data separately. The source tutorial compares a 0.5 mm strip at 300 °C with a 1 mm strip at 400 °C, supplies Arrhenius constants \\(D_0=2.3\\times10^{-4}\\) m²/s and \\(Q=280\\) kJ/mol, and asks to neglect thermodynamic-factor variation. Its wording calls those constants host-metal self-diffusion data even though the response concerns solute B. A quantitative B relaxation prediction needs the B mobility; the calculation below is conditional on using the supplied constants as its proxy. Host self diffusion does not establish that proxy physically. Using absolute temperatures 573.15 and 673.15 K gives proxy coefficients \\(6.98435\\times10^{-30}\\) and \\(4.31421\\times10^{-26}\\) m²/s. With common Phi, \\(\\tau_1/\\tau_2=(d_1/d_2)^2D_2/D_1=1544.24\\) . The thinner but colder specimen relaxes much more slowly in this model. Setting Phi to one gives enormous times \\(3.62672\\times10^{21}\\) and \\(2.34855\\times10^{18}\\) s. That scale is another reason to treat the inputs as an exercise rather than an experimentally practical relaxation protocol. The ratio direction is stated explicitly because reversing it gives its reciprocal. 17.4 Electrical measurements see charge transport and active carriers L38 22:59 For one mobile ionic species with number density \\(n_c\\) in m⁻³ and charge \\(q_e\\) in coulombs, a simple independent-carrier Einstein model gives conductivity \\(\\sigma=n_cq_e^2D_\\sigma/(k_BT)\\) , in S/m. Here \\(k_B\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\(D_\\sigma\\) is the conductivity diffusivity defined by that relation. Conductivity weights collective charge displacement, whereas tracer diffusion weights individual tagged-particle displacement. Correlations can make them differ. Using the chapter's convention, the Haven ratio is \\(H_R=D^*/D_\\sigma\\) . Its interpretation requires a consistent carrier density and the same species, phase, and conditions. Electronic conduction, grain-boundary resistance, electrode polarization, and multiple mobile ionic species can contaminate a naive single-carrier inversion. Measuring conductivity alone does not uniquely identify both carrier concentration and diffusivity. Impedance spectroscopy separates responses by frequency. A synthetic parallel resistor–capacitor element has \\(Z(\\omega)=R_e/(1+i\\omega R_eC_e)\\) , with resistance \\(R_e\\) in ohms and capacitance \\(C_e\\) in farads. Writing real and imaginary parts gives a semicircle \\((\\Re Z-R_e/2)^2+(-\\Im Z)^2=(R_e/2)^2\\) . The plotted sign convention uses negative imaginary impedance. Its peak is at \\(\\omega R_eC_e=1\\) . Figure 17.1 uses \\(R_e=1000\\) ohms and \\(C_e=10^{-6}\\) F, giving peak angular frequency 1000 rad/s and peak negative imaginary impedance 500 ohms. A series resistance would shift the circle; additional phases or interfaces can add arcs. Identifying an equivalent-circuit element with a specific physical process requires more evidence than fitting a semicircle. Figure 17.1. Synthetic response conventions. The mechanical panel distinguishes modulus loss from loss tangent. The electrical panel uses negative imaginary impedance for a parallel RC model. Neither panel assigns a unique microscopic mechanism from curve shape alone. Spreading resistance profiling measures a local electrical resistance on a prepared semiconductor section or bevel. Conversion to carrier concentration needs geometry and mobility calibration. Electrically active dopants change carrier populations, so this method can track diffusion-related activation profiles in materials such as silicon or gallium arsenide. It does not directly count every chemical dopant atom: inactive complexes, compensation, and mobility changes can separate chemical concentration from electrically active concentration. The laboratory evaluates the verified tutorial inputs, labels the host-to-solute proxy assumption, checks the loss-tangent peak convention, and draws the RC semicircle. Saved output reports the enormous conditional Gorsky times rather than silently presenting them as a practical measurement. It fits no experimental loss spectrum or impedance data. 17.5 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Which loss peaks at one? For \\(E_0=100\\) and \\(E_\\infty=120\\) in common modulus units, compare the peak positions of normalized modulus loss and loss tangent. Worked solution The normalized modulus loss is \\(y/(1+y^2)\\) , so its derivative vanishes at y one. The loss tangent is \\(20y/(100+120y^2)\\) . Differentiating gives a numerator proportional to \\(100-120y^2\\) , so its peak is \\(y=\\sqrt{100/120}=0.912871\\) . Rescaling time to the geometric mean relaxation time puts that peak at \\(\\omega\\tau_R=1\\) . The phrase “Debye peak” must therefore identify the response and time convention. 2. Recover the Snoek factor Use four jumps per site, individual rate w, jump length a/2, and orientation decay rate 6w to derive (17.2). Worked solution The total rate is 4w. The uncorrelated three-dimensional coefficient is \\(D=4w(a/2)^2/6=wa^2/6\\) . The population time is \\(\\tau_S=1/(6w)\\) , so \\(w=1/(6\\tau_S)\\) and \\(D=a^2/(36\\tau_S)\\) . Inserting 0.287 nm and one microsecond gives \\(2.28803\\times10^{-15}\\) m²/s. Using total rate in place of individual w in the orientation equation would create a coordination-factor error. 3. Interpret an activation plot Derive the slope of ln angular frequency versus inverse peak temperature and explain why a positive activation enthalpy makes relaxation faster at high temperature. Worked solution At the specified single-time peak, \\(\\omega\\tau_0e^{H/(RT_p)}=1\\) . Taking logarithms gives slope \\(-H/R\\) against \\(1/T_p\\) . As temperature rises, \\(H/(RT)\\) decreases, so the positive exponential in the time decreases. Rates have the opposite Arrhenius sign because they are proportional to inverse time. Changes of relaxation strength or multiple times require a more detailed peak model. 4. State the Gorsky ratio direction For the two verified tutorial strips, calculate sample-one time divided by sample-two time under the supplied mobility-proxy assumption. Worked solution The thickness ratio squared is one quarter. The coefficient ratio is \\(\\exp[(Q/R)(1/573.15-1/673.15)]\\) , since the Arrhenius prefactor cancels. Multiplication gives 1544.24. The reciprocal answers the opposite question. The formula additionally assumes a common thermodynamic factor and the same relaxation-mode geometry. Because the supplied constants are called host self-diffusion constants, interpreting them as B transport needs an explicit proxy assumption; the numerical ratio alone does not validate it. 5. What does a semicircle determine? Derive the parallel RC circle and peak frequency. Does one fitted circle prove ionic bulk diffusion? Worked solution Set \\(y=\\omega R_eC_e\\) . Then \\(\\Re Z=R_e/(1+y^2)\\) and \\(-\\Im Z=R_ey/(1+y^2)\\) . Eliminating y gives the circle centered at \\(R_e/2\\) with radius \\(R_e/2\\) ; its height peaks at y one. A fitted arc can constrain an effective resistance and time scale in this circuit model. Electrode, grain-boundary, or electronic responses can produce related shapes, so physical assignment needs geometry, temperature, frequency-range, and carrier evidence. 6. Local motion versus long-range diffusion A loss peak persists when specimen thickness is changed, while another time grows as thickness squared. What interpretation is supported? Worked solution The thickness-independent time is compatible with local reorientation such as a Snoek process, provided the defect population and stress coupling remain comparable. The thickness-squared time supports a specimen-spanning diffusion mode such as Gorsky redistribution under fixed boundaries. Neither scaling alone uniquely identifies the microscopic species or jump network. An electrically active profile can supply complementary carrier evidence, while a tracer profile can test long-range tagged transport. Keep those observables distinct before combining them into a mechanism claim. 17.6 Sources and limits L38 supplies the method sequence and L40 the worked input problems. The selected L40 frames at 16:47, 17:48, 18:48, and 19:49 establish the stated input values and the supplied Snoek relation. The rate-network derivation, explicit finite-strength loss convention, and RC calculation are original teaching developments. Relaxation spectra and electrical signals can mix several processes. A single-time fit or equivalent circuit is a model for an observable, not proof that every measured event contributes to macroscopic mass transport. NMR next exposes that distinction through phase accumulation and local-field correlation times."}, {"number": 18, "title": "NMR diffusion measurements and local jump times", "url": "chapter-18.html", "text": "A spin echo can lose amplitude because atoms changed position in a field gradient or because their local magnetic environments fluctuated. Those are different observations. The first can encode a displacement distribution directly; the second yields a correlation time whose relation to diffusion depends on a microscopic model. Lecture 39 supplies NMR foundations, gradient echoes, and indirect relaxation. Lecture 40 supplies Bloch-equation and motional-narrowing questions; Lecture 41's opening NMR reference is context only. The pulse-sequence timing is checked against the original Stejskal–Tanner paper at printed pp. 289–290. Numerical parameters below are synthetic. 18.1 Precession, excitation, and relaxation are separate terms L39 00:21 A nucleus with angular momentum \\(\\boldsymbol I\\) can have magnetic moment \\(\\boldsymbol\\mu=\\gamma\\boldsymbol I\\) , where the gyromagnetic ratio \\(\\gamma\\) has units rad/(s·T) in the angular-frequency convention. In a uniform static magnetic field \\(\\boldsymbol B=B_0\\boldsymbol e_z\\) , the magnetic energy is \\(-\\boldsymbol\\mu\\cdot\\boldsymbol B\\) . For a spin-one-half nucleus, the two Zeeman levels differ by \\(\\hbar|\\gamma|B_0\\) , with reduced Planck constant hbar in J·s. Their thermal population imbalance produces an equilibrium longitudinal magnetization. A radio-frequency field near the Larmor angular frequency \\(\\omega_0=|\\gamma|B_0\\) rotates magnetization away from that equilibrium direction. The classical ensemble magnetization \\(\\boldsymbol M\\) then precesses and relaxes. A phenomenological Bloch–Torrey model for an isotropic constant diffusivity D is \\[\n\\partial_t\\boldsymbol M=\\gamma\\boldsymbol M\\times\\boldsymbol B\n-\\frac{M_x\\boldsymbol e_x+M_y\\boldsymbol e_y}{T_2}\n-\\frac{M_z-M_{\\rm eq}}{T_1}\\boldsymbol e_z\n+D\\nabla^2\\boldsymbol M. \\tag{18.1}\n\\] Here \\(T_1\\) is the longitudinal recovery time and \\(T_2\\) the transverse coherence-decay time, both in seconds; \\(M_{\\rm eq}\\) is the uniform equilibrium longitudinal magnetization. The first term is precession, the next two describe relaxation, and the last transports magnetization with diffusing spins. Flow, exchange between environments, spatially varying equilibrium, and anisotropic diffusion require additional terms or a modified operator. Inversion recovery probes the return of longitudinal magnetization after a pulse sequence changes its sign. Transverse free-induction decay also contains static field inhomogeneity, often summarized by an apparent \\(T_2^*\\) ; refocusing echoes can remove a reversible static contribution without undoing every dynamic process. The distinction prevents static broadening from being misidentified as irreversible diffusion. There is no universal inequality \\(T_2\\leq T_1\\) . In a simple passive Markov two-level model with an additional nonnegative pure-dephasing rate, \\(T_2^{-1}=(2T_1)^{-1}+T_\\varphi^{-1}\\) , implying \\(T_2\\leq2T_1\\) within that model. Many solids have much shorter transverse times, but that common regime is not the stronger universal theorem. Relaxation conventions and the measured sequence must accompany reported times. 18.2 A field gradient converts displacement into phase L39 08:32 Take one spatial direction x and a field gradient \\(g(t)\\) , in T/m. The local angular frequency shift is \\(\\gamma g(t)x\\) . Radio-frequency refocusing pulses reverse the sign with which some parts of the trajectory contribute to the final phase. Let \\(g_{\\rm eff}(t)\\) denote that signed effective gradient. A spin at trajectory \\(x(t)\\) accumulates phase \\(\\phi=\\gamma\\int_0^{T_E}g_{\\rm eff}(t)x(t)dt\\) , where \\(T_E\\) is echo time. A balanced echo has \\(\\int_0^{T_E}g_{\\rm eff}dt=0\\) , so a stationary spin's position-dependent phase refocuses. Define \\(q_g(t)=\\gamma\\int_0^t g_{\\rm eff}(s)ds\\) , in m⁻¹. The subscript distinguishes this gradient moment from a nuclear-scattering wavevector. Integration by parts gives \\(\\phi=-\\int_0^{T_E}q_g(t)dx(t)\\) when the endpoint moments vanish. For unrestricted Brownian motion with coefficient D, increments have zero mean and variance \\(2Ddt\\) . The stochastic integral is Gaussian with variance \\(\\langle\\phi^2\\rangle=2D\\int q_g^2dt\\) . The characteristic function of a zero-mean Gaussian is \\(\\langle e^{i\\phi}\\rangle=e^{-\\langle\\phi^2\\rangle/2}\\) . The normalized diffusion attenuation is therefore \\[\n\\frac{S(g)}{S(0)}=e^{-bD},\\qquad\nb=\\int_0^{T_E}q_g^2(t)dt. \\tag{18.2}\n\\] The b value has units s/m², making the exponent dimensionless. Normalizing by a zero-gradient sequence at identical timing removes a common transverse-relaxation factor in this simple model. It does not remove flow phase, exchange, restricted geometry, or gradient-dependent artifacts. The Gaussian-increment assumption is the hinge of the monoexponential free-diffusion result. For a constant physical gradient with a refocusing pulse at time tau and echo at twice tau, the effective gradient changes sign halfway. The moment is triangular: \\(q_g=\\gamma gt\\) on the first interval and \\(q_g=\\gamma g(2\\tau-t)\\) on the second. Integrating its square gives \\(b=2\\gamma^2g^2\\tau^3/3\\) . Including independent transverse relaxation, the unnormalized echo factor is \\(\\exp[-2\\tau/T_2-(2/3)\\gamma^2g^2D\\tau^3]\\) . The coefficient two-thirds belongs to this sequence and timing convention. 18.3 Derive the finite-pulse Stejskal–Tanner factor L39 16:45 Consider two rectangular effective-gradient pulses of equal magnitude g, duration \\(\\delta\\) , and opposite sign, with leading edges separated by \\(\\Delta\\geq\\delta\\) . The first occupies \\([0,\\delta]\\) , and the second \\([\\Delta,\\Delta+\\delta]\\) . Between them, the gradient moment is constant even though the applied effective gradient is zero. At the end it returns to zero. The moment rises as \\(\\gamma gt\\) during the first pulse, remains \\(\\gamma g\\delta\\) until time Delta, then falls as \\(\\gamma g(\\Delta+\\delta-t)\\) . Its squared integral is the sum of two ramps and a plateau: \\[\nb=\\gamma^2g^2\\left[\\frac{\\delta^3}{3}\n+\\delta^2(\\Delta-\\delta)+\\frac{\\delta^3}{3}\\right]\n=\\gamma^2g^2\\delta^2\\left(\\Delta-\\frac\\delta3\\right). \\tag{18.3}\n\\] Equation (18.2) then gives the free-diffusion pulsed-gradient echo. The finite-pulse correction is not optional unless the pulse is short relative to separation at the required accuracy. Using only \\(\\gamma^2g^2\\delta^2\\Delta\\) overestimates b and consequently underestimates a fitted D from the same attenuation. Leading-edge separation is specified because defining Delta as a gap instead changes the algebraic expression. Stejskal and Tanner's original 1965 paper , printed pp. 289–290, develops the gradient-echo attenuation with explicit timing. The inspected original copy confirms the constant-gradient coefficient and the rectangular-pulse \\(\\delta^2(\\Delta-\\delta/3)\\) term. This citation supports the pulse model, while the derivation above makes its assumptions and units locally inspectable. The laboratory uses synthetic \\(\\gamma=10^8\\) rad/(s·T), \\(\\delta=0.005\\) s, \\(\\Delta=0.05\\) s, and \\(D=2\\times10^{-12}\\) m²/s. At g ten T/m, b is \\(1.208333\\times10^{12}\\) s/m² and normalized echo is 0.0892185. Fitting ln echo against b recovers \\(2\\times10^{-12}\\) m²/s. Numerical integration of the actual piecewise moment agrees with (18.3) to relative error \\(3.45\\times10^{-8}\\) . These are model checks, not a claim that those settings suit a particular spectrometer or material. 18.4 Local-field fluctuations produce a different inverse problem L39 18:48 Indirect diffusion-sensitive NMR observes fluctuations of magnetic dipolar, electric quadrupolar, or electronic local environments. A local reorientation can modulate those environments without transporting a nucleus far across the specimen. For a minimal magnetic-field model, let h(t) be a stationary, zero-mean scalar field fluctuation in tesla. Define its correlation \\(C(t)=\\langle h(t)h(0)\\rangle\\), in T². A simple model is \\(C(t)=C_0e^{-|t|/\\tau_c}\\), where \\(C_0=\\langle h^2\\rangle\\) is the field variance and \\(\\tau_c\\gt0\\) is a correlation time in seconds. Other interactions require their corresponding fluctuating coupling instead of this scalar field. The two-sided angular-frequency spectral density is \\[\nJ(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}C(t)e^{-i\\omega t}dt\n=\\frac{2C_0\\tau_c}{1+\\omega^2\\tau_c^2}. \\tag{18.4}\n\\] The result follows by adding the two half-line exponential integrals, or integrating the cosine transform. Relaxation rates sample combinations of these spectral densities at zero, Larmor, and sometimes harmonic frequencies, weighted by the interaction geometry and spin quantum numbers. Equation (18.4) alone does not fix a universal T1 or T2 expression for every nucleus. Spin locking introduces a rotating-frame time \\(T_{1\\rho}\\) ; metallic electrons can add a Korringa-type contribution. Separate channels must not be automatically assigned to diffusion. At fixed nonzero frequency, the function \\(\\tau_c/(1+\\omega^2\\tau_c^2)\\) rises and then falls, peaking at \\(\\omega\\tau_c=1\\) . A single measured relaxation rate can therefore correspond to either a short or a long correlation time. In the normalized example, values \\(\\omega\\tau_c=0.1\\) and ten both give 0.0990099. Multi-frequency and temperature measurements can help select a regime, but only with a justified interaction model. A minimal calculation explains motional narrowing . Assume the scalar field h(t) is Gaussian and produces a frequency fluctuation \\(\\delta\\omega(t)=\\gamma h(t)\\). Its free-precession phase is \\(\\theta(t)=\\gamma\\int_0^t h(s)ds\\). Stationarity gives \\(\\langle\\theta^2\\rangle=2\\gamma^2\\int_0^t(t-s)C(s)ds\\). For the exponential correlation, the normalized coherence is therefore \\[\n\\langle e^{i\\theta(t)}\\rangle\n=\\exp\\!\\left\\{-\\gamma^2C_0\\left[\\tau_ct-\\tau_c^2(1-e^{-t/\\tau_c})\\right]\\right\\}.\n\\] At \\(t\\ll\\tau_c\\), this becomes \\(\\exp(-\\gamma^2C_0t^2/2)\\), the quasistatic Gaussian dephasing limit. At \\(t\\gg\\tau_c\\), its logarithmic decay rate tends to \\(T_{2,\\rm dyn}^{-1}=\\gamma^2C_0\\tau_c\\). In the fast-fluctuation regime \\(|\\gamma|\\sqrt{C_0}\\tau_c\\ll1\\), the coherence survives many correlation times and is approximately exponential over its decay scale. At fixed field variance, shortening the correlation time then reduces this linewidth contribution and lengthens transverse coherence. This scalar Gaussian model illustrates the mechanism; it does not supply every dipolar or quadrupolar relaxation rate. Confined hopping, molecular rotations, and exchanges within a local cluster can decorrelate a field without unlimited displacement, so narrowing alone does not determine long-range diffusion. To convert a microscopic waiting time \\(\\tau_j\\) into long-time three-dimensional diffusivity, independent isotropic jumps of length ell give \\(D=\\ell^2/(6\\tau_j)\\). A correlated jump model may instead give \\(D=f\\ell^2/(6\\tau_j)\\), where f is the specified model's dimensionless tracer correlation factor; independence gives f equal to one. A local-field correlation time need not equal that waiting time: several jumps can be needed to randomize a local environment, or one rotation can do so without net translation. This is why direct gradient attenuation and indirect relaxation require different interpretation even when both are called NMR diffusion measurements. Figure 18.1. Distinct NMR inversions. Gradient attenuation encodes a displacement model; local relaxation samples a spectral density that is not one-to-one in correlation time. Both panels are synthetic and use the stated angular-frequency convention. The laboratory writes actual numerical output , checks the pulse moment by quadrature, and fits noiseless synthetic echo data. It establishes no confidence interval for an experiment and includes no restricted diffusion, flow, exchange, or pulse imperfections. Those would change the observation model, not merely add more digits to D. 18.5 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Identify Bloch–Torrey terms Which terms in (18.1) describe precession, longitudinal recovery, transverse decay, and diffusion? Why does a gradient not replace the relaxation terms? Worked solution The cross product rotates magnetization around the field. The term proportional to \\(M_z-M_{\\rm eq}\\) restores longitudinal magnetization, while the x and y terms reduce transverse coherence. The Laplacian transports magnetization carried by moving spins. A gradient changes position-dependent precession and, with motion, the phase distribution; it does not account for all local spin–lattice or spin–spin relaxation. Removing T1 and T2 because a diffusion term is present would discard physically distinct processes. 2. A constant-gradient echo Integrate the triangular moment for a refocusing pulse at tau. Recover the two-thirds coefficient. Worked solution The first half contributes \\(\\gamma^2g^2\\int_0^\\tau t^2dt=\\gamma^2g^2\\tau^3/3\\) . The second half is the reflected triangle and contributes the same amount. Thus \\(b=2\\gamma^2g^2\\tau^3/3\\) , and the normalized diffusion factor is \\(e^{-bD}\\) . A common independent transverse decay over total time twice tau multiplies it by \\(e^{-2\\tau/T_2}\\) . Confusing tau with total echo time changes a cubic factor, not just notation. 3. Quantify the narrow-pulse bias For the synthetic delta 0.005 s and Delta 0.05 s, by what fraction does omitting delta/3 overestimate b? What happens to an inferred coefficient? Worked solution The relative overestimate is \\(\\Delta/(\\Delta-\\delta/3)-1=\\delta/(3\\Delta-\\delta)=0.0344828\\) , or 3.448%. With fixed measured attenuation, fitted D equals the true D times \\((\\Delta-\\delta/3)/\\Delta=0.966667\\) , a 3.333% underestimate. The percentages differ because one is normalized by the true b and the other by the true D. The correction requires the leading-edge separation convention. 4. Derive the Lorentzian local spectrum Transform the exponential correlation and explain its inverse ambiguity at one frequency. Worked solution Evenness gives \\(J=2C_0\\int_0^\\infty e^{-t/\\tau_c}\\cos(\\omega t)dt\\) . Taking the real part of \\(2C_0/(1/\\tau_c+i\\omega)\\) gives (18.4). With \\(u=\\omega\\tau_c\\) , the normalized response is \\(u/(1+u^2)\\) , unchanged by replacing u with its reciprocal. A single rate proportional to that spectrum cannot generally distinguish fast and slow branches without additional information. 5. Counterexample to a universal time inequality In the simple relation \\(T_2^{-1}=(2T_1)^{-1}+T_\\varphi^{-1}\\) , what occurs when pure dephasing vanishes? Worked solution Setting \\(T_\\varphi^{-1}=0\\) gives \\(T_2=2T_1\\) , which directly contradicts a proposed universal \\(T_2\\leq T_1\\) inequality. Nonnegative pure dephasing yields the weaker bound within the stated passive Markov model. Many solids occupy a much stronger-dephasing regime, but an empirical tendency cannot substitute for the actual model conditions. 6. Relaxation without transport A nucleus rotates between orientations in a fixed cage and has a temperature-dependent correlation time. Can that time determine long-range D? Worked solution No. Rotation can change dipolar or quadrupolar fields and therefore NMR relaxation while the nucleus remains confined. Its mean-square displacement saturates instead of growing indefinitely as \\(6Dt\\) . A long-range coefficient would require a model of cage escape or translational jumps, with jump length, waiting-time statistics, and correlations. Gradient-time dependence or complementary tracer/scattering measurements can help distinguish localized reorientation from transport, but the local relaxation time alone is insufficient. 18.6 Sources and scope L39 supplies the NMR spine, and L40's bounded relaxation questions supply diagnostic prompts. The original Stejskal–Tanner paper was inspected at printed pp. 289–290; the pulse factors are both cited and derived locally. No empirical nuclear constant is inferred from the synthetic gyromagnetic ratio used in the laboratory. Gradient echoes require an accessible displacement and coherence window. Indirect relaxation requires an interaction and correlation model. The next chapter uses recoil-free nuclear resonance and neutron scattering to observe frequency and wavevector dependence, adding information about jump geometry while retaining its own resolution and linewidth conventions."}, {"number": 19, "title": "Mössbauer and neutron scattering probes", "url": "chapter-19.html", "text": "A broadened nuclear resonance can report motion, but its width also contains the finite lifetime of the nuclear state and the instrument response. Neutron scattering adds wavevector dependence, which can distinguish a continuum displacement model from discrete jumps. Both methods require a consistent dictionary between lifetime, angular frequency, energy, and full or half width. Lecture 41 supplies Mössbauer and quasielastic neutron methods. The evaluated Fe57 half-life was checked in the BIPM DDEP Co57 report, PDF p. 11, Tables 2–3. The jump-model formulas below are derived locally; the original Chudley–Elliott paper was available only at publisher-abstract level. 19.1 Recoil-free resonance preserves momentum conservation L41 00:21 Emission or absorption of a gamma photon transfers momentum. For an isolated nucleus the associated recoil energy can move emission and absorption out of resonance. In a crystal, a fraction of events can occur without creating or destroying a lattice phonon. Momentum is then accepted by the crystal as a whole with negligible recoil energy on the nuclear-line scale. This recoil-free fraction enables the Mössbauer effect. It does not mean that photon momentum disappears. A source and absorber can be brought through resonance by moving one relative to the other. At speeds small compared with the speed of light \\(c_{\\rm light}\\) , a source velocity component v along the photon path shifts energy by \\(\\Delta E\\simeq E_\\gamma v/c_{\\rm light}\\) . A measured transmission versus velocity spectrum is therefore an energy spectrum expressed through the Doppler conversion. Hyperfine magnetic and electric interactions, chemical environment, thickness, source structure, and motion can all change its shape. For the Fe57 resonance used with a Co57 source, the inspected BIPM DDEP report, p. 11, Table 2 lists the 14.41295 keV level and half-life \\(t_{1/2}=98.0(4)\\) ns. An exponential population \\(P(t)=e^{-t/\\tau_n}\\) reaches one half at \\(t_{1/2}=\\tau_n\\ln2\\) . Its mean lifetime is consequently 141.384 ns using the central value, rather than 98 ns. The reported precision of the evaluated input should not be inferred from the extra digits of that arithmetic conversion. A decaying transition amplitude has factor \\(e^{-t/(2\\tau_n)}\\) . Its frequency response has a Lorentzian intensity profile with angular-frequency half width at half maximum \\(1/(2\\tau_n)\\) . Multiplying by reduced Planck constant \\(\\hbar\\) converts angular frequency to energy. Thus the natural single-transition energy full width is \\[\n\\Gamma_{E,\\rm natural}^{\\rm FWHM}=\\frac{\\hbar}{\\tau_n}. \\tag{19.1}\n\\] The laboratory computes 4.6555 neV for the central half-life input, corresponding to a single-line Doppler full width about 0.09683 mm/s at the stated resonance energy. Identical Lorentzian source and absorber lines would contribute additive widths in a transmission convolution, before other broadening mechanisms. One should not substitute the half-life into (19.1) or confuse the width of one transition with a complete measured source–absorber spectrum. 19.2 Motion enters through a self-correlation function L41 09:35 Let \\(\\boldsymbol r(t)\\) be the position of a tagged resonant atom. At wavevector \\(\\boldsymbol q\\) , in m⁻¹, define its intermediate self-scattering function \\(I_s(\\boldsymbol q,t)=\\langle e^{i\\boldsymbol q\\cdot[\\boldsymbol r(t)-\\boldsymbol r(0)]}\\rangle\\) . It is the characteristic function of the displacement distribution. The average includes an equilibrium ensemble or a justified stationary time average. This object connects nuclear resonance motion broadening and incoherent neutron scattering without equating their experimental resolutions. For unrestricted isotropic Gaussian diffusion with coefficient D, each Cartesian displacement has variance \\(2D|t|\\) . The Gaussian characteristic function gives \\(I_s=e^{-Dq^2|t|}\\) , where \\(q=|\\boldsymbol q|\\) . More generally, suppose the relevant correlation has a single exponential rate \\(r(q)>0\\) , in s⁻¹. Define the angular-frequency spectrum with the explicit convention \\[\nS_s(q,\\omega)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\nI_s(q,t)e^{-i\\omega t}dt\n=\\frac{r(q)}{\\pi[\\omega^2+r(q)^2]}. \\tag{19.2}\n\\] The last equality follows by the exponential cosine transform. The spectrum integrates to one over angular frequency and has angular-frequency HWHM r. Since \\(E=\\hbar\\omega\\) , energy HWHM is \\(\\hbar r\\) and energy FWHM is \\(2\\hbar r\\) . A width in hertz instead uses \\(\\omega=2\\pi\\nu\\) . These distinctions account for common factors of two and two-pi; none is a physical discrepancy once the conventions are aligned. In a simple recoil-free resonance model, multiplying the natural time correlation by a motion correlation that decays as \\(e^{-r|t|}\\) adds r to its angular HWHM. The additional energy FWHM is then \\(2\\hbar r\\) . This statement assumes independent exponential broadening and a compatible line model. Hyperfine multiplets, distributions of environments, and nonexponential motion require more than one Lorentzian width. If high-wavevector decorrelation corresponds to independent jumps with total rate \\(\\Gamma\\) and length ell, one obtains \\(r\\simeq\\Gamma\\) and \\(D=\\ell^2\\Gamma/6\\) . Only then does an additional energy FWHM \\(\\Delta\\Gamma_E\\) imply \\(D=\\ell^2\\Delta\\Gamma_E/(12\\hbar)\\) . A local reorientation or correlated vacancy encounter need not obey that identification. The precise jump derivation below establishes when it is justified. 19.3 Neutrons supply both momentum and energy transfer L41 21:04 A neutron with wavevector \\(\\boldsymbol k\\) has momentum \\(\\hbar\\boldsymbol k\\) and nonrelativistic kinetic energy \\(\\hbar^2k^2/(2m_n)\\) , with neutron mass \\(m_n\\) . Scattering from initial to final states transfers wavevector \\(\\boldsymbol q=\\boldsymbol k_i-\\boldsymbol k_f\\) and energy \\(\\hbar\\omega=E_i-E_f\\) to the specimen. The sign convention should be recorded because instruments may display specimen energy gain or loss differently. An elastic component has zero energy transfer within resolution. Inelastic features can reflect phonon excitation and other modes. Quasielastic neutron scattering (QENS) resolves a narrow distribution near zero transfer associated with relaxational dynamics. For incoherent scattering dominated by single-particle correlations, the self-function in (19.2) is the relevant idealized object. Coherent scattering instead contains correlations between different atoms as well, and can also carry transport information. It is incorrect to claim that only incoherent scattering ever probes diffusion. In the continuum free-diffusion limit, the predicted angular HWHM is \\(Dq^2\\) . Measuring width at several q values tests this dependence and distinguishes it from a q-independent instrumental or local time scale. A linewidth too narrow relative to resolution cannot reliably determine arbitrarily small D; a broad feature can leave the accessible energy window. The method's time and length windows follow from resolution and wavevector coverage, rather than a universal diffusivity interval. The synthetic laboratory chooses jump length 0.3 nm and mean waiting time one nanosecond, giving \\(D=1.5\\times10^{-11}\\) m²/s in the independent isotropic model. At \\(q=10^9\\) m⁻¹, the continuum angular HWHM is \\(1.5\\times10^7\\) s⁻¹ and energy HWHM is 9.87318 neV. The corresponding full widths are twice those values. These are calculated illustration scales, not measured diffusion for a named element. 19.4 Derive jump-sensitive widths and the low-q limit L41 30:18 Suppose jumps occur as a Poisson process of total rate \\(\\Gamma=1/\\tau_j\\) , with independent identically distributed jump vectors \\(\\boldsymbol\\ell\\) . Let their characteristic function be \\(\\varphi(\\boldsymbol q)=\\langle e^{i\\boldsymbol q\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\ell}\\rangle\\) . Conditional on exactly n jumps, the displacement characteristic function is \\(\\varphi^n\\) . Averaging over the Poisson count gives \\[\nI_s(\\boldsymbol q,t)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\ne^{-\\Gamma t}\\frac{(\\Gamma t)^n}{n!}\\varphi^n\n=\\exp[-\\Gamma t(1-\\varphi(\\boldsymbol q))],\\quad t\\geq0. \\tag{19.3}\n\\] For a symmetric jump distribution the characteristic function is real. The rate entering (19.2) is \\(r=\\Gamma(1-\\varphi)\\) . With fixed length ell and uniformly distributed directions, averaging over polar angle yields \\(\\varphi=\\tfrac12\\int_{-1}^1e^{iq\\ell u}du=\\sin(q\\ell)/(q\\ell)\\) . Therefore \\[\nr(q)=\\frac1{\\tau_j}\\left[1-\\frac{\\sin(q\\ell)}{q\\ell}\\right]\n\\simeq\\frac{\\ell^2q^2}{6\\tau_j}=Dq^2\\quad(q\\ell\\ll1). \\tag{19.4}\n\\] The low-q limit depends only on the second displacement moment. At larger q, the discrete jump geometry becomes visible; the continuum quadratic law cannot be extrapolated indefinitely. For six equally probable jumps along cubic axes, \\(\\varphi=[\\cos(q_x\\ell)+\\cos(q_y\\ell)+\\cos(q_z\\ell)]/3\\) . Different orientations of a single crystal give different high-q rates even though they share the isotropic quadratic limit. Figure 19.1 compares the [100] and [111] directions with the isotropic-direction model. Chudley and Elliott's original jump-diffusion paper is a historical primary reference for this class of interpretation; only its indexed publisher abstract was inspected. Equations (19.3)–(19.4) are therefore supplied as independent derivations under their explicit assumptions, not attributed to an unread page or equation number. A vacancy can exchange repeatedly with the same atom, producing a sequence of correlated jumps. The independent-jump Poisson model then fails at the atom-jump level. One possible reduced description treats whole encounters as independent events with their own net-displacement distribution. Its rate and net displacement, rather than the count of every microscopic exchange, control the long-time coefficient. Returning jumps can decorrelate a local signal while contributing little net transport. Detailed finite-q spectra require the actual encounter model, not an arbitrary universal correction factor. 19.5 Fit the spectrum convolved with the measured resolution A measured spectrum is an intrinsic scattering model blurred by an instrument response, with backgrounds and sometimes elastic contributions. If \\(R(\\omega)\\) is a normalized resolution function, an idealized observation is \\(S_{\\rm obs}=R*S_s\\) , where the asterisk denotes integration over intermediate frequency. The official Mantid convolution-fitting documentation explicitly models this relation. It supports fitting the convolved physical model rather than pretending a fitted observed width is already an intrinsic one. Two normalized Lorentzians with HWHM a and b convolve to another Lorentzian with HWHM \\(a+b\\) . Their time-domain exponential factors multiply, adding decay rates. Two independent Gaussian widths instead add variances. A Gaussian convolved with a Lorentzian gives a Voigt profile; subtracting measured full widths linearly is not a valid general deconvolution. In the right panel of Figure 19.1, angular frequency is scaled by an arbitrary positive reference rate \\(r_{\\rm ref}\\) . The intrinsic Lorentzian has HWHM \\(0.1r_{\\rm ref}\\) . The Lorentzian resolution has HWHM \\(0.2r_{\\rm ref}\\) , giving combined HWHM \\(0.3r_{\\rm ref}\\) ; the alternative Gaussian resolution has standard deviation \\(0.2r_{\\rm ref}\\) , giving the plotted Voigt profile. These are explicitly chosen synthetic kernels, not resolution functions matched to equal full width. Figure 19.1. Original jump and resolution calculations. Finite-q shape contains information beyond a diffusion coefficient, while resolution convolution can obscure that information. The plotted rates and line shapes use declared synthetic parameters. A careful analysis calibrates resolution under relevant conditions, distinguishes coherent and incoherent weights for the sample, records energy versus frequency units and half versus full widths, and examines q and temperature dependence. A single broadened line does not uniquely select jump length, waiting time, dimensionality, or vacancy mechanism. Mixtures of localized and translating populations may add an elastic fraction plus several dynamic components, increasing the inverse problem's ambiguity. The laboratory computes the lifetime conversion, natural width, Doppler conversion, jump-model small-q limit, and synthetic resolution curves. It checks that the discrete model tends to the expected quadratic coefficient, then saves its actual output . It does not fit source nuclear or neutron spectra, infer an empirical jump mechanism, or validate a specific instrument resolution. 19.6 Exercises with complete solutions 1. Half-life is not mean lifetime Convert the evaluated 98 ns half-life into the mean lifetime and natural single-transition energy FWHM. Worked solution From \\(e^{-t_{1/2}/\\tau_n}=1/2\\) , \\(\\tau_n=98/\\ln2=141.384\\) ns using the central input. Then \\(\\hbar/\\tau_n=4.65549\\) neV is the natural single-line FWHM. Its HWHM is half that value. Substituting 98 ns directly into the mean-lifetime formula would overestimate the width by \\(1/\\ln2\\) . The arithmetic precision does not reduce the evaluated half-life uncertainty. 2. Derive the spectral normalization Show that the exponential self-correlation gives a normalized Lorentzian with angular HWHM r. Worked solution Evenness reduces the transform to \\(\\pi^{-1}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-rt}\\cos\\omega t\\,dt=r/[\\pi(r^2+\\omega^2)]\\) . Integrating over all omega gives one because the antiderivative is \\(\\pi^{-1}\\arctan(\\omega/r)\\) . The value at zero is \\(1/(\\pi r)\\) ; at absolute omega r the value is half as large. Thus HWHM is r and FWHM is twice r. Energy conversion multiplies either width by hbar. 3. Recover the diffusion coefficient from jumps Expand the isotropic fixed-length characteristic function at small q and identify D. Worked solution The expansion \\(\\sin u/u=1-u^2/6+O(u^4)\\) with \\(u=q\\ell\\) gives rate \\(r=\\ell^2q^2/(6\\tau_j)+O(q^4)\\) . Matching the continuum rate Dq² identifies \\(D=\\ell^2/(6\\tau_j)\\) . This also follows from a Poisson mean jump count t/tau and independent mean-square displacement ell² per jump. Correlated or confined jumps can invalidate that identification even while producing measurable local relaxation. 4. Why does crystal orientation matter? For six cubic-axis jumps, compare the characteristic functions along [100] and [111], and explain their common low-q limit. Worked solution Along [100], \\(\\varphi=(\\cos u+2)/3\\) . Along [111], each Cartesian projection is \\(u/\\sqrt3\\) , giving \\(\\varphi=\\cos(u/\\sqrt3)\\) . Both expand as \\(1-u^2/6+O(u^4)\\) , so they have the same isotropic quadratic diffusion coefficient. Their higher-order terms differ because finite wavevectors resolve the discrete directional network. Powder averaging can suppress some orientation information and needs its own averaging model. 5. When may widths be subtracted? A fitted observed line is broader than the resolution. State two cases with different valid broadening rules. Worked solution If both intrinsic and resolution profiles are Lorentzian under the same frequency convention, their HWHM values add and linear subtraction can recover the intrinsic HWHM. If both are independent Gaussians, variances add, so squared standard deviations are subtracted. A Lorentzian–Gaussian convolution is Voigt and generally requires fitting that convolved shape. Using linear full-width subtraction for arbitrary resolution can bias the inferred relaxation rate and therefore D. 6. Broadening without a unique mechanism A Mössbauer line broadens with temperature and a QENS width is approximately quadratic at small q. What is established, and what remains unidentified? Worked solution With controlled source and resolution, the first observation supports increased dynamics or a changed local spectral environment, but does not by itself isolate translation from other broadening. The second supports a diffusive long-wavelength correlation rate in the measured channel under its scattering model. Jump geometry, individual waiting time, vacancy encounters, and species-specific transport still require finite-q, orientation, isotope, structural, and temperature evidence. A common low-q coefficient does not imply a unique microscopic network. 19.7 Sources and boundaries L41 supplies both probe families. The BIPM report supports the evaluated level energy and half-life at its inspected tables. The Mantid page supports resolution convolution; the Chudley–Elliott reference is used at abstract-level historical scope, while the Poisson derivation is explicit here. The independent-jump model assumes stationary isotropic or specified crystalline jumps and exponential waiting times. Real spectra may contain coherent collective motion, correlated encounters, multiple species, localized fractions, and non-Lorentzian resolution. The following chapters introduce fast microstructural paths, for which a measured displacement distribution can depart strongly from homogeneous bulk diffusion even when each local region obeys a sensible transport law."}, {"number": 20, "title": "Grain-boundary diffusion and kinetic regimes", "url": "chapter-20.html", "text": "A tracer penetrates much farther along a grain boundary than through the adjoining crystal. Serial sectioning, however, collects both regions together. The resulting deep tail contains information about a fast path, its storage capacity, and its exchange with the grains. Extracting a boundary diffusivity requires deciding which of those quantities the experiment can actually separate. Source spine: L42–43. The conservation derivation, synthetic two-region calculation, and inverse-problem exercises below are original teaching developments. Boundary structure and preparation remain material-specific; the numerical inputs are not measurements of a named alloy. 20.1 A boundary transports and stores solute L42 00:22 A grain is a crystal region with a particular orientation. A grain boundary is the transition between neighboring grains. Its atomic arrangement depends on their misorientation, the boundary plane, and the atomic translations and chemical state at the interface. A low-angle boundary may be interpreted as an array of dislocations; a coherent twin has a specially ordered relation between the two crystals. Calling both objects “interfaces” does not give them equal transport coefficients. Let the tracer be a dilute distinguishable isotope or solute that does not appreciably change the host structure. Its molar concentration is \\(c\\) , in mol m⁻³. In a fixed lattice frame, its molar flux is \\(\\mathbf J\\) , in mol m⁻² s⁻¹. We initially assume isothermal, isotropic Fickian transport inside each region: \\(\\mathbf J=-D\\nabla c\\) in the grains and \\(\\mathbf J_b=-D_b\\nabla c_b\\) inside a boundary of width \\(\\delta\\) . Both diffusivities have units m² s⁻¹. The boundary width is an effective model thickness; it need not equal a uniquely measurable geometric thickness. Atomic disorder can change defect formation energies, jump barriers, and correlated motion. Boundaries therefore often provide rapid transport, with consequences for sintering, creep, precipitation, and film stability. This is an empirical tendency, not an ordering theorem for every boundary and species. A large diffusivity at one temperature also does not prove a smaller activation enthalpy: the prefactors may differ. At the atomic scale, vacancy, interstitial, and collective motion can all contribute. One measured macroscopic coefficient does not select one microscopic mechanism. For a foreign solute, local chemical equilibrium may enrich the boundary. Define the dimensionless segregation factor \\(s_b=c_b/c_s\\) , where \\(c_s\\) is the grain concentration immediately outside the boundary. We assume a dilute linear partition relation at fixed temperature, so \\(c_b=s_b c_s\\) with constant \\(s_b>0\\) . This relation can hold while both concentrations change with time. It asserts rapid interfacial equilibration, not global equilibrium or zero flux. For boundaries occupying volume fraction \\(g\\) , the locally equilibrated mean concentration is \\(\\bar c=[1-g+g s_b]c_s\\) . The factor in brackets is storage capacity relative to the grain. Thus increased boundary concentration can raise the stored amount without proportionally raising the distance an individual atom travels. Transport and storage must enter the same balance. 20.2 Deriving a boundary-plus-lattice balance L42 10:30 The Fisher construction idealizes a straight boundary perpendicular to the specimen surface. Let \\(z\\geq0\\) be depth and \\(y\\) the transverse coordinate, both in meters. The boundary occupies \\(|y|<\\delta/2\\) ; the adjacent grains extend away on either side. There is no variation in the third direction. Away from the boundary, conservation and the scalar constitutive law give \\[\\partial_t c=D(\\partial_y^2c+\\partial_z^2c). \\tag{20.1}\\] Suppose the boundary is sufficiently thin that its concentration is nearly uniform across its width. Its amount per unit boundary-plane area is then \\(\\delta c_b=s_b\\delta c_s\\) , in mol m⁻². Define \\(j_L,j_R\\) as fluxes directed out of the boundary into the left and right grains. They can have either sign. Integrate conservation over a boundary segment of depth \\(dz\\) and unit extent in the third direction. The net influx at the segment ends is \\(-\\partial_z(\\delta J_{b,z})dz\\) ; the side loss is \\((j_L+j_R)dz\\) . Dividing by \\(dz\\) yields \\[s_b\\delta\\,\\partial_t c_s=s_b\\delta D_b\\,\\partial_z^2c_s-j_L-j_R. \\tag{20.2}\\] Every term has units mol m⁻² s⁻¹. At the right interface, \\(j_R=-D\\partial_yc|_{\\delta/2+}\\) . At the left interface, the outward direction is negative \\(y\\) , so \\(j_L=+D\\partial_yc|_{-\\delta/2-}\\) . In a symmetric profile enriched near the boundary, both outward fluxes are positive and reduce the boundary amount. Defining the normal first prevents an unnoticed sign reversal. The grain solution takes the interface value \\(c=c_s\\) ; its normal flux provides the exchange term in (20.2). Consequently, the boundary does not diffuse independently when leakage is substantial. It feeds solute sideways into grains while conducting it deeper along \\(z\\) . Conversely, a depleted boundary can draw solute inward. The two equations conserve their combined amount when external surfaces carry zero flux, because the internal exchange terms cancel exactly. Boundary data matter as much as these equations. A maintained surface concentration prescribes values at \\(z=0\\) over time. An instantaneous thin tracer layer prescribes an initial areal amount, represented ideally by a delta distribution in depth. Those are different experiments. A measured section mean over transverse width \\(W\\) is \\(\\bar c(z,t)=W^{-1}[\\int_{\\mathrm{grains}}c\\,dy+\\delta c_b]\\) . A large local boundary concentration can contribute a small total signal because its sampling fraction is small. 20.3 Harrison regimes are scale comparisons L43 00:22 Let \\(d\\) denote a representative spacing between boundaries. The bulk penetration scale \\(\\ell_D=\\sqrt{Dt}\\) measures how far leakage spreads laterally during time \\(t\\) . It does not by itself measure the deeper distance traveled along the fast boundary. Harrison's classification organizes the relation between leakage fields, boundary width, and grain spacing. The original classification is associated with Harrison's 1961 paper ; here we derive the scale interpretation rather than assign universal numerical cutoffs to every microstructure. In the A regime , leakage fields from neighboring boundaries overlap substantially. If transverse equilibration is rapid, the specimen behaves at large scales like an effective medium. For parallel boundaries aligned with the imposed depth gradient, the total flux is \\(\\bar J_z=-[(1-g)D+g s_bD_b]\\partial_zc_s\\) . Combining this conductance with the storage derived in Section20.1 gives \\[D_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\frac{(1-g)D+g s_bD_b}{1-g+g s_b}. \\tag{20.3}\\] The familiar arithmetic average follows when \\(s_b=1\\) . A common gradient, connected parallel paths, constant geometry, and local partition equilibrium were essential. The A regime does not require \\(D_b\\approx D\\) ; a very fast boundary can coexist with overlapping slow leakage fields. An effective coefficient inferred from a broad profile does not separately identify the component coefficients. In the B regime , lattice leakage is appreciable but neighboring leakage fields are largely isolated: qualitatively \\(\\delta\\ll\\ell_D\\ll d\\) when segregation is modest. Serial-section profiles may show a near-surface bulk contribution and a deep boundary-induced tail. Quantitative asymptotics impose additional conditions. For the dilute straight-boundary formulation used here, define \\[P=s_b\\delta D_b,\\qquad \\alpha=\\frac{s_b\\delta}{2\\sqrt{Dt}},\\qquad \\beta=\\frac{P}{2D\\sqrt{Dt}}. \\tag{20.4}\\] The product \\(P\\) has units m³ s⁻¹; the other quantities are dimensionless. The quoted B-tail analysis requires small \\(\\alpha\\) and large \\(\\beta\\) , conventionally tested against \\(\\alpha<0.1\\) and \\(\\beta>10\\) for this particular asymptotic treatment. These tests supplement grain separation, source geometry, and detection limits. Merely finding a straight-looking tail is insufficient. In the C regime , exchange with the lattice is negligible over the observation time. The boundary approximately obeys one-dimensional diffusion. An instantaneous source then gives a Gaussian depth dependence, and a plot of the logarithm of boundary tracer concentration against \\(z^2\\) has slope \\(-1/(4D_bt)\\) . The approximation concerns negligible leakage, not an assertion that the lattice diffusivity is exactly zero. The measured amount may be extremely small because only the boundary volume carries the signal. 20.4 A B tail identifies a product L43 16:54 For the instantaneous-source B asymptotic, let \\(m_B=-d\\ln(\\bar c/c_{\\mathrm{ref}})/d(z^{6/5})>0\\) , where \\(c_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) is any fixed concentration reference. It only makes the logarithm dimensionless and does not affect its slope. The Le Claire analysis gives the following cited inverse relation, not a result derived by assuming that a tail is Gaussian: \\[P=1.308\\sqrt{D/t}\\,m_B^{-5/3}. \\tag{20.5}\\] The formula and its source conditions are given explicitly in Vaidya and coauthors, PDF page5, Eq.(3) . The slope has units m⁻⁶/⁵; its power contributes m². The square-root factor contributes m s⁻¹, producing the required m³ s⁻¹. Using the concentration itself instead of its logarithm would fail this dimensional and normalization check. The relation is an asymptotic inference tool; the present chapter does not reproduce the exact integral solution from which its numerical constant arises. If \\(s_b\\delta\\) increases tenfold while \\(D_b\\) decreases tenfold, the product is unchanged. For example, the synthetic pairs \\((s_b\\delta,D_b)=(5\\times10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm m,10^{-12}\\,\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}})\\) and \\((5\\times10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm m,10^{-13}\\,\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}})\\) both give \\(P=5\\times10^{-21}\\) m³s⁻¹. A valid B-tail product alone cannot distinguish them. Their complete profiles and validity parameters need not coincide outside the asymptotic window. A C-regime experiment can independently determine \\(D_b\\) , allowing \\(P/D_b=s_b\\delta\\) if both observations describe comparable boundary states at the same temperature. In practice they often require different temperatures. Extrapolation then adds assumptions about Arrhenius behavior, structural stability, segregation, and annealing history. Assigning a conventional thickness, such as a half-nanometer, converts the measured capacity length into an inferred segregation factor; it does not turn that thickness into an independently measured fact. For a dilute equilibrium partition law write \\(s_b=s_0\\exp[-\\Delta H_{\\mathrm{seg}}/(RT)]\\) , where \\(R\\) is the molar gas constant, \\(T\\) absolute temperature, and \\(\\Delta H_{\\mathrm{seg}}<0\\) means favorable segregation. With constant thickness and \\(D_b=D_{b0}\\exp[-Q_b/(RT)]\\) , the product has apparent enthalpy \\(Q_P=Q_b+\\Delta H_{\\mathrm{seg}}\\) . Favorable segregation can therefore make the product's slope smaller than the boundary mobility slope. A positive binding-enthalpy convention would write the same physics with the opposite sign in the definition. 20.5 A conservative two-region calculation L42 19:40 The reproducible laboratory ch20.py solves a dimensionless version of \\(c_t=\\nabla\\cdot(D\\nabla c)\\) in a closed rectangle. Transverse position runs from −1 to1 and depth from0 to4. The central strip has diffusivity20, the surrounding material1, and segregation is absent. An initial pulse occupies the first depth cell. This finite-width, finite-domain construction demonstrates exchange and conservation; it is not a numerical evaluation of the infinite-domain B-tail formula. Each shared face transfers one amount from one cell to its neighbor, with the same amount subtracted from the donor. Harmonic face diffusivities enforce a common flux across discontinuous material coefficients. With grid spacings \\(h_y,h_z\\) , the code chooses a time step below \\([2D_{\\max}(h_y^{-2}+h_z^{-2})]^{-1}\\) . All off-diagonal weights are nonnegative and their sum is at most one, so the update preserves nonnegative concentrations. Exterior face fluxes vanish. The outputs check combined mass and minimum concentration; they do not certify continuum convergence. At dimensionless time0.03, the stored total amount remains2 to rounding error in both runs. The depth second moment rises from0.0602732 for uniform material to0.116339 with the fast strip. The latter occupies approximately0.04918 of the discretized width. Although the total amount is unchanged, some atoms travel farther before leaking into the adjacent region. Refinement should compare moments and resolved profiles while holding the physical strip width fixed; changing the number of strip cells without checking that width changes the model. Figure20.1. Synthetic closed-domain calculation at time0.03. The logarithmic concentration axis exposes the low-amplitude tail. The static curves and saved output provide the complete print alternative. A separate SI-unit design calculation uses \\(D=10^{-18}\\) m²s⁻¹, \\(D_b=10^{-12}\\) m²s⁻¹, \\(s_b=10\\) , \\(\\delta=5\\times10^{-10}\\) m, \\(d=10^{-5}\\) m, and \\(t=3600\\) s. It gives \\(\\ell_D=6\\times10^{-8}\\) m, \\(\\alpha=0.0416667\\) , \\(\\beta=41666.7\\) , and \\(\\ell_D/d=0.006\\) . These satisfy the stated B asymptotic tests and separated-field scale comparison. Specimen geometry, source quality, and detection dynamic range still require experimental checks. 20.6 Exercises and worked solutions Internal flux cancellation. Integrate (20.1) in the grains and (20.2) along a boundary with closed external faces. Prove combined conservation. Solution The divergence theorem converts each grain integral into its boundary fluxes. At a grain–boundary interface, the grain receives \\(j_L\\) or \\(j_R\\) . Equation(20.2) loses precisely their sum. Along-depth endpoint fluxes and exterior grain fluxes vanish by the closed conditions. Thus \\(d[\\int c\\,dV+\\int\\delta c_b\\,dA]/dt=0\\) . Concentrations may change while this total remains fixed. Segregation capacity. Derive the effective coefficient for equilibrated parallel boundaries. Explain why using only the numerator in (20.3) is incorrect when segregation is strong. Solution Mean storage is \\(Kc_s\\) , with \\(K=1-g+gs_b\\) . Mean flux is \\(-A\\partial_zc_s\\) , with \\(A=(1-g)D+gs_bD_b\\) . For constant coefficients, conservation gives \\(K\\partial_tc_s=A\\partial_z^2c_s\\) ; equivalently \\(\\partial_t\\bar c=(A/K)\\partial_z^2\\bar c\\) . The numerator alone measures conductance relative to \\(c_s\\) , whereas the measured mean includes boundary storage. Product ambiguity. Can a perfect B-tail measurement distinguish the two synthetic parameter pairs in Section20.4? Solution Not through the product inversion: both give identical \\(P\\) . An independently known \\(D\\) removes one nuisance input but does not split \\(s_b\\delta\\) from \\(D_b\\) . A C measurement, a direct segregation measurement, or a richer validated full-profile model supplies additional information. Each proposed measurement must still be sensitive to the parameter it is intended to determine. C-tail inversion. Express \\(D_b\\) in terms of the negative slope \\(m_C\\) of \\(\\ln(\\bar c/c_{\\mathrm{ref}})\\) versus \\(z^2\\) . State two failure conditions. Solution The Gaussian exponent is \\(-z^2/(4D_bt)\\) , hence \\(D_b=-1/(4tm_C)\\) . The slope has units m⁻², yielding m²s⁻¹. Leakage changes the model, and a finite initial source width broadens the profile independently of diffusion. Depth-resolution broadening is another possible bias. A straight fitted line over a short range cannot by itself exclude these alternatives. Temperature slope. Under the assumptions of Section20.4, why can \\(Q_P<Q_b\\) ? Solution Multiply the Arrhenius forms to get \\(P=s_0\\delta D_{b0}\\exp[-(Q_b+\\Delta H_{\\mathrm{seg}})/(RT)]\\) . Favorable segregation has negative \\(\\Delta H_{\\mathrm{seg}}\\) , so \\(Q_P\\) is reduced. This result assumes no temperature-dependent thickness or additional prefactor variation. It does not imply that the jump barrier itself fell. Design judgment. A long anneal produces a nearly Gaussian section profile. Is it legitimate to report the fitted coefficient as a boundary diffusivity? Solution No such identification follows from shape alone. An A-regime effective medium can be Gaussian, as can a leakage-free C-regime boundary. Compute the lattice penetration length from independent lattice data, compare it with grain spacing and boundary width, and inspect the tracer's sampled amount. Varying time or grain size tests whether overlap drives the profile. Report an effective coefficient until the pathway assignment is supported. The strongest inference is often a deliberately limited one: a measured product or an effective coefficient, accompanied by the assumptions under which it was extracted. That limited result can guide the next experiment more reliably than an unjustified microscopic label."}, {"number": 21, "title": "Dislocation pipe diffusion and pathway competition", "url": "chapter-21.html", "text": "A specimen can contain very little dislocation-core material and still transport most of a tracer through it. That possibility follows from conductance, but only if the cores connect the source to the destination and exchange solute with the lattice. A small geometric fraction is therefore neither evidence for irrelevance nor proof of dominance. Source spine: L44 develops cylindrical pipe diffusion and its kinetic regimes; L45 supplies pathway, regime, and probe tutorials. The geometric balances, synthetic conductance comparison, and exchange calculation are developed here. The nuclear-probe questions connect to Chapters18–19 rather than establish new universal linewidth laws. 21.1 From a dislocation line to a transport fraction L44 00:18 A dislocation is a line defect characterized by a Burgers vector, the closure failure of a lattice circuit around the line. Its core is the region where a smooth elastic description no longer adequately represents the local atomic arrangement. Core structure and stress can change the formation and migration of atomic defects. A pipe model represents this region as a cylinder of radius \\(a\\) with axial diffusivity \\(D_p\\) , embedded in a lattice of scalar diffusivity \\(D\\) . Both coefficients have units m²s⁻¹; \\(a\\) is in meters. Dislocation density \\(\\rho_d\\) is total line length divided by specimen volume, with units m⁻². For straight, parallel, nonoverlapping pipes spanning the specimen, multiply length density by the circular core area to obtain \\[\\phi_p=\\pi a^2\\rho_d,\\qquad L_d\\sim\\rho_d^{-1/2}.\\tag{21.1}\\] The first quantity is a volume fraction; \\(L_d\\) is an approximate spacing. The spacing estimate suppresses arrangement-dependent numerical factors. The fraction formula assumes distinct cylindrical cores and loses meaning as overlap becomes significant. For curved or tangled dislocations, line density alone does not specify axial connectivity or orientation. A disconnected loop can provide local redistribution without carrying a through-thickness flux. We use dilute tracer concentration \\(c_p\\) in the pipe and \\(c\\) in the lattice, both in mol m⁻³, with fluxes measured in the stationary lattice frame. Unless explicitly stated, there is no segregation at the interface. An effective core radius such as a fraction of a nanometer is a modeling convention; it should not be silently treated as a universal material constant. Since the inferred fraction scales as its square, radius uncertainty has direct consequences for inferred pipe diffusivity. 21.2 Cylindrical leakage follows geometry L44 04:36 Take \\(z\\) along a straight pipe and \\(r\\geq a\\) as radial distance from its axis. Assume rotational symmetry, constant coefficients, and no reaction. A cylindrical shell has volume proportional to \\(r\\,dr\\) , while its radial face area is proportional to \\(r\\) . Conservation therefore gives the axisymmetric lattice equation \\[\\partial_tc=D\\left[\\frac1r\\partial_r(r\\partial_rc)+\\partial_z^2c\\right].\\tag{21.2}\\] The radial term contains both \\(\\partial_r^2c\\) and \\(r^{-1}\\partial_rc\\) . Replacing it by a Cartesian second derivative discards the changing area through which the flux passes. A cylindrical model must keep that geometry even when the atomic core itself is represented only by an effective radius. Assume the core equilibrates across its radius sufficiently rapidly that \\(c_p(z,t)\\) is uniform over its cross section. Let \\(j_r=-D\\partial_rc|_{a+}\\) be outward molar flux from core to lattice. A segment of length \\(dz\\) stores \\(\\pi a^2c_pdz\\) , conducts through its two circular ends, and loses \\(2\\pi a j_rdz\\) through its circumference. Division by its volume yields \\[\\partial_tc_p=D_p\\partial_z^2c_p-\\frac{2}{a}j_r.\\tag{21.3}\\] The exchange coefficient \\(2/a\\) is circumference divided by area. It has units m⁻¹, so multiplication by flux gives mol m⁻³s⁻¹. For an enriched core, concentration decreases outward and \\(j_r>0\\) ; leakage lowers its stored concentration. The lattice receives the same amount. With no interfacial segregation, \\(c(a,z,t)=c_p(z,t)\\) . A partition factor would change this boundary relation and the relative storage, just as for grain boundaries. A useful reduced exchange model ignores spatial gradients temporarily and tracks two well-mixed regions. Let \\(\\lambda>0\\) , in s⁻¹, be the rate at which their concentration difference relaxes. Set \\(\\dot c_p=-(1-\\phi_p)\\lambda(c_p-c_l)\\) and \\(\\dot c_l=\\phi_p\\lambda(c_p-c_l)\\) , where \\(c_l\\) is lattice concentration. Multiplying by the volume fractions proves conservation. Subtraction gives \\(d(c_p-c_l)/dt=-\\lambda(c_p-c_l)\\) , so the difference decays exponentially. This original construction separates a local exchange time from an axial diffusion time; it is not the exact cylindrical leakage kernel. 21.3 Dominance requires geometry and exchange L44 18:20 For aligned connected pipes, rapid exchange, equal local concentration, and a shared axial gradient, add fluxes per total area. The result is \\(\\bar J_z=-[(1-\\phi_p)D+\\phi_pD_p]\\partial_zc\\) . With constant volume fractions, the effective coefficient is \\[D_{\\parallel}=(1-\\phi_p)D+\\phi_pD_p.\\tag{21.4}\\] The relative pipe contribution is \\(\\phi_pD_p/[(1-\\phi_p)D]\\) . Thus the small parameter is not the fraction alone: it is the fraction multiplied by the diffusivity contrast. This derivation is a parallel-conductance result. It does not apply unchanged to a network that exchanges slowly, terminates within the specimen, or is oriented perpendicular to the imposed gradient. For comparison, arrange two materials in series with length fractions \\(1-\\phi_p\\) and \\(\\phi_p\\) . Steady conservation requires a common flux, while their concentration drops add. Dividing total length times flux by total drop gives \\[D_{\\mathrm{series}}=\\left[(1-\\phi_p)/D+\\phi_p/D_p\\right]^{-1}.\\tag{21.5}\\] The same constituent coefficients and fractions can therefore have radically different effective transport. This series construction is a counterexample to inference from fraction alone; it is not a realistic drawing of one straight pipe. A dislocation network requires an orientation distribution and connectivity model before either idealization becomes appropriate. The synthetic laboratory takes \\(a=5\\times10^{-10}\\) m, \\(\\rho_d=10^{14}\\) m⁻², \\(D=10^{-20}\\) m²s⁻¹, and \\(D_p=10^{-12}\\) m²s⁻¹. It computes \\(\\phi_p=7.85398\\times10^{-5}\\) and spacing approximately \\(10^{-7}\\) m. Equation(21.4) gives \\(7.85498\\times10^{-17}\\) m²s⁻¹, whereas the series construction gives \\(1.00008\\times10^{-20}\\) m²s⁻¹. The connected pipe contribution exceeds the lattice contribution by approximately7854.60 even though the pipe fraction is tiny. Figure21.1. Density sensitivity at fixed radius and fixed constituent diffusivities. The curve assumes connected parallel paths and rapid exchange; it does not predict how annealing changes a real dislocation network. 21.4 Profiles distinguish regimes only under a model L44 21:30 Compare the lattice penetration length \\(\\sqrt{Dt}\\) with pipe spacing and radius. Overlapping leakage fields favor an A-regime effective-medium description. Isolated leakage fields permit a B-regime pipe tail. Negligible leakage permits C-regime transport along the core. These are scale classifications, not fixed temperature labels. The relevant time changes with the anneal, and dislocation recovery can change spacing while diffusion proceeds. Within the appropriate isolated-path asymptotics, a pipe-induced B tail is approximately exponential in depth, so \\(\\ln\\bar c\\) is approximately linear in \\(z\\) . The boundary B tail instead uses \\(z^{6/5}\\) . In C conditions, an instantaneous core source yields the one-dimensional Gaussian exponent \\(-z^2/(4D_pt)\\) . These distinct transforms provide diagnostics, but fitting one exponent over a narrow signal range is not proof of a pathway. Instrumental averaging, background subtraction, mixed pathways, and evolving structure can imitate a local slope. Serial sectioning measures the cross-sectional mean, not the concentration in one core. For an instantaneous initial tracer layer, write the complete mean as a lattice-only reference plus a pipe-induced perturbation. The perturbation can be negative near the source: fast paths remove tracer there and place it deeper. It is the sum that must remain nonnegative. A negative difference between two positive profiles is consistent with conservation. A maintained concentration source need not have the same redistribution signature, because it supplies additional tracer over time. A defensible discrimination experiment varies both time and microstructure. Measure the lattice coefficient independently, characterize line density and boundary density, and maintain comparable source preparation. Then compare profile changes across anneals with predictions for each candidate geometry. An independent spatial map locating enrichment at lines or planes provides stronger pathway evidence than a fitted exponent alone. The exact Bessel-function kernel for isolated cylindrical leakage is not reconstructed from caption fragments here; the governing balances define what a fuller model would have to solve. 21.5 Tutorial calculations and reproducibility L45 08:36 The source tutorial supplies an Arrhenius lattice coefficient with prefactor \\(D_0=1.2\\times10^{-4}\\) m²s⁻¹, molar activation enthalpy \\(Q=303000\\) Jmol⁻¹, temperature673K, and anneal time two weeks. Using \\(D=D_0e^{-Q/(RT)}\\) gives \\(D=3.65099\\times10^{-28}\\) m²s⁻¹. The time is1209600s and the lattice length is \\(2.10148\\times10^{-11}\\) m. With the separate boundary convention \\(s_b=1,\\delta=0.5\\) nm, the parameter \\(\\alpha=\\delta/(2\\sqrt{Dt})\\) equals11.8964. This fails the B-condition small-alpha test and supports negligible lattice leakage for that boundary model. It is not a universal pipe-regime threshold. The tutorial also connects the transport problem to scattering. For equal incident wavelengths and elastic scattering, wavevector magnitude is \\(q=(4\\pi/\\lambda_n)\\sin(\\theta/2)\\) , where \\(\\lambda_n\\) is neutron wavelength and \\(\\theta\\) the full scattering angle. Angles \\(\\pi/6\\) and \\(\\pi/4\\) therefore give ratio \\(\\sin(\\pi/12)/\\sin(\\pi/8)=0.676327\\) . The sine uses half the scattering angle. Converting this geometric quantity into a diffusivity requires the dynamic scattering model and linewidth convention developed in Chapter19. The executable ch21.py writes these quantities, the parallel/series comparison, and a closed two-compartment exchange check to the saved output . In the exchange check the conserved mean differs from its initial value by at most \\(1.12\\times10^{-16}\\) . Inputs are fixed; no random sampling or empirical fitting is hidden in the figure. Source polynomial and Arrhenius-table values whose exact slide coefficients have not been verified are not reproduced. Unit checks remain mandatory:0.1µm is100nm, and a natural-log intercept of−41 implies a prefactor \\(e^{-41}=1.56288\\times10^{-18}\\) in the stated reference units. 21.6 Exercises and worked solutions Radius sensitivity. The line density rises100-fold and core diameter rises by a factor \\(\\sqrt2\\) . How does the pipe term change? Solution Radius changes by the same factor as diameter. Therefore \\(a^2\\rho_d\\) changes by \\(2\\times100=200\\) . At fixed \\(D_p\\) , the term \\(\\phi_pD_p\\) rises200-fold. The total effective coefficient does not generally rise by exactly200 because the lattice contribution remains and the fraction affects it as well. Changes in connectivity or core chemistry would invalidate the fixed-parameter scaling. Leakage geometry. Derive the coefficient in front of outward flux in the thin-pipe balance. Solution For length \\(dz\\) , divide lateral loss \\(2\\pi a j_rdz\\) by volume \\(\\pi a^2dz\\) . The result is \\(2j_r/a\\) . A planar boundary has two faces per width and therefore a different geometry factor. Equal apparent thickness does not make the two leakage problems identical. Exchange solution. Solve the well-mixed model for conserved mean \\(c_0\\) and initial difference \\(\\Delta_0\\) . Solution The difference is \\(\\Delta=\\Delta_0e^{-\\lambda t}\\) . Conservation gives \\(\\phi_pc_p+(1-\\phi_p)c_l=c_0\\) . Solving the two linear equations yields \\(c_p=c_0+(1-\\phi_p)\\Delta\\) and \\(c_l=c_0-\\phi_p\\Delta\\) . Nonnegative initial values remain nonnegative because both approach their positive weighted mean monotonically. Connectivity counterexample. Explain why identical line densities need not give identical through-thickness fluxes. Solution One specimen may contain straight lines bridging the two surfaces, another closed loops. Both can have the same line length per volume. The first offers an uninterrupted axial route; the second requires lattice exchange between loops. Equation(21.4) assumes the former geometry. Line density constrains the amount of core material, not the complete transport network. Negative perturbation. Does a pipe correction below zero near the initial source violate positivity? Solution No. Define the correction as \\(\\Delta c=\\bar c_{\\mathrm{pipe}}-\\bar c_{\\mathrm{lattice}}\\) . It is negative wherever the pipe-bearing specimen has been depleted more strongly. Each complete concentration can remain positive. In a closed pulse experiment their integrated amounts agree, so negative and positive parts of the difference compensate. Checking the correction's sign alone tests the wrong physical quantity. Discriminating a tail. Two linear fits work equally well for \\(\\ln\\bar c\\) against \\(z\\) and \\(z^{6/5}\\) over a narrow interval. Design a useful next measurement. Solution Extend the measurable depth and concentration range, repeat at a different time, and independently characterize boundary and line densities. Jointly test the time-dependent profiles against pipe and boundary models with the same source and response function. A spatially resolved enrichment map is particularly valuable because it tests path geometry directly. More significant figures in the original short-range fit cannot resolve structural nonidentifiability. A fast-path model becomes useful when its geometry, exchange law, and measured averaging operation are all specified. The coefficient obtained after those choices is evidence about that model, with a stated domain of validity."}, {"number": 22, "title": "Ordered, nanocrystalline, and quasicrystalline materials", "url": "chapter-22.html", "text": "A high density of possible destinations does not guarantee rapid atomic transport. An ordered crystal penalizes some destinations chemically, a nanocrystal changes the amount and connectivity of interfacial material, and a quasicrystal changes the relation between local motion and long-range order. In each case, the transport model must describe which configurations are allowed and which sequence of moves produces lasting displacement. Source spine: ordered compounds in L46, nanocrystals in L47, and quasicrystals in L48. The explicit four-site cycle, shared-boundary geometry, and tensor-symmetry argument are developed here. Material-specific empirical tables and unresolved detailed regime-map formulas are not reconstructed from captions. 22.1 Sites and atoms are different objects L46 00:19 An ordered compound has chemically distinct sublattices: particular site sets preferentially contain particular species. In ideal B2 AB, put A sites at integer coordinates of a cubic lattice and B sites at its body centers. The Bravais lattice of the chemically ordered structure is simple cubic with a two-atom basis. Erasing the distinction between A and B gives the familiar body-centered arrangement, but the chemical ordering matters for both diffraction and diffusion. Write \\(A_B\\) for an A atom occupying a B site, an antisite , and \\(V_A\\) for a vacancy on an A site. These symbols distinguish an atom's chemical identity from the site's preferred occupant. A nearest-neighbor atom–vacancy exchange in B2 switches sublattices and can create an antisite. Subsequent exchanges may restore order or accumulate additional disorder. Thus a large local jump rate does not necessarily imply a large long-time tracer diffusivity. A compound can accommodate off-stoichiometry through structural vacancies, antisites, or combinations whose free energies depend on composition. A line compound is an idealized phase with negligible composition width; real ordered phases can have finite homogeneity ranges. Thermal defects coexist with those needed by composition. The equilibrium population and the transition rates are separate ingredients: chemical order changes both the probability of finding a configuration and the rate of leaving it. For comparison, ideal L1₂ A₃B has B at cube corners and A at face centers. Each nearest-neighbor shell has twelve sites, as in the underlying face-centered arrangement. An A face-center site has eight A and four B neighbors; a B corner has twelve A neighbors. Relative to a corner, the nearest face centers have coordinates given by permutations of \\((\\pm1/2,\\pm1/2,0)\\) in cubic-parameter units: three choices of the zero coordinate and four sign combinations give twelve sites. The majority A sublattice therefore has connected nearest-neighbor A sites, whereas B sites do not neighbor one another directly. This distinction changes which order-preserving vacancy paths are available. The source slide's statement that the minority L1₂ atom has only four A neighbors is inconsistent with this geometry. We use twelve. The correction affects mechanism reasoning: minority motion cannot be analyzed by importing the majority sublattice's coordination. Tracer coefficients that happen to be similar can suggest a coupled mechanism, but their numerical similarity is not a unique fingerprint. 22.2 A six-jump cycle restores chemical order L46 15:48 Consider four B2 sites, in units of the cubic lattice parameter \\(a_0\\) : site0 at \\((0,0,0)\\) , site1 at \\((1/2,1/2,1/2)\\) , site2 at \\((1,1,0)\\) , and site3 at \\((1/2,1/2,-1/2)\\) . Sites0 and2 belong to the A sublattice;1 and3 to B. Every edge of the cycle0–1–2–3–0 has length \\(\\sqrt3a_0/2\\) and joins unlike sublattices. Initially place a vacancy at0 and correct atoms at the other sites. Exchange the vacancy successively with occupants of sites1,2,3,0,1,2. The complete configuration, rather than just the vacancy trajectory, determines whether order is restored. In the table a dash denotes a vacancy, and entries identify atom species, not site types. Table22.1. One explicitly defined six-jump cycle. Completed jumps Site0 (A) Site1 (B) Site2 (A) Site3 (B) Antisites 0 — B A B 0 1 B — A B 1 2 B A — B 2 3 B A B — 3 4 — A B B 2 5 A — B B 1 6 A B — B 0 At the end the vacancy has moved to another A site by \\(a_0[110]\\) , the A atom has moved oppositely, and the B atoms have exchanged their individual positions. The final occupied sites are chemically correct. Order preservation is therefore compatible with motion of both chemical species. During the cycle, however, three antisites coexist at its most disordered stage. Their energies and the competing reverse jumps determine the probability of completing the cycle. This is one specified mechanism, not a theorem that every sequence of six jumps restores order. A sequence containing repeated immediate reversals may produce no net displacement. Converting cycle completion into a diffusivity requires the cycle frequency, possible orientations, species displacements, and intercycle correlations. Model-specific ratios between A and B tracer coefficients cannot be treated as universal bounds across ordered alloys. A triple defect in a specified B2 convention combines two vacancies on one sublattice with an antisite on the other, for example \\(2V_A+A_B\\) . Its motion can use coordinated sequences different from the single-vacancy cycle. An antistructural bridge uses pre-existing antisites to connect otherwise restricted transport paths. Sustained long-range motion requires suitable connectivity, often expressed as a percolation condition: enough linked accessible sites to span the specimen. Merely finding one antisite does not establish such a network. Composition-dependent vacancies and antisites can therefore change the dominant mechanism without a change in the compound's nominal structure. 22.3 Nanocrystals change amount, spacing, and stability L47 00:19 A nanocrystalline material contains grains with dimensions on the nanometer scale. The transport consequence is geometric before it is kinetic: there is more interface per volume, and a short absolute diffusion distance can cross a whole grain. Physical deposition, chemical synthesis, and biological routes can produce different porosity, impurities, stresses, and boundary states. Grain size alone is therefore an incomplete specification of a transport specimen. For an explicit geometry, tile space by cubic cells of repeat length \\(d\\) . Assign a shared boundary layer of full width \\(\\delta<d\\) to each cell face, leaving a crystalline interior cube of side \\(d-\\delta\\) . Counting the boundary material once gives \\[g=1-(1-\\delta/d)^3=3\\delta/d-3(\\delta/d)^2+(\\delta/d)^3.\\tag{22.1}\\] The leading term is \\(3\\delta/d\\) . Dividing six exposed cube faces by volume gives \\(6/d\\) , but each internal face belongs to two neighboring cells and must be shared. Confusing a full boundary width with a shell thickness on each side produces another factor of two. The finite expression also accounts for edge and corner overlaps within this idealized assignment; it is not an atom-counting law for every real boundary. With synthetic \\(\\delta=0.5\\) nm, a10nm cell gives \\(g=0.142625\\) , whereas100nm gives0.0149251. The thin-width estimates are0.15 and0.015. Holding \\(D=10^{-20}\\) m²s⁻¹ and \\(D_b=10^{-14}\\) m²s⁻¹ fixed, the parallel coefficients are \\(1.42626\\times10^{-15}\\) and \\(1.49261\\times10^{-16}\\) m²s⁻¹. The corresponding series coefficients remain close to the lattice value. More interfacial material has a large consequence only when it participates in an effective route. Figure22.1. Fixed constituent diffusivities and shared-boundary width. The topology comparison isolates geometry; a real grain-size series can also change boundary structure, segregation, and porosity. Grain growth makes the fraction and spacing time-dependent. Structural relaxation changes boundary mobility even at nearly fixed grain size. A fitted constant coefficient then averages an evolving specimen rather than measures one immutable boundary state. Measure size distributions before and after annealing, and seek intermediate-time information when growth is substantial. Porosity and triple junctions provide additional paths whose contribution need not scale with boundary area in the same way. 22.4 Grain size can move an experiment between regimes L47 08:35 Two distances are especially useful: bulk leakage length \\(\\ell_D=\\sqrt{Dt}\\) and leakage-free boundary length \\(\\ell_C=\\sqrt{D_bt}\\) . Compare both with grain spacing \\(d\\) . When \\(\\ell_C\\) spans many grains while \\(\\ell_D\\) remains short, a connected boundary network can conduct deeply without equilibrating grain interiors. When \\(\\ell_D\\) becomes comparable with spacing, adjacent leakage fields overlap and the isolated-boundary approximation fails. A B-regime scale can be obtained by a balance rather than by copying a detailed regime chart. Let \\(L_B\\) be penetration depth along an isolated boundary, \\(c_s\\) the adjacent lattice tracer concentration in molm⁻³, and \\(s_b=c_b/c_s\\) the dimensionless equilibrium segregation factor, where \\(c_b\\) is boundary tracer concentration on the same concentration-per-volume basis. Write \\(D\\) for lattice diffusivity and \\(D_b\\) for along-boundary diffusivity, both in m²s⁻¹. Assume rapid local boundary–lattice equilibrium and define \\(P=s_b\\delta D_b\\) , with units m³s⁻¹ and the full boundary width \\(\\delta\\) defined above. The divergence of boundary transport per boundary area scales as \\(Pc_s/L_B^2\\) . The growing leakage halo stores an amount of order \\(c_s\\sqrt{Dt}\\) per boundary area, whose time derivative scales as \\(c_s\\sqrt{D/t}\\) . Equating these scales gives \\[L_B\\sim(P^2t/D)^{1/4},\\qquad t_{B,d}\\sim Dd^4/P^2.\\tag{22.2}\\] The second estimate sets \\(L_B\\sim d\\) . Numerical prefactors and source dependence are intentionally absent from this scaling argument. It explains why boundary penetration can grow as the fourth root of time while ordinary leakage grows as the square root. It also explains why reducing grain size changes more than a transport fraction: the characteristic network distances are crossed sooner. The full source discussion subdivides finite-spacing regimes; the present estimates should not be substituted for its detailed transition formulas. The source calls the separated-fringe, two-branch case B2. In a coarse specimen, continued growth of lattice penetration relative to the fourth-root boundary scale can produce a nearly planar-front B4 description before the eventual A regime. In a finer specimen, the boundary penetration can exceed grain size while leakage fringes remain separate; this is the B2′ network extension. A C′ extension similarly denotes transport through a boundary network over many grains with negligible bulk leakage. Primed A labels distinguish effective-medium situations for these finer structures. These labels summarize relations among lengths; they do not define new atomistic jump mechanisms. Their exact transition prefactors require the corresponding network model. Strong segregation changes the effective capacity length from a purely geometric width to \\(s_b\\delta\\) . It can close the interval in which a simple isolated B asymptotic applies. With rapid equilibrium, the effective parallel coefficient must include storage as in Chapter20. Using \\(gD_b\\) as a dominant conductance additionally requires \\(gD_b\\gg(1-g)D\\) , not merely a large contrast \\(D_b/D\\) . A very small fraction can compensate a very large contrast. 22.5 Quasiperiodic order constrains a diffusion tensor L48 00:20 A quasicrystal has long-range order and sharp diffraction, yet lacks ordinary three-dimensional translational periodicity. Noncrystallographic rotational symmetries distinguish it from a periodic crystal. A decagonal structure can be periodic along its tenfold axis and quasiperiodic in the perpendicular plane. An icosahedral structure has a different set of three-dimensional rotational symmetries. Neither description means random atomic positions. The Fibonacci sequence illustrates one way nonperiodic order can have precise mathematical structure. Define \\(F_{n+1}=F_n+F_{n-1}\\) with positive initial terms. If ratios converge to \\(r>0\\) , dividing the recurrence by \\(F_n\\) gives \\(r=1+1/r\\) . The positive root is \\((1+\\sqrt5)/2=1.61803\\) ; the computed ratio34/21 is1.61905. This example explains a quasiperiodic construction, not a claim that every quasicrystal consists of one Fibonacci chain. For a homogeneous specimen with no applied field selecting another direction, write \\(\\mathbf J=-\\mathsf D\\nabla c\\) , with symmetric diffusivity tensor \\(\\mathsf D\\) . A symmetry rotation represented by an orthogonal matrix \\(\\mathsf R\\) must leave the physical law unchanged: \\(\\mathsf D=\\mathsf R\\mathsf D\\mathsf R^T\\) . A tenfold rotation about \\(z\\) makes the in-plane eigenvalues equal and removes coupling between that plane and the invariant axis. Thus \\[\\mathsf D_{\\mathrm{dec}}=\\operatorname{diag}(D_\\perp,D_\\perp,D_\\parallel),\\qquad \\mathsf D_{\\mathrm{ico}}=D_{\\mathrm{iso}}\\mathsf I.\\tag{22.3}\\] Here \\(\\mathsf I\\) is the identity matrix. Icosahedral rotations about multiple nonparallel axes eliminate the remaining special axis and force a scalar second-rank tensor. This argument concerns second-rank long-wavelength transport in a symmetry-preserving state; microscopic paths can still be highly structured. A uniaxial periodic crystal can have the same tensor form as the decagonal case, so tensor form alone does not identify quasiperiodic atomic dynamics. Phason degrees of freedom describe rearrangements among configurations associated with quasiperiodic order. They are not adequately defined as merely an extra species of conventional point defect. Phason-related rearrangements, vacancy-mediated jumps, and collective motion are possible transport ingredients. Positive activation volume is consistent with some vacancy mechanisms but does not uniquely establish one; anisotropy is likewise a constraint rather than a mechanism label. Compare species, temperature, structure, and competing models before assigning dominance. Tracer feasibility also requires care. Natural aluminum is effectively monoisotopic for stable-isotope contrast, but this does not mean radioactive aluminum isotopes do not exist. Available activity, lifetime, cost, and detector sensitivity determine practical isotope use. An inability to apply one tracer protocol is a measurement limit, not evidence that a species cannot diffuse. 22.6 Laboratory, exercises, and worked solutions The deterministic laboratory enumerates the six-jump configurations and counts antisites after every move, then computes the boundary fractions and geometry curves. Its saved output confirms antisite counts1,2,3,2,1,0 and the values printed above. The species model checks chemical order; it does not simulate transition-state energies or predict a cycle rate. The plotted coefficients are synthetic fixed inputs, not measurements of an ordered or nanocrystalline alloy. Track individual atoms. In Table22.1, distinguish the two B atoms by labels. Which move after a completed cycle? Solution The B atom initially at1 goes to0, then3; the B atom initially at3 goes to2, then1. The A atom initially at2 goes to1, then0. The final vacancy is at2. Chemical order is restored, but each participating atom has undergone a nonzero net displacement. Losing isotope labels would hide the B exchange, illustrating why tracer motion and chemical-order change are distinct observables. Derive the minority coordination. List the twelve nearest A neighbors of a B atom at the origin in ideal L1₂. Solution In units of the cubic parameter, the neighbors are permutations of \\((\\pm1/2,\\pm1/2,0)\\) . There are three choices of the zero coordinate and four sign combinations, giving twelve distinct positions, all face-center A sites, each at distance \\(a_0/\\sqrt2\\) . This direct count resolves the erroneous four-neighbor source statement without needing a fitted transport model. Shared-interface counting. Why does the leading boundary fraction contain3 rather than6? Solution A cube has six faces, but each internal interface is shared by two neighboring cubes. Unique area per volume is therefore \\(3/d\\) . Multiplication by the full assigned boundary width gives \\(3\\delta/d\\) . The exact expression subtracts edge overlaps and adds corner overlap. Counting isolated free surfaces answers a different geometry question. A grain-growth bias. Suppose an anneal doubles grain size while all constituent coefficients stay fixed. What happens to the leading parallel boundary contribution? Solution In the thin-width limit \\(g\\approx3\\delta/d\\) , so doubling \\(d\\) halves \\(gD_b\\) . A constant-coefficient fit averages this changing contribution. If the measured coefficient falls, the observation does not by itself prove a higher jump barrier: geometric loss of boundary material is already a possible cause. Exact comparison uses (22.1) when the thin-width approximation is insufficient. Check the B length. Verify dimensions of (22.2) and compare its time dependence with bulk leakage. Solution \\(P^2t/D\\) has dimensions \\((\\mathrm{m^6s^{-2}})\\mathrm s/(\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}})=\\mathrm{m^4}\\) ; its fourth root is a length. The ratio \\(L_B/\\ell_D\\) scales as \\(t^{-1/4}\\) for fixed coefficients. Thus leakage can catch up with a boundary fringe during a long anneal. Equality marks a scaling crossover, not a derivation of exact transition constants. Tensor versus mechanism. A decagonal specimen has \\(D_\\perp>D_\\parallel\\) . Does this prove phason-mediated diffusion? Solution No. Symmetry permits two independent coefficients but does not order them or specify microscopic carriers. Direction-dependent vacancy barriers or jump connectivity can also yield anisotropy. An adequate test compares quantitative temperature, species, and pressure dependences of competing models, ideally with an additional probe of configurational rearrangements. The tensor observation is a constraint on mechanisms, not a unique inversion. Order, size, and symmetry enter a diffusion model in different places: accessible configurations, geometric capacity and connectivity, and allowed constitutive tensors. Keeping those roles separate prevents a structural label from being mistaken for a kinetic explanation."}, {"number": 23, "title": "Diffusion mechanisms in metallic alloys", "url": "chapter-23.html", "text": "Two solutes in the same metal can differ enormously in mobility. Even one solute can move through several populations: substitutional atoms, interstitial atoms, trapped atoms, and atoms temporarily bound to a vacancy. A useful comparison must specify which population carries flux and which measured amount supplies the concentration gradient. Source spine: metallic self-diffusion and interstitial transport in L49, structural tutorials in L50, and dilute-solute mechanisms in L51. The two-channel, trap-capacity, and hybrid examples below are synthetic calculations. High-entropy-alloy evidence is developed separately in Chapter24. 23.1 A diffusion comparison needs a reference state L49 00:20 Self-diffusion measures motion of atoms in a chemically uniform material, usually by an isotope tracer. Its tracer coefficient \\(D^*\\) has units m²s⁻¹ and describes spreading without a bulk chemical-composition gradient. An intrinsic coefficient describes a component flux in a specified frame, while an interdiffusion coefficient describes coupled composition evolution. Those quantities can differ because thermodynamic driving forces, vacancy flow, and correlations enter differently. A table that mixes them does not provide a controlled material ranking. Absolute temperature \\(T\\) , in kelvin, controls thermal activation. Homologous temperature \\(T/T_m\\) divides by a specified melting temperature \\(T_m\\) . Comparing at equal \\(T\\) asks what happens in the same furnace; comparing at equal \\(T/T_m\\) asks about similar proximity to melting. These answer different questions. Alloy solidus and liquidus can differ, so even the normalization requires a convention. Empirical trends across face-centered, body-centered, and close-packed hexagonal metals can organize observations, but packing fraction does not uniquely determine transport. A vacancy-mediated tracer coefficient contains vacancy population, jump mobility, jump geometry, and correlation. An apparently more open lattice can still have an unfavorable formation free energy or migration saddle. Source numerical melting-point tables are not reproduced here because their caption exponents are inconsistent; the chapter's numerical examples are independent constructions. For a uniaxial crystal, symmetry permits \\(\\mathsf D=\\operatorname{diag}(D_\\perp,D_\\perp,D_\\parallel)\\) in axes aligned with its special direction. A unit measurement direction \\(\\mathbf n\\) has projected diffusivity \\(D_n=\\mathbf n^T\\mathsf D\\mathbf n\\) . If its angle to the axis is \\(\\vartheta\\) , this becomes \\(D_\\perp\\sin^2\\vartheta+D_\\parallel\\cos^2\\vartheta\\) . Different exchange rates and correlations can contribute to the two tensor components. Structural and magnetic transitions can alter those ingredients; an Arrhenius fit spanning a transition should not be interpreted as one unchanged mechanism. 23.2 Competing defect channels produce an apparent enthalpy L49 08:31 Suppose independent monovacancy and divacancy channels contribute \\(D_1,D_2\\) to one tracer coefficient: \\(D^*=D_1+D_2\\) . For dilute equilibrium vacancies, let \\(H_f\\) be the molar monovacancy formation enthalpy, \\(H_b>0\\) the energy released on binding two vacancies, and \\(H_{m2}\\) the divacancy migration enthalpy, all in Jmol⁻¹. Ignoring temperature-dependent prefactors and correlation derivatives, the divacancy channel has activation enthalpy \\[Q_2=2H_f-H_b+H_{m2}.\\tag{23.1}\\] The formation of two vacancies costs \\(2H_f\\) ; favorable binding reduces the pair formation cost; migration adds its saddle cost. A larger equilibrium fraction of associated vacancies does not automatically make pairs the dominant diffusion carrier, because their migration rates and correlation efficiencies may differ. The population and the displacement per population are distinct factors. For channels \\(D_j=A_je^{-Q_j/(RT)}\\) with positive constant prefactors \\(A_j\\) , molar gas constant \\(R\\) , and fixed \\(Q_j\\) , differentiate their sum with respect to inverse temperature. Since \\(dD_j/d(1/T)=-(Q_j/R)D_j\\) , the apparent activation enthalpy is \\[Q_{\\mathrm{app}}=-R\\frac{d\\ln D^*}{d(1/T)}=\\frac{\\sum_jD_jQ_j}{\\sum_jD_j}.\\tag{23.2}\\] This is a contribution-weighted average, bounded by the smallest and largest channel enthalpies under these assumptions. Its weights change with temperature. Curvature in an Arrhenius plot can therefore arise from competing ordinary channels without requiring a single barrier to change continuously. Temperature-dependent correlations, prefactors, phase fractions, or defect sources require additional derivative terms and can invalidate the simple bounds. The synthetic laboratory uses \\((A_1,Q_1)=(10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}},180\\,\\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}})\\) and \\((A_2,Q_2)=(10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}},220\\,\\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}})\\) . At1000K, it obtains \\(D_1=3.96231\\times10^{-15}\\) and \\(D_2=3.22555\\times10^{-15}\\) m²s⁻¹, giving \\(Q_{\\mathrm{app}}=197.950\\) kJmol⁻¹. A central finite difference in inverse temperature independently reproduces this slope to the printed precision. Figure23.1. Two fixed channels produce a temperature-dependent apparent enthalpy. No material transition or changing microscopic barrier was inserted. 23.3 Interstitial mobility and trapped storage L49 19:52 An interstitial solute occupies a site between the host lattice sites. In the dilute independent-jump limit, a direct interstitial does not need an equilibrium vacancy population before it can move. Its coefficient can therefore involve primarily migration, geometry, and correlation rather than the sum of vacancy formation and migration enthalpies. Setting tracer correlation factor to one additionally assumes statistically independent equivalent jumps and available destination sites. Exclusion, interactions, or traps can violate those assumptions even for the same chemical species. Hydrogen illustrates why mobility, solubility, and apparent permeation must be separated. Lattice diffusivity describes motion of a mobile hydrogen population. Solubility describes how much is accommodated under specified chemical potential and phase conditions. A permeation experiment also involves entry and exit surfaces, trapping, specimen dimensions, and sometimes evolving microstructure. Rapid lattice motion alone cannot rank embrittlement severity, which depends on where hydrogen accumulates and how it interacts with stress and fracture processes. Isotope substitution can probe mass dependence, but a classical square-root mass argument is not universal when quantum effects or different trapping populations matter. Consider an original dilute reversible-trap model. Let \\(c_m\\) and \\(c_t\\) be mobile and trapped molar concentrations. Suppose traps are immobile and equilibrate rapidly with \\(c_t=Kc_m\\) , where constant dimensionless \\(K\\geq0\\) includes trap density and association. The total is \\(c_{\\mathrm{tot}}=(1+K)c_m\\) , but only mobile atoms carry flux \\(J=-D_m\\partial_xc_m\\) . Conservation then gives \\[\\partial_tc_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\frac{D_m}{1+K}\\partial_x^2c_{\\mathrm{tot}},\\qquad D_{\\mathrm{app}}=D_m/(1+K).\\tag{23.3}\\] The apparent coefficient is reduced by storage, not by an assumed reduction in every mobile jump rate. Synthetic \\(D_m=10^{-9}\\) m²s⁻¹ and \\(K=99\\) give \\(D_{\\mathrm{app}}=10^{-11}\\) m²s⁻¹. Saturation makes \\(c_t(c_m)\\) nonlinear, slow exchange introduces an additional time scale, and irreversible trapping destroys the local-equilibrium reduction. Report which model a fitted coefficient represents. 23.4 Solubility and dilute-solute jump networks L51 00:21 A substitutional solid solution places solute atoms on host lattice sites; an interstitial solution places them between those sites. Solubility is an equilibrium phase-stability question. Hume–Rothery size, crystal-structure, electronegativity, and valence considerations are useful empirical heuristics for substitutional compatibility, not sufficient conditions for a stable phase. Full free energies and competing phases determine a phase boundary. Similarly, the largest empty geometric void need not be the lowest-free-energy interstitial site: local strain and chemical bonding matter. Carbon site preference in iron cannot be settled by drawing hard spheres alone. A dilute impurity coefficient is defined by the limit of vanishing solute concentration in a specified host phase. “Impurity” does not require an unwanted addition or a universal concentration threshold. Dilution suppresses solute–solute interactions but leaves solute–vacancy binding and the correlated return of a neighboring vacancy. For an FCC substitutional solute, the five-frequency model names five classes of vacancy exchange: \\(\\omega_0\\) far from solute, \\(\\omega_1\\) rotation within its neighbor shell, \\(\\omega_2\\) direct solute exchange, \\(\\omega_3\\) dissociation from the shell, and \\(\\omega_4\\) association into it. Each rate is in s⁻¹ with its specified per-exchange convention. Solute transport depends on neighbor probability, exchange frequency, jump length, and the probability that subsequent motion undoes a preceding displacement. Binding favors vacancy occupancy near the solute, but it can also favor repeated backtracking. The correlation factor depends on rate ratios, not just on binding free energy. At equilibrium, association and dissociation must satisfy detailed balance after neighbor multiplicities and state energies are included; the rates cannot be fitted as wholly unconstrained numbers. Thus stronger binding is not a general proof of faster tracer diffusion. Chapter8 develops the correlation structure; here the point is that equilibrium enrichment and transport efficiency play different roles. Historical Lazarus–Le Claire electrostatic models approximate how a solute's valence difference changes vacancy interactions and saddle energies through a screened electronic potential. Effective charge in such a metallic model is not a literal unscreened ionic charge placed in a vacuum. Size effects, relaxation, transition-metal electronic structure, and prefactor changes can defeat a valence-only prediction. These models are useful as explicit hypotheses with limited chemical domains. A successful qualitative trend does not license extrapolation to every host or pressure range; no unverified source table is used here to assign numerical accuracy. 23.5 A tiny fast population can dominate flux L51 21:52 A hybrid solute can occupy substitutional and interstitial states. A schematic dissociative reaction is \\(B_s\\rightleftharpoons B_i+V\\) : a substitutional B atom leaves its lattice site to occupy an interstitial site, creating a vacancy. Proper mass action uses dimensionless activities and specified sublattice normalizations. A vacancy concentration is not interchangeable with the concentration of interstitial B atoms. Let \\(c_s,c_i\\) be the two B concentrations and \\(D_s,D_i\\) their state-specific diffusion coefficients. Suppose exchange between states is rapid on the profile-evolution time scale, defects are buffered, and the fractions \\(\\theta_i=c_i/(c_s+c_i)\\) and \\(\\theta_s=1-\\theta_i\\) are spatially constant. Add the two fluxes, substitute \\(c_j=\\theta_jc_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\) , and obtain \\[J_B=-D_{\\mathrm{hyb}}\\partial_xc_{\\mathrm{tot}},\\qquad D_{\\mathrm{hyb}}=\\theta_iD_i+(1-\\theta_i)D_s.\\tag{23.4}\\] With synthetic \\(\\theta_i=10^{-6},D_i=10^{-8}\\) m²s⁻¹ and \\(D_s=10^{-17}\\) m²s⁻¹, the result is \\(1.00100\\times10^{-14}\\) m²s⁻¹. The interstitial population supplies approximately99.9001% of this coefficient while representing only one part per million of the solute. Spectroscopy that detects mostly substitutional B would therefore not establish that substitutional jumps dominate its flux. If vacancy activity varies in space, the fractions can vary too. Differentiating \\(c_i=\\theta_i c_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\) then produces an extra term proportional to \\(c_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\partial_x\\theta_i\\) ; (23.4) alone no longer describes the complete flux. Slow state exchange requires coupled reaction–diffusion equations. Semiconductor processing in Chapter25 makes these source and sink limitations especially consequential. A dilute composition expansion is another local statement. With solute mole fraction \\(N\\) , write \\(D(N)=D(0)[1+b_1N+O(N^2)]\\) . The coefficient \\(b_1\\) is dimensionless for this fraction convention. An exponential fit \\(D(0)e^{b_1N}\\) has the same first derivative at zero but assumes particular higher-order terms. Synthetic \\(b_1=5,N=0.02\\) gives factors1.1 and1.10517. Agreement at small concentration is not evidence for the same broad-range physics. 23.6 Laboratory, exercises, and worked solutions The executable laboratory evaluates the models in their stated units, differentiates the two-channel sum numerically, and writes actual output . The finite-difference apparent enthalpy differs from its analytic value by less than one part per million. This tests the mathematical reduction for synthetic constants; it does not validate a particular metal's mechanism. Pair activation. Compute \\(Q_2\\) for synthetic \\(H_f=100,H_b=30,H_{m2}=60\\) kJmol⁻¹. Solution Equation(23.1) gives \\(2(100)-30+60=230\\) kJmol⁻¹. Binding enters with a minus sign because it releases energy. Reversing the sign convention for binding without changing the equation would incorrectly increase the pair formation cost. Apparent-slope bounds. Prove the bounds for (23.2), then name a way they can fail experimentally. Solution The weights \\(w_j=D_j/\\sum_kD_k\\) are positive and sum to one, so \\(\\min Q_j\\leq\\sum_jw_jQ_j\\leq\\max Q_j\\) . If a prefactor or correlation factor varies with temperature, its logarithmic derivative adds to the apparent slope. A structural transition or nonequilibrium defect population can likewise defeat the fixed-channel assumptions. A measured slope outside the proposed bounds rejects that simple model, not arithmetic itself. Trap saturation. If local equilibrium gives \\(c_t=C_TK_ac_m/(1+K_ac_m)\\) , derive the differential capacity. Solution Here \\(C_T\\) is trap capacity in molm⁻³ and \\(K_a\\) has units m³mol⁻¹. Differentiate total concentration: \\(dc_{\\mathrm{tot}}/dc_m=1+C_TK_a/(1+K_ac_m)^2\\) . Conservation becomes this capacity times \\(\\partial_tc_m=D_m\\partial_x^2c_m\\) . The capacity approaches one as traps saturate. It is generally incorrect to replace it by one fixed \\(1+K\\) over the whole concentration range. Hybrid dominance. Find the condition for the interstitial channel to contribute more than the substitutional channel. Solution Require \\(\\theta_iD_i>(1-\\theta_i)D_s\\) , or \\(\\theta_i>D_s/(D_i+D_s)\\) . For a very large mobility contrast, this threshold can be extremely small. Dominance of the detected population and dominance of transport are therefore different questions. B2 diffraction check. For elastic scattering, let \\(f_A,f_B\\) be the complex single-atom scattering amplitudes, with \\(i^2=-1\\) defining the imaginary unit. At reciprocal vector \\(\\mathbf q=(2\\pi/a_0)(h,k,l)\\), with integer Miller indices \\(h,k,l\\) and cubic lattice parameter \\(a_0\\), an atom at fractional coordinate \\(\\mathbf r_j=(x_j,y_j,z_j)\\) contributes amplitude \\(f_j e^{2\\pi i(hx_j+ky_j+lz_j)}\\). Add these amplitudes for the B2 basis at0 and \\((1/2,1/2,1/2)\\), and identify a superlattice example. Solution The origin contributes \\(f_A\\); the body-center basis atom contributes \\(f_Be^{i\\pi(h+k+l)}\\). Their sum is \\(F_{hkl}=f_A+f_Be^{i\\pi(h+k+l)}\\). Even index sums give \\(f_A+f_B\\); odd sums give \\(f_A-f_B\\). The100 reflection can reveal chemical order when the species scatter differently. The110 index sum is even and is therefore allowed in the unlabeled body-centered structure, contrary to the captioned tutorial statement. With indistinguishable basis scattering, odd reflections vanish. Concentration units. Rewrite the dilute linear factor using atomic percent \\(p=100N\\) . Solution Substitute \\(N=p/100\\) to obtain \\(1+(b_1/100)p+O(p^2)\\) , with the higher-order coefficient also rescaled. Quoting the same numerical slope for a fraction and a percent creates a factor100 error. Always state which composition coordinate the derivative uses. The models show why a single rate ranking is rarely a mechanism explanation. A useful interpretation specifies the carrier populations, their mobility and memory, the concentration being measured, and the state of the material during the experiment."}, {"number": 24, "title": "High-entropy alloys and the evidence for diffusion claims", "url": "chapter-24.html", "text": "“Sluggish diffusion” is a testable comparison only after the tracer, phase, temperature, and reference material are named. Counting alloying elements does not supply those controls. A multicomponent alloy can have a high mixing entropy and still possess a fast diffusion path, a low melting range, or an Arrhenius crossover that reverses its ranking. Source spine: L52, with ideal-entropy calculations connected to the L55 tutorial. The entropy proof and Arrhenius examples below are original derivations. The experimental boundary example is separately identified and linked to its primary paper; the plotted laboratory curves are synthetic. 24.1 What high configurational entropy establishes L52 00:20 A multi-principal-element alloy contains several components in substantial proportions rather than one overwhelmingly dominant solvent. “High-entropy alloy” is used with composition-based or ideal-entropy-based conventions. The label describes a design space, not a theorem that every member is a single-phase random solution. Define \\(N_i\\geq0\\) as component mole fractions satisfying \\(\\sum_{i=1}^nN_i=1\\) , where \\(n\\) is the number of included species. For an ideal substitutional solution with independently occupied equivalent sites, counting arrangements gives molar configurational mixing entropy \\[\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{mix}}=-R\\sum_{i=1}^nN_i\\ln N_i,\\qquad 0\\ln0:=0.\\tag{24.1}\\] Here \\(R\\) is the molar gas constant in Jmol⁻¹K⁻¹. The limiting convention follows from \\(x\\ln x\\to0\\) as \\(x\\to0+\\) . The independent-site assumption matters: chemical ordering or correlations change the configurational entropy relative to this ideal expression. To maximize (24.1) for fixed component count, use a Lagrange multiplier for the sum constraint. Stationarity of \\(-\\sum_iN_i\\ln N_i+\\lambda(\\sum_iN_i-1)\\) gives \\(-\\ln N_i-1+\\lambda=0\\) ; hence all nonzero interior fractions are equal. The Hessian is diagonal with negative entries \\(-1/N_i\\) , so the function is strictly concave and the stationary point is the global maximum. Thus \\[\\Delta S_{\\max}=R\\ln n,\\qquad N_i=1/n.\\tag{24.2}\\] The laboratory computes \\(R\\ln5=13.3816\\) Jmol⁻¹K⁻¹. The synthetic fractions0.4,0.15,0.15,0.15,0.15 give12.5115Jmol⁻¹K⁻¹. More equal occupation increases ideal entropy for fixed species count. This calculation says nothing yet about vacancy formation, migration barriers, or the diffusion coefficient. Phase stability requires comparing competing molar Gibbs free energies \\(G=H-TS\\) , where \\(H\\) is molar enthalpy, \\(S\\) molar entropy, and \\(T\\) absolute temperature. A large ideal mixing entropy alone does not prove that a disordered solution beats an ordered compound or a phase-separated state. The lecture discusses four proposed effects: entropy stabilization, local lattice distortion, nonadditive composition-dependent properties often called a cocktail effect, and comparatively slow diffusion. The first concerns competing free energies; distortion concerns local atomic environments; nonadditivity concerns a chosen property; sluggishness requires a specified kinetic comparison. They are hypotheses or observations to test in named systems. Equation(24.2) proves none of them as a universal kinetic law. 24.2 Match the coefficient before matching the materials L52 12:42 A tracer experiment follows one named species in a chemically uniform matrix. An interdiffusion experiment follows a composition field containing several interacting species. A grain-boundary B-tail experiment can identify \\(P=s_b\\delta D_b\\) rather than \\(D_b\\) alone. These measurements can all involve the same alloy while answering different questions. Lower interdiffusion need not mean lower tracer mobility: thermodynamic factors and cross-coupled fluxes can change the relation between them. For a multicomponent solution, the flux of species \\(i\\) can depend on several chemical-potential gradients. One illustrative linear-response form is \\(\\mathbf J_i=-\\sum_jL_{ij}\\nabla(\\mu_j/T)\\) in a specified frame, where \\(\\mu_j\\) is molar chemical potential and \\(L_{ij}\\) are phenomenological coefficients with units set by the flux and force convention. Independent composition coordinates are required because fractions sum to one. A pseudo-binary couple designed to keep spectator concentrations nearly constant can simplify inference, but measured deviations from that condition must be checked. A pseudo-binary fit does not automatically identify a complete multicomponent mobility matrix. Radiotracer sectioning, stable-isotope SIMS, electron-probe composition measurements, atomistic simulations, and phase-field or continuum calculations supply different observables. A simulation can estimate hop statistics or profile evolution only within its Hamiltonian and dynamical assumptions. A composition profile includes instrument resolution and calibration. A tracer experiment samples the species chosen and the paths populated during its anneal. Agreement among methods is strongest when the forward models predict the actual measured quantity rather than compare coefficients that merely share units. Barrier distributions add another distinction. A chemically diverse neighborhood can raise some barriers and lower others. Averaging barriers and then exponentiating is generally different from averaging activated rates. For parallel independent channels with common attempt scale, \\(\\langle e^{-E/(k_BT)}\\rangle\\geq e^{-\\langle E\\rangle/(k_BT)}\\) by convexity, where \\(E\\) is a per-event barrier and \\(k_B\\) Boltzmann's constant. A real connected network can instead be limited by serial bottlenecks and return correlations. This inequality exposes why one average barrier is insufficient; it does not by itself predict the effective diffusivity of a random alloy. 24.3 An Arrhenius crossover reverses a ranking L52 20:50 Let two tracer coefficients be \\(D_A=A_Ae^{-Q_A/(RT)}\\) and \\(D_B=A_Be^{-Q_B/(RT)}\\) , with positive prefactors in m²s⁻¹ and constant molar enthalpies in Jmol⁻¹. Their ratio obeys \\[\\ln(D_A/D_B)=\\ln(A_A/A_B)-\\frac{Q_A-Q_B}{RT}.\\tag{24.3}\\] Setting the ratio to one gives \\(T_c=(Q_A-Q_B)/[R\\ln(A_A/A_B)]\\) when the denominator is nonzero. A physical positive crossover exists only if numerator and denominator have the same sign. Equal prefactors with unequal enthalpies have no finite crossover; equal enthalpies give a temperature-independent ratio. The formula presumes both phases and mechanisms remain valid at the calculated temperature. Choose synthetic A with prefactor \\(10^{-6}\\) m²s⁻¹ and enthalpy180kJmol⁻¹, and B with \\(10^{-8}\\) m²s⁻¹ and140kJmol⁻¹. The computed crossover is1044.67K. At800K, A gives \\(1.76781\\times10^{-18}\\) m²s⁻¹ and B \\(7.22964\\times10^{-18}\\) m²s⁻¹. At1200K, A gives \\(1.46203\\times10^{-14}\\) m²s⁻¹ and B \\(8.05522\\times10^{-15}\\) m²s⁻¹. The ranking reverses although each coefficient remains an ordinary Arrhenius law. Figure24.1. Original synthetic comparison. These curves are not fits to the cited high-entropy-alloy data. Now assign synthetic melting temperatures1500K to A and2000K to B. At equal homologous temperature0.6, compare A at900K with B at1200K. Their coefficients are \\(3.57500\\times10^{-17}\\) and \\(8.05522\\times10^{-15}\\) m²s⁻¹. This is a different experiment from a shared-temperature anneal. Both comparisons are legitimate when their question and temperature convention are explicit. 24.4 Boundary evidence and a falsifiable comparison L52 15:43 A primary Ni radiotracer study compares CoCrFeNi and CoCrFeMnNi using B- and C-regime grain-boundary measurements. It reports a crossover near800K: the five-component alloy is slower below it and faster above it. The primary locators are Vaidya etal., PDF pages5–8, Eq.(3), Table1, and Figures6–8 ; the published article is Scientific Reports7,12293 . This is species- and alloy-specific evidence against a universal monotonic component-count rule, not a rate law for all high-entropy alloys. The same experiment illustrates an inference chain. B conditions identify a triple product; C conditions identify a boundary coefficient. Comparing them requires a common boundary-state model and temperature extrapolation. A local atom-probe observation can constrain segregation in the sampled boundaries, with finite sampling and sensitivity limits. Absence of detectable segregation simplifies conversion between a product and a coefficient; it is not a prerequisite for studying boundary diffusion in general. Boundary energies inferred through a diffusion correlation or model are estimates, not direct calorimetric measurements. To test a broader sluggishness claim, define a null hypothesis and an observable before selecting a normalization. For example: at each specified absolute temperature, a named Ni tracer coefficient in a single FCC phase is lower in alloy B than in alloy A by more than the combined measurement uncertainty. Keep the source protocol, specimen preparation, grain-size distribution, impurity content, and profile analysis comparable. Verify phase identity before and after annealing. If a boundary quantity is measured, characterize boundary geometry and segregation rather than silently calling the result bulk diffusion. A useful experiment spans temperatures on both sides of a possible crossover and includes repeated specimens. Report uncertainties on slopes and intercepts together: they are often correlated, and a crossover obtained far outside the measured range can be poorly constrained. A negative result is scientifically informative when it specifies which universal assertion it rejects. It does not imply that no individual multicomponent alloy ever has slow diffusion or useful kinetic stability. The reproducible laboratory calculates ideal entropies, the synthetic crossover, and both temperature comparisons, then checks equality of the two rates at the computed crossover. Its output contains all input parameters. It does not digitize published curves or estimate confidence intervals from missing raw data. The primary paper provides the experimental counterexample, while the code isolates the algebra behind a ranking reversal. 24.5 Exercises and worked solutions Entropy deficit. Express the difference between maximum ideal entropy and that of a general composition as a nonnegative quantity. Solution Subtract (24.1) from \\(R\\ln n\\) : \\(\\Delta S_{\\max}-\\Delta S=R\\sum_iN_i\\ln(N_i/(1/n))\\) . This is \\(R\\) times the relative entropy of the composition distribution to the uniform distribution. Its nonnegativity follows from concavity of entropy, already established by the negative Hessian. Equality requires equiatomic composition. The identity quantifies ideal compositional unevenness; it does not quantify diffusion retardation. No crossover. What happens if two Arrhenius coefficients have equal activation enthalpies? Solution Equation(24.3) reduces to \\(\\ln(D_A/D_B)=\\ln(A_A/A_B)\\) . The ratio is constant. Unequal prefactors yield no crossover; equal prefactors make the coefficients identical throughout the assumed common domain. A numerical routine that divides by a vanishing enthalpy difference without treating this case misrepresents the model. Coefficient mismatch. A smaller B-tail triple product is offered as proof of smaller bulk tracer diffusion. Diagnose the inference. Solution The product \\(s_b\\delta D_b\\) measures a boundary pathway with segregation and effective width. Bulk tracer diffusion is a different coefficient. A smaller product can arise from smaller boundary mobility, segregation capacity, or width without any specified change in bulk mobility. Measure the bulk branch or an independent bulk tracer specimen and compare like quantities. Barrier averaging. Explain why the convexity inequality in Section24.2 does not prove disorder always accelerates diffusion. Solution The inequality averages independent rates with a shared prefactor and parallel interpretation. Long-range diffusion also requires connected paths and includes bottlenecks, occupancy probabilities, and correlated returns. A low barrier into a deep trap can be followed by a rare escape and poor net displacement. A barrier distribution alone omits state energies and network topology. Those missing objects determine whether a rate average represents transport. Test scope. What universal statement can the primary Ni boundary crossover reject, and what statement does it leave open? Solution It rejects a claim that simply adding a principal component must always lower the same boundary transport coefficient at every temperature. It leaves open whether a particular species in another phase or alloy has low diffusion over a specified temperature interval. It also leaves open bulk or interdiffusion comparisons until their own observables and data are examined. The conclusion should have the same scope as the measurement. Experimental design. Propose an observable that would distinguish reduced tracer mobility from a reduced thermodynamic driving force in an interdiffusion comparison. Solution Measure species tracer diffusion in uniform compositions spanning the couple, and obtain independently assessed chemical-potential derivatives for the same phase and temperature. Compare these with composition-profile evolution in an explicit multicomponent transport model. The tracer experiment constrains motion without the chemical-composition gradient; thermodynamic data constrain the force. Additional cross-correlation information may still be required to identify the full kinetic matrix. One scalar profile cannot generally supply every matrix entry. A defensible comparison names its observable and controls before assigning a material-class explanation. Mixing entropy is a well-defined thermodynamic quantity; a diffusion ranking is a conditional experimental result."}, {"number": 25, "title": "Defect-mediated diffusion in semiconductors", "url": "chapter-25.html", "text": "A dopant profile can change when the electronic state changes, even at fixed temperature and unchanged crystal geometry. It can also change when oxidation injects point defects or implantation creates damage. Treating semiconductor diffusion as one immutable material constant can therefore confuse electronic equilibrium, atomic mobility, and the supply of defects that make motion possible. Source spine: L53–55. Charged-defect population laws, balanced reaction models, and source–sink calculations are developed below. The oxygen diffusivity table was checked against the L55 source frame; its extrapolation remains an exercise-model prediction. The source's printed oxygen-solubility units are not adopted as validated physical number densities. 25.1 Establish the electronic variables first L53 00:21 A semiconductor has occupied and unoccupied electronic states separated by an energy gap. Let \\(E_C\\) and \\(E_V\\) denote conduction- and valence-band edges, and \\(E_g=E_C-E_V>0\\) the gap. An electron in the conduction band carries negative elementary charge; a hole represents a missing valence-band electron and carries positive charge in the band-transport description. The Fermi level \\(E_F\\) is the electronic chemical potential. All energies in the following expressions use one common reference and are in joules per particle, unless electronvolts are explicitly stated. A donor tends to supply an electron and become positively ionized; an acceptor tends to receive an electron and become negatively ionized. Their ionization, compensation, and activation determine free-carrier densities. Chemically incorporated dopant concentration is therefore not identical to electrically active dopant concentration. Precipitates, complexes, and inactive sites can contain dopant atoms without producing the carrier population inferred from the nominal dose. Under thermal equilibrium and nondegenerate band statistics, define effective state densities \\(N_C,N_V\\) , in m⁻³. Electron and hole number densities are \\[n=N_Ce^{-(E_C-E_F)/(k_BT)},\\qquad p=N_Ve^{-(E_F-E_V)/(k_BT)},\\qquad np=n_i^2=N_CN_Ve^{-E_g/(k_BT)}.\\tag{25.1}\\] Here \\(k_B\\) is Boltzmann's constant and \\(T\\) absolute temperature. Multiplication cancels \\(E_F\\) ; taking the square root gives the intrinsic density. Degenerate doping and carrier injection can invalidate these simple exponentials or the common-equilibrium mass-action relation. These conditions must be checked before replacing a Fermi-level dependence by a power of \\(n/n_i\\) . Silicon and germanium have tetrahedrally coordinated diamond structures, but their atomic defects can have multiple charge states and relaxed geometries. Diffusion masks and surface processing provide boundary conditions, not just drawings of a concentration step. A high-temperature barrier must survive the actual thermal and chemical environment; a temporary lithographic resist should not be assumed to be such a barrier. The models here explain transport physics rather than prescribe a fabrication recipe. 25.2 A charged defect exchanges electrons with a reservoir L53 05:29 Let \\(X^q\\) denote an atomic defect with effective charge \\(qe\\) , where \\(e>0\\) is elementary charge and signed integer \\(q\\) counts missing electrons relative to the neutral state. With other atomic reservoirs fixed, write its formation free energy as \\(G_f^q(E_F)=G_f^q(0)+qE_F\\) . This convention makes a positive charge more expensive as the electronic chemical potential rises. In a dilute equilibrium model, its dimensionless site fraction is proportional to its Boltzmann weight: \\[x_X^q=A_X^q\\exp\\left[-\\frac{G_f^q(0)+qE_F}{k_BT}\\right].\\tag{25.2}\\] The factor \\(A_X^q\\) represents the chosen site normalization and degeneracy factors not already included in the free energy. Negative charge states become more favorable as \\(E_F\\) rises. This changes the number of available atomic carriers. It does not say that their migration barriers or correlation factors are the same in every charge state. For an illustrative vacancy-mediated tracer model, write \\(D^*=\\sum_qx_V^qB_V^q+\\sum_qx_I^qB_I^q\\) , where \\(V\\) denotes vacancies, \\(I\\) self-interstitials, and each \\(B\\) has units m²s⁻¹ per unit defect fraction. It packages the atom-displacement geometry, defect mobility, and tracer correlations for the specified channel. Defining this convention avoids multiplying a number density by a diffusivity and incorrectly calling the result m²s⁻¹. A restricted Ge-style model retains neutral and singly negative vacancy contributions. Refer the charged contribution to the intrinsic Fermi level \\(E_{Fi}\\) . Equation(25.1) gives \\(n/n_i=e^{(E_F-E_{Fi})/(k_BT)}\\) when the band model and intrinsic reference are consistent. Equation(25.2) then gives \\[D^*(n)=D_{V^0}^*+(n/n_i)D_{V^-;i}^*.\\tag{25.3}\\] The second coefficient is the negative-vacancy contribution at intrinsic electronic conditions, not the entire intrinsic diffusivity. At \\(n=n_i\\) , the intrinsic total is the sum of both terms. Additional negative charge states would introduce higher powers only under analogous equilibrium assumptions. Synthetic coefficients \\(10^{-18}\\) and \\(2\\times10^{-18}\\) m²s⁻¹ give \\(2.1\\times10^{-17}\\) m²s⁻¹ at \\(n/n_i=10\\) . This is a demonstration of sensitivity, not a calibrated Ge fit or a universal law for Si. 25.3 Defect equilibrium requires sources and sinks L53 12:42 A highly perfect single crystal can have few internal sources or sinks for atomic defects. Surface ambient, oxide growth, implantation damage, interfaces, and dislocations can then control populations over the process time. Oxidation can inject self-interstitials, while implantation can produce excess vacancies and interstitials and extended damage. Temperature fixes an equilibrium target; it does not guarantee that the specimen reaches that target before the diffusion experiment ends. Let \\(x(t)\\) be a dimensionless supersaturation of one defect population relative to its equilibrium value. An original well-mixed source–sink model is \\(\\dot x=G-k(x-1)\\) , where source strength \\(G\\) and removal coefficient \\(k>0\\) are in s⁻¹. For constant inputs and initial value \\(x(0)=1\\) , integrating the linear equation gives \\[x(t)=1+(G/k)(1-e^{-kt}).\\tag{25.4}\\] The stationary supersaturation is \\(1+G/k\\) , not one when the source persists. Synthetic \\(G=0.09\\) s⁻¹ and \\(k=0.01\\) s⁻¹ give \\(x(100\\,\\mathrm s)=6.68909\\) . If a dilute diffusion channel scales with this population, its contribution is enhanced even with unchanged single-defect mobility. Turning the source off leaves an exponential return toward equilibrium. Changing sink density can change this relaxation and hence the observed dopant profile. Spatially varying injection needs defect diffusion and reactions as well: \\(\\partial_tn_I=-\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf j_I+S_I-\\mathcal R_I\\) , where \\(n_I\\) is defect number density, \\(\\mathbf j_I\\) number flux, and source and removal terms have units m⁻³s⁻¹. Vacancy–interstitial recombination couples the equations. Electrostatic fields and charged-species chemical potentials may be required if local neutrality is not an adequate approximation. A constant-D Fick profile is a restricted reduction of this larger system. A primary first-principles study explicitly treats dopant mechanisms with equilibrium and nonequilibrium point-defect populations: Nichols, Van de Walle, and Pantelides, Physical Review B40,5484, abstract . The accessible abstract supports the importance of both vacancy and interstitial channels; no numerical transition barrier or uninspected equation from its full article is used here. For Si, mechanism fractions must therefore be tied to dopant, temperature, electronic conditions, and processing history. Figure25.1. Electronic population control and nonequilibrium defect supply are distinct routes to changed atomic transport. Both panels use declared synthetic parameters. 25.4 Dopant pairs must conserve atoms and charge L54 16:43 Let \\(A_s\\) denote a substitutional dopant, \\(V\\) a native vacancy, and \\(I\\) a host self-interstitial. Define \\(P_V\\) as a dopant–vacancy complex and \\(P_I\\) as a dopant–self-interstitial complex. The notation avoids calling both an interstitial dopant atom and a two-defect complex “AI.” Pair formation can be written \\(A_s+V\\rightleftharpoons P_V\\) or \\(A_s+I\\rightleftharpoons P_I\\) . The products contain the dopant and the specified native defect. For a charged reaction, either the pair charge equals the reactant charge sum or electronic carriers appear explicitly. For example, \\(A_s^0+V^-\\rightleftharpoons P_V^-\\) conserves charge as written. A reaction yielding neutral \\(P_V^0\\) instead must release an electron on the product side. Charge balance cannot be recovered by changing only the equilibrium constant after writing an unbalanced reaction. A mass-action rate for one neutral bookkeeping example is \\(r=k_fn_sn_I-k_rn_P\\) , where the number densities are in m⁻³, association coefficient \\(k_f\\) in m³s⁻¹, and dissociation coefficient \\(k_r\\) in s⁻¹. Reaction contributions are \\(\\dot n_s=-r,\\dot n_I=-r,\\dot n_P=r\\) . Hence \\(n_s+n_P\\) conserves dopant and \\(n_I+n_P\\) conserves the tracked excess-interstitial inventory in this closed subsystem. Diffusion, generation, and annihilation add corresponding flux and source terms. Strong attraction increases pair occupancy but can also cause reversible local shuttling. Long-range dopant displacement requires translation of the complex, exchange into new configurations, or decorrelation through partial dissociation. A total diffusivity obtained by summing channels needs a consistent coarse graining that does not double-count the same jump sequence. The laboratory integrates a dimensionless version of the closed reaction equations and checks both conserved sums to rounding accuracy; it does not assign fitted pair rates to a named dopant. 25.5 Hybrid solutes probe the supply of native defects L54 22:54 A substitutional dopant may become an interstitial dopant by a dissociative reaction \\(A_s\\rightleftharpoons A_i+V\\) . A kick-out reaction instead uses a host self-interstitial: \\(A_s+I\\rightleftharpoons A_i\\) , with restoration of the regular host atom implicit in the lattice-site bookkeeping. The second expression conserves the excess-atom count: the host interstitial fills the regular site as the dopant is displaced. Charge states and electrons or holes must be added consistently for a particular material. For rapid state exchange and spatially constant fractions, total dopant flux reduces to \\(J_A=-[\\theta_iD_i+(1-\\theta_i)D_s]\\partial_xc_A\\) . Here \\(c_A\\) is total dissolved dopant molar concentration and \\(\\theta_i\\) its interstitial fraction. A tiny fraction can dominate through a large \\(D_i/D_s\\) . This is the same reduction derived in Chapter23; its applicability is especially sensitive to native defects in semiconductors. If interstitial supply is restricted, the conversion reaction can become rate-limiting. If vacancy activity varies, the local partition between \\(A_s\\) and \\(A_i\\) varies. Then separate dopant-state and defect equations are needed. A dependence on dislocation density or surface condition is a useful diagnostic of source–sink kinetics, but it does not uniquely identify dissociative versus kick-out transport without additional constraints. Distinct microscopic models can produce the same reduced coefficient under one annealing condition. 25.6 Solubility, oxygen, and gettering concern different inventories L54 00:22 Retrograde solubility describes an equilibrium phase boundary with a solubility maximum below the host melting range, followed by decreasing solubility on further heating over some interval. A single Arrhenius expression with a fixed positive dissolution enthalpy is monotone and cannot describe the entire curve. Total incorporated dopant, dissolved dopant, and electrically active dopant are different inventories. Crossing a solubility boundary can redistribute material among them without conserving the active fraction. Oxygen can enter silicon during Czochralski growth through interaction with the quartz environment. Subsequent thermal treatment can precipitate oxygen-containing phases in selected regions. Intrinsic gettering uses internal sinks associated with such structures to concentrate metallic contaminants away from sensitive device regions. Gettering redistributes impurities; it does not annihilate their atoms, guarantee capture of every species, or make precipitates harmless wherever they form. Sink location, capacity, binding, and the diffusion time to reach them all matter. The L55 oxygen exercise supplies diffusivities at600,650,700,750,800,850°C: respectively \\(3.24\\times10^{-20},2.00\\times10^{-19},1.03\\times10^{-18},4.48\\times10^{-18},1.71\\times10^{-17},5.77\\times10^{-17}\\) m²s⁻¹. These entries were checked at the source frame1248s. Fitting \\(\\ln[D/(1\\,\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}})]=a+b/T\\) after converting to kelvin gives \\(b=-29363.9\\) K and \\(a=-11.2460\\) . The corresponding molar enthalpy \\(-Rb\\) is244.145kJmol⁻¹, and extrapolation to1000°C gives \\(D=1.25703\\times10^{-15}\\) m²s⁻¹. This is a fit to the lecture exercise, not independent validation of oxygen transport at that higher temperature. The same slide labels solubility as m⁻³ while listing very small negative-exponent values. The later calculation carries that convention into a sub-unit number density. Without the original data provenance, these units and values do not justify a physical oxygen-solubility prediction. A transcription must not silently replace exponents or convert an unspecified fraction into a number density. This is a scientific data-quality limit rather than a reason to discard the independently usable diffusivity-fit exercise. 25.7 Measurement limits, laboratory, and worked exercises L55 15:41 A radioactive tracer loses detectable activity during annealing. With the tutorial's rounded 31 Si half-life of2.6h, activity fraction after elapsed time \\(t_h\\) , in hours, is \\(2^{-t_h/2.6}\\) . The code obtains0.0695332 after10h and \\(2.64194\\times10^{-12}\\) after100h. The fraction is not mathematically zero, but initial activity and detector background can make the signal unusable. This restricts that radiotracer protocol; stable-isotope heterostructures with SIMS provide another route to silicon self-diffusion measurements. The laboratory separates source-table fitting from synthetic defect calculations and saves all numerical output . Pair integration uses scaled densities and dimensionless rates and conserves both tracked inventories within \\(5\\times10^{-16}\\) . The source–sink and charged-vacancy panels test different models. Their agreement with their own equations is a numerical check, not evidence that one microscopic mechanism governs a particular wafer. Fermi-level sign. At fixed temperature, raise \\(E_F\\) by \\(\\Delta E>0\\) . How does a singly negative defect population change? Solution Equation(25.2) gives ratio \\(e^{\\Delta E/(k_BT)}\\) for \\(q=-1\\) . A singly positive state instead changes by \\(e^{-\\Delta E/(k_BT)}\\) . These are equilibrium population factors. A diffusion coefficient also depends on the state-specific mobility and correlation, so a population increase alone need not determine its total change when multiple channels compete. Charge-balanced pairing. Repair \\(A_s^0+V^-\\rightleftharpoons P_V^0\\) . Solution Add an electron on the product side: \\(A_s^0+V^-\\rightleftharpoons P_V^0+e^-\\) . Charge is−1 on both sides. Its mass-action relation includes electron activity. Alternatively a negative pair would conserve charge without the electronic reactant, but that is a different state and equilibrium constant. The chemical labels and charge states jointly define the reaction. Source removal. If the source in (25.4) is turned off at time \\(t_0\\) , find the subsequent supersaturation. Solution For \\(t\\geq t_0\\) , solve \\(\\dot x=-k(x-1)\\) with initial value \\(x(t_0)\\) . The result is \\(x(t)=1+[x(t_0)-1]e^{-k(t-t_0)}\\) . A finite sink relaxation time means the diffusion enhancement can persist after source removal. An instantaneous switch to equilibrium defect concentration would contradict this model. Reaction conservation. Identify two invariants of the closed pair equations and explain what destroys each. Solution Adding the dopant equations gives \\(d(n_s+n_P)/dt=0\\) . Adding interstitial and pair equations gives \\(d(n_I+n_P)/dt=0\\) . Boundary dopant loss or precipitation removes the first from the chosen subsystem; defect injection, annihilation, or recombination changes the second. Reactions within the pair subsystem alone preserve both. A model that consumes native defects without tracking them or a reservoir misses this inventory constraint. Arrhenius units. Why must the oxygen fit use kelvin and why does \\(-Rb\\) give Jmol⁻¹? Solution The exponential depends on absolute thermal energy, so reciprocal Celsius is not the Arrhenius coordinate. A slope against \\(1/T\\) has units kelvin. Multiplying by \\(R\\) , in Jmol⁻¹K⁻¹, produces Jmol⁻¹. A per-atom energy in electronvolts instead uses Boltzmann's constant expressed in eVK⁻¹. Using a base10 logarithm adds a factor \\(\\ln10\\) to the conversion. Those conventions cannot be mixed silently. Gettering evidence. A metal concentration falls in the active layer after oxygen precipitation. What additional evidence is needed to call the process successful intrinsic gettering? Solution Show where the metal went, whether precipitate-associated sinks contain it, and whether the active-region electrical properties improved under the actual process history. Compare a control anneal without the intended sink structure and assess precipitate location and defect damage. A lower dissolved concentration can result from precipitation or migration elsewhere; it does not establish universal removal or device benefit. Total atoms remain conserved unless the specimen exchanges them with its environment."}, {"number": 26, "title": "Diffusion and structural relaxation in metallic glasses", "url": "chapter-26.html", "text": "Two metallic-glass specimens at the same temperature can retain different structures because they were cooled and annealed differently. A tracer profile can then record an integrated history of mobility rather than one stationary coefficient. The inverse problem is to determine which part of that history the profile actually contains. Source spine: metallic glasses in L56 and the glass tutorial in L59. The accumulated-time derivation and equal-profile construction are original developments. Metallic glasses are the scope here; the discussion is not a universal transport model for oxide, polymer, or molecular glasses. 26.1 A glass lacks periodicity, not all order L56 00:20 A metallic glass is an amorphous metallic material lacking the long-range translational periodicity of a crystal. Nearest-neighbor coordination, chemical preferences, and short- or medium-range structural correlations can nevertheless be well developed. Broad diffraction features distinguish its average structure from a periodic crystal's sharp Bragg pattern; broadness does not establish that every atom has an uncorrelated random environment. Without an ordinary crystal lattice, crystalline dislocations and grain boundaries are not a complete description of its structural defects. The absence of those particular objects does not imply absence of spatial heterogeneity, free-volume-like configurations, or localized rearrangements. Mechanical and magnetic properties depend on composition and history. In particular, high strength does not establish universal tensile ductility, and no single property ranking applies to every metallic glass. The relevant state includes more than temperature and composition. Cooling rate, deposition route, prior annealing, and residual stress can change the initial configuration. A diffusion experiment therefore needs a structural preparation protocol and time origin. “The diffusivity of the alloy” is ambiguous if the glass continues relaxing during the measurement. Crystal formation during the anneal introduces another phase and requires a multiphase model rather than a stationary amorphous coefficient. Primary X-ray photon correlation work directly examines thermal-history-dependent aging in a particular metallic glass, rather than assuming that only the supercooled liquid evolves: Ruta etal., abstract, introduction, and experimental description , published as Physical Review Letters109,165701 . That experiment concerns Mg–Cu–Y structural dynamics. It supports the relevance of sub-transition aging; it does not provide the lecture's Vitreloy4 tracer coefficients or a universal law for every glass. 26.2 Formation competes with crystallization kinetics L56 03:22 Cooling a liquid slowly can permit nucleation and crystal growth. Cooling sufficiently rapidly can avoid detectable crystallization and retain an amorphous configuration. A time–temperature–transformation diagram records a specified detectable transformed fraction after isothermal holding. Its “nose” indicates a temperature range where crystallization reaches that criterion most quickly. At small undercooling, the thermodynamic driving force is weak; at deep undercooling, atomic rearrangement is slow. Their competition can produce the intermediate-temperature minimum in transformation time. This explanation is kinetic and thermodynamic together. The driving force is not universally an exact inverse-temperature law, and the transformation time is not just a tracer jump time. Nucleation barriers, growth, heterogeneous sites, and the chosen detectable fraction matter. A cooling path that misses one idealized isothermal nose is a useful design picture, but real continuous-cooling kinetics require their own treatment. Critical cooling rate and the thickness that can vitrify are strongly composition dependent. The operational glass-transition temperature \\(T_g\\) is detected through a change in response, such as heat capacity, over the observation time of a particular heating protocol. It depends on heating rate and prior history. A heat-capacity step does not by itself establish an equilibrium second-order phase transition. A later crystallization exotherm identifies another process, not the same event under a different name. The fictive temperature is a parameter describing the structural state, often obtained by matching an extrapolated liquid-state enthalpy or volume to that of the frozen configuration. It need not equal the measured \\(T_g\\) , and no universal small offset between them is implied. Two samples can therefore share the same measurement temperature but have different enthalpy, density, and relaxation state. These distinctions explain why a diffusion comparison must specify its preparation and annealing schedule. Tracer diffusion and viscosity measure related but distinct motions. Viscosity describes resistance to collective shear flow; tracer diffusion follows individual atomic displacements. A hydrodynamic Stokes–Einstein relation introduces a continuum fluid and effective probe-size model that need not hold unchanged for atoms in an aging glass. A qualitative viscosity trend cannot substitute for a species-specific tracer measurement. We do not infer an unmeasured glass coefficient from an assumed atomic radius. 26.3 Relaxation changes the transport state L56 12:39 Structural relaxation changes the amorphous configuration without requiring crystallization. It can alter enthalpy, density, viscosity, mechanical response, and atomic mobility. Relaxation occurs below as well as near the operational transition. A small density change accompanied by a large diffusion change is possible because a rate depends exponentially on free-energy barriers; it does not prove that one scalar free-volume parameter uniquely controls every jump. For a deliberately reduced model, assume an isothermal specimen whose structural state is spatially uniform but changes with elapsed time. Let \\(D(t)\\geq0\\) be a scalar instantaneous tracer diffusivity, in m²s⁻¹, and \\(c(x,t)\\) the dilute tracer molar concentration on the infinite line \\(-\\infty<x<\\infty\\) . With no reaction, drift, or concentration-dependent mobility, \\[\\partial_tc=D(t)\\partial_x^2c,\\qquad \\tau(t)=\\int_0^tD(s)\\,ds.\\tag{26.1}\\] The accumulated variable \\(\\tau\\) has units m², not seconds. If \\(c(x,t)=u(x,\\tau(t))\\) , the chain rule gives \\(\\partial_tc=D(t)\\partial_\\tau u\\) . Wherever \\(D>0\\) , cancellation yields \\(\\partial_\\tau u=\\partial_x^2u\\) . On an interval with zero diffusivity, \\(\\tau\\) and the concentration remain fixed, so the same accumulated-time representation extends continuously through that interval. For integrable nonnegative \\(D\\) , the heat-semigroup construction supplies the corresponding weak solution. For an instantaneous plane source of areal molar amount \\(M\\) , in molm⁻², the ordinary concentration density below applies at times with \\(\\tau(t)>0\\) . If \\(\\tau(t)=0\\) , no spreading has occurred: the tracer remains in its initial plane distribution, rather than having a finite Gaussian density. The second-moment identity extends to that zero-width limit. \\[c(x,t)=\\frac{M}{\\sqrt{4\\pi\\tau(t)}}\\exp[-x^2/(4\\tau(t))],\\qquad \\langle x^2\\rangle=2\\tau(t).\\tag{26.2}\\] The angle brackets denote the normalized tracer second moment; symmetry makes its mean position zero. Integrating the Gaussian conserves \\(\\int c\\,dx=M\\) . This is an exact consequence of the reduced model. Spatially varying aging, nonlocal jump memory, stress drift, or coexistence with crystals does not automatically reduce to one accumulated scalar. Time-dependent boundary concentrations must also be transformed through the mapping \\(t\\mapsto\\tau\\) , rather than held fixed by assumption. 26.4 A final profile loses the time history L56 16:45 Define the time-averaged diffusivity \\(\\bar D(t)=\\tau(t)/t\\) for \\(t>0\\) . Differentiating \\(t\\bar D(t)=\\tau(t)\\) gives \\[D(t)=\\bar D(t)+t\\frac{d\\bar D}{dt}.\\tag{26.3}\\] A single final Gaussian identifies \\(\\tau(t)\\) , hence one average, but not the derivative in (26.3). A time series from identically prepared specimens can constrain the history, subject to uncertainty and numerical differentiation. If preparation differs across anneals, treating their averages as samples of one history is not justified. The laboratory constructs two strictly nonnegative histories over \\(0\\leq t\\leq t_f\\) . History A has constant \\(D_A=D_0\\) . History B has \\(D_B=D_0[1+a(1-2t/t_f)]\\) , with \\(0<a<1\\) . Integration gives \\(\\tau_B=D_0[(1+a)t-at^2/t_f]\\) . At the final time both histories have \\(\\tau=D_0t_f\\) and exactly the same complete pulse profile. At half-time their accumulated values differ. For synthetic \\(D_0=10^{-18}\\) m²s⁻¹, \\(t_f=3600\\) s, and \\(a=0.8\\) , the final accumulated value is \\(3.6\\times10^{-15}\\) m² and root-mean-square width84.8528nm. At half-time, A has \\(1.8\\times10^{-15}\\) m² and B \\(2.52\\times10^{-15}\\) m². A half-time measurement would distinguish them even though an arbitrarily precise final profile would not. Figure26.1. Exact nonidentifiability within the uniform-D(t) model. Intermediate-time profiles supply information that the final profile lacks. The source tutorial at L59 08:30 specifies \\(D(t)=10^{-15}\\,\\mathrm{m^2h^{-1}}[300-u/3-u^2/30]\\) , where \\(u=t/(1\\,\\mathrm h)\\) . Integrating the polynomial gives \\(\\bar D=10^{-15}[300-u/6-u^2/90]\\) m²h⁻¹. At48h, \\(\\bar D=2.664\\times10^{-13}\\) m²h⁻¹, or \\(7.4\\times10^{-17}\\) m²s⁻¹, whereas instantaneous \\(D=2.072\\times10^{-13}\\) m²h⁻¹. The source slide's handwritten answer agrees; the caption's different exponent is transcription noise. The polynomial reaches zero at90h and must not be extended beyond that into negative diffusivity. 26.5 A kink or a small activation volume is not a unique mechanism L56 17:47 The source discusses a Vitreloy4 example with an apparent break between temperature slopes and emphasizes incomplete relaxation as a competing explanation. A kink can reflect different structural histories in the measurements, multiple transport contributions, or a mechanism change. Without controlled equilibration and phase checks, a pair of straight lines does not select among them. Exact compositions and numerical kink temperatures are not inferred from unread data tables here. Pressure supplies another derivative. Define apparent molar activation volume \\(V_{\\mathrm{app}}=-RT(\\partial\\ln D/\\partial p)_T\\) , in m³mol⁻¹, where pressure \\(p\\) is in pascals. A value near zero means that the measured logarithmic rate has weak net pressure sensitivity over the sampled range. It does not prove that no localized configurations participate. Formation and migration contributions can cancel, different channels can compensate, and a crystalline-vacancy decomposition may not be the right description of an amorphous rearrangement. A collective event moves several atoms in a correlated manner. For example, a synthetic chain can shift each atom into the space vacated by its neighbor; a labeled atom moves even if the net displacement of the chain's center is small. Distinguishing tracer displacement from collective charge or density displacement requires tracking identities and cross-correlations. Such motion is not exclusive to glasses. Primary work on a different Zr–Cu–Ni–Al glass reports structural correlations and sub-transition local rearrangement, as discussed in Liu etal., Results and Figure5 discussion . The inspected publisher-indexed text supports that scoped statement, not numerical parameters for Vitreloy4. 26.6 Laboratory, exercises, and worked solutions The laboratory writes the two histories, their identical final profile, the source polynomial calculation, and Gaussian quadrature checks to saved output . The normalized profile integrates to one within \\(2\\times10^{-12}\\) ; its second moment agrees with \\(2\\tau\\) within the finite integration-window error. The continuum equal-profile result is analytic; the zero numerical difference is a check of its implementation. Average versus instantaneous. If \\(D(t)\\) decreases monotonically, compare it with \\(\\bar D(t)\\) . Solution Every earlier value \\(D(s)\\) , for \\(0\\leq s\\leq t\\) , is at least \\(D(t)\\) . Integrate and divide by \\(t\\) to obtain \\(\\bar D(t)\\geq D(t)\\) . Equation(26.3) then gives \\(d\\bar D/dt\\leq0\\) . Equality throughout requires a constant history, apart from sets of zero measure. Source polynomial domain. Find the nonnegative-time endpoint of the tutorial model. Solution Set \\(300-u/3-u^2/30=0\\) , or \\(u^2+10u-9000=0\\) . Its roots are90 and−100. Thus the specified polynomial is nonnegative for \\(0\\leq t\\leq90\\) h. Beyond that it predicts backward diffusion and is physically inadmissible as a scalar Fickian coefficient. A finite-range polynomial fit must not be extrapolated indefinitely. History ambiguity. Prove that infinitely many histories yield the same final pulse profile. Solution Start from any positive history and add an integrable perturbation \\(h(t)\\) with zero integral over the experiment and sufficiently small amplitude to preserve nonnegativity. Its final \\(\\tau\\) is unchanged, so (26.2) gives the identical full profile. There are infinitely many such zero-integral functions. More accurate final depth measurements cannot identify an unobserved time dependence within this model. Boundary transformation. If surface concentration is prescribed as \\(c(0,t)=g(t)\\) , what is its accumulated-time form? Solution Where the map is invertible, write \\(u(0,\\tau)=g(t(\\tau))\\) . A constant surface value stays constant; a time-varying one generally changes its functional form. If \\(D=0\\) over an interval, an ordinary inverse is not unique, and the boundary compatibility must be addressed separately. The simple pulse result avoids this boundary complication by working on the infinite line. Kinetic transition. Why can two heating rates give different apparent \\(T_g\\) without changing composition? Solution The transition is operationally detected when structural relaxation becomes relevant to the observation time. Changing heating rate changes the time spent in each temperature interval and the structure reached before the signal is measured. Composition alone therefore does not fix the observed onset. A thermal protocol and detection convention belong in the reported value. Pressure inference. Does zero apparent activation volume prove independent single-atom jumps? Solution No. It constrains one pressure derivative of the measured rate. Collective and localized models can both have small net derivatives, and contributions with opposite signs can cancel. Additional observables, such as species-dependent displacement correlations, structural dynamics, or controlled history dependence, are needed to discriminate mechanisms. The derivative is useful evidence with limited identifiability."}, {"number": 27, "title": "Ionic solids, defect chemistry, and coupled charge transport", "url": "chapter-27.html", "text": "Adding divalent ions to a monovalent ionic crystal can increase vacancies while decreasing interstitials. Conductivity can initially fall even though one defect population rises. Understanding that behavior requires two separate constraints—mass action and charge neutrality—and a third distinction between moving matter and moving net charge. Source spine: ionic defect chemistry and silver halides in L57, alkali halides and correlations in L58, and the ionic tutorial in L59. The explicit population solutions, minimum, and association model below are original derivations. Numerical inputs are synthetic; no garnet correlation value is transferred to a halide. 27.1 Effective charge is measured relative to a host site L57 00:20 An ionic crystal has chemically distinct cation and anion site sets. In a monovalent AB crystal, the perfect occupied cation site contributes charge \\(+e\\) and the anion site \\(-e\\) , where \\(e>0\\) is elementary charge in coulombs. A cation vacancy has effective charge \\(-e\\) relative to an occupied cation site; an anion vacancy has effective charge \\(+e\\) . These are missing-ion charges relative to the reference crystal, not charges of a material particle placed in the empty site. A divalent cation replacing a monovalent cation has effective charge \\(+e\\) . It can be compensated by one monovalent cation vacancy with effective charge \\(-e\\) . Thus Ca on an Ag site is effectively+1 and an Ag vacancy−1 in units of \\(e\\) . A claim that this substitution automatically creates a−2 vacancy confuses the host charge with the dopant's actual valence. A missing divalent host ion would be a different bookkeeping problem. In Schottky disorder , cation and anion vacancies are produced in a charge- and stoichiometry-preserving combination, with the removed formula unit accommodated at a reservoir such as a surface. In cation Frenkel disorder , a cation leaves its regular site and occupies an interstitial site, producing an oppositely charged vacancy–interstitial pair. Anion Frenkel disorder is defined analogously. Crystal structures differ in coordination and available sites; a particular material's dominant disorder cannot be deduced from charge balance alone. Let \\(N_s\\) be the number of regular cation sites per volume, in m⁻³. We normalize defect number densities by this common reference: \\(v=n_V/N_s\\) , \\(i=n_I/N_s\\) , and dopant concentration \\(d=n_D/N_s\\) . If the interstitial-site density differs from \\(N_s\\) , \\(i\\) is not its occupied site fraction. The corresponding multiplicity factor belongs in the activity or equilibrium constant. Equal charged defect numbers need not mean equal occupancy fractions on different site sets. 27.2 Mass action fixes a product; neutrality fixes a difference L57 06:29 For ideal dilute cation Frenkel disorder at fixed temperature, write \\(vi=K_F\\) , where dimensionless \\(K_F>0\\) includes the site-normalization convention. This mass-action product follows from a formation free energy. With no other charged species, neutrality gives \\(v=i\\) , so each concentration is \\(\\sqrt{K_F}\\) . Neutrality alone cannot specify their common magnitude: it permits every equal positive pair. Now add fully ionized divalent substitutional dopants at normalized concentration \\(d\\geq0\\) , neglecting association and electronic conduction. Positive interstitial and dopant charges balance negative vacancies, giving \\(v-i=d\\) . Substituting \\(i=K_F/v\\) gives \\(v^2-dv-K_F=0\\) . The physical positive solution is \\[v=\\frac{d+\\sqrt{d^2+4K_F}}2,\\qquad i=\\frac{K_F}{v}.\\tag{27.1}\\] The other quadratic root is negative and cannot be a population. When \\(d\\ll\\sqrt{K_F}\\) , both populations remain near the intrinsic value. When \\(d\\gg\\sqrt{K_F}\\) , \\(v\\approx d\\) and \\(i\\approx K_F/d\\) . Doping increases vacancies while suppressing interstitials because the equilibrium product stays fixed. If both Schottky and Frenkel disorder or electronic carriers are important, additional mass-action relations and a fuller neutrality sum must be solved together. Silver halides motivate the cation-Frenkel picture. An interstitial can jump directly between available sites or move by interstitialcy : an interstitial atom displaces a regular atom into another position, so the identity carrying the excess occupancy changes. Tracking the defect position alone does not track one labeled Ag atom. Collinear and noncollinear knock-on geometries can produce different tracer correlations. Vacancy and interstitialcy contributions can coexist, and the appropriate coefficients depend on material and conditions. 27.3 Doping can produce a conductivity minimum L57 20:55 Let \\(\\mu_v,\\mu_i>0\\) be magnitudes of electrical drift mobilities of vacancies and interstitials, in m²V⁻¹s⁻¹. In the independent dilute-carrier approximation, each monovalent population contributes positively to conductivity. With the common normalization above, \\[\\sigma=eN_s(\\mu_vv+\\mu_ii)=eN_s\\left(\\mu_vv+\\mu_iK_F/v\\right).\\tag{27.2}\\] Here \\(\\sigma\\) is in Sm⁻¹. Opposite carrier charges produce opposite drift directions and therefore positive contributions to the same conventional current. At fixed temperature suppose mobilities do not change with dopant concentration. Since \\(v\\) increases monotonically with \\(d\\) , differentiate with respect to \\(v\\) . The minimum satisfies \\(\\mu_v-\\mu_iK_F/v^2=0\\) , hence \\[v_{\\min}=\\sqrt{\\mu_iK_F/\\mu_v},\\quad i_{\\min}=\\sqrt{\\mu_vK_F/\\mu_i},\\quad d_{\\min}=v_{\\min}-i_{\\min}.\\tag{27.3}\\] At that point the vacancy and interstitial conductivity contributions are equal. For nonnegative divalent doping the minimum is accessible only if \\(\\mu_i\\geq\\mu_v\\) ; equality places it at zero doping. If interstitials are much faster, initial suppression of that population outweighs the growing vacancy contribution. At larger doping, the vacancy term eventually dominates. Association, changing mobilities, nonideal activities, or electronic conduction can shift or remove this ideal minimum. The synthetic laboratory takes \\(K_F=10^{-12}\\) and mobility ratio100, reporting conductivity in units \\(eN_s\\mu_v\\) . Initially \\(v=i=10^{-6}\\) and scaled conductivity is \\(1.01\\times10^{-4}\\) . The minimum occurs at \\(d=9.9\\times10^{-6}\\) , with \\(v=10^{-5},i=10^{-7}\\) and scaled conductivity \\(2\\times10^{-5}\\) . These are algebraic teaching inputs, not measured AgCl concentrations or mobilities. Figure27.1. Fixed-temperature ideal Frenkel model. The minimum follows from competing population contributions at fixed mobility, not a minimum in each individual jump rate. 27.4 Schottky intrinsic and extrinsic slopes differ L58 00:20 For a monovalent alkali-halide model with equal cation and anion site densities, let \\(v_c,v_a\\) be their vacancy fractions. Ideal Schottky mass action gives \\(v_cv_a=K_S\\) . Without other charges, \\(v_c=v_a=\\sqrt{K_S}\\) . If \\(K_S=K_{S0}e^{-H_S/(RT)}\\) , where \\(H_S\\) is molar formation enthalpy of a vacancy pair, each isolated vacancy population contains a factor \\(e^{-H_S/(2RT)}\\) . Suppose a cation tracer channel is proportional to \\(v_c e^{-H_m/(RT)}\\) , where \\(H_m\\) is its migration enthalpy and prefactors and correlations are treated as temperature independent. Its intrinsic Arrhenius enthalpy is \\(H_S/2+H_m\\) . With divalent doping strong enough to fix the free cation vacancy population near \\(d\\) , the extrinsic slope is approximately \\(H_m\\) . The anion vacancy population is simultaneously suppressed to \\(K_S/d\\) ; doping one carrier does not leave the counterpart unchanged. Association can invalidate the free-vacancy plateau at low temperature. Close to melting, additional mechanisms or vacancy pairs can matter. A neutral pair can be more abundant at higher temperature even though binding itself is favored at lower temperature: free vacancy formation supplies many more potential partners. In a dilute intrinsic estimate, pair concentration scales as \\(K_av_cv_a=K_aK_S\\) . If \\(K_a\\propto e^{H_b/(RT)}\\) with positive binding enthalpy \\(H_b\\) , the product contains \\(e^{-(H_S-H_b)/(RT)}\\) . For \\(H_S>H_b\\) , absolute pair concentration rises with temperature despite decreasing association strength at fixed free populations. An approximate intrinsic–extrinsic crossover occurs when \\(\\sqrt{K_S}\\sim d\\) . With synthetic \\(K_{S0}=1,H_S=200\\) kJmol⁻¹ and \\(d=10^{-4}\\) , equality gives1305.84K. This estimate neglects association, activity corrections, and phase changes. It is a model design calculation rather than a transition temperature assigned to a named salt. 27.5 Charge displacement includes cross-correlations L58 10:39 For independent dilute carriers of number density \\(n\\) , charge \\(q\\) , and particle diffusion coefficient \\(D\\) , the Einstein relation between diffusion and electrical drift gives \\(\\mu=|q|D/(k_BT)\\) . Multiplying carrier number, charge magnitude, and drift mobility gives the Nernst–Einstein conductivity \\(\\sigma=nq^2D/(k_BT)\\) . Its units are Sm⁻¹. This result assumes the same independent motion underlies thermal spreading and linear electrical response. For a measured partial conductivity \\(\\sigma_j\\) attributed to one specified charge species, define a converted charge diffusivity \\(D_j^\\sigma=k_BT\\sigma_j/(n_jq_j^2)\\) . Its ratio to tracer diffusion is the dimensionless Haven ratio \\(H_{R,j}=D_j^*/D_j^\\sigma\\) . Conductivity itself is not a diffusivity. Total ionic conductivity is a sum of partial contributions, but a single arbitrary \\(n,q\\) cannot convert every multivalent, multispecies total into a uniquely defined species coefficient. The difference is visible in the equilibrium Einstein form for an isotropic three-dimensional system of volume \\(V\\) : \\[\\sigma=\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\left\\langle\\left|\\sum_\\alpha q_\\alpha\\Delta\\mathbf r_\\alpha(t)\\right|^2\\right\\rangle}{6Vk_BTt}.\\tag{27.4}\\] The index \\(\\alpha\\) runs over mobile charged particles, \\(\\Delta\\mathbf r_\\alpha\\) is their unwrapped displacement, and brackets denote an equilibrium ensemble average. The formula assumes a well-defined stationary linear-response diffusive limit. Expanding the square produces same-particle terms and cross-particle terms. A tracer coefficient depends on a labeled particle's mean-square displacement; conductivity also depends on how different particles move together. For a neutral bound pair whose opposite charges translate by the same displacement, their combined charge displacement cancels, even though both atoms move. Its neutral translation contributes to matter transport but not to net electrical current. Dissociation and reassociation can still change conductivity by changing free carriers and correlations. A vacancy representation requires consistent mapping from ionic motion; one must not add vacancy and ion charge displacement as if they were independent carriers describing the same exchanges. A modern lattice-gas study makes the distinction explicit using single-particle factor \\(f\\) and collective factor \\(f_I\\) : \\(D^*=fD_0\\) , \\(D^\\sigma=f_ID_0\\) , hence \\(H_R=f/f_I\\) . See Morgan, PDF pages3–4, Eqs.(1.1)–(1.6) , published as Royal Society Open Science4,170824 . The paper studies garnet geometry; its numerical factors are not halide measurements. Synthetic \\(f=0.6,f_I=0.8\\) give \\(H_R=0.75\\) . For \\(n=10^{27}\\) m⁻³, \\(T=600\\) K, and \\(D^\\sigma=10^{-12}\\) m²s⁻¹, the code computes \\(\\sigma=0.00309875\\) Sm⁻¹ and \\(D^*=7.5\\times10^{-13}\\) m²s⁻¹. 27.6 Association needs free and bound inventories L58 17:46 For an isolated aliovalent-dopant model, let \\(d_0\\) be total dopant fraction, \\(u\\) free positive dopants, \\(v\\) free negative compensating vacancies, and \\(b\\) neutral bound complexes, all using the same normalization. Neglect intrinsic defects. Neutrality gives \\(u=v\\) ; inventory gives \\(d_0=u+b\\) ; dilute association gives \\(b=K_auv\\) . Eliminate \\(b,v\\) to obtain \\[u=v=\\frac{2d_0}{1+\\sqrt{1+4K_ad_0}},\\qquad p_b=b/d_0=1-u/d_0.\\tag{27.5}\\] The rationalized form avoids subtracting two nearly equal numbers at weak association. Synthetic \\(d_0=10^{-4},K_a=10^6\\) gives \\(v=9.51249\\times10^{-6}\\) and bound fraction0.904875. Free vacancies, not total vacancy inventory, supply an independent-carrier conductivity model. Bound complexes may migrate or dissociate; their occupancy alone is not a diffusivity. Homovalent impurities can bind vacancies through elastic and polarization effects without a valence mismatch. Heterovalent impurities add an electrostatic contribution. A five-frequency model can distinguish exchange, rotation, association, dissociation, and distant-host rates, with vacancy return correlations as in Chapter23. Isotope effects also become contribution weighted when several channels transport a tracer; an observed intermediate isotope response does not uniquely determine the channel fractions without their individual responses. The source's association formula illustrates a notation trap. A bounded degree \\(p_b=b/(b+u)\\) is different from association odds \\(r=b/u\\) ; they obey \\(p_b=r/(1+r)\\) . If diffusivity is modeled as a full-occupancy value times occupancy, write \\(D=D_{\\mathrm{sat}}p_b=D_{\\mathrm{sat}}r/(1+r)\\) . At \\(r=1\\) , occupancy is one half; saturation requires \\(r\\to\\infty\\) or \\(p_b\\to1\\) . The L58 slide uses fraction terminology and the odds-shaped formula with inconsistent limiting language. We retain the distinction rather than copy that limit. For an original check with odds0.5 and1, the ratio \\([r/(1+r)]_{0.5}/[r/(1+r)]_1=2/3\\) . These declared inputs are not a reconstruction of the tutorial's separate numerical comparison. 27.7 Laboratory, exercises, and worked solutions L59 11:35 The deterministic laboratory evaluates (27.1)–(27.5), checks the mass-action product and association inventory, and writes actual output . Its plotted conductivity is scaled and its correlation example is synthetic. Neither is a digitized halide dataset. These checks expose population and unit errors without substituting for material-specific measurements. Charge compensation. How many monovalent cation vacancies compensate one divalent dopant on a monovalent cation site? Solution The substitution adds effective charge+1. A vacancy removes one host+1 ion and therefore contributes effective charge−1. One vacancy compensates one dopant. Assigning−2 to that vacancy would overcompensate and violate the stated reference-site bookkeeping. Other charged defects or electronic carriers can change which compensating species is thermodynamically favored, but not these individual effective charges. Population constraints. Derive the strong-doping Frenkel limit and identify which equation fixes each feature. Solution Neutrality requires \\(v-i=d\\) . For \\(d\\gg\\sqrt{K_F}\\) , the positive solution gives \\(v\\approx d\\) . Mass action then fixes \\(i=K_F/v\\approx K_F/d\\) . Neutrality sets the vacancy excess; mass action sets the suppressed minority population. Neither relation alone gives both concentrations. Minimum accessibility. What does the ideal Frenkel model predict when vacancy mobility exceeds interstitial mobility? Solution The unconstrained minimum has \\(v_{\\min}<\\sqrt{K_F}\\) , hence negative \\(d_{\\min}\\) . Positive divalent doping only accesses \\(v\\geq\\sqrt{K_F}\\) . The derivative in that domain is nonnegative, so conductivity rises from the intrinsic point. A minimum is not guaranteed by Frenkel disorder alone; its accessible location depends on mobility contrast. Intrinsic/extrinsic slope. Under the fixed-prefactor assumptions, how can two temperature ranges of one salt have different activation enthalpies without different vacancy migration barriers? Solution Intrinsic vacancy concentration contributes formation factor \\(e^{-H_S/(2RT)}\\) , giving total slope \\(H_S/2+H_m\\) . In a dopant-controlled free-vacancy plateau, the population is approximately fixed, leaving slope \\(H_m\\) . The migration barrier can be unchanged. Low-temperature association adds further population dependence and must be separated from a genuine migration change. Neutral translation. Two opposite monovalent ions in a permanently bound pair translate by the same vector. Compare tracer and charge displacements. Solution Each labeled ion has nonzero squared displacement. The pair's charge displacement is \\(e\\Delta\\mathbf r-e\\Delta\\mathbf r=0\\) . In (27.4), negative cross terms cancel the positive individual terms. Adding independent-ion Nernst–Einstein contributions would miss that cancellation and overestimate conductivity for the permanently bound translation. Dissociation creates a different dynamical situation. Association limits. Obtain weak- and strong-association limits of (27.5). Solution When \\(K_ad_0\\ll1\\) , expanding the square root gives \\(u\\approx d_0\\) and \\(b\\approx K_ad_0^2\\) . When \\(K_ad_0\\gg1\\) , the quadratic balance gives \\(u\\approx\\sqrt{d_0/K_a}\\) and \\(p_b\\approx1-1/\\sqrt{K_ad_0}\\) . Free carrier concentration falls while most dopants are bound. This says nothing by itself about how fast a complex migrates or dissociates. Neutrality, equilibrium, and transport each supply a different part of the model. Their combination explains how more defects can coexist with less current and how atoms can move while their collective charge displacement cancels."}, {"number": 28, "title": "Synthesis: from a transport question to defensible evidence", "url": "chapter-28.html", "text": "A precise concentration profile can be explained by the wrong microscopic story. Completing a diffusion study therefore requires two achievements: a forward model that predicts the measured quantity, and an experiment that distinguishes that model from plausible alternatives. Precision helps only after those alternatives produce different observables. Source spine: the course-wide revision in L60. This chapter organizes its continuum, atomistic, measurement, and materials connections around an original cumulative study. The synthetic nonidentifiability example and proposed extra observable are derived here, not reported measurements of a named material. 28.1 Start with an amount, a frame, and a boundary L60 00:20 Suppose a coating must retain a solute during an anneal. The engineering question concerns the amount remaining in a specified region after a specified time. A tracer experiment instead asks how labeled atoms spread. An electrical measurement asks how charge responds to an applied field. These tasks may involve the same atoms but require different observables and different constitutive reductions. For a conserved molar concentration \\(c\\) , in molm⁻³, and molar flux \\(\\mathbf J\\) , in molm⁻²s⁻¹, a stationary-frame balance is \\(\\partial_tc=-\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf J+S\\) , with source \\(S\\) in molm⁻³s⁻¹. A reaction can remove one chemical state while conserving total atoms across several states. A moving boundary can transfer inventory between regions. A good balance identifies the conserved total before adopting a single-species source term. The constitutive law supplies additional physics. \\(\\mathbf J=-D\\nabla c\\) assumes an appropriate scalar Fickian coefficient; anisotropy requires a tensor, interacting components may require chemical-potential gradients, and an evolving structure may require time-dependent coefficients. In cylindrical geometry, radial area changes with radius. At a moving interface, the concentration jump and interface speed enter conservation. These features belong to the model, not to an after-the-fact adjustment of an inconvenient solution. State the reference frame explicitly. A marker tracks lattice motion relative to a specimen frame; an intrinsic flux is defined relative to a chosen lattice frame. A Matano plane arises from a profile mass-balance construction under its assumptions. Neither plane is automatically the initial contact plane or the marker plane. An apparent marker displacement therefore provides additional information about flux imbalance; it does not establish that every atomic event follows a vacancy mechanism. Boundary preparation selects the forward solution. A finite deposited amount, maintained surface concentration, prescribed flux, and impermeable surface are different conditions. An infinite-line Gaussian can approximate an internal narrow tracer layer before external surfaces matter; a half-space surface source has different normalization. A finite specimen must eventually feel its boundaries. Recording specimen size and source width makes that approximation testable. 28.2 Connect carriers to the quantity an instrument measures L60 07:30 A microscopic description supplies jump lengths, rates, and memory. For independent isotropic jumps of length \\(\\ell\\) at total successful rate \\(\\Gamma\\) in three dimensions, \\(D=\\Gamma\\ell^2/6\\) . A per-neighbor rate differs by a coordination factor. Vacancy return or constrained ordered sequences add correlation. Equilibrium defect formation controls carrier abundance, while migration controls motion conditional on a carrier. Apparent Arrhenius and pressure derivatives can mix these contributions. Thermodynamics supplies another part of the chain. A phase diagram constrains coexistence and solubility; it does not set a reaction-layer width or a diffusion time scale by itself. A local interface can be at equilibrium while the whole diffusion zone is evolving. The number of sequential layers in a couple is not obtained by applying one local phase-rule expression as a global layer-count theorem. CALPHAD free energies and mobility data have distinct roles, and a proprietary simulation demonstration is not reproduced merely by drawing a similar profile. An instrument usually observes a transformed or averaged field. Serial sectioning averages across fast and slow paths. SIMS adds depth calibration, matrix response, and finite source or sputter broadening. Electron-probe resolution can smear a narrow composition gradient. Pulsed-gradient NMR encodes displacement over its gradient timing; relaxation NMR measures spectral responses related to local dynamics through a model. Mechanical loss, Mössbauer broadening, and quasielastic neutron scattering have different time and wavevector windows. Conductivity includes collective charge correlations. Coefficients inferred from these signals become comparable only through their defined forward models. Table28.1. An observable and an informative complementary constraint. Primary observable Typical ambiguity Useful independent constraint One Gaussian tracer profile Initial width, response width, diffusion product Zero-time calibration and an anneal-time series Boundary B-tail product Segregation capacity versus boundary mobility C-regime coefficient or calibrated segregation measurement Deep short-circuit tail Line versus plane pathways and leakage Time variation and spatially resolved path characterization Dopant chemical profile Active fraction and native-defect supply Electrical activation and source–sink controls Ionic conductivity Carrier density and collective correlations Defect chemistry and species-resolved tracer motion Final profile in an aging glass Many histories share one accumulated mobility Identically prepared intermediate-time specimens Material structure determines which alternatives deserve attention. Ordered compounds constrain chemical configurations; nanocrystals add dense, possibly evolving interfacial networks; quasicrystal symmetry constrains the tensor without uniquely identifying phason transport. Multicomponent alloy labels do not establish sluggishness. Semiconductor electronic conditions alter charged populations, while ionic neutrality couples populations of opposite effective charge. A glass can retain local order and relax during an anneal. Each observation should be interpreted within those material-specific conditions, rather than by a universal structural slogan. 28.3 A complete profile does not split a population–mobility product L60 24:51 Consider an original synthetic experiment on a chemically uniform specimen containing an internal narrow tracer layer. Work on the infinite line, in a stationary lattice frame, at fixed temperature. Assume dilute noninteracting tracer, no drift or loss, no fast-path heterogeneity, and a time-independent vacancy-mediated coefficient. Parameterize it as \\[D^*=x_VB_V,\\tag{28.1}\\] where \\(x_V>0\\) is dimensionless vacancy fraction and \\(B_V>0\\) , in m²s⁻¹, packages jump geometry, conditional exchange rate, and tracer correlation per unit vacancy fraction. This is a deliberately restricted mechanism model. It makes no assertion that a correlation factor is directly measured or that all vacancies in a real specimen are equivalent. Let the initial tracer layer be a normalized centered Gaussian with standard deviation \\(\\sigma_0\\) . A centered independent Gaussian measurement response has width \\(\\sigma_R\\) . Constant-coefficient diffusion adds variance \\(2D^*t\\) , and independent Gaussian convolution adds the response variance. Thus the observed normalized profile is \\[p_{\\mathrm{obs}}(x,t)=\\frac{e^{-x^2/[2s^2(t)]}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi s^2(t)}},\\qquad s^2(t)=\\sigma_0^2+2x_VB_Vt+\\sigma_R^2.\\tag{28.2}\\] Here \\(p_{\\mathrm{obs}}\\) has units m⁻¹ and integrates to one; multiplying by a conserved areal tracer amount gives a concentration. The zero mean and full-line geometry are part of the construction. Real source or response shapes need not be Gaussian, but their calibration is still necessary. Model A has \\(x_V=10^{-6},B_V=10^{-10}\\) m²s⁻¹. Model B has \\(x_V=10^{-4},B_V=10^{-12}\\) m²s⁻¹. Both give \\(D^*=10^{-16}\\) m²s⁻¹. With \\(\\sigma_0=20\\) nm, \\(\\sigma_R=15\\) nm, and \\(t=3600\\) s, both give \\(s^2=7.20625\\times10^{-13}\\) m² and observed width0.848896µm. Their entire observed profiles agree, not merely a fitted slope or second moment. More generally, replacing \\(x_V\\) by \\(a x_V\\) and \\(B_V\\) by \\(B_V/a\\) , for any positive factor that keeps the dilute model admissible, leaves every profile at every time unchanged. This proves structural nonidentifiability of the two factors from these tracer profiles. An anneal-time series can separate the slope \\(2D^*\\) from the fixed source-plus-response intercept. It still cannot split \\(x_VB_V\\) . The laboratory uses900,1800,3600,7200s and recovers \\(D^*=10^{-16}\\) m²s⁻¹ together with intercept \\(6.25\\times10^{-16}\\) m². That verifies profile evolution and broadening bookkeeping under the assumed model. It does not tell whether the observed coefficient came from few highly mobile defects or many less effective defects. The distinction is the experimental target of the next section. 28.4 Choose an observable with a different parameter dependence L60 21:47 Suppose a separately calibrated defect measurement gives dimensionless signal \\(y=\\kappa x_V\\) with known positive calibration \\(\\kappa\\) . Differential dilatometry or a calibrated trapping measurement, developed in Chapter6 , can motivate a population constraint, but each needs its own response model, defect specificity, and saturation checks. The linear signal here is a synthetic idealization; it is not a universal positron or volume law. Set synthetic \\(\\kappa=0.5\\) . Models A and B predict signals \\(5\\times10^{-7}\\) and \\(5\\times10^{-5}\\) , respectively. If a measurement resolves these values and the calibration applies to the same defect population present during annealing, it distinguishes the models. Inversion becomes \\(x_V=y/\\kappa\\) and \\(B_V=D^*\\kappa/y\\) . If the specimen is quenched for measurement, defect survival and redistribution during the quench require verification. The information gain is visible algebraically. Define dimensionless logarithmic parameters \\(a=\\ln x_V\\) and \\(b=\\ln[B_V/B_{\\mathrm{ref}}]\\) , with fixed positive reference \\(B_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) in m²s⁻¹. The tracer log observable \\(\\ln[D^*/B_{\\mathrm{ref}}]=a+b\\) has sensitivity row \\((1,1)\\) . The independent signal log \\(\\ln(y/\\kappa)=a\\) has row \\((1,0)\\) . Together they give \\[\\mathsf J=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\det\\mathsf J=-1.\\tag{28.3}\\] The single-observable Jacobian has rank one; the augmented matrix has rank two. This establishes local identifiability of these two positive parameters within the chosen model. It does not establish that every alternative microscopic model has been eliminated. If the calibration \\(\\kappa\\) is also unknown, another parameter enters and the ambiguity can return. Measurement noise and weak sensitivity create practical uncertainty even when rank is formally full. Figure28.1. An independent observable separates a chosen pair of microscopic explanations. Repeating an observable with the same population–mobility product cannot supply the missing direction in parameter space. The same design principle explains the complementary measurements in Table28.1. A C-regime experiment constrains boundary mobility separately from a B product; electrical activation constrains a dopant inventory different from chemical total; charge transport and tracer displacement contain different correlations. A useful extra experiment changes parameter dependence. It should be selected from the ambiguity in the model, not merely because another instrument is available. 28.5 A cumulative study has explicit rejection tests L60 28:57 For the synthetic study, first characterize phase identity, grain and dislocation structure, source width, specimen dimensions, and instrument response. Then measure a time series on equivalently prepared specimens, checking conserved tracer amount and linear variance growth. Persistent non-Gaussian tails, changing phase, or time-dependent slope reject the simple homogeneous stationary model and direct attention to leakage, trapping, or relaxation. A good residual is necessary but cannot identify factors that enter only through one product. Next obtain the independent population observable under conditions linked to the anneal. Fit both observables jointly, propagate calibration uncertainty, and test whether one parameter set predicts all times and conditions. Changing temperature, electronic state, sink density, or pressure can supply further constraints, but each intervention may also change the mechanism or structural state. A temperature sweep across a phase transition cannot be treated as a clean perturbation of one constant model. The scientific report should preserve input units, preparation, measured signals, forward equations, fit domain, residuals, and alternatives not excluded. Original research is needed for empirical mechanism claims; the source revision's bibliography is not reconstructed from unread slide text. Earlier chapters integrate the inspected primary sources beside their supported claims. The reproducible laboratory and saved output complete the synthetic study with equal-profile, time-series, and rank checks. They establish the stated mathematical example, not an experimental material result. 28.6 Cumulative exercises and worked solutions Perfect data. Would exact profiles at every time determine both parameters in (28.1)? Solution No. The transformation \\((x_V,B_V)\\mapsto(ax_V,B_V/a)\\) leaves their product and hence every forward profile unchanged. Exact observations remove statistical uncertainty in the product but leave a continuum of parameter pairs. This is structural nonidentifiability. It differs from a poorly resolved estimate that could improve with more precise data of the same kind. Resolution bias. If one profile is inverted using a zero-width source and perfect instrument despite the stated nonzero widths, what coefficient is inferred? Solution The naive estimate is \\(D_{\\mathrm{naive}}=s^2/(2t)=D^*+(\\sigma_0^2+\\sigma_R^2)/(2t)\\) . It overestimates diffusion. The bias decreases with anneal time for constant widths. A time-series slope separates this fixed intercept from diffusion, but cannot by itself distinguish initial width from instrument width. Zero-time and response calibrations address those separate terms. Unknown calibration. Suppose \\(\\kappa\\) is unknown. Explain why adding the population signal can leave an ambiguity. Solution There are now three unknown positive parameters with observables \\(D^*=x_VB_V\\) and \\(y=\\kappa x_V\\) . The transformation \\(x_V\\to ax_V,B_V\\to B_V/a,\\kappa\\to\\kappa/a\\) leaves both unchanged. A separate calibration or another independent observable is required. An instrument's sensitivity is not equivalent to known parameter specificity. Model rejection. A tracer second moment is linear in time, but the profile has persistent deep tails. Does the moment validate the Gaussian model? Solution No. Many non-Gaussian distributions share the same second moment, and mixtures can have linear variance. Compare the full profile with the instrument-convolved forward prediction and test tail reproducibility, amount conservation, and microstructural localization. A homogeneous Gaussian model predicts all its shape, not only its variance. The tail may motivate fast paths or multiple populations, but its existence alone does not uniquely select either. A research claim. Formulate a defensible conclusion after the two observables agree with Model A and reject Model B. Solution State that, within the homogeneous dilute vacancy model and the calibrated population-response assumptions, the measured tracer coefficient and independent signal support the lower-population/higher-mobility parameter set over the specified alternative. Report uncertainties and the annealing domain. Do not conclude that every possible defect geometry or microscopic correlation model has been excluded. New observables would be needed to decompose the mobility factor or challenge the common mechanism assumption itself. A complete diffusion argument connects conserved inventory, carrier statistics, constitutive law, boundary preparation, instrument response, and identifiable parameters. The final scientific product is a conditional explanation with explicit tests that could overturn it."}];
